{"topics":[],"grammarPoints":[],"lessons":[],"exercises":[],"phrases":{"format":1,"phrases":[{"id":"phrase.a-bit-more-clarity","text":"a bit more clarity","kind":"collocation","form":"a bit more clarity","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"a need for greater understanding or precision.","example":"The instructions need a bit more clarity for new users to follow them easily.","plain":"The instructions need to be clearer for new users to follow them easily.","note":"Gentle way to suggest something is unclear.","audio":"audio/cd98965a73735045.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-challenging-role","text":"a challenging role","kind":"collocation","form":"a challenging role","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A job position that requires significant effort, skill, and problem-solving abilities.","example":"I'm looking for a challenging role where I can continuously develop my expertise.","plain":"I'm looking for a job position that requires significant effort where I can continuously develop my expertise.","note":"Indicates ambition and a desire for growth, not a fear of difficulty.","audio":"audio/5d31485e289ab550.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-clear-advantage","text":"a clear advantage","kind":"collocation","form":"a clear advantage","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A distinct and obvious benefit or superiority over others.","example":"This new software offers a clear advantage in terms of data processing speed and accuracy.","plain":"This new software is distinctly better because of its faster and more accurate data processing.","note":"Used to highlight a strong positive point that is easily recognizable.","audio":"audio/d55a60652f37276c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-clear-understanding-of","text":"a clear understanding of","kind":"collocation","form":"a clear understanding of","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A precise and comprehensive grasp of a topic, situation, or requirements.","example":"Do you have a clear understanding of the challenges facing this industry?","plain":"Do you have a precise and comprehensive grasp of the challenges facing this industry?","note":"Indicates thorough comprehension. Often used in questions to assess knowledge.","audio":"audio/1034fcb54ea5cc68.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-collaborative-effort","text":"a collaborative effort","kind":"collocation","form":"a collaborative effort","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Work or activity done jointly by several people or groups.","example":"Achieving these ambitious targets will require a collaborative effort from every department.","plain":"Reaching these big goals will need everyone in every department to work together.","note":"Emphasizes teamwork and shared responsibility for success.","audio":"audio/900ab751ceab9cba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-collaborative-environment","text":"a collaborative environment","kind":"collocation","form":"a collaborative environment","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A workplace setting where teamwork, cooperation, and shared goals are emphasized.","example":"I thrive in a collaborative environment where ideas are openly shared and discussed.","plain":"I thrive in a workplace setting where teamwork and shared goals are emphasized.","note":"A positive term to describe a desired work culture, often used by candidates.","audio":"audio/1d0d66a15374889c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-collaborative-spirit","text":"a collaborative spirit","kind":"collocation","form":"a collaborative spirit","level":"C2","situations":["interviews","persuading"],"meaning":"A willingness and eagerness to work together with others towards a common goal.","example":"Our team thrives because everyone brings a collaborative spirit to daily tasks.","plain":"Our team thrives because everyone brings a willingness to work together to daily tasks.","note":"Emphasizes teamwork and positive interpersonal dynamics.","audio":"audio/f6682d334a82195e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-common-objective","text":"a common objective","kind":"collocation","form":"a common objective","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A shared goal or aim that all members of a group are working towards.","example":"Despite our different departmental goals, client satisfaction remains a common objective for us all.","plain":"Even though our departments have different goals, we all share the aim of making clients happy.","note":"Emphasizes unity and shared purpose, useful for building consensus.","audio":"audio/26204e7dc9f77db4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-compelling-reason","text":"a compelling reason","kind":"collocation","form":"a compelling reason","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A very strong and convincing justification for something.","example":"There's a compelling reason to invest in renewable energy: long-term cost savings and environmental benefits.","plain":"There's a very strong justification to put money into green energy: it saves money and helps the environment.","note":"Highlights a powerful and persuasive argument.","audio":"audio/7083f38826d07e9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-compelling-vision","text":"a compelling vision","kind":"collocation","form":"a compelling vision","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"An inspiring and attractive idea of what the future could be, which motivates people.","example":"The CEO presented a compelling vision for the company's future, which really energized the team.","plain":"The CEO showed an inspiring idea for the company's future, which truly motivated the team.","note":"Describes a powerful and attractive future state that can win people over.","audio":"audio/e2d3b50df6baae2e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-constructive-critique","text":"a constructive critique","kind":"collocation","form":"a constructive critique","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A detailed analysis and assessment of something, intended to be helpful and improve it.","example":"I'd like to offer a constructive critique of your project proposal.","plain":"I'd like to offer helpful feedback on your project proposal.","note":"Emphasizes that the criticism is intended to build up, not tear down.","audio":"audio/c80cac626a37417d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-constructive-dialogue","text":"a constructive dialogue","kind":"collocation","form":"a constructive dialogue","level":"C2","situations":["feedback","persuading"],"meaning":"a productive discussion aimed at improvement.","example":"I hope we can have a constructive dialogue about these project challenges.","plain":"I hope we can have a productive discussion about these project challenges.","note":"Emphasizes a positive, problem-solving approach to discussion.","audio":"audio/5b7ba3d01d319f0f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-counter-argument-might-be","text":"A counter-argument might be","kind":"collocation","form":"A counter-argument might be","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a potential argument that opposes or challenges a previously stated point.","example":"A counter-argument might be that the sample size was too small to generalize the findings.","plain":"An opposing argument could be that the sample size was too small to generalize the findings.","note":"A useful phrase for introducing a contrasting or opposing viewpoint in a balanced discussion.","audio":"audio/790e080ebc53b040.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-critical-juncture","text":"a critical juncture","kind":"collocation","form":"a critical juncture","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A crucial or decisive point in time, often when important decisions must be made.","example":"We are at a critical juncture in the project, and our next steps will determine its ultimate success.","plain":"We are at a very important point in the project, and what we do next will decide if it succeeds.","note":"Emphasizes the high stakes and importance of current decisions.","audio":"audio/2972218c94013d88.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-crucial-factor","text":"a crucial factor","kind":"collocation","form":"a crucial factor","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A decisive or extremely important element that influences an outcome.","example":"Employee engagement will be a crucial factor in the successful implementation of these changes.","plain":"How involved employees are will be an extremely important element for these changes to succeed.","note":"Emphasizes something that is absolutely essential for success or failure.","audio":"audio/cac4804fdd8c6b6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-crucial-point","text":"a crucial point","kind":"collocation","form":"a crucial point","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A very important or decisive detail or argument.","example":"You've raised a crucial point about the long-term maintenance costs of this new system.","plain":"You've brought up a very important detail about how much this new system will cost to maintain over time.","note":"Acknowledges the significance of a particular piece of information or argument.","audio":"audio/27e04140123606bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-delicate-matter","text":"a delicate matter","kind":"collocation","form":"a delicate matter","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A sensitive issue that requires careful handling and tact.","example":"Discussing salary adjustments is always a delicate matter.","plain":"Discussing salary adjustments is always a sensitive issue.","note":"Emphasizes the need for careful communication due to sensitivity.","audio":"audio/7e881709c0557b44.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-different-angle","text":"a different angle","kind":"collocation","form":"a different angle","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A new or alternative way of looking at a situation or problem.","example":"Perhaps we could approach this from a different angle to find a solution.","plain":"Perhaps we could approach this in a new way to find a solution.","note":"Suggests exploring new perspectives.","audio":"audio/90fd5ff5ea69a6a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-diplomatic-approach","text":"a diplomatic approach","kind":"collocation","form":"a diplomatic approach","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A sensitive and tactful way of dealing with difficult situations or people.","example":"I believe in taking a diplomatic approach when resolving conflicts within a team.","plain":"I believe in taking a sensitive way when resolving conflicts within a team.","note":"Shows emotional intelligence and conflict resolution skills.","audio":"audio/f0312676af03c7d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-fair-assessment","text":"a fair assessment","kind":"collocation","form":"a fair assessment","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"An evaluation or judgment that is reasonable and unbiased.","example":"I think that's a fair assessment of the project's challenges and successes.","plain":"I think that's a reasonable judgment of the project's challenges and successes.","note":"Indicates agreement with the accuracy and impartiality of the feedback.","audio":"audio/ee43784186b3d8dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-fair-point","text":"a fair point","kind":"collocation","form":"a fair point","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A reasonable and valid argument or observation.","example":"That's a fair point, and we should definitely take it into consideration.","plain":"That's a reasonable point, and we should definitely take it into consideration.","note":"Acknowledges the validity of someone's contribution.","audio":"audio/97758b8fdbc8e22f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-fundamental-principle","text":"a fundamental principle","kind":"collocation","form":"a fundamental principle","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A basic and essential rule or belief that forms the foundation of a system or idea.","example":"Transparency is a fundamental principle that guides all our interactions with clients and partners.","plain":"Being open is a basic rule that guides how we deal with clients and partners.","note":"Refers to a core belief or rule that is non-negotiable.","audio":"audio/798791f27e0c612e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-gentle-reminder","text":"a gentle reminder","kind":"collocation","form":"a gentle reminder","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite way to bring something to someone's attention again.","example":"Just a gentle reminder that the report is due by end of day tomorrow.","plain":"Please remember that the report is due by end of day tomorrow.","note":"Softens a request or a reminder of an obligation.","audio":"audio/e3bcb61c8754b23d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-growth-mindset","text":"a growth mindset","kind":"collocation","form":"a growth mindset","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"The belief that abilities and intelligence can be developed through dedication and hard work.","example":"We look for candidates who demonstrate a growth mindset and are eager to learn new skills.","plain":"We look for candidates who believe their abilities can be developed and are eager to learn new skills.","note":"A highly valued trait in modern workplaces, indicating adaptability and a desire for self-improvement.","audio":"audio/f71cd156e7faf08f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-hands-on-approach","text":"a hands-on approach","kind":"collocation","form":"a hands-on approach","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A method of working that involves direct personal involvement rather than just supervision.","example":"I prefer a hands-on approach to project management, working closely with the team.","plain":"I prefer a method of working that involves direct personal involvement in project management, working closely with the team.","note":"Describes a practical and engaged work style. Often valued in dynamic roles.","audio":"audio/e9236fd5c12ab496.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-highly-collaborative-environment","text":"a highly collaborative environment","kind":"collocation","form":"a highly collaborative environment","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A workplace setting where teamwork, cooperation, and shared goals are strongly emphasized.","example":"I thrive in a highly collaborative environment where diverse perspectives are valued.","plain":"I thrive in a workplace setting where teamwork and diverse perspectives are strongly emphasized.","note":"Emphasizes the importance of teamwork even more than 'a collaborative environment'.","audio":"audio/f7defe0f8522b7b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-key-aspect","text":"a key aspect","kind":"collocation","form":"a key aspect","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A very important part or feature of something.","example":"Customer feedback is a key aspect of our product development cycle, guiding our improvements.","plain":"What customers say is a very important part of how we develop products, helping us make them better.","note":"Similar to \"key consideration\" but refers more to a component or feature.","audio":"audio/96e4859d9182e3cb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-key-consideration","text":"a key consideration","kind":"collocation","form":"a key consideration","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"An important factor or element that must be taken into account when making a decision.","example":"Cost-effectiveness is always a key consideration when evaluating new vendor contracts.","plain":"How cost-effective something is always an important factor when we look at new vendor agreements.","note":"Highlights a crucial aspect that influences judgment or choice.","audio":"audio/514cd7d3a56bba86.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-key-difference-lies-in","text":"A key difference lies in","kind":"frame","form":"A key difference lies in","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Points out the most important distinction between two or more things.","example":"A key difference lies in the conceptualization of 'well-being' across these two studies.","plain":"The most important difference is how 'well-being' is defined in these two studies.","note":"A formal way to highlight a crucial point of divergence.","audio":"audio/75643d1eae3f27d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-key-factor","text":"a key factor","kind":"collocation","form":"a key factor","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"An important element or influence that contributes significantly to a result or situation.","example":"Effective communication was a key factor in the successful completion of the project.","plain":"Effective communication was an important element in the successful completion of the project.","note":"Highlights the most significant cause or influence. Useful for analysis.","audio":"audio/cf28f24b74675cc5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-learning-opportunity","text":"a learning opportunity","kind":"collocation","form":"a learning opportunity","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A situation or experience from which one can gain knowledge or skills.","example":"While the project didn't go as planned, it was certainly a learning opportunity.","plain":"While the project didn't go as planned, we certainly learned a lot from it.","note":"A positive reframing of a mistake or failure, emphasizing growth.","audio":"audio/2f04f87276588d15.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-legitimate-concern","text":"a legitimate concern","kind":"collocation","form":"a legitimate concern","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A valid and reasonable worry or issue that deserves attention.","example":"I believe that the environmental impact of our operations is a legitimate concern for our stakeholders.","plain":"I think our operations' effect on the environment is a valid worry for those invested in our company.","note":"Validates someone's worry, showing respect and a willingness to address it.","audio":"audio/5cf79a798298bdfb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-little-hesitant-about","text":"a little hesitant about","kind":"frame","form":"a little hesitant about","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To feel slightly unwilling or reluctant to do or agree with something.","example":"I'm a little hesitant about committing to such a tight deadline without more resources.","plain":"I'm slightly unwilling to commit to such a tight deadline without more resources.","note":"Expresses mild reluctance or caution, often used to voice a concern politely.","audio":"audio/31861477a1bc961b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-matter-of-priority","text":"a matter of priority","kind":"collocation","form":"a matter of priority","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Something that needs to be dealt with before other things.","example":"I think addressing client concerns should be a matter of priority.","plain":"I think addressing client concerns should be dealt with before other things.","note":"Emphasizes urgency or importance.","audio":"audio/408a3b3dc59c296d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-minor-adjustment","text":"a minor adjustment","kind":"collocation","form":"a minor adjustment","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"a small change or modification.","example":"I think this report just needs a minor adjustment to the formatting.","plain":"I think this report just needs a small change to the formatting.","note":"Understated way to suggest a small correction. Diplomatic.","audio":"audio/88c1e41add4d23e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-minor-point","text":"a minor point","kind":"collocation","form":"a minor point","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A small or less important detail or issue.","example":"That's a minor point, but it's worth clarifying before we proceed.","plain":"That's a small detail, but it's worth clarifying before we proceed.","note":"Used to downplay the significance of a criticism, making it easier to accept.","audio":"audio/0ae3788232ad34ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-mutually-beneficial-outcome","text":"A mutually beneficial outcome","kind":"collocation","form":"a mutually beneficial outcome","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","persuading"],"meaning":"A result where all parties involved gain something positive from the agreement.","example":"Our goal is always to achieve a mutually beneficial outcome for both our clients and our team.","plain":"Our goal is always to achieve a result that benefits both our clients and our team.","note":"Professional and diplomatic, emphasizing collaboration and shared success.","audio":"audio/a602c4dfb609f4c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-new-perspective","text":"a new perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"a new perspective","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A fresh or different way of looking at a situation or problem.","example":"Hearing from the junior staff offered a new perspective on the challenges of daily operations.","plain":"Listening to the junior staff gave us a fresh way to see the difficulties of everyday work.","note":"Encourages innovative thinking and a broader understanding.","audio":"audio/5eebd367e1f732e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-nuanced-approach","text":"a nuanced approach","kind":"collocation","form":"a nuanced approach","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A method that considers subtle differences and complexities in a situation.","example":"Addressing this sensitive issue requires a nuanced approach, considering all perspectives.","plain":"Dealing with this delicate problem needs a careful method that looks at all viewpoints.","note":"Implies sophistication and a deep understanding of complexities.","audio":"audio/ea3d5b920685afed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-persuasive-argument","text":"a persuasive argument","kind":"collocation","form":"a persuasive argument","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"An argument that is effective in convincing someone to believe or do something.","example":"She presented a persuasive argument for adopting a four-day work week, highlighting productivity gains.","plain":"She gave a convincing argument for a four-day work week, showing how it would make us more productive.","note":"Focuses on the effectiveness of the argument in winning people over.","audio":"audio/d5acd2a7e12cd057.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-pertinent-question","text":"A pertinent question","kind":"collocation","form":"a pertinent question","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A question that is highly relevant and appropriate to the topic.","example":"That's a pertinent question regarding the ethical implications of our AI development.","plain":"That's a very relevant question regarding the ethical implications of our AI development.","note":"More formal and sophisticated than \"good question,\" indicating deep relevance.","audio":"audio/0f09eaadcb730d21.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-pleasure-to-work-with","text":"a pleasure to work with","kind":"chunk","form":"a pleasure to work with","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"someone who is enjoyable and easy to collaborate with.","example":"You're always so collaborative; you're truly a pleasure to work with.","plain":"You're always so collaborative; it's truly enjoyable to work with you.","note":"Personal, warm praise. Focuses on interpersonal skills.","audio":"audio/e39e9eeb39f7a788.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-point-of-concern","text":"a point of concern","kind":"collocation","form":"a point of concern","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"an issue that causes worry or unease.","example":"The budget overrun is a point of concern that we need to address urgently.","plain":"The budget overrun is an issue that causes worry that we need to address urgently.","note":"More formal than \"a slight concern,\" indicates a more serious issue.","audio":"audio/bdc863a941a11dff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-point-of-contention","text":"a point of contention","kind":"collocation","form":"a point of contention","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A specific issue or topic that causes disagreement or argument.","example":"The allocation of resources became a point of contention during the team meeting.","plain":"The allocation of resources caused disagreement during the team meeting.","note":"Describes a specific area of dispute, often used in formal discussions.","audio":"audio/be6a41600fbc70c1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-positive-attitude","text":"a positive attitude","kind":"collocation","form":"a positive attitude","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"An optimistic and constructive way of thinking or feeling.","example":"Even during challenging projects, I maintain a positive attitude and motivate my team.","plain":"Even during challenging projects, I maintain an optimistic outlook and motivate my team.","note":"General positive trait; shows resilience and leadership.","audio":"audio/b035b3513484aa53.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-positive-influence","text":"a positive influence","kind":"collocation","form":"a positive influence","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Someone or something that has a good effect on others or a situation.","example":"Your calm demeanor is a positive influence on the whole team during stressful periods.","plain":"Your calm demeanor has a good effect on the whole team during stressful periods.","note":"Praises impact on others' morale or performance.","audio":"audio/bca872d397349de5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-positive-outcome","text":"a positive outcome","kind":"collocation","form":"a positive outcome","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A favorable or successful result.","example":"With careful planning and execution, I'm confident we can achieve a positive outcome for this project.","plain":"With good planning and doing the work well, I believe we can get a good result for this project.","note":"A general term for a good result, often used to express optimism.","audio":"audio/6499f3ed2b89f326.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-potential-area","text":"a potential area","kind":"collocation","form":"a potential area","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A possible aspect or part that could be developed or improved.","example":"This could be a potential area where we might face some resistance.","plain":"This could be an aspect where we might face some resistance.","note":"Hedges a suggestion for improvement or a foreseen problem.","audio":"audio/923eb7cd085298ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-potential-bottleneck","text":"a potential bottleneck","kind":"collocation","form":"a potential bottleneck","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A point in a process where progress is likely to slow down or stop.","example":"I foresee a potential bottleneck in the approval process if we don't streamline it.","plain":"I foresee a point where the approval process is likely to slow down if we don't streamline it.","note":"Identifies a future problem area.","audio":"audio/b765bb04aaa3d448.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-potential-drawback","text":"a potential drawback","kind":"collocation","form":"a potential drawback","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A possible disadvantage or negative aspect of a plan or situation.","example":"A potential drawback of this approach is the increased workload for the administrative staff.","plain":"A possible negative point of this method is that it will make more work for the office staff.","note":"Used to identify a possible negative consequence, often when weighing pros and cons.","audio":"audio/1682b15cb2c7b35a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-pressing-issue","text":"a pressing issue","kind":"collocation","form":"a pressing issue","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"An urgent or important problem that needs immediate attention.","example":"Customer retention is a pressing issue that requires our immediate focus.","plain":"Customer retention is an urgent problem that requires our immediate focus.","note":"Highlights the urgency and importance of a topic.","audio":"audio/3ad851cceb0e1681.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-proactive-approach","text":"a proactive approach","kind":"collocation","form":"a proactive approach","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A method of dealing with situations by causing things to happen rather than waiting for them.","example":"I always take a proactive approach to problem-solving, anticipating potential issues.","plain":"I always deal with situations by causing things to happen rather than waiting for them, anticipating potential issues.","note":"Highly valued in dynamic roles, indicating initiative and foresight.","audio":"audio/7049fa8e1859d180.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-proven-track-record","text":"a proven track record","kind":"collocation","form":"a proven track record","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Evidence of past successes and achievements in a particular area.","example":"Our ideal candidate will have a proven track record in managing large-scale projects.","plain":"Our ideal candidate will have evidence of past successes in managing large-scale projects.","note":"Frequently used in job descriptions and by candidates to highlight their reliability and success.","audio":"audio/b3971d28003e1bd7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-quick-point","text":"a quick point","kind":"collocation","form":"a quick point","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A brief comment or observation.","example":"I'd like to make a quick point about the timeline before we move on.","plain":"I'd like to make a brief comment about the timeline before we move on.","note":"Signals a short contribution. Polite and efficient.","audio":"audio/6aa723cf3360d30a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-quick-rundown","text":"a quick rundown","kind":"collocation","form":"a quick rundown","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A brief summary or overview of something.","example":"Before we dive into details, let me give you a quick rundown of the project's current status.","plain":"Before we dive into details, let me give you a brief summary of the project's current status.","note":"Common for providing context efficiently. Can be used by anyone.","audio":"audio/98d7e5e7cceeaeda.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-real-strength","text":"a real strength","kind":"collocation","form":"a real strength","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"something someone is very good at or excels in.","example":"Your ability to simplify complex ideas is a real strength, and it really helped the team.","plain":"You are very good at simplifying complex ideas, and it really helped the team.","note":"Positive, specific praise. Focuses on a skill or quality.","audio":"audio/d0f4770232ff26f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-results-oriented-approach","text":"a results-oriented approach","kind":"collocation","form":"a results-oriented approach","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A method of working that focuses primarily on achieving specific outcomes and goals.","example":"My management style is characterized by a results-oriented approach and clear goal setting.","plain":"My management style focuses primarily on achieving specific outcomes and clear goal setting.","note":"Highlights efficiency and effectiveness in achieving objectives. Desirable in many roles.","audio":"audio/c542082e7b1b06d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-balance","text":"a sense of balance","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of balance","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"An understanding of how different elements relate to each other, or a stable mental state.","example":"Achieving a good work-life balance is important, and I seek a company that supports a sense of balance.","plain":"Achieving a good work-life balance is important, and I seek a company that supports a stable mental state.","note":"Can refer to work-life or mental stability; shows self-awareness.","audio":"audio/b533a5a8ed528011.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-belonging","text":"a sense of belonging","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of belonging","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A feeling of being accepted and valued by a group or community.","example":"It's important for me to work in an environment where everyone feels a sense of belonging.","plain":"It's important for me to work in an environment where everyone feels accepted by a group.","note":"Highlights desire for inclusive and supportive workplace culture.","audio":"audio/563fc846e93a61c3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-community","text":"a sense of community","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of community","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A feeling of shared identity and connection among a group of people.","example":"I value companies that actively foster a sense of community among their employees.","plain":"I value companies that actively foster a feeling of shared identity among their employees.","note":"Similar to belonging, but often broader, referring to the entire organization.","audio":"audio/6ec104b875cf444e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-compassion","text":"a sense of compassion","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of compassion","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A feeling of sympathy and concern for the sufferings or misfortunes of others.","example":"Our company culture emphasizes a sense of compassion towards both employees and customers.","plain":"Our company culture emphasizes a feeling of sympathy for both employees and customers.","note":"Highlights a caring and supportive organizational culture.","audio":"audio/24f3fdecd82a0605.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-perspective","text":"a sense of perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of perspective","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"The ability to judge the relative importance of things.","example":"It's crucial to maintain a sense of perspective, especially when facing minor setbacks.","plain":"It's crucial to maintain the ability to judge the importance of things, especially when facing minor setbacks.","note":"Shows maturity and ability to prioritize; useful for managing stress.","audio":"audio/638d7eb358686ec7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-proportion","text":"a sense of proportion","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of proportion","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"The ability to judge the relative importance of things and react appropriately.","example":"I always try to keep a sense of proportion, not overreacting to small issues.","plain":"I always try to keep the ability to judge relative importance, not overreacting to small issues.","note":"Similar to \"sense of perspective,\" emphasizing balanced reactions.","audio":"audio/577ea47f9ca3ea54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sense-of-urgency","text":"a sense of urgency","kind":"collocation","form":"a sense of urgency","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A feeling that something is important and needs to be dealt with quickly.","example":"I approach all tasks with a sense of urgency, ensuring deadlines are consistently met.","plain":"I approach all tasks with a feeling that they need immediate attention, ensuring deadlines are consistently met.","note":"Shows proactivity and commitment to timely delivery.","audio":"audio/e6ad762a367d3885.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-shared-understanding","text":"a shared understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"a shared understanding","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A common agreement or comprehension among a group of people about a particular matter.","example":"Before moving forward, we need to establish a shared understanding of the project's scope.","plain":"Before we continue, we must all agree on what the project includes.","note":"Crucial for collaboration and avoiding miscommunication; implies consensus.","audio":"audio/e8bd0a2dad8b78b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-significant-asset","text":"a significant asset","kind":"collocation","form":"a significant asset","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Someone or something that is very valuable and beneficial.","example":"Your problem-solving skills are a significant asset to the team.","plain":"Your problem-solving skills are very valuable to the team.","note":"Strong, formal praise, emphasizing value.","audio":"audio/b994d82641bb06b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-significant-benefit","text":"a significant benefit","kind":"collocation","form":"a significant benefit","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A noticeable and important positive outcome or advantage.","example":"Implementing flexible working hours would bring a significant benefit to staff morale and retention.","plain":"Having flexible work hours would greatly improve staff happiness and keep employees longer.","note":"Emphasizes a substantial positive impact or gain.","audio":"audio/9bf4b48450c29fa5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-significant-contribution","text":"a significant contribution","kind":"collocation","form":"a significant contribution","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"an important part played in achieving something.","example":"Your analysis was a significant contribution to our understanding of the market trends.","plain":"Your analysis was an important part of our understanding of the market trends.","note":"Formal, acknowledges impact. Often used in team or project reviews.","audio":"audio/b7fbf6be769bb7d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-significant-factor","text":"A significant factor","kind":"collocation","form":"a significant factor","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"An important element or contributing cause.","example":"Employee engagement is a significant factor in overall team productivity.","plain":"Employee engagement is an important element in overall team productivity.","note":"A common academic and professional collocation to describe important influences.","audio":"audio/063f31df077920b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-slight-adjustment","text":"a slight adjustment","kind":"collocation","form":"a slight adjustment","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A small change or modification.","example":"We might need to make a slight adjustment to the budget for next quarter.","plain":"We might need to make a small change to the budget for next quarter.","note":"Understated way to suggest a change, implying it's not major.","audio":"audio/0f69ab5e3127e93e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-slight-complication","text":"a slight complication","kind":"collocation","form":"a slight complication","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A minor difficulty that makes a situation more complex.","example":"There's a slight complication with the scheduling, but I think we can work around it.","plain":"There's a minor difficulty with the scheduling, but I think we can work around it.","note":"An understated way to refer to a small, manageable problem.","audio":"audio/00325c179a3e25a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-slight-disconnect","text":"a slight disconnect","kind":"collocation","form":"a slight disconnect","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A small lack of connection or understanding between different parts or ideas.","example":"There seems to be a slight disconnect between the project goals and the proposed timeline.","plain":"There seems to be a small lack of connection between the project goals and the proposed timeline.","note":"Diplomatic way to point out an inconsistency or lack of alignment.","audio":"audio/92b073dd7435fb74.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-slight-misunderstanding","text":"a slight misunderstanding","kind":"collocation","form":"a slight misunderstanding","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A minor error in comprehension or interpretation.","example":"I think there might be a slight misunderstanding about the project scope.","plain":"I think there might be a minor error in understanding about the project scope.","note":"Diplomatic way to correct someone or point out an error.","audio":"audio/86d2cd52e1630557.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-slight-oversight","text":"a slight oversight","kind":"collocation","form":"a slight oversight","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"a small mistake made by failing to notice something.","example":"There was a slight oversight in the budget calculations, but it's easily fixed.","plain":"There was a small mistake in the budget calculations, but it's easily fixed.","note":"Diplomatic way to point out a small error. Downplays blame.","audio":"audio/54765bf03a75d6b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-standout-performance","text":"a standout performance","kind":"collocation","form":"a standout performance","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"An exceptionally good effort or result that is noticeably better than others.","example":"Your presentation yesterday was a standout performance; everyone was very impressed.","plain":"Your presentation yesterday was exceptionally good; everyone was very impressed.","note":"Strong praise, often used for a single event or achievement.","audio":"audio/2eb7f84269e20359.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strategic-imperative","text":"a strategic imperative","kind":"collocation","form":"a strategic imperative","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Something that is absolutely necessary for achieving long-term goals or competitive advantage.","example":"Digital transformation is no longer optional; it's a strategic imperative for modern businesses.","plain":"Changing to digital is no longer a choice; it's absolutely necessary for businesses today.","note":"Highlights something as a critical, non-negotiable requirement for future success.","audio":"audio/207cbac124ad4de1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strategic-shift","text":"a strategic shift","kind":"collocation","form":"a strategic shift","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A significant change in the overall plan or direction of an organization or project.","example":"I'd like to make a case for a strategic shift towards digital-first content creation.","plain":"I want to argue for a big change in our plan to focus on creating digital content first.","note":"Implies a major change in direction, often with long-term implications.","audio":"audio/7d7607c7725a3eeb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strategic-thinker","text":"a strategic thinker","kind":"collocation","form":"a strategic thinker","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Describes someone who can plan effectively for the long term, considering overall goals.","example":"We're looking for a strategic thinker who can contribute to our long-term vision.","plain":"We're looking for someone who can plan effectively for the long term and contribute to our vision.","note":"Highlights the ability to see the bigger picture and plan for future success.","audio":"audio/99f4a028e59d0a7d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-argument","text":"a strong argument","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong argument","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A well-supported and convincing set of reasons for a particular viewpoint.","example":"The data provides a strong argument for increasing our online advertising budget next quarter.","plain":"The data gives us good reasons to spend more on online ads next quarter.","note":"Refers to a robust and persuasive line of reasoning.","audio":"audio/518f9a72ce83789e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-communicator","text":"a strong communicator","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong communicator","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Describes someone who can express ideas clearly and effectively, both verbally and in writing.","example":"The role requires a strong communicator who can present complex information to diverse audiences.","plain":"The role requires someone who can express ideas clearly and effectively to diverse audiences.","note":"A fundamental skill in almost any professional role, highly valued.","audio":"audio/82a2e93daea96773.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-emphasis-on","text":"a strong emphasis on","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong emphasis on","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Indicates that particular importance or priority is given to something.","example":"Our company places a strong emphasis on employee well-being and professional growth.","plain":"Our company gives particular importance to employee well-being and professional growth.","note":"Useful for describing company values or priorities. Can be used by both sides.","audio":"audio/2a30da6f1e2ff0f6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-fit-for","text":"a strong fit for","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong fit for","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Describes someone or something that is highly suitable or compatible with a role or environment.","example":"I believe my background makes me a strong fit for your innovative team.","plain":"I believe my background makes me highly suitable for your innovative team.","note":"Commonly used by candidates to express their suitability for a position or company culture.","audio":"audio/820578b9bf983e58.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-foundation","text":"a strong foundation","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong foundation","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A solid and reliable basis upon which something can be built or developed.","example":"Our current client relationships provide a strong foundation for expanding into new markets.","plain":"Our existing client connections give us a solid basis to grow into new markets.","note":"Implies stability and potential for future growth, often used metaphorically.","audio":"audio/1c0e60df2874fa18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-sense-of-accountability","text":"a strong sense of accountability","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong sense of accountability","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A feeling of being responsible for one's actions and decisions, and for their outcomes.","example":"We expect all team members to have a strong sense of accountability for their tasks.","plain":"We expect all team members to feel responsible for their actions and decisions.","note":"Indicates reliability and trustworthiness. Highly valued in professional settings.","audio":"audio/b4f7ea17e2ffcdd7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-sense-of-ownership","text":"a strong sense of ownership","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong sense of ownership","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A feeling of personal responsibility and accountability for one's work or projects.","example":"We value employees who demonstrate a strong sense of ownership and initiative.","plain":"We value employees who demonstrate a feeling of personal responsibility and initiative for their work.","note":"Indicates a proactive and dedicated attitude, highly desirable in employees.","audio":"audio/1a1c422104c90f4a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-sense-of-purpose","text":"a strong sense of purpose","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong sense of purpose","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A clear understanding of one's goals and motivations, providing direction and meaning.","example":"Our team is driven by a strong sense of purpose to deliver innovative solutions.","plain":"Our team is driven by a clear understanding of its goals to deliver innovative solutions.","note":"Indicates motivation and alignment with a larger mission. Positive for both sides.","audio":"audio/c866b31def06cd16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-team-player","text":"a strong team player","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong team player","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Describes someone who works well with others and contributes effectively to group goals.","example":"We need a strong team player who can collaborate across departments.","plain":"We need someone who works well with others and can collaborate across departments.","note":"A common and positive descriptor for someone who contributes positively to group efforts.","audio":"audio/06236bfa4853f634.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-track-record-of","text":"a strong track record of","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong track record of","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Evidence of consistent success and achievement in a particular area.","example":"She has a strong track record of exceeding sales targets in previous roles.","plain":"She has consistent evidence of exceeding sales targets in previous roles.","note":"Similar to 'proven track record' but emphasizes consistency over time.","audio":"audio/0363b23a02cf657a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-strong-work-ethic","text":"a strong work ethic","kind":"collocation","form":"a strong work ethic","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A belief in the moral benefit and importance of hard work and diligence.","example":"We seek candidates who possess a strong work ethic and a commitment to excellence.","plain":"We seek candidates who believe in the importance of hard work and a commitment to excellence.","note":"A highly valued trait in employees, indicating dedication and reliability.","audio":"audio/8e45155da86e5a4e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-subtle-difference","text":"a subtle difference","kind":"collocation","form":"a subtle difference","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A slight, delicate, or hard-to-notice distinction between things.","example":"There's a subtle difference in how these two marketing campaigns target different age groups.","plain":"There's a slight distinction in how these two marketing campaigns aim at various age groups.","note":"Implies a nuanced understanding and careful observation.","audio":"audio/4e25e199215f77b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-sustainable-model","text":"a sustainable model","kind":"collocation","form":"a sustainable model","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A system or approach that can be maintained over a long period without depleting resources or causing harm.","example":"Our aim is to develop a sustainable model for growth that balances profit with environmental responsibility.","plain":"Our goal is to create a long-lasting way to grow that balances making money with protecting the environment.","note":"Often used in discussions about long-term planning, environment, or business viability.","audio":"audio/57a30b7f43a3ccb3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-testament-to","text":"a testament to","kind":"collocation","form":"a testament to","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"clear evidence or proof of a quality or fact.","example":"The project's success is a testament to your meticulous planning and dedication.","plain":"The project's success clearly shows your meticulous planning and dedication.","note":"Formal and strong praise, often for a significant achievement.","audio":"audio/830a5fa90ae55224.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-valuable-asset","text":"a valuable asset","kind":"collocation","form":"a valuable asset","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Describes someone or something that is highly beneficial or advantageous.","example":"I believe my problem-solving skills would make me a valuable asset to your team.","plain":"I believe my problem-solving skills would be highly beneficial to your team.","note":"A strong way for candidates to describe their potential contribution.","audio":"audio/789236b751b8d354.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-valuable-contribution","text":"a valuable contribution","kind":"collocation","form":"a valuable contribution","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Something that adds significant worth or benefit to a project or team.","example":"Your insights during the meeting were a valuable contribution to our strategy discussion.","plain":"Your insights during the meeting were very helpful for our strategy discussion.","note":"Standard, positive feedback. Can be used for ideas or effort.","audio":"audio/d023b866d3f408e8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-very-insightful-question","text":"A very insightful question","kind":"chunk","form":"A very insightful question","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Praises a question for showing deep understanding or thought.","example":"A very insightful question, and one that gets to the heart of the matter.","plain":"That's a very thoughtful question, and one that gets to the heart of the matter.","note":"Highly complimentary, used for particularly well-considered questions.","audio":"audio/b29fbaf3b0807c0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-word-of-caution","text":"a word of caution","kind":"chunk","form":"a word of caution","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A warning or advice to be careful about something.","example":"Just a word of caution about relying too heavily on those preliminary figures.","plain":"I just want to warn you about relying too heavily on those preliminary figures.","note":"Polite way to offer a warning or express a reservation.","audio":"audio/2515af29d99307ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.a-workable-solution","text":"a workable solution","kind":"collocation","form":"a workable solution","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A practical and effective answer to a problem that can be successfully implemented.","example":"Let's focus on developing a workable solution that can be implemented within our current budget.","plain":"Let's concentrate on creating a practical answer that we can put into action with our existing budget.","note":"Describes a solution that is realistic and achievable.","audio":"audio/01e1259c958f587b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.afraid-i-dont-quite-agree","text":"afraid I don't quite agree","kind":"chunk","form":"afraid I don't quite agree","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite and indirect way to express disagreement.","example":"I'm afraid I don't quite agree with your assessment of the market trends.","plain":"I politely disagree with your assessment of the market trends.","note":"Softens direct disagreement, making it less confrontational.","audio":"audio/437ef9fd68f9d6f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.align-with-my-values","text":"align with my values","kind":"collocation","form":"align with my values","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be consistent or in agreement with one's personal principles and beliefs.","example":"I'm seeking a company whose mission and values align with my values of social responsibility.","plain":"I'm seeking a company whose mission and values are consistent with my principles of social responsibility.","note":"Shows a candidate's thoughtful approach to career choices, beyond just job duties.","audio":"audio/12f623c33c41524c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.all-the-best","text":"All the best","kind":"chunk","form":"All the best","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A common and friendly way to say goodbye, wishing someone good fortune.","example":"I'm off to my next meeting, all the best with your presentation!","plain":"I'm off to my next meeting, I wish you good luck with your presentation!","note":"Versatile and widely used for ending conversations or emails.","audio":"audio/ec14ac824a922014.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allocate-some-time","text":"allocate some time","kind":"collocation","form":"allocate some time","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To assign a specific period for a task or discussion.","example":"We should allocate some time at the end to discuss any new business.","plain":"We should set aside some time at the end to discuss any new business.","note":"Professional way to manage the agenda and schedule.","audio":"audio/5c536ea99399c0a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allow-me-to","text":"allow me to","kind":"frame","form":"allow me to","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask permission to do or say something.","example":"Allow me to share some data that might shed light on this issue.","plain":"May I share some data that might shed light on this issue?","note":"Formal and very polite. Often used when presenting information.","audio":"audio/3608a5b7d843451e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.allow-me-to-correct-myself","text":"Allow me to correct myself","kind":"chunk","form":"Allow me to correct myself","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A formal way to ask permission to amend a previous statement.","example":"Allow me to correct myself; the data was collected in Q3, not Q2.","plain":"Let me correct my previous statement; the data was collected in Q3, not Q2.","note":"More formal than \"Let me rephrase that,\" used for a clear factual correction.","audio":"audio/c7ade34d99cb0158.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.an-alternative-approach","text":"an alternative approach","kind":"collocation","form":"an alternative approach","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"A different method or way of dealing with a situation or problem.","example":"It's worth considering whether an alternative approach to customer service might improve satisfaction.","plain":"We should think about if a different way of doing customer service could make people happier.","note":"Suggests exploring other options, showing flexibility.","audio":"audio/f5bf384e02578027.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.and-then-out-of-nowhere","text":"And then, out of nowhere","kind":"chunk","form":"And then, out of nowhere","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Introduces a sudden, unexpected event or appearance.","example":"And then, out of nowhere, a huge gust of wind nearly knocked me off my feet.","plain":"Suddenly, a huge gust of wind nearly knocked me off my feet.","note":"Builds suspense and emphasizes the unexpected nature of an event.","audio":"audio/9574fb54ef394a20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.appreciate-your-candor","text":"appreciate your candor","kind":"collocation","form":"appreciate your candor","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To be grateful for someone's honesty and directness, especially when it's difficult.","example":"I appreciate your candor in pointing out the flaws in my presentation.","plain":"I am grateful for your honesty in pointing out the flaws in my presentation.","note":"Acknowledges and values direct, honest feedback, even if it's critical.","audio":"audio/90d9d77e17a8ff94.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.are-we-aligned-on-this","text":"Are we aligned on this","kind":"chunk","form":"Are we aligned on this","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To check if everyone understands and agrees with the current plan or idea.","example":"Before moving on, are we aligned on this approach to client communication?","plain":"Before we continue, do we all agree on this way of talking to clients?","note":"Professional and collaborative, used to confirm mutual understanding and agreement.","audio":"audio/e6a21d378912ac0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.are-you-open-to","text":"Are you open to","kind":"frame","form":"Are you open to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asks if someone is receptive to a particular idea or activity.","example":"Are you open to joining us for a hike this weekend?","plain":"Would you like to join us for a hike this weekend?","note":"A polite and inclusive way to invite someone, suggesting flexibility.","audio":"audio/92bc8cc9acee6fbf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-an-aside","text":"As an aside","kind":"chunk","form":"As an aside","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Introduces a brief, related point that is not central to the main discussion.","example":"As an aside, the initial feedback from the pilot group was overwhelmingly positive.","plain":"The initial feedback from the pilot group was overwhelmingly positive, just as a side note.","note":"Useful for adding extra context or interesting but non-essential information.","audio":"audio/95fab9809927014d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-articulated-by-x","text":"As articulated by X","kind":"frame","form":"As articulated by Foucault","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Refers to a concept or idea as expressed clearly by a specific author or theorist.","example":"As articulated by Foucault, power structures are often subtle and pervasive.","plain":"Foucault clearly expressed that power structures are often subtle and pervasive.","note":"A formal way to credit the precise wording or formulation of an idea.","audio":"audio/f43e0f6a16099980.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-x-has-demonstrated","text":"As X has demonstrated","kind":"frame","form":"As Dr. Lee has demonstrated","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Refers to a specific person's work to support a point, highlighting their proof.","example":"As Dr. Lee has demonstrated, the phenomenon is more complex than initially assumed.","plain":"Dr. Lee has shown that the phenomenon is more complex than initially assumed.","note":"A formal way to cite an expert's established findings.","audio":"audio/29871ce4d1378898.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-x-has-observed","text":"As X has observed","kind":"frame","form":"As Dr. Kim has observed","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Cites a specific person's insight or finding.","example":"As Dr. Kim has observed, the phenomenon exhibits significant variability across different contexts.","plain":"Dr. Kim noted that the phenomenon shows significant variability across different contexts.","note":"A common, slightly formal way to refer to an expert's insight.","audio":"audio/1b334cd4f21077a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.as-x-rightly-observes","text":"As X rightly observes","kind":"frame","form":"As Dr. Chen rightly observes","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce a point by attributing it to someone else, implying their observation is correct.","example":"As Dr. Chen rightly observes, the data clearly indicates a shift in consumer behavior.","plain":"Dr. Chen correctly observes that the data clearly indicates a shift in consumer behavior.","note":"A formal way to cite an expert, adding weight to the statement.","audio":"audio/20ce5a36aeae15b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.assign-responsibility-for","text":"assign responsibility for","kind":"collocation","form":"assign responsibility for","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To designate who is accountable for a particular task.","example":"We need to assign responsibility for tracking these metrics to a specific team member.","plain":"We need to designate who is accountable for tracking these metrics to a specific team member.","note":"Clear and professional for delegating tasks.","audio":"audio/4ac4e27246416281.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.at-your-discretion","text":"at your discretion","kind":"chunk","form":"at your discretion","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"According to your own judgment or choice.","example":"You can choose your departure time at your discretion, within the operating hours.","plain":"You can choose your departure time yourself.","note":"Formal, grants freedom of choice to the other person.","audio":"audio/925410f2eedae573.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.at-your-earliest-convenience","text":"at your earliest convenience","kind":"chunk","form":"at your earliest convenience","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"As soon as you can; as soon as it is easy for you.","example":"Please contact the front desk at your earliest convenience to confirm your departure.","plain":"Please contact the front desk as soon as you can to confirm your departure.","note":"Very formal and polite, typically used in written or official requests.","audio":"audio/9e9a6407586c4321.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.be-in-two-minds","text":"be in two minds","kind":"collocation","form":"am in two minds","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To be unable to decide between two courses of action.","example":"I am in two minds about whether to visit the museum or the art gallery.","plain":"I can't decide whether to visit the museum or the art gallery.","note":"Common for expressing indecision.","audio":"audio/b67c5815dc276277.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.be-on-the-same-page","text":"be on the same page","kind":"collocation","form":"are on the same page","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To have the same understanding or agreement as someone else.","example":"Before we book, let's make sure we are on the same page about our budget.","plain":"Before we book, let's make sure we agree on our budget.","note":"Common in planning, ensures mutual understanding.","audio":"audio/8033c9332279acdc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.bear-with-me-for-a-moment","text":"Bear with me for a moment","kind":"chunk","form":"Bear with me for a moment","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To ask the audience to be patient while the speaker pauses or collects themselves.","example":"Bear with me for a moment while I pull up the next slide.","plain":"Please be patient for a moment while I pull up the next slide.","note":"A polite request for patience, often used when there's a slight technical delay or a pause.","audio":"audio/6fdccf87f824248a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.before-i-go-any-further","text":"Before I go any further","kind":"chunk","form":"Before I go any further","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To pause and check understanding or invite questions before continuing.","example":"Before I go any further, are there any questions about the initial project scope?","plain":"Do you have any questions about the initial project scope before I continue?","note":"Useful for breaking up a presentation and ensuring the audience is following along.","audio":"audio/6ddb35bc85ccb214.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.before-we-proceed","text":"Before we proceed","kind":"chunk","form":"Before we proceed","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A formal way to pause or introduce a preliminary remark before continuing.","example":"Before we proceed, are there any immediate questions about the data presented so far?","plain":"Are there any immediate questions about the data presented so far before we continue?","note":"Polite and slightly formal, good for checking understanding or taking a brief break.","audio":"audio/18a8d917813a9dde.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.boil-it-down-to","text":"boil it down to","kind":"collocation","form":"boil it down to","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to reduce a complex idea to its most essential elements.","example":"If we boil it down to its core, the problem is a lack of clear communication between departments.","plain":"The core problem is a lack of clear communication between departments.","note":"Suggests getting to the fundamental truth or essence of something.","audio":"audio/c8e9f352bcb21289.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.briefly-interject","text":"briefly interject","kind":"collocation","form":"briefly interject","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"To politely and quickly interrupt to add a point.","example":"If I may briefly interject, I think we should also consider the budget implications.","plain":"If I can interrupt quickly, we should consider the budget.","note":"A formal and polite way to add a point, often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/cf178136f73fe616.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.building-on-this-idea","text":"Building on this idea","kind":"chunk","form":"Building on this idea","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a new point or argument that develops or extends a previously mentioned concept.","example":"Building on this idea, we propose a new framework that integrates both qualitative and quantitative data.","plain":"Extending this idea, we propose a new framework that integrates both qualitative and quantitative data.","note":"A useful phrase for showing progression of thought or how one idea leads to another.","audio":"audio/70b86cd8d5f3fdd6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.building-upon-xs-work","text":"Building upon X's work","kind":"frame","form":"Building upon Dr. Chen's work","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that one's own research or ideas extend or develop from another's contributions.","example":"Building upon Dr. Chen's work, we explored the long-term effects of early intervention.","plain":"We extended Dr. Chen's work by exploring the long-term effects of early intervention.","note":"Used to show how current work advances previous scholarship.","audio":"audio/5e298a5d7ece5040.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-close-of-business","text":"by close of business","kind":"chunk","form":"by close of business","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"By the end of the working day.","example":"Please send your feedback on the draft report by close of business tomorrow.","plain":"Please send your feedback on the draft report by the end of the working day tomorrow.","note":"Common in business for setting deadlines.","audio":"audio/42e8e2d03303870e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-no-means","text":"by no means","kind":"chunk","form":"by no means","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Definitely not; not at all.","example":"The journey was by no means easy, but the destination was worth it.","plain":"The journey was not easy at all, but the destination was worth it.","note":"Strong emphasis on negation; sounds quite formal.","audio":"audio/ea84655f496b1195.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-way-of-illustration","text":"By way of illustration","kind":"chunk","form":"By way of illustration","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to introduce an example or analogy to help explain a concept.","example":"By way of illustration, think of our team as a well-oiled machine, each part crucial.","plain":"Think of our team as a well-oiled machine, each part crucial.","note":"More formal. Suggests a deliberate effort to make something clearer through an example.","audio":"audio/a369f65793f9a056.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.by-when-can-we-expect","text":"By when can we expect","kind":"frame","form":"By when can we expect","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A question to establish a deadline or expected completion time for a task.","example":"By when can we expect the initial report on the market analysis?","plain":"When can we expect the initial report on the market analysis?","note":"Professional and practical, used to set clear expectations for deliverables.","audio":"audio/00de7f2ca22b837b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.can-we-park-that-for-now","text":"Can we park that for now","kind":"chunk","form":"Can we park that for now","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to suggest postponing a discussion point to a later time.","example":"That's an interesting tangent, but can we park that for now and return to the main topic?","plain":"That's an interesting side point, but can we postpone it and return to the main topic?","note":"Informal but widely used in professional settings to manage meeting flow.","audio":"audio/5f4a723578a18510.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.catch-up-properly","text":"catch up properly","kind":"collocation","form":"catch up properly","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To spend quality time with someone to learn about their recent life events and experiences.","example":"It's been ages, we really must catch up properly soon and hear all your news.","plain":"It's been ages, we really must meet soon and hear all your news.","note":"Used when you haven't seen someone in a while and want a substantial conversation, not just a quick chat.","audio":"audio/0af6c641ff3b222e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.choppy-video-feed","text":"choppy video feed","kind":"collocation","form":"choppy video feed","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"The visual transmission is not smooth, appearing to jump or skip frames.","example":"The choppy video feed is making it difficult to see your screen share clearly.","plain":"Your video is not smooth, so I can't see your screen share well.","note":"Describes a common video call problem; polite and descriptive.","audio":"audio/4c951e6fb63f4544.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.circulate-the-minutes","text":"circulate the minutes","kind":"collocation","form":"circulate the minutes","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To distribute the official record of a meeting.","example":"The administrative assistant will circulate the minutes of this meeting by Friday.","plain":"The administrative assistant will distribute the official record of this meeting by Friday.","note":"Standard professional practice after a meeting.","audio":"audio/2324a5c2fd36913c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.coming-back-to-my-point","text":"Coming back to my point","kind":"chunk","form":"Coming back to my point","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To return to the main topic after a digression or interruption.","example":"Coming back to my point about market research, the data clearly supports our strategy.","plain":"Returning to my main point about market research, the data clearly supports our strategy.","note":"Useful for refocusing the audience after a brief digression or interruption.","audio":"audio/2b182c71142af4ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.commend-your-initiative","text":"commend your initiative","kind":"collocation","form":"commend your initiative","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"to praise someone for taking action independently.","example":"I want to commend your initiative in proposing that new process improvement.","plain":"I want to praise you for taking the initiative to propose that new process improvement.","note":"Formal praise for proactivity.","audio":"audio/2ab83b14e214de26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consider-this-an-invitation","text":"Consider this an invitation","kind":"chunk","form":"Consider this an invitation","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A direct and clear way to extend an invitation to someone.","example":"Consider this an invitation to our annual summer barbecue.","plain":"You are invited to our annual summer barbecue.","note":"A slightly formal but friendly way to ensure someone knows they are invited.","audio":"audio/b683f782bc3849b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consider-this-scenario","text":"Consider this scenario","kind":"frame","form":"Consider this scenario","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To introduce a hypothetical situation or example for the listener to think about.","example":"Consider this scenario: what if our main competitor launched a similar product next month?","plain":"What if our main competitor launched a similar product next month? Think about that situation.","note":"A formal way to present a hypothetical situation to illustrate a point or explore possibilities.","audio":"audio/2df81aa8b9763039.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consistent-with-previous-research","text":"Consistent with previous research","kind":"chunk","form":"Consistent with previous research","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that current findings align with what has been found in earlier studies.","example":"Consistent with previous research, our study found a significant correlation between these variables.","plain":"Our study found a significant correlation between these variables, which matches earlier research.","note":"A standard phrase to show continuity with existing literature.","audio":"audio/912990f7d5da2a55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consistently-delivers","text":"consistently delivers","kind":"collocation","form":"consistently delivers","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"reliably achieves good results or meets expectations.","example":"Sarah consistently delivers high-quality work, which is invaluable to our team.","plain":"Sarah reliably produces high-quality work, which is invaluable to our team.","note":"Highlights reliability and sustained performance. Professional context.","audio":"audio/fa45cabad7c3e330.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consistently-exceeds-expectations","text":"consistently exceeds expectations","kind":"collocation","form":"consistently exceeds expectations","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Always performs better than what is required or anticipated.","example":"Your work consistently exceeds expectations, which is fantastic.","plain":"Your work is always better than what is expected, which is fantastic.","note":"Strong praise for sustained high performance.","audio":"audio/35d99c307573d2d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.consistently-high-quality","text":"consistently high quality","kind":"collocation","form":"consistently high quality","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Always producing work or results of an excellent standard.","example":"We've noticed your work is of consistently high quality, which is greatly valued.","plain":"We've noticed your work is always excellent, which is greatly valued.","note":"Highlights reliability and excellence over time.","audio":"audio/b7b17d4566b3d50d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.contingent-on","text":"Contingent on","kind":"frame","form":"Contingent on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Dependent on something else happening or being true.","example":"Our investment is contingent on the project securing all necessary permits.","plain":"Our investment depends on the project getting all the required permits.","note":"Formal and emphasizes dependency; often used in financial or legal discussions.","audio":"audio/36280ffe9786131e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.conversely-it-can-be-argued","text":"Conversely, it can be argued","kind":"frame","form":"Conversely, it can be argued","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces an opposing argument or viewpoint.","example":"Conversely, it can be argued that these interventions may inadvertently create new dependencies.","plain":"On the other hand, one could argue that these interventions might accidentally create new dependencies.","note":"A formal way to introduce a contrasting perspective.","audio":"audio/42e08142e25ab44a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-be-argued-that","text":"could be argued that","kind":"frame","form":"It could be argued that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To introduce a point that is open to debate or discussion, often a critical one.","example":"It could be argued that the current strategy isn't sustainable in the long term.","plain":"Someone might say that the current strategy isn't sustainable in the long term.","note":"Impersonal and academic, used to present a critical point without owning it directly.","audio":"audio/abc5a3edf0a30e47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-be-refined","text":"could be refined","kind":"collocation","form":"could be refined","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Has the potential to be improved or made more precise and elegant.","example":"The initial draft of the proposal could be refined to present a stronger argument.","plain":"The initial draft of the proposal could be improved to present a stronger argument.","note":"A polite and constructive way to suggest improvements without being overly critical.","audio":"audio/7a7467db5907ae51.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-benefit-from","text":"could benefit from","kind":"frame","form":"could benefit from","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"would improve if something were added or changed.","example":"Your proposal could benefit from a clearer executive summary.","plain":"Your proposal would improve with a clearer executive summary.","note":"Polite, indirect suggestion for improvement.","audio":"audio/f9a8ee010d2ffb87.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-i-just-jump-in","text":"Could I just jump in","kind":"chunk","form":"Could I just jump in","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly informal way to ask for permission to interrupt and speak.","example":"Could I just jump in here? I have some data that might be relevant to this discussion.","plain":"May I speak now? I have some data that might be relevant to this discussion.","note":"Common and polite, less formal than 'interject', suitable for most professional meetings.","audio":"audio/8fb800475d126b4b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-we-briefly-touch-on","text":"Could we briefly touch on","kind":"frame","form":"Could we briefly touch on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite request to discuss a topic quickly without going into extensive detail.","example":"Before we finish, could we briefly touch on the upcoming team-building event?","plain":"Before we finish, could we quickly discuss the upcoming team-building event?","note":"Efficient and polite, used to cover a minor point within time constraints.","audio":"audio/fc85c1aa1d01f68c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-we-get-a-quick-update","text":"Could we get a quick update","kind":"frame","form":"Could we get a quick update","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite request for a brief status report on a particular item.","example":"Could we get a quick update on the progress of the new software rollout?","plain":"Could you give us a brief update on the progress of the new software rollout?","note":"Efficient and professional, used to gather information without lengthy discussion.","audio":"audio/ce0f18d080325544.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-elaborate-further","text":"Could you elaborate further","kind":"frame","form":"Could you elaborate further","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite request for more detailed explanation or additional information.","example":"That's an interesting point; could you elaborate further on the implications?","plain":"That's an interesting point; could you explain the implications in more detail?","note":"Professional and clear, used to encourage a deeper dive into a topic.","audio":"audio/6bb8c1b871d9b83a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.could-you-walk-us-through","text":"Could you walk us through","kind":"frame","form":"Could you walk us through","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite request for someone to explain a process, document, or data in detail.","example":"Could you walk us through the latest sales figures and highlight any trends?","plain":"Could you explain the latest sales figures to us and highlight any trends?","note":"Collaborative and clear, used to invite a detailed explanation from an expert.","audio":"audio/1cddadbdb3d0691e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.credit-where-credits-due","text":"credit where credit's due","kind":"chunk","form":"Credit where credit's due","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Acknowledging someone's contribution or success, even if it's unexpected or from a rival.","example":"Credit where credit's due, Sarah's innovative idea significantly improved our workflow.","plain":"Sarah's innovative idea significantly improved our workflow, and she deserves recognition for it.","note":"Used to ensure proper recognition, often when the person might otherwise be overlooked.","audio":"audio/3197d3ca22323034.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.demonstrate-resilience","text":"demonstrate resilience","kind":"collocation","form":"demonstrate resilience","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To show the ability to recover quickly from difficulties or setbacks.","example":"In times of change, it's crucial for leaders to demonstrate resilience and adaptability.","plain":"In times of change, it's crucial for leaders to show the ability to recover quickly from difficulties.","note":"A professional way to describe bouncing back from challenges. Highly valued in dynamic environments.","audio":"audio/98316ac6a99f6067.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.demonstrated-strong-leadership","text":"demonstrated strong leadership","kind":"collocation","form":"demonstrated strong leadership","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Showed effective qualities of guiding and inspiring others.","example":"You really demonstrated strong leadership in managing that difficult client project.","plain":"You really showed effective leadership in managing that difficult client project.","note":"Specific praise for leadership skills.","audio":"audio/ba5e8931b8c589d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.did-you-want-to-add-anything","text":"did you want to add anything","kind":"chunk","form":"Did you want to add anything","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Invites the other person to contribute further to the discussion.","example":"Before we conclude this topic, did you want to add anything, Sarah?","plain":"Do you have any further comments or points to make?","note":"A polite way to ensure everyone has a chance to speak. Inclusive.","audio":"audio/3fac6cc0c3bf2422.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.distorted-sound","text":"distorted sound","kind":"collocation","form":"distorted sound","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Audio quality that is poor, making voices or sounds unclear and unnatural.","example":"I'm finding the distorted sound makes it hard to understand your words.","plain":"The unclear sound makes it hard to understand you.","note":"A precise way to describe poor audio quality, indicating a technical issue.","audio":"audio/e552ba53ae115e30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-keep-in-touch","text":"Do keep in touch","kind":"chunk","form":"Do keep in touch","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A friendly and polite request for someone to maintain contact with you.","example":"It was great seeing you after so long; do keep in touch!","plain":"It was great seeing you after so long; please stay in contact!","note":"A warm and personal way to express a desire for continued connection.","audio":"audio/2ad9746877c5519c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.do-you-fancy","text":"Do you fancy","kind":"chunk","form":"Do you fancy","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asks if someone would like to do something or is interested in an activity.","example":"Do you fancy grabbing a drink after work on Friday?","plain":"Would you like to grab a drink after work on Friday?","note":"Common in British English, less so in American English. Casual and friendly.","audio":"audio/b215d2f3b30c9cc3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.does-that-clarify-things","text":"Does that clarify things","kind":"chunk","form":"Does that clarify things","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to ask if a recent explanation has made a situation or concept clearer.","example":"I hope my example about the budget helped. Does that clarify things for you?","plain":"I hope my example about the budget helped. Is it clearer now?","note":"Used after providing additional information or a rephrasing.","audio":"audio/d18320242f25aa19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.double-check-the-booking","text":"double-check the booking","kind":"collocation","form":"double-check the booking","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To verify reservation details again to ensure accuracy.","example":"I'd like to double-check the booking for our flight tomorrow, just to be safe.","plain":"I want to confirm our flight reservation again for tomorrow.","note":"Common for important arrangements; shows diligence.","audio":"audio/ee80aa7c4ed81e88.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.draw-on-my-experience","text":"draw on my experience","kind":"collocation","form":"draw on my experience","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To use one's past knowledge and skills to handle a current situation or task.","example":"I can draw on my experience in project management to lead this initiative effectively.","plain":"I can use my past knowledge in project management to lead this initiative effectively.","note":"Commonly used by candidates to highlight how their past roles prepare them for new challenges.","audio":"audio/6b48eb4b1d9175c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drawing-a-parallel-between","text":"Drawing a parallel between","kind":"collocation","form":"Drawing a parallel between","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To highlight similarities or connections between two different things or situations.","example":"Drawing a parallel between historical economic crises and current trends can offer valuable insights.","plain":"Highlighting similarities between historical economic crises and current trends can offer valuable insights.","note":"Used to make a comparison that illuminates understanding.","audio":"audio/4a9378390ddbb83a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drawing-on-the-work-of","text":"Drawing on the work of","kind":"frame","form":"Drawing on the work of","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that one's ideas or arguments are based on or inspired by another's research.","example":"Drawing on the work of Professor Chen, we developed a novel framework for data analysis.","plain":"Using Professor Chen's work, we developed a novel framework for data analysis.","note":"A standard academic phrase for citing or referencing previous research, common in papers and presentations.","audio":"audio/6802c7ff92bad9b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drawing-on-xs-framework","text":"Drawing on X's framework","kind":"frame","form":"Drawing on Bourdieu's framework","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that one's analysis or discussion is based on a particular theoretical structure.","example":"Drawing on Bourdieu's framework, we can analyze the cultural capital within this community.","plain":"Using Bourdieu's framework, we can analyze the cultural capital within this community.","note":"Common in academic discourse to acknowledge theoretical foundations.","audio":"audio/ef26f1edf98cd467.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drive-initiatives","text":"drive initiatives","kind":"collocation","form":"drive initiatives","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To lead and propel new projects or plans forward to achieve specific goals.","example":"I'm looking for a role where I can drive initiatives and make a tangible impact.","plain":"I'm looking for a role where I can lead new projects and make a tangible impact.","note":"Suggests leadership, proactivity, and a results-oriented approach. Highly valued.","audio":"audio/4b9ace706d1a82a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.drop-me-a-line","text":"drop me a line","kind":"chunk","form":"drop me a line","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To send someone a short message, usually by email or text.","example":"If you have any questions, just drop me a line.","plain":"If you have any questions, just send me a message.","note":"An informal way to ask someone to contact you, often in writing. Common in British English.","audio":"audio/41844ba2f8c023b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.echoing-xs-concerns","text":"Echoing X's concerns","kind":"frame","form":"Echoing Professor Davies' concerns","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Expresses agreement with worries or issues previously raised by someone else.","example":"Echoing Professor Davies' concerns, the ethical implications of AI development warrant careful scrutiny.","plain":"Like Professor Davies, we are concerned that the ethical implications of AI development need careful scrutiny.","note":"Shows alignment with another's apprehension or critical view.","audio":"audio/a625c6fdd4113caa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.explain-in-detail","text":"explain in detail","kind":"collocation","form":"explain in detail","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To provide a thorough and comprehensive explanation.","example":"Could you explain in detail how the new software integration will work?","plain":"Could you provide a thorough explanation of how the new software integration will work?","note":"Direct request for a comprehensive explanation.","audio":"audio/c11844e690473495.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.explore-the-possibility","text":"explore the possibility","kind":"collocation","form":"explore the possibility","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To investigate or consider whether something is feasible or could happen.","example":"Before deciding, we should explore the possibility of collaborating with an external agency.","plain":"Before we decide, we should investigate if we can work with an outside agency.","note":"Suggests an open-minded and thorough approach to decision-making.","audio":"audio/7a071486aad990da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.express-my-gratitude","text":"express my gratitude","kind":"collocation","form":"express my gratitude","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To formally or politely thank someone.","example":"I'd like to express my gratitude for your excellent service.","plain":"I want to thank you for your excellent service.","note":"More formal than \"thank you,\" used for significant appreciation.","audio":"audio/ac1f3e6317b6dac3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.feel-free-to","text":"feel free to","kind":"frame","form":"feel free to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An informal way to give permission or encourage someone to do something.","example":"Feel free to bring a plus one if you like, the more the merrier!","plain":"You can bring a plus one if you like, the more the merrier!","note":"A polite and welcoming way to offer options or permission to a guest.","audio":"audio/96a196fc8c11aa16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.feel-free-to-approach-me","text":"Feel free to approach me","kind":"frame","form":"Feel free to approach me","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Invites individuals to speak to you directly after the presentation.","example":"Feel free to approach me during the break if you have any confidential queries.","plain":"Please come and speak to me during the break if you have any confidential queries.","note":"Friendly and open, encourages one-on-one discussion.","audio":"audio/1720048dcf9a499b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.follow-suit","text":"follow suit","kind":"collocation","form":"followed suit","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To do the same thing as someone else has just done.","example":"When one passenger complained about the food, others quickly followed suit.","plain":"When one passenger complained about the food, others did the same.","note":"Common in describing group behavior or trends.","audio":"audio/43a37eb9ef251ef2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.following-xs-methodology","text":"Following X's methodology","kind":"frame","form":"Following Dr. Gupta's methodology","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that one's research approach is based on or inspired by another's established method.","example":"Following Dr. Gupta's methodology, we employed a mixed-methods approach for data analysis.","plain":"We used a mixed-methods approach for data analysis, similar to Dr. Gupta's method.","note":"Used to credit the source of a research method.","audio":"audio/3857ffa6704fdcc5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.food-for-thought","text":"food for thought","kind":"chunk","form":"food for thought","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Something that provides material for careful consideration or reflection.","example":"Your presentation gave us a lot of food for thought regarding our future strategy.","plain":"Your presentation gave us a lot to think about regarding our future strategy.","note":"Suggests that an idea or information is valuable for contemplation.","audio":"audio/2154c169e653b795.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.for-context","text":"For context","kind":"chunk","form":"For context","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Provides necessary background information to help the listener understand the situation.","example":"For context, this was before they implemented the new security protocols at the office.","plain":"To give you background, this was before they implemented the new security protocols at the office.","note":"A concise way to offer relevant background information.","audio":"audio/b0289960ae93c08e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.forgive-me-i-got-ahead-of-myself","text":"Forgive me, I got ahead of myself","kind":"chunk","form":"Forgive me, I got ahead of myself","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To apologize for discussing something prematurely or out of order.","example":"Forgive me, I got ahead of myself; we'll cover the budget details in the next section.","plain":"Sorry, I spoke too soon; we'll cover the budget details in the next section.","note":"A polite way to acknowledge jumping the gun or revealing information too early.","audio":"audio/dcc1ce2b60430d8d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.further-elaborate","text":"further elaborate","kind":"collocation","form":"further elaborate","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"To provide more details or further explain a previous point.","example":"I'd like to further elaborate on that point regarding our initial findings.","plain":"I want to give more details about our initial findings.","note":"Used when expanding on a point, often after someone else has mentioned it.","audio":"audio/215e9fa04116650e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.gain-further-insight-into","text":"gain further insight into","kind":"collocation","form":"gain further insight into","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To acquire a deeper and more comprehensive understanding of a topic or situation.","example":"I'd like to gain further insight into the company's long-term strategic vision.","plain":"I'd like to acquire a deeper understanding of the company's long-term strategic vision.","note":"A professional way to express a desire for more detailed information.","audio":"audio/789cdc7e4b4e25d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.gently-suggest","text":"gently suggest","kind":"collocation","form":"gently suggest","level":"C2","situations":["feedback","persuading"],"meaning":"To propose an idea or course of action in a soft, non-forceful manner.","example":"I'd like to gently suggest a different approach to managing client expectations.","plain":"I'd like to politely propose a different approach to managing client expectations.","note":"Emphasizes a soft, non-confrontational delivery of advice or criticism.","audio":"audio/b9904e7f39056561.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-around-to","text":"get around to","kind":"chunk","form":"get around to","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To finally do something after a delay or procrastination.","example":"I need to get around to booking my train tickets for next week.","plain":"I need to book my train tickets for next week soon.","note":"Implies a task that has been pending for a while.","audio":"audio/a0186fc62555a1f6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-ball-rolling-on","text":"get the ball rolling on","kind":"frame","form":"get the ball rolling on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To initiate a specific task or project.","example":"Let's get the ball rolling on the new marketing campaign next week.","plain":"Let's start the new marketing campaign next week.","note":"Informal but common. Focuses on starting a particular initiative.","audio":"audio/bfc8c56734a581f4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-hang-of","text":"get the hang of","kind":"chunk","form":"get the hang of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To learn how to do something, especially something new or tricky.","example":"It took me a while to get the hang of using the local public transport system.","plain":"It took me a while to learn how to use the local public transport system.","note":"Informal and common, implies a learning curve.","audio":"audio/683d7ee5ab391d7b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-impression","text":"get the impression","kind":"collocation","form":"get the impression","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To form an idea or opinion about something, often based on indirect evidence.","example":"I get the impression that the hotel staff are a bit overwhelmed today.","plain":"I think the hotel staff are a bit overwhelmed today.","note":"A polite way to express an observation or feeling without stating it as fact.","audio":"audio/fc4261ab8cc42991.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.get-the-most-out-of","text":"get the most out of","kind":"collocation","form":"get the most out of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To gain the maximum benefit or enjoyment from something.","example":"We're trying to get the most out of our short trip by planning carefully.","plain":"We're trying to maximize our short trip by planning carefully.","note":"Common for optimizing experiences or resources.","audio":"audio/aeaec874c85196ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.give-me-a-shout","text":"give me a shout","kind":"chunk","form":"give me a shout","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To contact someone, usually by phone or message.","example":"If you're in town next week, give me a shout and we can meet up.","plain":"If you're in town next week, contact me and we can meet up.","note":"An informal way to ask someone to get in touch. Common in British English.","audio":"audio/3445e0d2dd940d02.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.go-ahead-im-listening","text":"go ahead, I'm listening","kind":"chunk","form":"Go ahead, I'm listening","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Gives permission to the other person to speak and assures them of attention.","example":"Please, go ahead, I'm listening intently to your proposal.","plain":"You can speak now; I am paying attention to what you say.","note":"Encouraging and polite, often used when someone hesitates to speak.","audio":"audio/d989e3cb078d21c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.grab-a-bite","text":"grab a bite","kind":"collocation","form":"grab a bite","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To get something to eat, usually quickly and informally.","example":"Are you free to grab a bite after work?","plain":"Are you free to get something to eat after work?","note":"A very common, informal way to suggest eating together. Can be a full meal or a snack.","audio":"audio/01bf6ae38561f542.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-a-change-of-heart","text":"have a change of heart","kind":"collocation","form":"had a change of heart","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To change one's opinion or decision about something.","example":"I had a change of heart and decided to extend my stay for another week.","plain":"I changed my mind and decided to extend my stay for another week.","note":"Common for describing a shift in opinion or desire.","audio":"audio/6b5cab75496dd46a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-a-good-grasp-of","text":"have a good grasp of","kind":"collocation","form":"have a good grasp of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To have a thorough understanding of something.","example":"Our tour guide seems to have a good grasp of the city's history.","plain":"Our tour guide seems to understand the city's history well.","note":"Formal, indicates comprehensive knowledge.","audio":"audio/a31e41d968b763f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-a-word-with","text":"have a word with","kind":"collocation","form":"have a word with","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To speak briefly with someone, often to discuss a problem or request.","example":"I need to have a word with the hotel manager about the noise.","plain":"I need to speak briefly with the hotel manager about the noise.","note":"Informal and common, often used for a mild complaint or request.","audio":"audio/4990e4092dba4c9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.have-i-lost-you","text":"Have I lost you","kind":"chunk","form":"Have I lost you","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ask if the listener is still following the explanation or has become confused.","example":"I've covered a lot of technical terms; have I lost you at any point?","plain":"I've covered a lot of technical terms; are you still following me or are you confused?","note":"Informal and direct, shows concern for the listener's understanding.","audio":"audio/d6c2fb1e81f4bbd7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.he-made-a-point-of-saying","text":"He made a point of saying","kind":"frame","form":"He made a point of saying","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Emphasizes that someone deliberately and specifically stated something.","example":"He made a point of saying how much he appreciated everyone's hard work on the project.","plain":"He specifically mentioned how much he appreciated everyone's hard work on the project.","note":"Highlights a statement that was intentionally emphasized.","audio":"audio/2c37753069ca5371.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.highly-commendable","text":"highly commendable","kind":"collocation","form":"highly commendable","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Deserving of strong praise and approval.","example":"Your initiative in taking on that extra responsibility was highly commendable.","plain":"Your initiative in taking on that extra responsibility was very praiseworthy.","note":"Formal and appreciative, often used for actions showing good judgment or effort.","audio":"audio/79a7a5af91724d2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.highly-valued","text":"highly valued","kind":"collocation","form":"highly valued","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Considered to be very important or useful.","example":"Your contributions to the project are highly valued by everyone.","plain":"Everyone considers your contributions to the project to be very important.","note":"Standard, sincere praise, often used in formal settings.","audio":"audio/4e4d4d0de5e98f90.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.honed-my-skills","text":"honed my skills","kind":"collocation","form":"honed my skills","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To refine and improve one's abilities through practice and experience.","example":"In my previous role, I honed my skills in data analysis and strategic planning.","plain":"In my previous role, I refined my abilities in data analysis and strategic planning.","note":"A sophisticated way to describe skill development, implying precision and expertise.","audio":"audio/eaf1acb9312bfc55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-absolutely-delightful","text":"How absolutely delightful","kind":"chunk","form":"How absolutely delightful","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong pleasure or joy about something.","example":"Oh, a surprise visit from your sister? How absolutely delightful!","plain":"Oh, a surprise visit from your sister? That is very pleasing!","note":"A slightly more formal or effusive expression of joy.","audio":"audio/6149493cc63f04c3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-do-we-move-forward","text":"How do we move forward","kind":"frame","form":"How do we move forward","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A question seeking suggestions or a plan for progressing with a task or issue.","example":"We've identified the problem, but now how do we move forward with a solution?","plain":"We've identified the problem, but what should we do next to find a solution?","note":"Action-oriented and practical, used to shift from discussion to planning.","audio":"audio/cad014eb51a7d117.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-do-you-envision","text":"how do you envision","kind":"frame","form":"How do you envision","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Asks about someone's mental picture or plan for a future situation or role.","example":"How do you envision this team evolving over the next couple of years?","plain":"What is your plan for this team evolving over the next couple of years?","note":"A forward-looking question, useful for candidates to gauge future opportunities and company vision.","audio":"audio/3149b2ae01612e10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-does-that-strike-you","text":"How does that strike you","kind":"chunk","form":"how does that strike you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asks for someone's opinion or reaction to a suggestion or idea.","example":"I was thinking of a picnic in the park; how does that strike you?","plain":"I was thinking of a picnic in the park; what do you think?","note":"A slightly more formal or thoughtful way to ask for feedback on a proposal.","audio":"audio/466048b42d683f85.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.how-would-you-feel-about","text":"How would you feel about","kind":"frame","form":"How would you feel about","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To propose an alternative or a compromise in a gentle, open-ended way.","example":"How would you feel about splitting the additional costs evenly between our two departments?","plain":"What do you think about splitting the additional costs evenly between our two departments?","note":"A soft, collaborative way to introduce a new idea or counter-offer. Invites discussion.","audio":"audio/9f264fe03772d65b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-that-must-be","text":"I appreciate that must be","kind":"frame","form":"I appreciate that must be","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Shows understanding and respect for someone's challenging experience.","example":"I appreciate that must be incredibly frustrating, but we need to find a solution.","plain":"I understand that is incredibly frustrating, but we need to find a solution.","note":"A polite and empathetic opening, often used before suggesting a path forward.","audio":"audio/7da84f1a8ed2026f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-the-opportunity","text":"I appreciate the opportunity","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate the opportunity","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude for being considered for a role or for the chance to interview.","example":"I appreciate the opportunity to discuss how my skills align with your company's goals.","plain":"I am grateful for the chance to discuss how my skills align with your company's goals.","note":"A formal and polite way to express thanks at the beginning or end of an interview.","audio":"audio/d2d44a992bf9d336.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-the-question","text":"I appreciate the question","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate the question","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express gratitude for an audience member's query.","example":"I appreciate the question; it highlights a common challenge in this field.","plain":"Thank you for the question; it highlights a common challenge in this field.","note":"A polite and encouraging way to respond to questions, fostering a positive atmosphere.","audio":"audio/e8288f1f36ae50cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-you-taking-the-call","text":"I appreciate you taking the call","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate you taking the call","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude to the other person for accepting and participating in the call.","example":"I appreciate you taking the call on such short notice.","plain":"Thank you for accepting and participating in this phone call.","note":"A polite and professional way to show appreciation, especially if the call was unexpected.","audio":"audio/2f306c1f2875601d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-your-input","text":"I appreciate your input","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate your input","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite way to thank someone for their contribution or suggestions.","example":"I appreciate your input on the new marketing strategy; it gives us a lot to consider.","plain":"Thank you for your suggestions on the new marketing strategy; it gives us a lot to consider.","note":"Standard, polite acknowledgement of feedback.","audio":"audio/cd646b4b3cc90e57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-your-insights","text":"I appreciate your insights","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate your insights","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","calls"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude for valuable ideas, perspectives, or understanding shared.","example":"I appreciate your insights on the market trends; they were very helpful.","plain":"Thank you for your valuable ideas about market trends; they helped a lot.","note":"A polite and professional way to thank someone for their contribution.","audio":"audio/b3bc80c39f3e6c9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-appreciate-your-position","text":"I appreciate your position","kind":"chunk","form":"I appreciate your position","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To acknowledge and show understanding of the other party's stance, even if one disagrees.","example":"I appreciate your position on the payment terms, but we simply cannot agree to net 90 days.","plain":"I understand your stance on the payment terms, but we simply cannot agree to net 90 days.","note":"A polite way to acknowledge the other side before stating a counter-position or refusal.","audio":"audio/4527fbd9736eaeea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-appreciate-that","text":"I can appreciate that","kind":"chunk","form":"I can appreciate that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges and understands someone's point or situation, often empathetically.","example":"You're feeling overwhelmed by the workload; I can appreciate that.","plain":"You're feeling overwhelmed by the workload; I understand that.","note":"A polite and empathetic way to acknowledge someone's feelings or perspective.","audio":"audio/df3f584496a83ab0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-appreciate-your-concern","text":"I can appreciate your concern","kind":"chunk","form":"I can appreciate your concern","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges and validates someone's worries or anxieties about a situation.","example":"Given the recent changes in policy, I can appreciate your concern about job security.","plain":"Given the recent changes in policy, I understand why you are worried about job security.","note":"A diplomatic and empathetic phrase, often used in professional or sensitive discussions.","audio":"audio/ba41ed3dd997bfb0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-only-imagine-the-pressure","text":"I can only imagine the pressure","kind":"frame","form":"I can only imagine the pressure","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses empathy for the stress or demands someone is experiencing.","example":"With two young children and a full-time job, I can only imagine the pressure you're under.","plain":"With two young children and a full-time job, I understand how much stress you must feel.","note":"A common empathetic phrase, acknowledging the difficulty of someone's situation.","audio":"audio/db4879ea6c89b81b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-only-imagine-the-strain","text":"I can only imagine the strain","kind":"frame","form":"I can only imagine the strain","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses deep empathy for the mental or emotional pressure someone is under.","example":"Working two jobs while studying for your master's, I can only imagine the strain.","plain":"Working two jobs while studying for your master's, I understand how much pressure you must feel.","note":"A heartfelt way to acknowledge someone's significant stress or difficulty.","audio":"audio/500ae8679446e79b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-see-where-youre-coming-from","text":"I can see where you're coming from","kind":"chunk","form":"I can see where you're coming from","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses understanding of someone's perspective or reasoning, even if you don't fully agree.","example":"I can see where you're coming from regarding the budget cuts, but we need to find a solution.","plain":"I understand your perspective regarding the budget cuts, but we need to find a solution.","note":"A common, diplomatic phrase for acknowledging another's viewpoint.","audio":"audio/26262ee2cbfaca81.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-see-why-youd-feel","text":"I can see why you'd feel","kind":"frame","form":"I can see why you'd feel","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses understanding of the reasons behind someone's emotions.","example":"With all the changes, I can see why you'd feel a bit overwhelmed.","plain":"With all the changes, I understand the reasons for you feeling a bit overwhelmed.","note":"A gentle way to acknowledge and validate someone's emotional state.","audio":"audio/20b685fddc5d3f87.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-can-well-imagine","text":"I can well imagine","kind":"chunk","form":"I can well imagine","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses understanding of how difficult or challenging a situation must be.","example":"The project deadline was moved up by a month, so I can well imagine the pressure you're under.","plain":"The project deadline was moved up by a month, so I understand the pressure you're under.","note":"A polite and empathetic way to acknowledge someone's difficult experience.","audio":"audio/6e12da436143ead6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-cannot-stress-enough-that","text":"I cannot stress enough that","kind":"frame","form":"I cannot stress enough that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To strongly emphasize the critical importance of a particular point.","example":"I cannot stress enough that data security must be our top priority.","plain":"Data security must be our top priority; this is extremely important.","note":"A very strong emphasis, used for truly critical points. Can sound a bit dramatic if overused.","audio":"audio/f216a3d2c1b3320d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-completely-understand-why","text":"I completely understand why","kind":"frame","form":"I completely understand why","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Affirms full comprehension of someone's reasons or feelings.","example":"Given the circumstances, I completely understand why you decided to resign.","plain":"Given the circumstances, I fully comprehend your reasons for resigning.","note":"Used to show strong agreement and validation of someone's decision or emotion.","audio":"audio/c1faeb2a1b873870.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-couldnt-be-happier-for-you","text":"I couldn't be happier for you","kind":"frame","form":"I couldn't be happier for you","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses the highest degree of happiness for someone else's success or joy.","example":"After all your hard work, I couldn't be happier for you getting that scholarship.","plain":"After all your hard work, I am extremely happy for you getting that scholarship.","note":"A very strong and sincere expression of shared joy.","audio":"audio/d2baa83a28f73216.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-couldnt-be-more-pleased","text":"I couldn't be more pleased","kind":"chunk","form":"I couldn't be more pleased","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses maximum happiness or satisfaction about a situation or outcome.","example":"With the project finishing ahead of schedule, I couldn't be more pleased.","plain":"With the project finishing ahead of schedule, I am extremely happy.","note":"A strong, emphatic expression of satisfaction, often used in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/0be9efc5d62f28fa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-couldnt-help-but-laugh","text":"I couldn't help but laugh","kind":"frame","form":"I couldn't help but laugh","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes an uncontrollable or involuntary reaction of amusement.","example":"I couldn't help but laugh when he accidentally spilled coffee all over his boss's notes.","plain":"I had to laugh when he accidentally spilled coffee all over his boss's notes.","note":"Conveys a strong, spontaneous emotional response.","audio":"audio/e81e2e02142fa2f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-couldnt-help-but-notice","text":"I couldn't help but notice","kind":"frame","form":"I couldn't help but notice","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Used to politely introduce an observation about someone or something nearby, often as a conversation starter.","example":"I couldn't help but notice your interesting book; is it a new release?","plain":"I saw your interesting book and wanted to ask if it's a new release.","note":"A polite way to comment on something without seeming intrusive. Can be used with 'that' or a noun phrase.","audio":"audio/030238d1aca0c96b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-didnt-quite-catch-that","text":"I didn't quite catch that","kind":"chunk","form":"I didn't quite catch that","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I didn't fully hear or understand what you just said.","example":"The audio cut out for a moment, so I didn't quite catch that last point.","plain":"I didn't hear your last point because the audio cut out.","note":"A polite and common way to indicate you missed part of the conversation.","audio":"audio/92cd30bde0bad6df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-distinctly-remember-thinking","text":"I distinctly remember thinking","kind":"frame","form":"I distinctly remember thinking","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Recalls a specific thought or internal reaction at a particular moment in the past.","example":"I distinctly remember thinking, 'This can't be happening right now,' as the system crashed.","plain":"I clearly recall thinking, 'This can't be happening right now,' as the system crashed.","note":"Adds a personal, immediate dimension to the recounted event.","audio":"audio/ec99c5b217537721.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-dont-believe-weve-met","text":"I don't believe we've met","kind":"chunk","form":"I don't believe we've met","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to initiate an introduction when you're unsure if you've been introduced before.","example":"I don't believe we've met, I'm Sarah from the marketing department.","plain":"I think we haven't met before, I'm Sarah from the marketing department.","note":"Formal and polite, suitable for professional or semi-formal settings.","audio":"audio/d278bbb2ff11bc49.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-firmly-believe-that","text":"I firmly believe that","kind":"frame","form":"I firmly believe that","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong and unwavering conviction about something.","example":"I firmly believe that investing in employee training will yield long-term positive results for the company.","plain":"I strongly think that training employees will bring good long-term outcomes for the company.","note":"A powerful way to convey personal conviction, often used when trying to persuade.","audio":"audio/c9d02971734c7f8c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-gather-that","text":"I gather that","kind":"frame","form":"I gather that","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Indicating that you have inferred something from what you've heard or observed.","example":"I gather that you've been quite busy with the new project lately.","plain":"I understand from what I've heard that you've been quite busy with the new project lately.","note":"Polite way to show you've been paying attention and making connections.","audio":"audio/ff631a3c1cfda700.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-gathered-that","text":"I gathered that","kind":"frame","form":"I gathered that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Conveys an inference or understanding derived from what was said or observed, rather than a direct statement.","example":"I gathered that they were planning to merge the two departments next quarter, though it wasn't official.","plain":"I understood that they were planning to merge the two departments next quarter, though it wasn't official.","note":"Implies an understanding based on hints or indirect information.","audio":"audio/962ff36f067a9a3b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-got-a-bit-sidetracked","text":"I got a bit sidetracked","kind":"chunk","form":"I got a bit sidetracked","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Explains that you have deviated from the main topic of discussion.","example":"I got a bit sidetracked discussing the historical context; let's return to the present.","plain":"I went off topic discussing the historical context; let's return to the present.","note":"Informal and self-deprecating, used to acknowledge a digression.","audio":"audio/bc401a3dc4eafe4b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-had-a-feeling-that","text":"I had a feeling that","kind":"frame","form":"I had a feeling that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses a premonition or intuition about a situation.","example":"I had a feeling that something wasn't quite right with the new project proposal.","plain":"I suspected that something wasn't quite right with the new project proposal.","note":"Often used to build suspense or explain a subsequent action.","audio":"audio/d3e511b99bbd2de3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-had-hoped-for-something-different","text":"I had hoped for something different","kind":"chunk","form":"I had hoped for something different","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses disappointment that an outcome is not what was desired or expected.","example":"After all the discussions, I had hoped for something different from the final proposal.","plain":"After all the discussions, I expected a different outcome from the final proposal.","note":"A polite and slightly indirect way to express disappointment or dissatisfaction.","audio":"audio/ec0d8dbce77cabbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-had-rather-hoped","text":"I had rather hoped","kind":"frame","form":"I had rather hoped","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a polite, often understated, disappointment about an outcome.","example":"I had rather hoped we could finish this by Friday, but it seems unlikely now.","plain":"I had hoped we could finish this by Friday, but it seems unlikely now.","note":"A slightly formal and understated way to express disappointment.","audio":"audio/29d4729750347872.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hadnt-anticipated-that","text":"I hadn't anticipated that","kind":"frame","form":"I hadn't anticipated that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something was not foreseen or prepared for.","example":"The sudden surge in demand, I hadn't anticipated that at all.","plain":"The sudden surge in demand, I had not foreseen that at all.","note":"A slightly more formal way to express surprise or lack of foresight.","audio":"audio/7b01db0fb3a4124e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hadnt-considered-that","text":"I hadn't considered that","kind":"chunk","form":"I hadn't considered that","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Expressing that a new idea or perspective has been introduced to you.","example":"I hadn't considered that, but it makes a lot of sense in this context.","plain":"I didn't think of that before, but it makes a lot of sense in this context.","note":"Polite way to acknowledge new information or a different viewpoint.","audio":"audio/bba484ab1767aace.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hadnt-expected-that","text":"I hadn't expected that","kind":"frame","form":"I hadn't expected that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something was not foreseen or predicted.","example":"The sudden resignation of the CEO, I hadn't expected that at all.","plain":"The sudden resignation of the CEO, I had not predicted that at all.","note":"A common way to express surprise or lack of foresight.","audio":"audio/773e927f90eecf37.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hadnt-quite-anticipated-that","text":"I hadn't quite anticipated that","kind":"chunk","form":"I hadn't quite anticipated that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild surprise or a slight lack of foresight regarding an outcome.","example":"The sudden surge in demand, I hadn't quite anticipated that.","plain":"The sudden surge in demand, I didn't fully expect that.","note":"A polite way to admit a slight misjudgment or express mild surprise.","audio":"audio/95d949673a5c85ea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hadnt-thought-of-that","text":"I hadn't thought of that","kind":"chunk","form":"I hadn't thought of that","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A genuine and polite admission that someone has presented a new and valid perspective.","example":"You're right, I hadn't thought of that potential consequence.","plain":"You're right, I didn't consider that potential consequence.","note":"Shows openness to new ideas and respect for the other person's input.","audio":"audio/053787230f48cc20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-concern-about","text":"I have a concern about","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a concern about","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a specific worry or issue regarding a plan or situation.","example":"I have a concern about the potential impact of these changes on our long-standing clients.","plain":"I am worried about the potential impact of these changes on our long-standing clients.","note":"Direct but polite, focuses on a specific point of contention.","audio":"audio/07d7f6d97aa03e5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-counter-argument","text":"I have a counter-argument","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a counter-argument","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To present an opposing argument or point of view.","example":"While your points are strong, I have a counter-argument regarding the long-term market trends.","plain":"While your points are strong, I have an opposing argument regarding the long-term market trends.","note":"Direct and academic, suitable for structured discussions.","audio":"audio/e6b2113837c33dca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-interpretation","text":"I have a different interpretation","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different interpretation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative understanding or explanation of facts or events.","example":"Based on the same data, I have a different interpretation of what caused the decline.","plain":"Based on the same data, my understanding of what caused the decline is different.","note":"Suggests a re-evaluation of existing information.","audio":"audio/7b68eed4a29244c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-opinion","text":"I have a different opinion","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different opinion","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state directly but politely that your view differs from another's.","example":"While I respect your analysis, I have a different opinion on the best course of action.","plain":"While I respect your analysis, my view on the best course of action is different.","note":"Straightforward and clear, but still polite.","audio":"audio/848856d1cee253dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-opinion-on-that","text":"I have a different opinion on that","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different opinion on that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state directly but politely that your view differs from another's.","example":"While I respect your analysis, I have a different opinion on that particular point.","plain":"While I respect your analysis, my view on that particular point is different.","note":"Straightforward and clear, but still polite.","audio":"audio/5b7d45ef789386c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-perspective","text":"I have a different perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different perspective","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative way of looking at a situation or problem.","example":"I have a different perspective on how we should allocate resources for the next quarter.","plain":"My way of looking at how we should allocate resources for the next quarter is different.","note":"Similar to \"different take,\" but perhaps slightly more formal.","audio":"audio/0735f1ef90250331.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-perspective-on-the-situation","text":"I have a different perspective on the situation","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different perspective on the situation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative way of looking at a situation or problem.","example":"I have a different perspective on the situation, especially concerning the long-term implications.","plain":"My way of looking at the situation is different, especially concerning the long-term implications.","note":"A polite way to introduce a contrasting viewpoint.","audio":"audio/30d79adc3dd75ec9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-perspective-on-this","text":"I have a different perspective on this","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different perspective on this","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative way of looking at a situation or problem.","example":"I have a different perspective on this, especially concerning the long-term implications.","plain":"My way of looking at this is different, especially concerning the long-term implications.","note":"A polite way to introduce a contrasting viewpoint.","audio":"audio/96b5dbefb2b96ec1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-take-on-that","text":"I have a different take on that","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different take on that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative perspective or interpretation of a situation or idea.","example":"While I understand your analysis, I have a different take on that particular issue.","plain":"While I understand your analysis, I have a different interpretation of that particular issue.","note":"A good way to introduce a contrasting viewpoint without directly saying \"you're wrong.\"","audio":"audio/9d715261283b46a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-understanding","text":"I have a different understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different understanding","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state that your comprehension of a situation or agreement differs from another's.","example":"I have a different understanding of the terms we agreed upon in our last meeting.","plain":"My comprehension of the terms we agreed upon in our last meeting is different.","note":"Useful for clarifying misunderstandings or politely correcting someone's memory.","audio":"audio/04013b0b82fc3256.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-understanding-of-that","text":"I have a different understanding of that","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different understanding of that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state that your comprehension of a situation or agreement differs from another's.","example":"I have a different understanding of that particular clause in the contract.","plain":"My comprehension of that particular clause in the contract is different.","note":"Useful for clarifying misunderstandings or politely correcting someone's memory.","audio":"audio/fce8724084ad24a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-understanding-of-the-situation","text":"I have a different understanding of the situation","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different understanding of the situation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state that your comprehension of a situation or agreement differs from another's.","example":"I have a different understanding of the situation, especially regarding the timeline.","plain":"My comprehension of the situation is different, especially regarding the timeline.","note":"Useful for clarifying misunderstandings or politely correcting someone's memory.","audio":"audio/4b54a7f5ba2d61bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-different-view","text":"I have a different view","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a different view","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state that your opinion or perspective differs from what has been said.","example":"While I appreciate your analysis, I have a different view on the root cause of the problem.","plain":"While I appreciate your analysis, my opinion on the root cause of the problem is different.","note":"A straightforward and polite way to introduce a contrasting opinion.","audio":"audio/b8658db69c6f1b69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-minor-point-of-contention","text":"I have a minor point of contention","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a minor point of contention","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a small disagreement or area of dispute.","example":"I have a minor point of contention regarding the proposed timeline for phase two.","plain":"I have a small disagreement regarding the proposed timeline for phase two.","note":"Formal and precise, used for specific, smaller disagreements.","audio":"audio/dd79e085d2ff8940.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-minor-point-of-disagreement","text":"I have a minor point of disagreement","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a minor point of disagreement","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a small disagreement or area of dispute.","example":"I have a minor point of disagreement regarding the proposed timeline for phase two.","plain":"I have a small disagreement regarding the proposed timeline for phase two.","note":"Formal and precise, used for specific, smaller disagreements.","audio":"audio/c218ba700c6acab2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-minor-reservation","text":"I have a minor reservation","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a minor reservation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a small doubt or concern about a plan or idea.","example":"I have a minor reservation about the proposed changes to the project scope.","plain":"I have a small doubt about the proposed changes to the project scope.","note":"Similar to \"slight reservation,\" but emphasizes the smallness of the concern.","audio":"audio/a6b45f12d10dc641.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-minor-reservation-about-the-proposal","text":"I have a minor reservation about the proposal","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a minor reservation about the proposal","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a small doubt or concern about a plan or idea.","example":"I have a minor reservation about the proposal to expand into that new market.","plain":"I have a small doubt about the proposal to expand into that new market.","note":"Similar to \"slight reservation,\" but emphasizes the smallness of the concern.","audio":"audio/b290a63b763eb7f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-concern","text":"I have a slight concern","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight concern","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor worry or issue regarding a plan or situation.","example":"I have a slight concern about the timeline for the next phase of the project.","plain":"I have a minor worry about the timeline for the next phase of the project.","note":"A gentle way to voice a specific, minor worry.","audio":"audio/2baf1044e4546ace.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-concern-about-the-implications","text":"I have a slight concern about the implications","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight concern about the implications","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor worry or issue regarding a plan or situation.","example":"I have a slight concern about the implications of this decision for our international partners.","plain":"I have a minor worry about the implications of this decision for our international partners.","note":"A gentle way to voice a specific, minor worry about consequences.","audio":"audio/0f530d31f657e1a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-disagreement","text":"I have a slight disagreement","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight disagreement","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor difference of opinion in a polite and understated way.","example":"I have a slight disagreement with the proposed wording for the new policy document.","plain":"I have a minor difference of opinion with the proposed wording for the new policy document.","note":"Very gentle and diplomatic, suitable for minor points.","audio":"audio/2ca14599d1c7df57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-quibble","text":"I have a slight quibble","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight quibble","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor objection or criticism about a small detail.","example":"I have a slight quibble with the formatting of the report, but the content is excellent.","plain":"I have a minor objection to the formatting of the report, but the content is excellent.","note":"Very understated and informal, used for small, non-critical points.","audio":"audio/ef736eb93dcd7f54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-reservation","text":"I have a slight reservation","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight reservation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor doubt or concern about a plan or idea.","example":"I have a slight reservation about launching the product without more extensive user testing.","plain":"I have a minor doubt about launching the product without more extensive user testing.","note":"Polite and understated way to voice a concern without outright rejecting an idea.","audio":"audio/1bbfdcbb777eb06b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-reservation-about-that","text":"I have a slight reservation about that","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight reservation about that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor doubt or concern about a specific point or idea.","example":"I have a slight reservation about that particular clause in the contract.","plain":"I have a minor doubt about that particular clause in the contract.","note":"Focuses on a very specific, small point of concern.","audio":"audio/3a0f23615f3f51ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-a-slight-reservation-about-that-point","text":"I have a slight reservation about that point","kind":"collocation","form":"I have a slight reservation about that point","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a minor doubt or concern about a specific point or idea.","example":"I have a slight reservation about that point regarding the budget allocation.","plain":"I have a minor doubt about that point regarding the budget allocation.","note":"Focuses on a very specific, small point of concern.","audio":"audio/f0d4c2add1d9a8af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-my-reservations-about","text":"I have my reservations about","kind":"frame","form":"I have my reservations about","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses doubts or hesitations regarding a plan, idea, or situation.","example":"I have my reservations about the feasibility of implementing such a large change quickly.","plain":"I have doubts about the feasibility of implementing such a large change quickly.","note":"A polite and professional way to express skepticism.","audio":"audio/71b20296092d9347.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-to-disagree","text":"I have to disagree","kind":"frame","form":"I have to disagree","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A direct but still polite way to state your disagreement, often implying a strong conviction.","example":"On this particular issue, I have to disagree with the proposed course of action.","plain":"On this particular issue, I must disagree with the proposed course of action.","note":"More direct than \"I'm inclined to disagree,\" but still respectful.","audio":"audio/8e808848df68c24e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-have-to-take-issue-with","text":"I have to take issue with","kind":"frame","form":"I have to take issue with","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To disagree strongly but politely with a specific point or statement.","example":"I have to take issue with the claim that our team lacks motivation.","plain":"I must disagree with the claim that our team lacks motivation.","note":"More direct and firm than some other phrases, but still professional.","audio":"audio/b1cca843447c0691.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hope-im-making-sense","text":"I hope I'm making sense","kind":"chunk","form":"I hope I'm making sense","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire that the audience is understanding the explanation.","example":"I hope I'm making sense as I explain these complex technical specifications.","plain":"I hope you are understanding me as I explain these complex technical specifications.","note":"A polite way to check for audience comprehension, often used with complex topics.","audio":"audio/7f9e77ec277f5d49.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hope-ive-made-that-clear","text":"I hope I've made that clear","kind":"chunk","form":"I hope I've made that clear","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely check if the audience has understood the explanation.","example":"I hope I've made that clear regarding the new reporting procedures.","plain":"I hope you understand the new reporting procedures.","note":"A common way to conclude an explanation, inviting questions if there's still confusion.","audio":"audio/b1c4525ba459ab9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hope-that-clarifies","text":"I hope that clarifies","kind":"frame","form":"I hope that clarifies","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Asks if the explanation provided has made the point clear.","example":"I hope that clarifies the distinction between the two software versions.","plain":"I hope that makes the distinction between the two software versions clear.","note":"A polite way to check for understanding after an explanation.","audio":"audio/458bee2687a61f52.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hope-that-makes-sense","text":"I hope that makes sense","kind":"chunk","form":"I hope that makes sense","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely check if the audience has understood the explanation.","example":"I hope that makes sense, especially for those new to this technical concept.","plain":"I hope you understand that, especially for those new to this technical concept.","note":"A common, slightly informal way to invite questions if clarity is still needed.","audio":"audio/56c7f64f65b362d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hope-this-has-been-informative","text":"I hope this has been informative","kind":"chunk","form":"I hope this has been informative","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely express the wish that the audience found the presentation useful.","example":"I hope this has been informative and provided valuable insights for your work.","plain":"I hope you found this useful and it provided valuable insights for your work.","note":"A polite and common closing remark, expressing the speaker's intent.","audio":"audio/332326c29548ef3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-hope-you-dont-mind-me-asking","text":"I hope you don't mind me asking","kind":"frame","form":"I hope you don't mind me asking","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite preface to a potentially personal or slightly intrusive question.","example":"I hope you don't mind me asking, but how long have you been working in this field?","plain":"May I ask how long you've been working in this field?","note":"Shows consideration for the other person's privacy. Use for slightly sensitive topics.","audio":"audio/386eeb2791092fbd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-just-wanted-to-add","text":"I just wanted to add","kind":"frame","form":"I just wanted to add","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to contribute an additional piece of information or a brief comment.","example":"I just wanted to add that our recent customer survey results support this direction.","plain":"I wanted to mention that our recent customer survey results support this direction.","note":"Informal and concise, used to make a quick, relevant contribution.","audio":"audio/0968c42504d90ee8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-just-wanted-to-add-that","text":"I just wanted to add that","kind":"frame","form":"I just wanted to add that","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introduces an additional piece of information or a supplementary comment.","example":"Before we move on, I just wanted to add that the client seemed very pleased.","plain":"I would like to include this extra piece of information.","note":"A common and polite way to contribute to a conversation, often after someone else has spoken.","audio":"audio/b937d5eafe49bb70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-just-wanted-to-check","text":"I just wanted to check","kind":"frame","form":"I just wanted to check","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for confirmation or to verify a piece of information.","example":"I just wanted to check if everyone received the pre-reading materials for today.","plain":"I wanted to confirm if everyone received the pre-reading materials for today.","note":"Informal and considerate, used to ensure everyone is prepared or informed.","audio":"audio/98ff9135524aa26f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-just-wanted-to-chime-in","text":"I just wanted to chime in","kind":"chunk","form":"I just wanted to chime in","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"To add a quick comment or opinion to a conversation.","example":"I just wanted to chime in and say I agree with your assessment.","plain":"I wanted to add that I agree with you.","note":"A slightly informal but common way to contribute to a discussion.","audio":"audio/548b547e5a15b39d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-just-wanted-to-confirm","text":"I just wanted to confirm","kind":"frame","form":"I just wanted to confirm","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to verify information or an understanding.","example":"I just wanted to confirm the deadline for submitting the final report.","plain":"I wanted to verify the deadline for submitting the final report.","note":"Professional and practical, used to ensure accuracy and avoid misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/3889ab060fdb8fe2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-just-wanted-to-underscore-that","text":"I just wanted to underscore that","kind":"frame","form":"I just wanted to underscore that","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Used to emphasize a point that has already been made.","example":"I just wanted to underscore that the budget constraints are quite strict this quarter.","plain":"I wanted to emphasize that the budget limits are very strict this quarter.","note":"A slightly formal and emphatic way to highlight an important point.","audio":"audio/23cca8c5d7f3253d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-know-how-frustrating-it-can-be","text":"I know how frustrating it can be","kind":"frame","form":"I know how frustrating it can be","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses empathy by relating to someone's experience of annoyance.","example":"Waiting for bureaucracy to move slowly, I know how frustrating it can be.","plain":"Waiting for bureaucracy to move slowly, I understand that is very annoying.","note":"A common empathetic phrase, often implying shared experience.","audio":"audio/1a509ce4d661879f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-made-a-slight-error","text":"I made a slight error","kind":"chunk","form":"I made a slight error","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that you said something incorrectly or inaccurately before.","example":"I made a slight error earlier when I mentioned the deadline; it's actually next Friday.","plain":"I said something wrong earlier when I mentioned the deadline; it's actually next Friday.","note":"A direct and professional way to correct a factual error.","audio":"audio/1ed46827a089e445.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-must-admit-im-a-little-surprised","text":"I must admit I'm a little surprised","kind":"frame","form":"I must admit I'm a little surprised","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild surprise, often implying a hint of disappointment or skepticism.","example":"Given his experience, I must admit I'm a little surprised by his decision.","plain":"Given his experience, I am somewhat surprised by his decision.","note":"A polite and understated way to express unexpectedness, often with a negative nuance.","audio":"audio/497103f30bfcc32f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-must-respectfully-decline","text":"I must respectfully decline","kind":"collocation","form":"I must respectfully decline","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A very formal and polite way to refuse an invitation or offer.","example":"I must respectfully decline your kind invitation due to a prior commitment.","plain":"I have to politely refuse your kind invitation due to a prior commitment.","note":"Used in formal contexts, conveying utmost politeness and regret.","audio":"audio/5c428f50cb8dd7a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-must-say-im-surprised","text":"I must say I'm surprised","kind":"chunk","form":"I must say I'm surprised","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a direct, often polite, admission of unexpectedness.","example":"I must say I'm surprised by the sudden change in the project's direction.","plain":"I am surprised by the sudden change in the project's direction.","note":"A polite and common way to express surprise, often used to introduce a reaction.","audio":"audio/b8722e1b6a82d857.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-must-stress-that","text":"I must stress that","kind":"frame","form":"I must stress that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to strongly emphasize a point, indicating its critical importance.","example":"I must stress that adherence to safety protocols is non-negotiable.","plain":"I need to emphasize that adherence to safety protocols is non-negotiable.","note":"A direct and forceful way to highlight a critical rule or fact.","audio":"audio/23c1d49c73640233.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-mustnt-keep-you","text":"I mustn't keep you","kind":"chunk","form":"I mustn't keep you","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to end a conversation, suggesting you are aware of the other person's time.","example":"I mustn't keep you any longer; I know you have a tight schedule.","plain":"I shouldn't hold you up any longer; I know you have a tight schedule.","note":"A slightly more formal and very polite way to signal the end of a chat.","audio":"audio/e4b17d3da5d6d3b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-propose-that","text":"I propose that","kind":"frame","form":"I propose that","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A formal way to put forward an idea or plan for consideration.","example":"I propose that we form a small task force to investigate this further.","plain":"I suggest that we form a small task force to investigate this further.","note":"More formal than \"I suggest\" and often used for significant proposals.","audio":"audio/8ac2d7aecb36a82e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-quite-understand-why","text":"I quite understand why","kind":"frame","form":"I quite understand why","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses full comprehension of someone's reasons or feelings.","example":"After working so many extra hours, I quite understand why you'd want to take a long weekend.","plain":"After working so many extra hours, I completely understand your desire for a long weekend.","note":"A polite and empathetic way to show you grasp someone's perspective or motivation.","audio":"audio/489358c1190e88eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-remember-distinctly","text":"I remember distinctly","kind":"frame","form":"I remember distinctly","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"emphasizes the clarity and certainty of a memory being shared","example":"I remember distinctly the moment I realized I'd left my passport at home.","plain":"I clearly remember the moment I realized I'd left my passport at home.","note":"Adds authority and detail to a memory. Slightly formal but common in speech.","audio":"audio/c2c8b8f39dbbbeaf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-see-where-youre-coming-from","text":"I see where you're coming from","kind":"frame","form":"I see where you're coming from","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing","feedback"],"meaning":"To acknowledge understanding of someone's perspective before expressing a different view or reservation.","example":"I see where you're coming from, but I'm concerned about the practical implementation.","plain":"I understand your perspective, but I'm concerned about the practical implementation.","note":"A common, empathetic opening to a polite disagreement, showing you've listened.","audio":"audio/282192e1decd299d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-see-your-point-but","text":"I see your point but","kind":"frame","form":"I see your point but","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To acknowledge someone's argument before presenting a counter-argument or different perspective.","example":"I see your point but I think we need to consider the long-term impact on employee morale.","plain":"I understand your point, however, I think we need to consider the long-term impact on employee morale.","note":"A diplomatic way to express partial disagreement or introduce a nuance.","audio":"audio/65fd17fc3e49f354.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-should-clarify-my-statement","text":"I should clarify my statement","kind":"frame","form":"I should clarify my statement","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates a need to make an earlier remark more precise or understandable.","example":"I should clarify my statement about the budget; it applies only to operational costs.","plain":"I need to make my statement about the budget clearer; it applies only to operational costs.","note":"A polite way to self-correct or add necessary detail.","audio":"audio/f0a6ac5476d940cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-should-have-been-clearer","text":"I should have been clearer","kind":"chunk","form":"I should have been clearer","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To take responsibility for a lack of clarity in one's communication.","example":"I should have been clearer about the scope of the pilot program.","plain":"I apologize, I did not explain the scope of the pilot program clearly enough.","note":"A humble and professional way to acknowledge one's own role in a misunderstanding.","audio":"audio/2754362b7b2b53e6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-should-have-been-more-precise","text":"I should have been more precise","kind":"frame","form":"I should have been more precise","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Expresses regret for not having been exact or detailed enough in an earlier statement.","example":"I should have been more precise about the budget figures; they exclude marketing costs.","plain":"I should have been more exact about the budget figures; they exclude marketing costs.","note":"A self-correction that shows attention to detail and accuracy.","audio":"audio/319bc4a02ac24b13.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-tend-to-agree","text":"I tend to agree","kind":"chunk","form":"I tend to agree","level":"C2","situations":["meetings","feedback"],"meaning":"expresses a general inclination to concur with an idea.","example":"I tend to agree that we need to streamline our internal processes.","plain":"I generally agree that we need to streamline our internal processes.","note":"Soft way to express agreement, often with a slight reservation or nuance.","audio":"audio/3b65c8f5300f30b0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-that-concludes-our-discussion","text":"I think that concludes our discussion","kind":"frame","form":"I think that concludes our discussion","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A formal way to state that the conversation or meeting has reached its end.","example":"I think that concludes our discussion for today, unless anyone has further points.","plain":"I believe we are finished talking for today, unless there's anything else.","note":"A formal and definitive way to close a meeting or conversation.","audio":"audio/a90097e59af8734f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-that-covers-it","text":"I think that covers it","kind":"chunk","form":"I think that covers it","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I believe we have discussed all necessary points and nothing important was missed.","example":"I think that covers it for today's agenda; thank you for your input.","plain":"I believe we discussed everything on the agenda today.","note":"A polite way to conclude a discussion, often inviting others to add anything missed.","audio":"audio/5fea4a052feba589.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-thats-all-for-now","text":"I think that's all for now","kind":"chunk","form":"I think that's all for now","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggests that there are no more immediate topics to discuss, signaling an end.","example":"Unless anyone has anything else, I think that's all for now.","plain":"I believe we have finished discussing everything for this moment.","note":"A polite and common way to conclude a meeting or discussion. Neutral.","audio":"audio/4f158d93cec45bd2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-were-good-here","text":"I think we're good here","kind":"chunk","form":"I think we're good here","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I believe we have successfully completed our discussion or achieved our goal.","example":"I think we're good here; all my questions have been answered.","plain":"I think we're done; all my questions are answered.","note":"A casual but clear way to signal the end of a specific topic or the call itself.","audio":"audio/9b0bdfa697a2b1cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-weve-covered","text":"I think we've covered","kind":"frame","form":"I think we've covered","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that all necessary topics or points have been discussed.","example":"I think we've covered all the essential points on our agenda for today.","plain":"I believe we have discussed all the essential points on our agenda for today.","note":"A polite way to signal readiness to conclude a discussion or the meeting.","audio":"audio/a4626f380765eea6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-think-weve-covered-everything","text":"I think we've covered everything","kind":"chunk","form":"I think we've covered everything","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggests that all necessary topics have been discussed and the meeting can end.","example":"Well, I think we've covered everything on the agenda for today.","plain":"I believe we have discussed all the necessary points.","note":"A common way to signal the end of a discussion or meeting. Neutral and professional.","audio":"audio/18d7466c8a5c40d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-trust-that","text":"I trust that","kind":"frame","form":"I trust that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express an expectation or belief that something is true or will happen.","example":"I trust that you found this overview of our progress informative.","plain":"I hope you found this overview of our progress informative.","note":"A formal and polite way to express confidence in the audience's understanding or the value of the content.","audio":"audio/226523b337346879.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-trust-thats-clear","text":"I trust that's clear","kind":"chunk","form":"I trust that's clear","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Expresses confidence that the audience has understood the explanation.","example":"I trust that's clear regarding the new policy on expense claims.","plain":"I believe you understand the new policy on expense claims.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to conclude an explanation.","audio":"audio/0ff108d19da7cc5f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-trust-this-was-informative","text":"I trust this was informative","kind":"chunk","form":"I trust this was informative","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A polite closing remark, expressing confidence that the audience found the information beneficial.","example":"I trust this was informative in understanding our new strategic direction.","plain":"I believe this information was beneficial in understanding our new strategic direction.","note":"A slightly more formal and confident way to conclude a presentation.","audio":"audio/9c8a06c802c9a607.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-trust-youll-agree-that","text":"I trust you'll agree that","kind":"frame","form":"I trust you'll agree that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To express confidence that the audience will concur with a statement.","example":"I trust you'll agree that these findings present a compelling case for change.","plain":"I believe you'll agree that these findings present a compelling case for change.","note":"A polite and persuasive way to seek agreement from the audience.","audio":"audio/cc95e8af3e45d519.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-very-much-look-forward-to","text":"I very much look forward to","kind":"frame","form":"I very much look forward to","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Expressing strong anticipation and eagerness for a future event or interaction.","example":"I very much look forward to our next meeting to discuss this further.","plain":"I am very eager for our next meeting to discuss this further.","note":"More formal and emphatic than \"I'm looking forward to it\".","audio":"audio/3d9e8e48d7d22fea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-want-to-draw-your-attention-to","text":"I want to draw your attention to","kind":"frame","form":"I want to draw your attention to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Directs the audience's focus to a specific detail or piece of information.","example":"I want to draw your attention to the significant growth in our online sales figures.","plain":"I want you to notice the significant growth in our online sales figures.","note":"A direct and polite way to highlight something important.","audio":"audio/5b8d061cf32e56e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-about-to-say","text":"I was about to say","kind":"frame","form":"I was about to say","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"indicates that the speaker was on the verge of making a point","example":"You just mentioned the budget, which is what I was about to say.","plain":"You just mentioned the budget, which is what I was going to say.","note":"A conversational phrase to show agreement or that you had the same thought.","audio":"audio/f43dc7d9f062d5c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-hoping-to","text":"I was hoping to","kind":"frame","form":"I was hoping to","level":"C2","situations":["travel","socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire or intention, often as a gentle suggestion for plans.","example":"I was hoping to get everyone together for a board game night soon.","plain":"I want to get everyone together for a board game night soon.","note":"A soft, indirect way to propose an activity, making it less demanding.","audio":"audio/e63537281cec1c56.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-hoping-you-could","text":"I was hoping you could","kind":"frame","form":"I was hoping you could","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A gentle and indirect way to ask for help or a favour.","example":"I was hoping you could recommend a good local restaurant that serves vegetarian options.","plain":"Can you recommend a good local restaurant that serves vegetarian options?","note":"Very polite and less demanding than a direct question.","audio":"audio/75c942da13bdb95d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-just-about-to","text":"I was just about to","kind":"frame","form":"I was just about to","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Indicating that you were on the verge of doing or saying something.","example":"I was just about to ask you the same question about the project.","plain":"I was going to ask you the same question about the project.","note":"Common for showing shared thoughts or intentions.","audio":"audio/b402372417c02d73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-was-just-wondering-if","text":"I was just wondering if","kind":"frame","form":"I was just wondering if","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A gentle way to introduce a question or a suggestion, making it sound less demanding.","example":"I was just wondering if you'd tried the new coffee shop downstairs.","plain":"Have you tried the new coffee shop downstairs?","note":"Softens the inquiry, making it more conversational and less like an interrogation.","audio":"audio/2fd9a6bb36a310d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wont-deny-that","text":"I won't deny that","kind":"frame","form":"I won't deny that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Acknowledges a fact or truth, often one that might be slightly negative or difficult to admit.","example":"I won't deny that the initial results were somewhat disappointing for the team.","plain":"I admit that the initial results were somewhat disappointing for the team.","note":"A polite way to concede a point or acknowledge a reality.","audio":"audio/3940ed022c32889d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wont-keep-you","text":"I won't keep you","kind":"chunk","form":"I won't keep you","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to end a conversation, implying you don't want to take up more of their time.","example":"Well, I won't keep you, I know you have a busy afternoon ahead.","plain":"I don't want to take up any more of your time, I know you have a busy afternoon ahead.","note":"Polite and considerate way to signal the end of a chat.","audio":"audio/2a46e28477a5c5c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wont-keep-you-any-longer","text":"I won't keep you any longer","kind":"chunk","form":"I won't keep you any longer","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to end a conversation, implying respect for the other person's time.","example":"I think we've addressed all points, so I won't keep you any longer.","plain":"I will not take up any more of your time.","note":"Formal and considerate, often used in professional or slightly more formal personal calls.","audio":"audio/f43e1a05f81cf3bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-would-venture-to-say","text":"I would venture to say","kind":"frame","form":"I would venture to say","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Expresses a cautious opinion or suggestion, acknowledging it might be a bold or unproven claim.","example":"I would venture to say that the preliminary results indicate a significant paradigm shift.","plain":"I think the preliminary results indicate a significant paradigm shift, though I'm not entirely sure.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to offer an opinion or hypothesis, often used when speculating.","audio":"audio/3cf2b12063a289de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-be-surprised","text":"I wouldn't be surprised","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't be surprised","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Expressing that a particular outcome or situation is expected or plausible.","example":"I wouldn't be surprised if they announced a new policy next quarter.","plain":"I would expect them to announce a new policy next quarter.","note":"Indicates a strong likelihood or a feeling of inevitability.","audio":"audio/a77dac36b1e43a82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-blame-you-for","text":"I wouldn't blame you for","kind":"frame","form":"I wouldn't blame you for","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates that someone's reaction or decision is justified and expected.","example":"After that treatment, I wouldn't blame you for wanting to find a new job.","plain":"After that treatment, I would not criticize you for wanting to find a new job.","note":"Shows strong support and validation for someone's potentially strong reaction.","audio":"audio/0fa7b029a6290ad2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-have-thought-so","text":"I wouldn't have thought so","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't have thought so","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses mild surprise or a change in expectation about a situation or fact.","example":"I wouldn't have thought so, but apparently, the new system is quite intuitive to use.","plain":"I wouldn't have expected it, but apparently, the new system is quite intuitive to use.","note":"Shows a slight shift from a previous assumption or expectation.","audio":"audio/fac2067bb39dabad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-miss-it-for-anything","text":"I wouldn't miss it for anything","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't miss it for anything","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme eagerness and determination to attend an event.","example":"Your birthday bash? I wouldn't miss it for anything!","plain":"Your birthday party? I absolutely must be there!","note":"A very enthusiastic and emphatic way to accept an invitation.","audio":"audio/75b01c05efed7e33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-miss-it-for-the-world","text":"I wouldn't miss it for the world","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't miss it for the world","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An emphatic way to accept an invitation, showing great enthusiasm.","example":"Your birthday party? I wouldn't miss it for the world!","plain":"Your birthday party? I definitely want to be there!","note":"Expresses strong eagerness and commitment to attend an event. Very enthusiastic.","audio":"audio/25896d48e411d90f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-put-it-past-him","text":"I wouldn't put it past him","kind":"frame","form":"I wouldn't put it past him","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses a negative expectation about someone's character, implying they are capable of a bad action.","example":"I wouldn't put it past him to try and take credit for someone else's hard work.","plain":"I wouldn't be surprised if he tried to take credit for someone else's hard work.","note":"Used to express a cynical or knowing view of someone's likely behavior.","audio":"audio/ee6d7db4a778acce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-quite-put-it-that-way","text":"I wouldn't quite put it that way","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't quite put it that way","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite disagreement with someone's phrasing or interpretation of a situation.","example":"While I agree with the sentiment, I wouldn't quite put it that way in a formal report.","plain":"While I agree with the sentiment, I would use different words in a formal report.","note":"A diplomatic way to suggest alternative phrasing or a different interpretation.","audio":"audio/15ddf4d285ba9f98.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.i-wouldnt-wish-that-on-anyone","text":"I wouldn't wish that on anyone","kind":"chunk","form":"I wouldn't wish that on anyone","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong empathy by stating that a difficult experience is something no one should endure.","example":"Dealing with that kind of bureaucratic nightmare, I wouldn't wish that on anyone.","plain":"Dealing with that kind of bureaucratic nightmare is so bad, I hope no one else has to experience it.","note":"A strong, empathetic expression of sympathy for a very unpleasant experience.","audio":"audio/e82abd28dd9af282.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-appreciate-it-if","text":"I'd appreciate it if","kind":"frame","form":"I'd appreciate it if","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to make a request or express a preference.","example":"I'd appreciate it if you could confirm our reservation via email before tomorrow.","plain":"Please confirm our reservation via email before tomorrow.","note":"A very common and polite way to make a request, slightly more formal than \"please\".","audio":"audio/5065feca1ab20ec4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-argue-the-opposite","text":"I'd argue the opposite","kind":"frame","form":"I'd argue the opposite","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To state a directly contrasting opinion in a polite, yet firm, manner.","example":"While that's a valid point, I'd argue the opposite regarding the project's long-term viability.","plain":"While that's a valid point, I believe the project's long-term viability is actually the reverse.","note":"A more direct but still polite way to express a strong counter-argument.","audio":"audio/34b05592973b3467.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-delighted-to-have-you","text":"I'd be delighted to have you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd be delighted to have you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A very polite and warm way to invite someone to your home or event.","example":"I'd be delighted to have you over for dinner next Saturday.","plain":"I would love for you to come over for dinner next Saturday.","note":"A gracious and welcoming invitation, often used for more formal or special occasions.","audio":"audio/59fd4b6cba2e8aab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-grateful-if","text":"I'd be grateful if","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be grateful if","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A very polite and formal way to make a request.","example":"I'd be grateful if you could provide a map of the local area.","plain":"Please provide a map of the local area.","note":"Formal and deferential, often used in written communication or formal requests.","audio":"audio/48751ba72d840e06.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-happy-to-contribute","text":"I'd be happy to contribute","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd be happy to contribute","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offers to provide something or help financially for a shared event.","example":"I'd be happy to contribute a dessert to the potluck.","plain":"I would gladly bring a dessert to the potluck.","note":"A polite and proactive offer to share the burden or cost of an event.","audio":"audio/4566f78af7a65f16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-happy-to-lend-a-hand","text":"I'd be happy to lend a hand","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd be happy to lend a hand","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Offers to help with a task or chore.","example":"I'd be happy to lend a hand with the tidying up.","plain":"I would gladly help with the tidying up.","note":"A friendly and proactive offer of practical assistance.","audio":"audio/5e8fceb402bc15d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-hesitant-to-agree","text":"I'd be hesitant to agree","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be hesitant to agree","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses reluctance to give full agreement, implying reservations or partial disagreement.","example":"I'd be hesitant to agree to those terms without reviewing the full contract details first.","plain":"I would be reluctant to agree to those terms without reviewing the full contract details first.","note":"A polite and indirect way to signal reservations or partial disagreement.","audio":"audio/aaabd90c9c58c3f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-inclined-to-think-otherwise","text":"I'd be inclined to think otherwise","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be inclined to think otherwise","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely state a preference for a different conclusion or opinion, implying disagreement.","example":"While the data suggests one thing, I'd be inclined to think otherwise given our past experiences.","plain":"While the data suggests one thing, I would prefer to think differently given our past experiences.","note":"A formal and understated way to express a differing opinion or conclusion.","audio":"audio/70b23510fd3fa26a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-interested-to-know","text":"I'd be interested to know","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be interested to know","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for information or express curiosity.","example":"I'd be interested to know if there are any local events happening this weekend.","plain":"I want to know if there are any local events happening this weekend.","note":"A polite and indirect way to ask a question.","audio":"audio/11737bdca0b690dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-remiss-not-to-mention","text":"I'd be remiss not to mention","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be remiss not to mention","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","persuading"],"meaning":"To state that one feels obligated to bring up a point, implying it would be a mistake to omit it.","example":"I'd be remiss not to mention the potential impact of currency fluctuations on this long-term contract.","plain":"I feel obligated to mention the potential impact of currency fluctuations on this long-term contract.","note":"Formal and emphasizes the importance of the point being raised.","audio":"audio/13ecd3d51c99dc62.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-thrilled-to","text":"I'd be thrilled to","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd be thrilled to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses great pleasure and enthusiasm about an invitation or opportunity.","example":"I'd be thrilled to join you for the celebration.","plain":"I would be very happy to join you for the celebration.","note":"A strong and genuine expression of happiness to accept an invitation.","audio":"audio/6cfa83cecd47fdf1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-be-wary-of","text":"I'd be wary of","kind":"frame","form":"I'd be wary of","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express caution or concern about a particular idea or action, implying potential negative outcomes.","example":"I'd be wary of making such a significant investment without thoroughly researching the market.","plain":"I would be cautious about making such a significant investment without thoroughly researching the market.","note":"Used to express a strong sense of caution or potential risk, often implying disagreement with a proposed action.","audio":"audio/ddc5874ee3a131dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-beg-to-differ","text":"I'd beg to differ","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd beg to differ","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A slightly softer version of \"I beg to differ,\" indicating polite disagreement.","example":"I'd beg to differ on the idea that experience alone guarantees success in this role.","plain":"I would politely disagree with the idea that experience alone guarantees success in this role.","note":"The \"d\" (would) makes it slightly less direct and more tentative than \"I beg to differ.\"","audio":"audio/1ebabcd9e6a16eb6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-go-along-with-that","text":"I'd go along with that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd go along with that","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To agree with a suggestion or proposal.","example":"I'd go along with that proposal, as it seems like the most practical solution.","plain":"I would agree with that proposal, as it seems like the most practical solution.","note":"Common, slightly informal way to express agreement.","audio":"audio/40488d2895e3e950.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-hesitate-to-endorse","text":"I'd hesitate to endorse","kind":"frame","form":"I'd hesitate to endorse","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express reluctance to fully support or approve something, indicating a significant reservation.","example":"While the idea has merit, I'd hesitate to endorse it without a more detailed risk assessment.","plain":"While the idea has merit, I wouldn't support it without a more detailed risk assessment.","note":"A formal and cautious way to express strong reservations or a refusal to approve.","audio":"audio/d9776c1d585e3326.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-hesitate-to-say","text":"I'd hesitate to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'd hesitate to say","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses reluctance to agree with a statement, implying disagreement without stating it directly.","example":"I'd hesitate to say that the project was a complete success given the budget overruns.","plain":"I would be reluctant to say that the project was a complete success given the budget overruns.","note":"An understated way to express disagreement or reservation.","audio":"audio/ab388c80cf0761f1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-imagine-that","text":"I'd imagine that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd imagine that","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Expressing a reasonable assumption or expectation about a situation.","example":"I'd imagine that working from home has its own set of challenges.","plain":"I would assume that working from home has its own set of challenges.","note":"Polite way to show empathy or understanding by making an educated guess.","audio":"audio/8afcd4a6952f19c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-just-add-that","text":"I'd just add that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd just add that","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to contribute an additional piece of information or a brief comment.","example":"I'd just add that the client expressed satisfaction with the initial prototype.","plain":"I would also like to mention that the client expressed satisfaction with the initial prototype.","note":"Informal and concise, used to make a quick, relevant contribution.","audio":"audio/480d74a2fb09cf9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-lean-towards-a-different-approach","text":"I'd lean towards a different approach","kind":"frame","form":"I'd lean towards a different approach","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express a preference for an alternative method or idea, subtly disagreeing with the current one.","example":"I understand your reasoning, but I'd lean towards a different approach for resolving this conflict.","plain":"I understand your reasoning, but I prefer a different approach for resolving this conflict.","note":"A polite and indirect way to suggest an alternative without directly rejecting the current idea.","audio":"audio/cc4ac5378ac6fa17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-add-a-caveat","text":"I'd like to add a caveat","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to add a caveat","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To introduce a warning or condition that limits a general statement or agreement.","example":"I'd like to add a caveat that these projections are highly dependent on stable economic conditions.","plain":"I want to add a warning that these projections are highly dependent on stable economic conditions.","note":"Formal and precise, used to qualify an agreement or statement.","audio":"audio/0f7d3ee43fd4245f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-add-a-point-of-clarification","text":"I'd like to add a point of clarification","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to add a point of clarification","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To interject with additional information that might alter the understanding or challenge a statement.","example":"Before we conclude, I'd like to add a point of clarification regarding the project's scope.","plain":"Before we conclude, I want to add some clarifying information regarding the project's scope.","note":"A polite way to introduce a detail that might subtly contradict or refine what was said.","audio":"audio/f491bedfec70dd9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-briefly-comment","text":"I'd like to briefly comment","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to briefly comment","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire to make a short remark or observation.","example":"I'd like to briefly comment on the last point before we move on.","plain":"I want to say something short about the last topic before we continue.","note":"A polite and professional way to signal an upcoming short contribution.","audio":"audio/679b146f6bc5277e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-briefly-outline","text":"I'd like to briefly outline","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to briefly outline","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To present the main points or structure of a topic concisely.","example":"I'd like to briefly outline our strategy for market entry in the coming year.","plain":"I want to quickly present the main points of our market entry strategy for the coming year.","note":"A formal way to introduce a summary or overview of a plan or topic.","audio":"audio/d4b5f2ddc11d8b20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-briefly-summarise","text":"I'd like to briefly summarise","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to briefly summarise","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide a quick overview of the main points.","example":"I'd like to briefly summarise the key findings from our market research.","plain":"I want to quickly summarise the key findings from our market research.","note":"A polite and formal way to introduce a concise summary.","audio":"audio/5a77be444977ad75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-briefly-touch-upon","text":"I'd like to briefly touch upon","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to briefly touch upon","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To mention a topic quickly without going into extensive detail.","example":"I'd like to briefly touch upon the ethical considerations of AI development.","plain":"I want to quickly mention the ethical considerations of AI development.","note":"A polite and formal way to introduce a minor point or a topic that won't be fully explored.","audio":"audio/9c403096d55cb4d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-challenge-the-assumption-that","text":"I'd like to challenge the assumption that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to challenge the assumption that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely question the basis or premise of an argument or statement.","example":"I'd like to challenge the assumption that our target audience is solely interested in low prices.","plain":"I want to question the basic idea that our target audience is solely interested in low prices.","note":"Direct and analytical, used to get to the root of a disagreement.","audio":"audio/94b0409e83c36339.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-challenge-the-notion","text":"I'd like to challenge the notion","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to challenge the notion","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely dispute a widely held belief or idea.","example":"I'd like to challenge the notion that success is solely dependent on individual effort.","plain":"I want to dispute the idea that success is solely dependent on individual effort.","note":"Targets a general concept or common belief.","audio":"audio/ee89a053a12aa1ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-circle-back","text":"I'd like to circle back","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to circle back","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To express a desire to return to a previously discussed topic later.","example":"I'd like to circle back to the budget discussion once we have more clarity on funding.","plain":"I want to return to the budget discussion once we have more clarity on funding.","note":"Polite and common, used to defer a topic without dismissing it. Slightly informal.","audio":"audio/ceb8e1bed39dd5ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-clarify-one-point","text":"I'd like to clarify one point","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to clarify one point","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely interject and correct or elaborate on a specific detail, often implying a subtle disagreement.","example":"Before we move on, I'd like to clarify one point about the project timeline.","plain":"Before we move on, I want to correct a detail about the project timeline.","note":"Used to gently correct or add precision to a statement, often to prevent misunderstanding.","audio":"audio/e5fa45b043074a6d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-conclude-by-saying","text":"I'd like to conclude by saying","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to conclude by saying","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce the final remarks or summary of the presentation.","example":"I'd like to conclude by saying that our future looks promising with these initiatives.","plain":"In conclusion, our future looks promising with these initiatives.","note":"A formal and polite way to signal the end of the presentation and deliver a final message.","audio":"audio/69b2e661589d9474.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-draw-attention-to","text":"I'd like to draw attention to","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to draw attention to","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To politely direct focus towards a specific point or issue.","example":"I'd like to draw attention to the potential cost savings of this new approach.","plain":"I want to highlight the potential cost savings of this new approach.","note":"A formal way to emphasize a particular aspect.","audio":"audio/897ce788256a0acb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-draw-your-attention-back-to","text":"I'd like to draw your attention back to","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to draw your attention back to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To redirect the audience's focus to a previous point or visual aid.","example":"I'd like to draw your attention back to the initial market analysis on slide three.","plain":"Please look again at the initial market analysis on slide three.","note":"A polite way to refocus the audience, often after a digression or a complex explanation.","audio":"audio/3847547772a7650f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-echo-that-sentiment","text":"I'd like to echo that sentiment","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to echo that sentiment","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses agreement with a feeling or opinion that someone else has stated.","example":"I'd like to echo that sentiment; teamwork is crucial for this project's success.","plain":"I agree with that feeling; working together is essential for this project.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to express strong agreement.","audio":"audio/a52a1cb2d5346356.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-elaborate-on","text":"I'd like to elaborate on","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to elaborate on","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide more details or a fuller explanation of a specific topic.","example":"I'd like to elaborate on the methodology used for our recent customer survey.","plain":"I want to provide more details about the methodology used for our recent customer survey.","note":"A polite and formal way to signal a deeper dive into a particular subject.","audio":"audio/4170399c0dd12037.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-get-your-take-on","text":"I'd like to get your take on","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to get your take on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for someone's opinion or perspective on a specific matter.","example":"I'd like to get your take on the potential impact of these changes on our workflow.","plain":"I want to hear your opinion on the potential impact of these changes on our workflow.","note":"Collaborative and inclusive, used to solicit individual viewpoints.","audio":"audio/7fe586b5d3b14a5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-inquire-about","text":"I'd like to inquire about","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to inquire about","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A formal and polite way to ask for information.","example":"I'd like to inquire about the availability of a room for tonight.","plain":"I want to ask about the availability of a room for tonight.","note":"Formal and professional, suitable for customer service interactions.","audio":"audio/6d7e583f848b17c0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-make-a-case-for","text":"I'd like to make a case for","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to make a case for","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To argue in favor of a particular idea or course of action.","example":"I'd like to make a case for investing more heavily in renewable energy sources.","plain":"I want to argue for investing more heavily in renewable energy sources.","note":"A formal and direct way to advocate for something.","audio":"audio/0d0fcbea3d22bdc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-make-it-clear","text":"I'd like to make it clear","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to make it clear","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To state something explicitly to avoid misunderstanding.","example":"I'd like to make it clear that this is a collaborative effort, not a directive.","plain":"I want to state clearly that this is a collaborative effort, not a directive.","note":"Used to ensure there is no ambiguity about a point.","audio":"audio/43b31fd65536e51f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-make-the-point","text":"I'd like to make the point","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to make the point","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce a specific argument or observation.","example":"I'd like to make the point that our current resources are stretched thin.","plain":"I want to state that our current resources are stretched thin.","note":"A direct way to introduce a key argument.","audio":"audio/da2a5a44d9aa069a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-assessment","text":"I'd like to offer a different assessment","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer a different assessment","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely present an alternative evaluation or judgment of a situation or proposal.","example":"While I respect your analysis, I'd like to offer a different assessment of the project's risks.","plain":"While I respect your analysis, I want to present a different evaluation of the project's risks.","note":"A formal way to introduce a contrasting evaluation, often used in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/0b41bf35bf6f9b09.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-conclusion","text":"I'd like to offer a different conclusion","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer a different conclusion","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely present an alternative outcome or deduction, implying disagreement with the one stated.","example":"Based on these figures, I'd like to offer a different conclusion regarding our market share.","plain":"Based on these figures, I want to present a different outcome regarding our market share.","note":"Used to suggest an alternative interpretation of facts, leading to a different understanding.","audio":"audio/d1ddf247e9a8d017.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-framework","text":"I'd like to offer a different framework","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer a different framework","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely suggest an alternative conceptual structure for understanding or addressing an issue.","example":"To analyze this problem, I'd like to offer a different framework that considers social impact.","plain":"To analyze this problem, I want to suggest an alternative conceptual structure that considers social impact.","note":"Used in academic or complex discussions to propose a new way of thinking about a topic.","audio":"audio/e83dccd3365096b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-model","text":"I'd like to offer a different model","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer a different model","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely propose an alternative representation or system for understanding a concept or process.","example":"The current model is useful, but I'd like to offer a different model for predicting consumer behavior.","plain":"The current model is useful, but I want to propose an alternative system for predicting consumer behavior.","note":"Used in technical or analytical discussions to suggest an alternative way of conceptualizing something.","audio":"audio/7a61c83bf77a5a19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-different-strategy","text":"I'd like to offer a different strategy","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer a different strategy","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely propose an alternative plan of action, implying the current one is not the most suitable.","example":"Considering the new market conditions, I'd like to offer a different strategy for our expansion.","plain":"Considering the new market conditions, I want to propose an alternative plan for our expansion.","note":"A formal way to suggest an alternative plan, often in a business or strategic discussion.","audio":"audio/4a14f434c7368d4e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-nuance","text":"I'd like to offer a nuance","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a nuance","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To introduce a subtle distinction or qualification to a statement, often to refine or challenge it.","example":"I'd like to offer a nuance to that argument, considering the cultural differences involved.","plain":"I want to add a subtle distinction to that argument, considering the cultural differences involved.","note":"Sophisticated way to refine an argument rather than directly contradict it.","audio":"audio/19d4de64e1bc46ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-a-qualification-to-that","text":"I'd like to offer a qualification to that","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd like to offer a qualification to that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To add a condition or restriction to a statement, often to limit its scope or accuracy.","example":"I'd like to offer a qualification to that, as these results are preliminary.","plain":"I want to add a condition to that, as these results are preliminary.","note":"Formal, used to refine or limit a statement's applicability.","audio":"audio/597c9eeef1379e5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-offer-an-alternative","text":"I'd like to offer an alternative","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to offer an alternative","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely suggest a different option or solution, implying disagreement with the current one.","example":"I'd like to offer an alternative approach to solving the current staffing shortage.","plain":"I want to suggest a different approach to solving the current staffing shortage.","note":"A constructive way to disagree by proposing another option.","audio":"audio/7652a687b47160a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-open","text":"I'd like to open","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to open","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A formal way to state your intention to begin the meeting or a specific discussion point.","example":"Good morning, everyone. I'd like to open this meeting by welcoming our new team members.","plain":"Good morning. I want to begin this meeting by welcoming our new team members.","note":"Formal and polite, typically used by the meeting chair to set a professional tone.","audio":"audio/66016a1f19035553.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-put-it-this-way","text":"I'd like to put it this way","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd like to put it this way","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To rephrase an idea, often to make it clearer or more impactful.","example":"I'd like to put it this way: our current approach is simply not sustainable.","plain":"Let me say it like this: our current approach is simply not sustainable.","note":"Used to introduce a more direct or concise explanation.","audio":"audio/e43550d2ad0cd527.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-question-that","text":"I'd like to question that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to question that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely express doubt or ask for clarification about a statement or assumption.","example":"I'd like to question that assumption about our target demographic's spending habits.","plain":"I want to ask about that assumption about our target demographic's spending habits.","note":"A polite and direct way to challenge a statement or underlying belief.","audio":"audio/7210cf59607d975f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-request","text":"I'd like to request","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to request","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and formal way to ask for something.","example":"I'd like to request a late check-out, if that's at all possible.","plain":"I want to ask for a late check-out, if that's at all possible.","note":"A standard, polite, and slightly formal way to make a request.","audio":"audio/6898055eea6bce24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-respectfully-add","text":"I'd like to respectfully add","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to respectfully add","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely introduce an additional point that might contradict or qualify what was just said.","example":"I'd like to respectfully add that the previous quarter's results tell a different story.","plain":"I want to politely add that the previous quarter's results tell a different story.","note":"A formal and polite way to introduce a contrasting piece of information.","audio":"audio/a24866bd41da4eef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-respectfully-challenge","text":"I'd like to respectfully challenge","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to respectfully challenge","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely question or dispute an idea, assumption, or statement, emphasizing respect.","example":"I'd like to respectfully challenge the assumption that our current strategy is sufficient.","plain":"I want to politely question the assumption that our current strategy is sufficient.","note":"Formal and deferential, suitable for challenging superiors or established ideas.","audio":"audio/67af4a6ebf075d7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-respectfully-challenge-that","text":"I'd like to respectfully challenge that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to respectfully challenge that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely question or dispute an idea, assumption, or statement, emphasizing respect.","example":"I'd like to respectfully challenge that assumption about our customers' willingness to pay more.","plain":"I want to politely question that assumption about our customers' willingness to pay more.","note":"Formal and deferential, suitable for challenging superiors or established ideas.","audio":"audio/6f40c2d0cd6a4398.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-respectfully-point-out","text":"I'd like to respectfully point out","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to respectfully point out","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely draw attention to a fact or detail that contradicts a previous statement.","example":"I'd like to respectfully point out that the deadline was actually last Friday.","plain":"I want to politely mention that the deadline was actually last Friday.","note":"Formal and polite, used for corrections or bringing up overlooked facts.","audio":"audio/3f85af123f1c4a63.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-respectfully-suggest","text":"I'd like to respectfully suggest","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to respectfully suggest","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative idea or course of action in a very polite and deferential way.","example":"I'd like to respectfully suggest a slightly different approach to managing this conflict.","plain":"I want to politely suggest a slightly different approach to managing this conflict.","note":"Very formal and diplomatic, often used when proposing an alternative to a superior's idea.","audio":"audio/4b57e2a51b3e73b3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-start-by","text":"I'd like to start by","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to start by","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A polite and common way to introduce the initial topic or action of a presentation.","example":"I'd like to start by thanking you all for attending today's session.","plain":"I want to thank you all for attending today's session.","note":"Very common and polite. Good for setting a professional tone.","audio":"audio/24e10c7efba5fb34.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-suggest-a-different","text":"I'd like to suggest a different","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to suggest a different","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To politely introduce an alternative idea or approach.","example":"I'd like to suggest a different approach to our marketing campaign this quarter.","plain":"I want to suggest an alternative approach to our marketing campaign this quarter.","note":"A diplomatic way to propose a change of direction.","audio":"audio/08839c8c7854d212.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-underscore-the-fact-that","text":"I'd like to underscore the fact that","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to underscore the fact that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely emphasize a particular truth or reality.","example":"I'd like to underscore the fact that these results are preliminary.","plain":"I want to emphasize that these results are preliminary.","note":"A strong and formal way to highlight a crucial piece of information or a caveat.","audio":"audio/b369bdc6a7816364.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-like-to-walk-you-through","text":"I'd like to walk you through","kind":"frame","form":"I'd like to walk you through","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Offers to guide the audience step-by-step through a process, data, or explanation.","example":"I'd like to walk you through the new onboarding process for remote employees.","plain":"I want to guide you through the new onboarding process for remote employees.","note":"Creates a sense of guidance and clarity, often used for procedures or complex data.","audio":"audio/5a53790069cd6261.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-push-back-on-that","text":"I'd push back on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd push back on that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To gently resist or challenge an idea or statement, indicating disagreement or a need for more discussion.","example":"I'd push back on that assumption; our initial findings suggest a different trend.","plain":"I would challenge that assumption; our initial findings suggest a different trend.","note":"Common in professional settings, signals a polite but firm disagreement.","audio":"audio/854b69e1feea4856.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-question-whether","text":"I'd question whether","kind":"frame","form":"I'd question whether","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Introduces a doubt or challenge to an assumption or statement in a polite manner.","example":"I'd question whether those figures fully account for the seasonal variations in sales.","plain":"I doubt if those figures fully account for the seasonal variations in sales.","note":"A good way to raise a specific point of doubt or ask for clarification.","audio":"audio/e74bec21a92461ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-rather-not-speculate","text":"I'd rather not speculate","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd rather not speculate","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely decline to offer an opinion or guess about something uncertain.","example":"I'd rather not speculate on future market fluctuations without more data.","plain":"I prefer not to guess about future market fluctuations without more data.","note":"A diplomatic way to avoid making unsubstantiated claims or predictions.","audio":"audio/27ba9d76677418f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-respectfully-disagree","text":"I'd respectfully disagree","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd respectfully disagree","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A formal and polite way to state disagreement, emphasizing respect for the other person.","example":"I'd respectfully disagree with the premise that our current challenges are solely due to external factors.","plain":"I would politely disagree with the idea that our current challenges are solely due to external factors.","note":"Very formal and deferential, suitable for high-stakes or hierarchical discussions.","audio":"audio/a52e44a72250ca04.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-take-a-different-view","text":"I'd take a different view","kind":"chunk","form":"I'd take a different view","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express that your opinion or perspective on a topic is not the same as the one just presented.","example":"I'd take a different view on the potential risks involved with this new venture.","plain":"My opinion on the potential risks involved with this new venture is different.","note":"A polite and common way to introduce an alternative perspective.","audio":"audio/e19ef8d9f851af5f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-take-issue-with","text":"I'd take issue with","kind":"collocation","form":"I'd take issue with","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To disagree strongly but politely with a statement or idea.","example":"I'd take issue with the idea that our team isn't committed to the project's success.","plain":"I disagree with the idea that our team isn't committed to the project's success.","note":"A polite but firm way to challenge a statement. Often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/d25832b3a714aee5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.id-venture-to-say","text":"I'd venture to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'd venture to say","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"I would cautiously suggest or state, often when expressing an opinion that might be unexpected.","example":"I'd venture to say that the new strategy might not be as effective as we hope.","plain":"I would cautiously suggest that the new strategy might not be as effective as we hope.","note":"Hedges a strong opinion, making it sound more polite or less assertive.","audio":"audio/daf12a5798eab905.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-address-that-in-a-moment","text":"I'll address that in a moment","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll address that in a moment","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates that you will answer the question very soon, but not immediately.","example":"I'll address that in a moment, after I've finished explaining this slide.","plain":"I will answer that very soon, after I've finished explaining this slide.","note":"Used to manage the flow of questions and keep the presentation on track.","audio":"audio/2426b988bae586b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-ask-them-to-get-back-to-you","text":"I'll ask them to get back to you","kind":"frame","form":"I'll ask them to get back to you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to request that the person you're trying to reach returns your call or message.","example":"I'll ask them to get back to you as soon as they're available.","plain":"I will tell them to contact you when they are free.","note":"A common and polite way to assure someone their message will be acted upon.","audio":"audio/0ddefa953ae08f36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-around-afterwards","text":"I'll be around afterwards","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be around afterwards","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Offers to answer further questions informally after the presentation has concluded.","example":"I'll be around afterwards if anyone has more specific questions they'd like to discuss.","plain":"I will be available after the presentation if anyone has more specific questions.","note":"A polite way to offer continued engagement without extending the formal Q&A.","audio":"audio/b55fed94252c4e81.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-back-in-the-office","text":"I'll be back in the office","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be back in the office","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States the time or date when one will return to work and be available.","example":"I'm away until Monday, but I'll be back in the office then.","plain":"I will return to work at the office on that specific day.","note":"A common phrase for informing others of one's return from absence. Neutral.","audio":"audio/855de9e71bde2839.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-in-touch-as-soon-as","text":"I'll be in touch as soon as","kind":"frame","form":"I'll be in touch as soon as","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to contact someone immediately after a specific event or condition is met.","example":"I'll be in touch as soon as I have the final details.","plain":"I'll contact you immediately after I have the final details.","note":"A common way to promise prompt communication once certain information is available.","audio":"audio/6435a9ad24c0c6f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-in-touch-by-the-end-of","text":"I'll be in touch by the end of","kind":"frame","form":"I'll be in touch by the end of","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to contact someone before a specific deadline.","example":"I'll be in touch by the end of the week with the updated schedule.","plain":"I'll contact you before the end of the week with the updated schedule.","note":"A common way to set a clear expectation for when communication will occur.","audio":"audio/ef45ea13ffabbac6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-in-touch-later","text":"I'll be in touch later","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be in touch later","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to contact someone at a subsequent, unspecified time.","example":"I'm busy right now, but I'll be in touch later today.","plain":"I'm busy right now, but I'll contact you later today.","note":"A common, informal way to indicate future communication, but not immediately.","audio":"audio/8824b06818e83aaf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-in-touch-regarding","text":"I'll be in touch regarding","kind":"frame","form":"I'll be in touch regarding","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to contact someone about a specific topic.","example":"I'll be in touch regarding the details for the upcoming event.","plain":"I'll contact you about the details for the upcoming event.","note":"A slightly more formal way to promise communication about a particular subject.","audio":"audio/a69cb7aeac7a32a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-in-touch-with-an-update","text":"I'll be in touch with an update","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be in touch with an update","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to contact someone to provide new information.","example":"I'll check the schedule and I'll be in touch with an update.","plain":"I'll check the schedule and I'll contact you with new information.","note":"A common, polite way to promise to provide further details or progress reports.","audio":"audio/a0386e3a15409863.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-in-touch-with-you","text":"I'll be in touch with you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be in touch with you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to contact someone.","example":"I'll check my schedule and I'll be in touch with you about next week.","plain":"I'll check my schedule and I'll contact you about next week.","note":"A standard, polite way to indicate future communication. Slightly more formal than 'I'll be in touch'.","audio":"audio/cb9c749d6029f792.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-sure-to-come","text":"I'll be sure to come","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be sure to come","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and friendly way to confirm attendance at an event.","example":"I'll be sure to come along, thanks for the invite.","plain":"I will definitely come, thanks for the invite.","note":"Conveys a positive and reliable intention to join.","audio":"audio/a584709529f87c1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-sure-to-come-along","text":"I'll be sure to come along","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be sure to come along","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and friendly way to confirm attendance at an event.","example":"I'll be sure to come along, thanks for thinking of me.","plain":"I will definitely come, thanks for thinking of me.","note":"Conveys a positive and reliable intention to join.","audio":"audio/a106ee263b6ec747.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-sure-to-tell-her","text":"I'll be sure to tell her","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be sure to tell her","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will definitely inform her of your message or request.","example":"Don't worry, I'll be sure to tell her you called about the meeting.","plain":"I will definitely tell her you called about the meeting.","note":"A reassuring phrase, emphasizing certainty that the message will be delivered.","audio":"audio/1b8456f7fa280fb3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-there-with-bells-on","text":"I'll be there with bells on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be there with bells on","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An enthusiastic and informal way to confirm attendance, implying eagerness.","example":"Your party? Absolutely, I'll be there with bells on!","plain":"Your party? Absolutely, I'll be there and very excited!","note":"A very informal and enthusiastic way to accept an invitation. Shows great eagerness.","audio":"audio/e6d58d8f8a7f28e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-be-there-with-pleasure","text":"I'll be there with pleasure","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll be there with pleasure","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and enthusiastic way to accept an invitation.","example":"I'll be there with pleasure, thank you for the invitation.","plain":"I will gladly be there, thank you for the invitation.","note":"Conveys both acceptance and appreciation for the invitation.","audio":"audio/7a96c3d7be87a799.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-call-you-right-back","text":"I'll call you right back","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll call you right back","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to redial immediately, usually after a dropped call or connection issue.","example":"It seems we've lost the connection; I'll call you right back.","plain":"Our call ended unexpectedly, so I will phone you again immediately.","note":"A common and reassuring phrase when a call drops. Implies urgency.","audio":"audio/0a916c43bb3d4c17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-certainly-be-there","text":"I'll certainly be there","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll certainly be there","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A firm and reassuring commitment to attend an event.","example":"I'll certainly be there, looking forward to catching up.","plain":"I will definitely be there, looking forward to catching up.","note":"Conveys strong intention and reliability, often used with enthusiasm.","audio":"audio/e04ea3278e71c73a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-certainly-consider-that","text":"I'll certainly consider that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll certainly consider that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A polite way to say you will think about the suggestion, without committing to it.","example":"That's an interesting idea; I'll certainly consider that for the next phase.","plain":"That's an interesting idea; I will definitely think about that for the next phase.","note":"Polite, non-committal response, often used when you need time to evaluate.","audio":"audio/9a610b9f6d903646.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-check-my-diary","text":"I'll check my diary","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll check my diary","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to say you need to consult your schedule before committing.","example":"That sounds interesting, I'll check my diary and get back to you.","plain":"That sounds interesting, I'll check my schedule and get back to you.","note":"A standard, polite response when you need to confirm availability. Common in British English.","audio":"audio/5f7983caec2193e6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-check-on-that-for-you","text":"I'll check on that for you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll check on that for you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to investigate or find information on behalf of the other person.","example":"I'm not sure about that detail, but I'll check on that for you.","plain":"I will find the information you need.","note":"A helpful and polite offer to assist. Professional and proactive.","audio":"audio/5625dade515f0f78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-circle-back-on-that","text":"I'll circle back on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll circle back on that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To return to a topic or issue later, often after gathering more information or taking action.","example":"I need to check with legal first, but I'll circle back on that question by end of day.","plain":"I need to check with legal first, but I'll get back to you on that question by end of day.","note":"Common in corporate settings, implies a return to a discussion after an interim step.","audio":"audio/1b64e8bfed156b02.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-come-back-to-that","text":"I'll come back to that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll come back to that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To promise to address a question or topic at a later point in the presentation.","example":"I'll come back to that specific point during the discussion section, if that's okay.","plain":"I will address that specific point during the discussion section, if that's okay.","note":"A common way to manage questions and maintain the flow of the presentation.","audio":"audio/2a3d131dcc93d60a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-confirm-that-with-you","text":"I'll confirm that with you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll confirm that with you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to verify information and communicate it back to the other person.","example":"I'm not certain of the exact date, but I'll confirm that with you by email.","plain":"I will check that information and then tell you it is correct.","note":"A professional commitment to provide accurate information. Reassuring.","audio":"audio/b741f5bb496bd3ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-convey-your-message","text":"I'll convey your message","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll convey your message","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to communicate the exact content of a message to the intended recipient.","example":"I'll convey your message to the team leader as soon as she returns.","plain":"I will give your message to the team leader when she comes back.","note":"A formal and precise way to promise to pass on information.","audio":"audio/4021d61f87e27c8d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-defer-to-your-expertise","text":"I'll defer to your expertise","kind":"frame","form":"I'll defer to your expertise","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates that one trusts and respects another person's specialized knowledge on a topic.","example":"I'll defer to your expertise on the technical specifications for this new software.","plain":"I will let you decide on the technical details for this new software because you know more.","note":"A very polite and professional way to acknowledge someone's superior knowledge.","audio":"audio/c596dfcf891cea2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-definitely-make-it","text":"I'll definitely make it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll definitely make it","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A strong assurance that one will be able to attend an event.","example":"I'll definitely make it to your housewarming party.","plain":"I will certainly be able to come to your housewarming party.","note":"A common and confident way to confirm attendance.","audio":"audio/029413e39b1d9579.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-do-my-best-to","text":"I'll do my best to","kind":"frame","form":"I'll do my best to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Promises to try hard to achieve something, often to attend an event.","example":"I'll do my best to join you for the evening.","plain":"I will try hard to join you for the evening.","note":"A common and polite way to express intention, but with a hint of uncertainty.","audio":"audio/bbee740bccd57e36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-drop-them-a-line","text":"I'll drop them a line","kind":"collocation","form":"I'll drop them a line","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to send a short message, usually an email or text, to someone.","example":"I'll drop them a line to confirm the details of our meeting.","plain":"I will send them a brief message.","note":"Informal and friendly, implying a quick, non-urgent communication. Common.","audio":"audio/ed4a77808ad84b90.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-drop-them-an-email","text":"I'll drop them an email","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll drop them an email","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will send an email to the person or people.","example":"Since I can't reach them by phone, I'll drop them an email instead.","plain":"I can't reach them by phone, so I'll send an email.","note":"A common, slightly informal but widely accepted way to say \"send an email\".","audio":"audio/3fc699fbac721b1f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-drop-you-a-text","text":"I'll drop you a text","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll drop you a text","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to send a short message via mobile phone.","example":"I'm heading out now, so I'll drop you a text when I arrive.","plain":"I will send you a text message.","note":"Informal and common, especially for quick updates or personal communication.","audio":"audio/e0864691c18087cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-ensure-it-doesnt-happen-again","text":"I'll ensure it doesn't happen again","kind":"frame","form":"I'll ensure it doesn't happen again","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"I will make certain that this mistake or issue is not repeated.","example":"I understand the error; I'll ensure it doesn't happen again.","plain":"I understand the error; I will make certain it is not repeated.","note":"Strong commitment to corrective action.","audio":"audio/5dac0198f1b94bdc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-follow-up-with-an-email","text":"I'll follow up with an email","kind":"collocation","form":"I'll follow up with an email","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to send an email containing further information or a summary.","example":"I'll follow up with an email containing the meeting minutes and action points.","plain":"I will send you an email later with more details.","note":"A standard professional closing, indicating next steps. Very common.","audio":"audio/bde87c42048eb1fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-follow-up-with-them-directly","text":"I'll follow up with them directly","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll follow up with them directly","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States an intention to contact the person concerned without an intermediary.","example":"I'll follow up with them directly if I don't hear back by tomorrow afternoon.","plain":"I will contact them myself if they don't reply by tomorrow afternoon.","note":"A professional way to indicate taking personal responsibility for communication.","audio":"audio/57b1c86c8b33f337.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-back-to-you-with-an-answer","text":"I'll get back to you with an answer","kind":"frame","form":"I'll get back to you with an answer","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to provide a response or solution after finding out the necessary information.","example":"I'll get back to you with an answer about the project timeline by end of day.","plain":"I will reply to you with information about the project timeline by the end of the day.","note":"A common and professional way to commit to providing a specific response.","audio":"audio/2c0da9f005d7d889.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-it-sorted","text":"I'll get it sorted","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get it sorted","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To arrange or resolve a problem or situation.","example":"Don't worry about the scheduling conflict; I'll get it sorted.","plain":"Don't worry about the scheduling conflict; I'll resolve it.","note":"Common in British English, means to fix or organize something.","audio":"audio/6354ce75cfab796d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-someone-to-call-you","text":"I'll get someone to call you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get someone to call you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to arrange for another person to contact the caller.","example":"I'm not the right person, but I'll get someone to call you who can help.","plain":"I will arrange for another person to phone you.","note":"A common phrase when redirecting a query. Professional and helpful.","audio":"audio/4bb1e96f32e4660e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-straight-to-the-point","text":"I'll get straight to the point","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get straight to the point","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to be direct and concise, avoiding unnecessary preamble.","example":"I know you're busy, so I'll get straight to the point about the urgent matter.","plain":"I will discuss the main topic immediately without delay.","note":"A professional and efficient opening, showing respect for the other person's time.","audio":"audio/0549d2b036e0861d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-that-sorted","text":"I'll get that sorted","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get that sorted","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to resolve a problem or organize something efficiently.","example":"Don't worry about the booking, I'll get that sorted for you by tomorrow.","plain":"I will arrange or fix that problem for you.","note":"Common in British English, implies efficiency and resolution. Informal but professional.","audio":"audio/f10c1c857a8cd4a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-the-next-one","text":"I'll get the next one","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get the next one","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An offer to pay for the next round of drinks or meal.","example":"You got this round, I'll get the next one.","plain":"You paid for this round, I'll pay for the next one.","note":"A common, informal gesture of reciprocity when socializing, especially at a bar or restaurant.","audio":"audio/41240db671a084b0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-them-to-call-you","text":"I'll get them to call you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get them to call you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to arrange for the intended person to phone the caller.","example":"She's in a meeting, but I'll get them to call you back as soon as possible.","plain":"I will ask that person to phone you.","note":"A common and helpful response when taking a message for someone else. Neutral.","audio":"audio/d832b8cd78aac694.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-get-to-the-bottom-of-that","text":"I'll get to the bottom of that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll get to the bottom of that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To discover the true reason or cause of something, especially a problem.","example":"There seems to be a misunderstanding; I'll get to the bottom of that.","plain":"There seems to be a misunderstanding; I'll find out the true reason for it.","note":"Implies a commitment to thorough investigation to resolve an issue.","audio":"audio/e7484c13a9336744.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-give-that-some-thought","text":"I'll give that some thought","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll give that some thought","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To promise to consider an idea or suggestion carefully.","example":"That's an interesting perspective; I'll give that some thought.","plain":"That's an interesting perspective; I'll think about it carefully.","note":"Similar to \"I'll consider that,\" but perhaps implies a slightly longer period of consideration.","audio":"audio/e72a84cbb571f7fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-give-them-a-heads-up","text":"I'll give them a heads-up","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll give them a heads-up","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to provide advance notice or a warning about something.","example":"I'll give them a heads-up that you'll be calling about the proposal.","plain":"I will tell them in advance that you will call about the proposal.","note":"A common and informal idiom for providing a pre-notification.","audio":"audio/2f6617e764fbea46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-hand-it-over-to-you","text":"I'll hand it over to you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll hand it over to you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates that one is finished speaking and is passing the turn to another person.","example":"That's all from my side for now, so I'll hand it over to you, Mark.","plain":"I have finished speaking, and now it is your turn to talk.","note":"Common in presentations or structured discussions to transition speakers. Professional.","audio":"audio/22e86fc5d3265866.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-have-them-call-you","text":"I'll have them call you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll have them call you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will arrange for the person or people to phone you.","example":"He's in a meeting right now, but I'll have them call you back.","plain":"He's busy, but I'll arrange for him to call you back.","note":"A common phrase used by receptionists or assistants to promise a return call.","audio":"audio/44e65b800bfe2cc9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-have-to-pass-on","text":"I'll have to pass on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll have to pass on","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to decline an invitation or opportunity.","example":"I'll have to pass on the invitation this time, thank you though.","plain":"I won't be able to accept the invitation this time, thank you though.","note":"A common and friendly way to decline, implying it's just for this occasion.","audio":"audio/8611c75dd05ba6e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-hold-for-a-moment","text":"I'll hold for a moment","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll hold for a moment","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates willingness to wait on the line while the other person attends to something.","example":"No problem, I'll hold for a moment while you retrieve the file.","plain":"I will wait on the phone line for a short time.","note":"A polite and patient response when asked to wait during a call. Professional.","audio":"audio/eaa7a258ddcc517f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-hold-my-questions","text":"I'll hold my questions","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll hold my questions","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States an intention to save questions for a later time in the discussion.","example":"I'll hold my questions until the end of your presentation, if that's alright.","plain":"I will ask my questions after your presentation, if that's okay.","note":"A polite way to manage turn-taking and avoid interruptions.","audio":"audio/a62a6fe234df3700.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-hold-my-thought","text":"I'll hold my thought","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll hold my thought","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will keep my idea or comment to myself for now and share it later.","example":"You seem to be on a roll, so I'll hold my thought until you're done.","plain":"You're speaking well, so I'll save my idea for later.","note":"Indicates a willingness to defer to the current speaker, showing politeness.","audio":"audio/b5b4ef593f0f1cf6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-investigate-that","text":"I'll investigate that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll investigate that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"promises to examine a problem or issue thoroughly.","example":"You mentioned the data discrepancy; I'll investigate that immediately.","plain":"You mentioned the data discrepancy; I'll examine that immediately.","note":"Professional commitment to finding facts or solutions.","audio":"audio/20615de56f1a9c42.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-jot-that-down","text":"I'll jot that down","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll jot that down","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will quickly write down or remember the information you just provided.","example":"Thank you for the details; I'll jot that down for the report.","plain":"Thanks for the details; I'll write them down for the report.","note":"A slightly more informal but common way to say you'll record information.","audio":"audio/ded746c5e99a520b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-just-quickly-touch-on","text":"I'll just quickly touch on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll just quickly touch on","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To briefly mention a topic without going into great detail.","example":"I'll just quickly touch on the regulatory changes before we conclude.","plain":"I will briefly mention the regulatory changes before we conclude.","note":"Signals that a point is minor or will not be explored in depth. Good for time management.","audio":"audio/baddc6fd7ee0a0a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-keep-an-eye-out-for-you","text":"I'll keep an eye out for you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll keep an eye out for you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A promise to look for someone at a specific location or event.","example":"It'll be busy at the concert, but I'll keep an eye out for you.","plain":"It'll be busy at the concert, but I'll look for you.","note":"A common, informal way to assure someone you'll try to find them in a crowd.","audio":"audio/27dc3b8a17c326dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-keep-my-comments-brief","text":"I'll keep my comments brief","kind":"collocation","form":"I'll keep my comments brief","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to speak concisely and not take up too much time.","example":"I know we're short on time, so I'll keep my comments brief.","plain":"I will speak for only a short time.","note":"Polite and considerate, especially in time-sensitive meetings. Professional.","audio":"audio/259a7b0800b1c652.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-keep-you-in-the-loop","text":"I'll keep you in the loop","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll keep you in the loop","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to regularly update someone with information about a situation or project.","example":"I'll keep you in the loop regarding any developments with the new client proposal.","plain":"I will regularly inform you about any news concerning the new client proposal.","note":"A common and friendly idiom, implying ongoing communication.","audio":"audio/412719822308fc0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-leave-you-to-it","text":"I'll leave you to it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll leave you to it","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to end a conversation, indicating you are allowing the other person to continue their activity.","example":"I'll leave you to it; I can see you're busy with those reports.","plain":"I'll let you continue your work; I can see you're busy with those reports.","note":"Shows consideration and respect for the other person's tasks.","audio":"audio/85e862ae56dd529a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-let-you-finish","text":"I'll let you finish","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll let you finish","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates a willingness to wait for the other person to complete their thought before speaking.","example":"I have a question, but I'll let you finish your explanation first.","plain":"I will wait for you to complete your statement before I speak.","note":"A polite way to manage turn-taking, showing respect for the other speaker.","audio":"audio/468a80402000b301.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-let-you-get-back-to-it","text":"I'll let you get back to it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll let you get back to it","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to end a conversation, implying the other person has other tasks to attend to.","example":"I'll let you get back to it; I know you have a busy afternoon ahead.","plain":"I'll let you return to your work; I know you have a busy afternoon ahead.","note":"Shows consideration and respect for the other person's time.","audio":"audio/4873db01885a828f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-let-you-get-back-to-your-day","text":"I'll let you get back to your day","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll let you get back to your day","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely ends a call, acknowledging the other person's schedule and responsibilities.","example":"Thanks for your time; I'll let you get back to your day now.","plain":"I will end our call so you can resume your other activities.","note":"A considerate and professional way to conclude a call, especially if it ran long.","audio":"audio/051cc613ee6e64f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-let-you-get-on","text":"I'll let you get on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll let you get on","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","calls"],"meaning":"I will end the call so you can continue with your other tasks or activities.","example":"Well, I'll let you get on with your busy schedule now.","plain":"I'll end the call so you can do your other work.","note":"A polite and considerate way to end a call, especially if the other person is busy.","audio":"audio/bad8fb63194d68d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-look-forward-to-seeing-you-again","text":"I'll look forward to seeing you again","kind":"frame","form":"I'll look forward to seeing you again","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and hopeful way to express anticipation of a future meeting.","example":"I'll look forward to seeing you again at the next industry conference.","plain":"I hope to see you again at the next industry conference.","note":"A standard, polite closing that suggests future interaction.","audio":"audio/1df07a28b0042d6c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-every-effort","text":"I'll make every effort","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll make every effort","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Promises to try very hard to achieve something, often to attend an event.","example":"I'll make every effort to be there on time.","plain":"I will try my hardest to be there on time.","note":"Conveys strong intention, but acknowledges potential difficulties.","audio":"audio/0bda2a5d30e37871.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-sure-they-get-the-message","text":"I'll make sure they get the message","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll make sure they get the message","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Assures the caller that their information will definitely reach the intended person.","example":"Yes, I'll make sure they get the message about the revised deadline.","plain":"I will ensure that the person receives your information.","note":"A strong assurance that the message will be delivered. Professional and helpful.","audio":"audio/aee1e67478f0c16c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-sure-theyre-aware","text":"I'll make sure they're aware","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll make sure they're aware","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to inform someone about a specific piece of information.","example":"I'll make sure they're aware of the updated delivery schedule.","plain":"I will ensure they know about the new delivery schedule.","note":"A professional and reassuring way to confirm information will be passed on.","audio":"audio/0a29ebbcc5353214.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-sure-to-do-that","text":"I'll make sure to do that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll make sure to do that","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Gives a strong assurance that a specific action will be taken.","example":"Yes, I'll make sure to do that before the end of the day.","plain":"I will definitely perform that action.","note":"A firm and reassuring commitment to complete a task. Professional.","audio":"audio/51b1958fd875d4ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-make-sure-to-incorporate","text":"I'll make sure to incorporate","kind":"frame","form":"I'll make sure to incorporate","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"I will ensure that I include or integrate something.","example":"That's a good idea; I'll make sure to incorporate it into the next draft.","plain":"That's a good idea; I will ensure I include it into the next draft.","note":"Strong commitment to act on feedback.","audio":"audio/b5c7787c1ec6cdf2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-pass-your-details-on","text":"I'll pass your details on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll pass your details on","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to provide the caller's contact information to another person.","example":"She's not available, but I'll pass your details on so she can call you.","plain":"I will give your contact information to her.","note":"A standard phrase when taking a message or facilitating a callback. Neutral.","audio":"audio/df4987b9cac7565b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-pause-there","text":"I'll pause there","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll pause there","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates a temporary stop in one's speech, inviting questions or comments.","example":"I'll pause there and see if anyone has any immediate questions.","plain":"I will stop speaking now to allow for questions or comments.","note":"Useful for managing the flow of a presentation or detailed explanation. Professional.","audio":"audio/7b702f801a514507.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-put-you-through","text":"I'll put you through","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll put you through","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to connect the caller to another person or department.","example":"Certainly, I'll put you through to customer service now.","plain":"I will connect your call to the correct person or department.","note":"A standard phrase used by receptionists or operators. Professional.","audio":"audio/74750033bb52de78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-relay-the-message","text":"I'll relay the message","kind":"collocation","form":"I'll relay the message","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to transmit the information received to the intended recipient.","example":"Don't worry, I'll relay the message to her as soon as she gets back.","plain":"I will deliver your information to the person it is for.","note":"A professional and reassuring response when taking a message for someone else.","audio":"audio/ceb84b444d7f1164.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-revert-to-you-on-that","text":"I'll revert to you on that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll revert to you on that","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to get back to the person with information or a response.","example":"I'll revert to you on that budget query once I've spoken to finance.","plain":"I will get back to you about the budget question after I talk to finance.","note":"Common in British English, slightly formal. Means \"I'll get back to you\".","audio":"audio/fc0a8ee2f58df950.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-see-what-i-can-do","text":"I'll see what I can do","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll see what I can do","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates willingness to try and help or accommodate, but without a firm promise.","example":"I'll see what I can do about shifting my schedule.","plain":"I will try to shift my schedule.","note":"A common, polite, and slightly non-committal response, implying effort will be made.","audio":"audio/4377422c7d66d39a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-send-out-a-summary","text":"I'll send out a summary","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll send out a summary","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A commitment to distribute a brief overview of the meeting's key points and decisions.","example":"I'll send out a summary of our discussion and the agreed action points by end of day.","plain":"I will distribute a summary of our discussion and the agreed action points by end of day.","note":"Professional and helpful, ensures everyone has a record of what was covered.","audio":"audio/f2b06257b08c47db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-send-you-a-reminder","text":"I'll send you a reminder","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll send you a reminder","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Promises to send a message to help the other person remember something.","example":"Don't worry about forgetting; I'll send you a reminder closer to the date.","plain":"I will send you a message to help you remember.","note":"A helpful and proactive offer, especially for appointments or deadlines. Neutral.","audio":"audio/7ed02b050411d90d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-take-responsibility-for-that","text":"I'll take responsibility for that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll take responsibility for that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To accept blame or accountability for a mistake or negative outcome.","example":"The error in the report was mine; I'll take responsibility for that.","plain":"The error in the report was mine; I'll accept the blame for it.","note":"Crucial for demonstrating accountability and professionalism.","audio":"audio/7c2294318eb9e4da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-try-my-best-to","text":"I'll try my best to","kind":"frame","form":"I'll try my best to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Promises to make a strong effort, often implying potential difficulty.","example":"I'll try my best to make it, but I have a prior engagement.","plain":"I will try very hard to come, but I have a prior engagement.","note":"A polite way to express willingness while also hinting at a possible conflict.","audio":"audio/096b69eabc171195.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ill-update-you-regularly","text":"I'll update you regularly","kind":"chunk","form":"I'll update you regularly","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I will provide you with frequent information on the progress or developments.","example":"I'll update you regularly on the project's status as soon as I have more information.","plain":"I'll give you regular updates on the project's status when I have more information.","note":"A clear and professional way to promise ongoing communication.","audio":"audio/bb2e1a8dd26fd296.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-bit-put-out","text":"I'm a bit put out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm a bit put out","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild annoyance or inconvenience.","example":"Having to rearrange my schedule at the last minute, I'm a bit put out.","plain":"Having to rearrange my schedule at the last minute, I am slightly annoyed.","note":"A common, understated British English expression for mild annoyance.","audio":"audio/d19d1284cd4b5019.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-disappointed-by","text":"I'm a little disappointed by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm a little disappointed by","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild dissatisfaction or regret about something.","example":"I'm a little disappointed by the lack of progress on the project.","plain":"I am somewhat dissatisfied with the lack of progress on the project.","note":"A common and polite way to express mild disappointment.","audio":"audio/8cf67110663394e8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-disappointed-to-hear","text":"I'm a little disappointed to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm a little disappointed to hear","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild regret or sadness upon receiving news that is not as hoped.","example":"I'm a little disappointed to hear that the conference has been postponed again.","plain":"I am slightly sad to hear that the conference has been postponed again.","note":"A polite and common way to express mild disappointment about news.","audio":"audio/df7b45051d4d231b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-a-little-unclear-on","text":"I'm a little unclear on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm a little unclear on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state that you don't fully understand a specific detail or aspect.","example":"I'm a little unclear on the exact check-out procedure for early departures.","plain":"I don't fully understand the exact check-out procedure for early departures.","note":"A polite and understated way to admit confusion or ask for more information.","audio":"audio/7384b2b241dbe837.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-able-to-make-it","text":"I'm able to make it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm able to make it","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Confirms that one can attend an event, often after previous uncertainty.","example":"Good news, I'm able to make it after all, so count me in!","plain":"Good news, I can come after all, so include me!","note":"Often used when confirming availability after checking one's schedule.","audio":"audio/8355cfb00701114a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-cant-quite-make-you-out","text":"I'm afraid I can't quite make you out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I can't quite make you out","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Politely states that the speaker cannot understand what the other person is saying.","example":"I'm afraid I can't quite make you out; the sound quality is very poor.","plain":"I can't understand you because the sound quality is bad.","note":"A polite and common expression, often used when the audio is muffled or unclear.","audio":"audio/e3217ca099de4b7b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-didnt-catch-your-name","text":"I'm afraid I didn't catch your name","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I didn't catch your name","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask someone to repeat their name if you missed it during an introduction.","example":"I'm afraid I didn't catch your name earlier; could you remind me?","plain":"Could you please tell me your name again? I missed it.","note":"More formal and polite than 'What's your name again?' Shows good manners.","audio":"audio/33335bc68cd088b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-dont","text":"I'm afraid I don't","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I don't","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to decline or state a lack of knowledge.","example":"I'm afraid I don't speak the local language very well.","plain":"I don't speak the local language very well.","note":"Softens a negative statement, showing regret.","audio":"audio/f0caaffd33e6f4b0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-dont-quite-agree","text":"I'm afraid I don't quite agree","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I don't quite agree","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","reacting"],"meaning":"A polite and indirect way to express disagreement.","example":"I'm afraid I don't quite agree with your assessment of the market trends.","plain":"I politely disagree with your assessment of the market trends.","note":"A very polite and common way to express disagreement, often in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/437ef9fd68f9d6f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-have-other-commitments","text":"I'm afraid I have other commitments","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm afraid I have other commitments","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to decline an invitation by stating existing obligations.","example":"I'm afraid I have other commitments that evening, so I can't join.","plain":"Unfortunately, I have other plans that evening, so I can't join.","note":"A common and diplomatic way to decline without giving too much detail.","audio":"audio/a59604a41b495ad6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-have-to-decline","text":"I'm afraid I have to decline","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I have to decline","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal and polite way to refuse an invitation.","example":"Thank you for the kind invitation, but I'm afraid I have to decline.","plain":"Thank you for the kind invitation, but I must refuse.","note":"A more formal and slightly more direct refusal than 'I can't make it'.","audio":"audio/1ee8f95d3411f657.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-have-to-run","text":"I'm afraid I have to run","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I have to run","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly apologetic way to state that you need to leave quickly.","example":"I'm afraid I have to run; I'm already late for my next appointment.","plain":"I need to leave quickly; I'm already late for my next appointment.","note":"Common and natural, often used when you're in a hurry.","audio":"audio/8e5440db6d611d7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-i-must-dash","text":"I'm afraid I must dash","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm afraid I must dash","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly formal way to say you need to leave quickly.","example":"I'm afraid I must dash, I have another appointment in five minutes.","plain":"I'm sorry, I have to leave quickly, I have another appointment in five minutes.","note":"More formal than \"I have to run\", implies a sudden need to depart.","audio":"audio/0610b67d2c431c39.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-im-not-up-to","text":"I'm afraid I'm not up to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid I'm not up to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Politely states that one lacks the energy or inclination for an activity.","example":"I'm afraid I'm not up to a big social gathering tonight.","plain":"Unfortunately, I don't have the energy for a big social gathering tonight.","note":"A gentle way to decline, implying a lack of energy or mood rather than disinterest.","audio":"audio/3ced14eae6836b4f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-my-schedule-is","text":"I'm afraid my schedule is","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid my schedule is","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Politely explains that one's calendar is full or difficult to manage.","example":"I'm afraid my schedule is rather packed for the foreseeable future.","plain":"Unfortunately, my schedule is very full for the foreseeable future.","note":"A diplomatic way to indicate limited availability without giving specific reasons.","audio":"audio/0d9bcf9522b669a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-that","text":"I'm afraid that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid that","level":"C2","situations":["travel","feedback"],"meaning":"To politely introduce bad news or a negative observation.","example":"I'm afraid that the proposed solution might not be feasible within our budget.","plain":"Unfortunately, the proposed solution might not be feasible within our budget.","note":"Softens negative feedback or news, making it sound more empathetic.","audio":"audio/885b831d642ea786.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-afraid-we-cant","text":"I'm afraid we can't","kind":"frame","form":"I'm afraid we can't","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To politely but firmly decline a request or state an inability to meet a demand.","example":"I'm afraid we can't accommodate that last-minute change without significantly impacting the timeline.","plain":"Unfortunately, we cannot accommodate that last-minute change without significantly impacting the timeline.","note":"A polite refusal. The \"I'm afraid\" softens the directness of \"we can't\".","audio":"audio/9b724149b23416bb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-aiming-to","text":"I'm aiming to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm aiming to","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To state one's objective or goal.","example":"I'm aiming to complete the revised proposal by the end of the week.","plain":"My goal is to complete the revised proposal by the end of the week.","note":"Clearly states an intention or target, often in response to feedback.","audio":"audio/b9e4afb2135bf1f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-all-ears","text":"I'm all ears","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm all ears","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"I am ready and eager to listen to what you have to say.","example":"Go ahead with your proposal; I'm all ears and ready to hear it.","plain":"Tell me your proposal; I'm ready to listen.","note":"An idiomatic expression indicating keen attention, suitable for informal to semi-formal.","audio":"audio/20ebcf221124126d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-all-for","text":"I'm all for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm all for","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses strong support or agreement for an idea or plan.","example":"I'm all for trying that new restaurant you mentioned.","plain":"I completely support trying that new restaurant you mentioned.","note":"A strong and enthusiastic way to show approval.","audio":"audio/23a929541ad7f208.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-all-for-that","text":"I'm all for that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm all for that","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses strong agreement or support for a suggestion or idea.","example":"A quiet evening in sounds perfect, I'm all for that.","plain":"A quiet evening in sounds perfect, I completely agree.","note":"A warm and enthusiastic way to show approval for a plan.","audio":"audio/e8bb9d70e8569367.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-always-looking-for","text":"I'm always looking for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm always looking for","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"I am continuously searching for or seeking something.","example":"I'm always looking for ways to streamline our workflow.","plain":"I am continuously searching for ways to streamline our workflow.","note":"Shows proactive attitude towards improvement.","audio":"audio/9eb064bde2d61d04.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-always-open-to","text":"I'm always open to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm always open to","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express continuous receptiveness to new ideas, feedback, or learning.","example":"I'm always open to constructive criticism that helps me grow.","plain":"I'm always receptive to constructive criticism that helps me grow.","note":"Signals a growth mindset and willingness to learn from others.","audio":"audio/9290638a883eb935.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-always-open-to-feedback","text":"I'm always open to feedback","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm always open to feedback","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A professional and polite way to invite constructive criticism or opinions.","example":"Please let me know your thoughts on the proposal; I'm always open to feedback.","plain":"Please let me know your thoughts on the proposal; I welcome constructive criticism.","note":"Conveys a receptive and collaborative attitude.","audio":"audio/9006d61f71fcf654.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-about-an-issue-with","text":"I'm calling about an issue with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling about an issue with","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to report a problem.","example":"I'm calling about an issue with my flight booking.","plain":"I'm calling because there's a problem with my flight booking.","note":"Direct and clear for reporting problems over the phone.","audio":"audio/f390c488cd03c1a1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-in-reference-to","text":"I'm calling in reference to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling in reference to","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States the specific subject or reason for making the phone call.","example":"Good morning, I'm calling in reference to the job application I submitted.","plain":"I am phoning you about the specific topic of...","note":"Formal and precise, used to clearly state the purpose of a call. Professional.","audio":"audio/49dfe90d08939d7c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-on-behalf-of","text":"I'm calling on behalf of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling on behalf of","level":"C2","situations":["travel","calls"],"meaning":"States that the caller is making the call for another person or organization.","example":"I'm calling on behalf of Mr. Smith regarding the outstanding invoice.","plain":"I am calling for Mr. Smith about the unpaid bill.","note":"A formal and professional way to introduce the purpose of a call.","audio":"audio/34e008488ddb0bfb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-arrange","text":"I'm calling to arrange","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to arrange","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to organize something.","example":"I'm calling to arrange a late check-out for tomorrow.","plain":"I'm calling to organize a late check-out for tomorrow.","note":"Direct and efficient for making arrangements over the phone.","audio":"audio/0e8cda8cb8bec0e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-arrange-a-pick-up","text":"I'm calling to arrange a pick-up","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to arrange a pick-up","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to organize someone or something to be collected.","example":"I'm calling to arrange a pick-up from the airport for my arriving guests.","plain":"I'm calling to organize someone to collect my arriving guests from the airport.","note":"Direct and practical, used for transport arrangements.","audio":"audio/8d1935e4c741ecd3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-arrange-a-tour","text":"I'm calling to arrange a tour","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to arrange a tour","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to organize a guided visit.","example":"I'm calling to arrange a tour of the city for my family next Tuesday.","plain":"I'm calling to organize a guided visit of the city for my family next Tuesday.","note":"Direct and practical, used for booking sightseeing.","audio":"audio/522ea7f0a4a33859.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-arrange-a-transfer","text":"I'm calling to arrange a transfer","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to arrange a transfer","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to organize transportation between two points.","example":"I'm calling to arrange a transfer from the hotel to the port for our cruise.","plain":"I'm calling to organize transportation from the hotel to the port for our cruise.","note":"Practical and direct, used for specific transport needs.","audio":"audio/b7adf94ac73a2a9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-arrange-for","text":"I'm calling to arrange for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to arrange for","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to organize a service.","example":"I'm calling to arrange for a taxi to the airport this evening.","plain":"I'm calling to organize a taxi to the airport this evening.","note":"Direct and efficient for scheduling services.","audio":"audio/8b31f121a8ad7888.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-arrange-transport","text":"I'm calling to arrange transport","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to arrange transport","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to organize a means of getting from one place to another.","example":"I'm calling to arrange transport from the hotel to the airport for tomorrow morning.","plain":"I'm calling to organize a way to get from the hotel to the airport for tomorrow morning.","note":"Practical and direct, used for travel logistics.","audio":"audio/c0de434f622bc97c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-check-on-my","text":"I'm calling to check on my","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to check on my","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to inquire about the status of something.","example":"I'm calling to check on my luggage that was delayed.","plain":"I'm calling to ask about the status of my delayed luggage.","note":"Common for following up on items or services.","audio":"audio/996dfe876905b225.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-check-on-my-luggage","text":"I'm calling to check on my luggage","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to check on my luggage","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to inquire about the status of your bags.","example":"I'm calling to check on my luggage, as it didn't arrive on my flight.","plain":"I'm calling to inquire about the status of my luggage, as it didn't arrive on my flight.","note":"Urgent and common, used for lost or delayed bags.","audio":"audio/7a2d0ecaf3527573.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-check-on-my-order","text":"I'm calling to check on my order","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to check on my order","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to inquire about the status of a purchase.","example":"I'm calling to check on my order for the custom-made souvenir.","plain":"I'm calling to ask about the status of my order for the custom-made souvenir.","note":"Common and polite, used for tracking purchases.","audio":"audio/5ac6ed7d9f5f863a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-clarify","text":"I'm calling to clarify","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to clarify","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to make something clearer.","example":"I'm calling to clarify the terms of my travel insurance policy.","plain":"I'm calling to make the terms of my travel insurance policy clearer.","note":"Professional and direct, used for seeking clarification.","audio":"audio/ddc070313260deed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-clarify-information","text":"I'm calling to clarify information","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to clarify information","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to make something clearer or get a clearer explanation.","example":"I'm calling to clarify information regarding the shuttle service schedule.","plain":"I'm calling to get a clearer explanation regarding the shuttle service schedule.","note":"Professional and polite, used when seeking precision.","audio":"audio/f0251fbefab6d6ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-clarify-my-booking","text":"I'm calling to clarify my booking","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to clarify my booking","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to make the details of your reservation clearer.","example":"I'm calling to clarify my booking, specifically the room type I requested.","plain":"I'm calling to make the details of my reservation clearer, specifically the room type I requested.","note":"Professional and polite, used for ensuring accuracy of a booking.","audio":"audio/cb0333ca62370e18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-details","text":"I'm calling to confirm details","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm details","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to verify specific information.","example":"I'm calling to confirm details of my car rental pick-up time tomorrow.","plain":"I'm calling to verify specific information about my car rental pick-up time tomorrow.","note":"Standard and efficient for checking arrangements.","audio":"audio/3c9b263513138671.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-my","text":"I'm calling to confirm my","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm my","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to verify a personal booking.","example":"I'm calling to confirm my hotel reservation for tonight.","plain":"I'm calling to verify my hotel reservation for tonight.","note":"Efficient for confirming personal arrangements.","audio":"audio/dff1c9773216c649.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-my-arrival","text":"I'm calling to confirm my arrival","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm my arrival","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to verify your planned time of reaching a destination.","example":"I'm calling to confirm my arrival time, as my flight is running a bit late.","plain":"I'm calling to verify my planned time of reaching, as my flight is running a bit late.","note":"Common courtesy when travel plans change slightly.","audio":"audio/792baec6e83b2687.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-my-departure","text":"I'm calling to confirm my departure","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm my departure","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to verify your planned time of leaving.","example":"I'm calling to confirm my departure time from the hotel tomorrow.","plain":"I'm calling to verify my planned time of leaving the hotel tomorrow.","note":"Common courtesy, especially for early check-outs or specific arrangements.","audio":"audio/aab64d0bb166c328.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-my-flight","text":"I'm calling to confirm my flight","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm my flight","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to verify the details of your air travel.","example":"I'm calling to confirm my flight details for tomorrow's departure to London.","plain":"I'm calling to verify the details of my air travel for tomorrow's departure to London.","note":"Standard and practical, used for travel verification.","audio":"audio/218284da7bbf2c26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-my-payment","text":"I'm calling to confirm my payment","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm my payment","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to verify that a payment has been processed.","example":"I'm calling to confirm my payment for the excursion, as I haven't received a receipt.","plain":"I'm calling to verify that my payment for the excursion has been processed.","note":"Practical and direct, used for financial verification.","audio":"audio/814cd4e02f8db525.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-receipt-of","text":"I'm calling to confirm receipt of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm receipt of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to verify that something has been received.","example":"I'm calling to confirm receipt of my booking confirmation email.","plain":"I'm calling to verify that I received my booking confirmation email.","note":"Formal and precise, used for official acknowledgements.","audio":"audio/a00acf6effea71c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-that","text":"I'm calling to confirm that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm that","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to verify a fact or arrangement.","example":"I'm calling to confirm that my airport shuttle is booked for tomorrow.","plain":"I'm calling to verify that my airport shuttle is booked for tomorrow.","note":"Clear and efficient for confirming information.","audio":"audio/7c195128e095e154.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-confirm-the-details-of","text":"I'm calling to confirm the details of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to confirm the details of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to verify specific arrangements.","example":"I'm calling to confirm the details of my airport transfer.","plain":"I'm calling to verify the specific arrangements for my airport transfer.","note":"Formal and precise, used for important arrangements.","audio":"audio/3b67c1b0a8d6f76d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-follow-up","text":"I'm calling to follow up","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to follow up","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to check on progress or previous communication.","example":"I'm calling to follow up on my reservation request from last week.","plain":"I'm calling to check on my reservation request from last week.","note":"A common and polite way to initiate a follow-up call.","audio":"audio/4681375286a13b88.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-follow-up-on","text":"I'm calling to follow up on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to follow up on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to check on a previous matter.","example":"I'm calling to follow up on my complaint about the noisy room.","plain":"I'm calling to check on my complaint about the noisy room.","note":"Professional and persistent, used for ongoing issues.","audio":"audio/86decee5aa3340e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-follow-up-on-a","text":"I'm calling to follow up on a","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to follow up on a","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to check on a previous request or issue.","example":"I'm calling to follow up on a request I made yesterday.","plain":"I'm calling to check on a request I made yesterday.","note":"Professional and persistent, used for ongoing matters.","audio":"audio/fdbbae18155e0c6e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-follow-up-on-a-query","text":"I'm calling to follow up on a query","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to follow up on a query","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to check the status of a previous question.","example":"I'm calling to follow up on a query I sent last week about airport transfers.","plain":"I'm calling to check the status of a question I sent last week about airport transfers.","note":"Professional and polite, used for checking on unanswered questions.","audio":"audio/076857fcfdd25c7a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-follow-up-on-a-request","text":"I'm calling to follow up on a request","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to follow up on a request","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to check the status of a previous appeal.","example":"I'm calling to follow up on a request I made for an extra pillow.","plain":"I'm calling to check the status of my previous appeal for an extra pillow.","note":"Polite and common, used for checking on pending appeals.","audio":"audio/ebf286c221d62edf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-inquire","text":"I'm calling to inquire","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to inquire","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask for information.","example":"I'm calling to inquire about the availability of a tour to the ancient ruins.","plain":"I'm calling to ask about the availability of a tour to the ancient ruins.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to start a call seeking information.","audio":"audio/6acca7596388456c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-make-a-complaint","text":"I'm calling to make a complaint","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to make a complaint","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to express dissatisfaction or report a problem.","example":"I'm calling to make a complaint about the noise from the room next door.","plain":"I'm calling to express my dissatisfaction about the noise from the room next door.","note":"Direct and formal, used for official grievances.","audio":"audio/3a01b42efdd8726b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-make-an-appeal","text":"I'm calling to make an appeal","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to make an appeal","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to make a serious request, often to reconsider a decision.","example":"I'm calling to make an appeal regarding the extra charges on my bill.","plain":"I'm calling to make a serious request regarding the extra charges on my bill.","note":"Formal and serious, used for challenging decisions or seeking reconsideration.","audio":"audio/59548a7c5517f1e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-make-an-inquiry","text":"I'm calling to make an inquiry","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to make an inquiry","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask a question or seek information.","example":"I'm calling to make an inquiry about your hotel's pet policy.","plain":"I'm calling to ask a question about your hotel's pet policy.","note":"Formal and polite, a general way to ask for information.","audio":"audio/3f5ad691eeae9652.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-make-an-inquiry-about","text":"I'm calling to make an inquiry about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to make an inquiry about","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A formal way to state the purpose of a call, seeking information.","example":"I'm calling to make an inquiry about local transportation options.","plain":"I'm calling to ask about local transportation options.","note":"Formal and polite, suitable for detailed information requests.","audio":"audio/33adaacd0ab25b62.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-report-a-lost-item","text":"I'm calling to report a lost item","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to report a lost item","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to inform about a missing possession.","example":"I'm calling to report a lost item; I think I left my wallet in the taxi.","plain":"I'm calling to inform you that I lost an item; I think I left my wallet in the taxi.","note":"Direct and urgent, used for missing belongings.","audio":"audio/5bfe9143b3e7ed8c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-report-a-maintenance-issue","text":"I'm calling to report a maintenance issue","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to report a maintenance issue","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to inform about a problem requiring repair.","example":"I'm calling to report a maintenance issue with the shower in my room.","plain":"I'm calling to inform you about a problem with the shower in my room that needs repair.","note":"Formal and direct, used for property repairs.","audio":"audio/10fbc332d644b8c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-report-a-malfunction","text":"I'm calling to report a malfunction","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to report a malfunction","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to inform about something not working correctly.","example":"I'm calling to report a malfunction with the air conditioning in my room.","plain":"I'm calling to inform you that the air conditioning in my room is not working correctly.","note":"Formal and direct, used for technical or equipment problems.","audio":"audio/54757d1c646da315.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-report-an-incident","text":"I'm calling to report an incident","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to report an incident","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to inform about an unexpected or problematic event.","example":"I'm calling to report an incident of a broken window in my hotel room.","plain":"I'm calling to inform you about a broken window in my hotel room.","note":"Formal and direct, used for serious issues requiring official notification.","audio":"audio/d9b2da0e7399f158.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-request-a-booking","text":"I'm calling to request a booking","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to request a booking","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask for a reservation.","example":"I'm calling to request a booking for a double room for three nights next week.","plain":"I'm calling to ask for a reservation for a double room for three nights next week.","note":"Polite and formal, used for making reservations.","audio":"audio/45de3f7ea3127757.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-request-a-refund","text":"I'm calling to request a refund","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to request a refund","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask for money back.","example":"I'm calling to request a refund for the tour that was cancelled due to bad weather.","plain":"I'm calling to ask for my money back for the tour that was cancelled due to bad weather.","note":"Direct and formal, used for financial claims.","audio":"audio/178499cc4e5cb557.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-request-a-wake-up-call","text":"I'm calling to request a wake-up call","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to request a wake-up call","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask for a phone call at a specific time to wake you up.","example":"I'm calling to request a wake-up call for 6 AM tomorrow morning.","plain":"I'm calling to ask for a phone call at 6 AM tomorrow morning to wake me up.","note":"Common and practical, used in hotels.","audio":"audio/817916b3e49a016a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-request-an-extension","text":"I'm calling to request an extension","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to request an extension","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask for more time or a longer stay.","example":"I'm calling to request an extension on my hotel stay for one more night.","plain":"I'm calling to ask for a longer stay at my hotel for one more night.","note":"Polite and formal, used when needing more time for something.","audio":"audio/2464a97242c85d07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-request-assistance","text":"I'm calling to request assistance","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to request assistance","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask for help.","example":"I'm calling to request assistance with my luggage, as I have many bags.","plain":"I'm calling to ask for help with my luggage, as I have many bags.","note":"Polite and formal, suitable for various types of help.","audio":"audio/510467b8e8313bae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-reschedule","text":"I'm calling to reschedule","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to reschedule","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to change the time or date of an appointment or event.","example":"I'm calling to reschedule my spa appointment for later this week.","plain":"I'm calling to change the time of my spa appointment for later this week.","note":"Common and direct, used for changing planned times.","audio":"audio/4be036bbf443cdbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-reschedule-an-activity","text":"I'm calling to reschedule an activity","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to reschedule an activity","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to change the time or date of a planned event.","example":"I'm calling to reschedule an activity, the cooking class, for another day.","plain":"I'm calling to change the time or date of the cooking class for another day.","note":"Direct and common, used for altering leisure plans.","audio":"audio/debb2079ee3f74b4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-reschedule-my","text":"I'm calling to reschedule my","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to reschedule my","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call, specifically to change an appointment time.","example":"I'm calling to reschedule my guided tour for next week.","plain":"I'm calling to change the time of my guided tour for next week.","note":"Direct and efficient for changing appointments.","audio":"audio/948553facde45551.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-see-if-i-can","text":"I'm calling to see if I can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to see if I can","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and casual way to inquire about a possibility for yourself.","example":"I'm calling to see if I can get a late check-out tomorrow.","plain":"I'm calling to ask if I can get a late check-out tomorrow.","note":"Informal and polite, used for personal requests.","audio":"audio/3e750c5554a512ee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-see-if-i-can-extend","text":"I'm calling to see if I can extend","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to see if I can extend","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to ask if it's possible to prolong a stay or deadline.","example":"I'm calling to see if I can extend my check-out time by an hour.","plain":"I'm calling to ask if it's possible to prolong my check-out time by an hour.","note":"Polite and indirect, used for flexible arrangements.","audio":"audio/17d88a82855e5186.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-calling-to-update-you-on","text":"I'm calling to update you on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm calling to update you on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state the purpose of a call is to provide the latest information.","example":"I'm calling to update you on my flight delay, which means I'll arrive later.","plain":"I'm calling to give you the latest information about my flight delay, which means I'll arrive later.","note":"Professional and polite, used to share new developments.","audio":"audio/a587137468d0c2cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-i-can","text":"I'm confident I can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident I can","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief in one's ability to perform a specific action or task.","example":"I'm confident I can quickly integrate into your team and contribute effectively.","plain":"I strongly believe I can quickly integrate into your team and contribute effectively.","note":"A direct and assertive way to convey self-assurance in one's capabilities.","audio":"audio/f8e7d971fed3b552.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-my-ability-to","text":"I'm confident in my ability to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in my ability to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief in one's capacity to successfully perform a particular action.","example":"I'm confident in my ability to lead diverse teams and achieve ambitious targets.","plain":"I strongly believe I have the capacity to lead diverse teams and achieve ambitious targets.","note":"A strong and direct statement of self-assurance, suitable for interviews.","audio":"audio/6e77eee43867e1b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-in-the-teams","text":"I'm confident in the team's","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident in the team's","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express belief in the capabilities of the group.","example":"I'm confident in the team's ability to deliver exceptional results on this project.","plain":"I believe the team can deliver exceptional results on this project.","note":"Boosts morale and shows trust in collective effort.","audio":"audio/1556f3127106f6a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-i-can","text":"I'm confident that I can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that I can","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief in one's ability to successfully perform a particular action.","example":"I'm confident that I can quickly adapt to new technologies and workflows.","plain":"I strongly believe I can quickly adapt to new technologies and workflows.","note":"A slightly more formal variant of 'I'm confident I can', emphasizing certainty.","audio":"audio/0433717011109e57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-my-skills","text":"I'm confident that my skills","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that my skills","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong belief that one's abilities are suitable or effective for a task or role.","example":"I'm confident that my skills in data analysis will be highly beneficial to your team.","plain":"I strongly believe that my skills in data analysis will be highly beneficial to your team.","note":"A direct and professional way to link one's abilities to the job requirements.","audio":"audio/806d9821127306eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-our","text":"I'm confident that our","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that our","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in the effectiveness of a group's resources or plans.","example":"I'm confident that our new marketing strategy will significantly boost sales.","plain":"I believe our new marketing strategy will significantly boost sales.","note":"Conveys assurance about a specific plan or resource.","audio":"audio/5bd7f1d24c981561.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-that-our-team","text":"I'm confident that our team","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident that our team","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To express strong belief in the capabilities of the group.","example":"I'm confident that our team can adapt quickly to these new market conditions.","plain":"I believe our team can adapt quickly to these new market conditions.","note":"Boosts morale and shows trust in collective effort.","audio":"audio/3022a6228698bb17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-confident-we-can-find","text":"I'm confident we can find","kind":"frame","form":"I'm confident we can find","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Expresses optimism and belief in the ability to discover a solution or way forward.","example":"I'm confident we can find a workable solution that addresses everyone's needs and concerns.","plain":"I believe we can discover a practical answer that deals with what everyone needs and worries about.","note":"Projects a positive and proactive attitude, encouraging collaboration.","audio":"audio/89fff904568b18b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-considering","text":"I'm considering","kind":"frame","form":"I'm considering","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates that one is thinking about a particular course of action or plan.","example":"I'm considering hosting a potluck dinner next month.","plain":"I'm thinking about hosting a potluck dinner next month.","note":"A slightly more formal way to express an idea that is still in the planning stage.","audio":"audio/d4111a1c96e8b4af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-delighted-to","text":"I'm delighted to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm delighted to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses great pleasure or happiness about something.","example":"I'm delighted to accept your invitation to the party.","plain":"I'm very happy to accept your invitation to the party.","note":"A polite and warm way to express pleasure, often when accepting an invitation.","audio":"audio/c6ef7d4040d4045f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-delighted-to-accept","text":"I'm delighted to accept","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm delighted to accept","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal and very polite way to agree to an invitation.","example":"I'm delighted to accept your kind invitation to the party.","plain":"I'm very happy to accept your invitation to the party.","note":"A gracious and appreciative way to formally accept an invitation.","audio":"audio/7d238aade99cc829.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-delighted-to-see","text":"I'm delighted to see","kind":"frame","form":"I'm delighted to see","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express great pleasure or satisfaction about something observed.","example":"I'm delighted to see the progress you've made on your professional development goals.","plain":"I'm very pleased to see the progress you've made on your professional development goals.","note":"Conveys strong positive emotion, often for visible improvements or achievements.","audio":"audio/4990895e5babc61f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-determined-to","text":"I'm determined to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm determined to","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express a strong intention to achieve something despite difficulties.","example":"I'm determined to address these issues and ensure they don't recur.","plain":"I strongly intend to address these issues and ensure they don't recur.","note":"Conveys strong willpower and resolve to overcome challenges.","audio":"audio/4ef80cfbd53930c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-eager-to-contribute-to","text":"I'm eager to contribute to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm eager to contribute to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong enthusiasm and desire to play an active part in something.","example":"I'm eager to contribute to your team's success and help achieve its strategic objectives.","plain":"I'm very enthusiastic about playing an active part in your team's success and helping achieve its strategic objectives.","note":"Shows proactivity and a team-oriented mindset. Highly positive.","audio":"audio/a9fe5cd9e89e6fc8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-eager-to-learn","text":"I'm eager to learn","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm eager to learn","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong enthusiasm and desire to acquire new knowledge or skills.","example":"I'm eager to learn about your company's innovative technologies and processes.","plain":"I'm very enthusiastic about acquiring knowledge about your company's innovative technologies and processes.","note":"Shows a proactive and adaptable mindset, highly valued in dynamic environments.","audio":"audio/fa37c1562c4c58d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-eager-to-take-on","text":"I'm eager to take on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm eager to take on","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong enthusiasm and desire to accept a new responsibility or challenge.","example":"I'm eager to take on new challenges and expand my skill set in this role.","plain":"I'm very enthusiastic about accepting new challenges and expanding my skill set in this role.","note":"Shows ambition and a willingness to grow. Positive for candidates.","audio":"audio/d6c81f42807ef3d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-eagerly-anticipating","text":"I'm eagerly anticipating","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm eagerly anticipating","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses strong and excited expectation for a future event.","example":"I'm eagerly anticipating our long-overdue catch-up.","plain":"I'm really looking forward to our long-overdue catch-up.","note":"A more sophisticated and slightly formal way to express excitement.","audio":"audio/98bb4498186d7d6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-flexible-around","text":"I'm flexible around","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm flexible around","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates willingness to adjust one's schedule to accommodate another's.","example":"I'm flexible around your work schedule, just let me know what works.","plain":"I can adjust to your work schedule, just tell me what works.","note":"A helpful and accommodating phrase when making plans.","audio":"audio/874c79eb3f1db107.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-game-for","text":"I'm game for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm game for","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses enthusiastic willingness to participate in an activity.","example":"I'm game for a hike this weekend if the weather holds.","plain":"I'm willing to go for a hike this weekend if the weather holds.","note":"A casual and eager way to agree to a plan, often implying adventure.","audio":"audio/e7291af1bb7d7e30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-game-if-you-are","text":"I'm game if you are","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm game if you are","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses enthusiastic willingness to participate if the other person is also willing.","example":"I'm game if you are for a spontaneous road trip this weekend.","plain":"I'm willing to go if you are for a spontaneous road trip this weekend.","note":"A casual and enthusiastic way to agree to a plan, often spontaneous ones.","audio":"audio/c7f7f8b585ab4242.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-genuinely-delighted-for-you","text":"I'm genuinely delighted for you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm genuinely delighted for you","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere and strong happiness for someone else's good fortune or success.","example":"After all your efforts, I'm genuinely delighted for you that you won the scholarship.","plain":"After all your efforts, I am truly very happy that you won the scholarship.","note":"A warm and sincere expression of joy for another person's achievement.","audio":"audio/c386c5990fbec886.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-genuinely-happy-for-you","text":"I'm genuinely happy for you","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm genuinely happy for you","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere joy and good wishes for someone else's success or good fortune.","example":"After all your hard work, I'm genuinely happy for you that you got the promotion.","plain":"After all your hard work, I am truly pleased that you got the promotion.","note":"A warm and sincere expression of happiness for another person's good news.","audio":"audio/57b819f6f6e3669b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-genuinely-sorry","text":"I'm genuinely sorry","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm genuinely sorry","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere regret or apology for something.","example":"I'm genuinely sorry for the misunderstanding; it was not my intention.","plain":"I am truly regretful for the misunderstanding; it was not my intention.","note":"A strong and sincere expression of apology or regret.","audio":"audio/0a84acb3c3955e46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-getting-a-lot-of-static","text":"I'm getting a lot of static","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm getting a lot of static","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describes poor audio quality where there is a crackling or hissing sound.","example":"I'm getting a lot of static on my end, making it hard to hear you clearly.","plain":"There's a lot of noise on the line, so I can't hear you well.","note":"Common for phone calls, less so for video calls unless the audio is very poor.","audio":"audio/30d9f05548f0664d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-getting-at-is","text":"I'm getting at is","kind":"frame","form":"I'm getting at is","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"clarifies the main point or intention of the speaker's statement","example":"What I'm getting at is, we need to consider the long-term implications.","plain":"My main point is, we need to consider the long-term implications.","note":"Used to make one's underlying message clear, especially after a lengthy explanation.","audio":"audio/009429c524dc2938.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-given-to-understand","text":"I'm given to understand","kind":"frame","form":"I'm given to understand","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Indicating that you have received information, often indirectly or from a reliable source.","example":"I'm given to understand that the new policy will be implemented next month.","plain":"I have been informed that the new policy will be implemented next month.","note":"Formal and slightly indirect, good for conveying information without stating a source.","audio":"audio/0c6a9cb7c46cde39.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-thats-resolved","text":"I'm glad that's resolved","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad that's resolved","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses satisfaction and relief that a problem or issue has been successfully dealt with.","example":"After weeks of negotiations, I'm glad that's resolved and we can move forward.","plain":"After weeks of negotiations, I am happy that the issue is settled and we can move forward.","note":"A common and practical expression of relief when a problem is fixed.","audio":"audio/1f6d5d32c3258d96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-we-connected","text":"I'm glad we connected","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad we connected","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A friendly and slightly informal way to express satisfaction at having met or established contact.","example":"I'm glad we connected at the networking event; it was very insightful.","plain":"I'm happy we met at the networking event; it was very insightful.","note":"Common in professional networking or social settings.","audio":"audio/cfbcdab37a16aaa5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-glad-we-touched-base","text":"I'm glad we touched base","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm glad we touched base","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A common, slightly informal way to express satisfaction at having briefly connected or communicated.","example":"I'm glad we touched base before the meeting; it clarified a few things.","plain":"I'm glad we spoke briefly before the meeting; it clarified a few things.","note":"Often used in professional contexts for quick updates or checks.","audio":"audio/87777c0576acdf82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to","text":"I'm happy to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To express willingness to perform a task or action.","example":"I'm happy to take the lead on drafting the initial project proposal.","plain":"I am willing to take the lead on drafting the initial project proposal.","note":"Polite and common way to volunteer for a task.","audio":"audio/c1861a26aa1f94fa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-confirm","text":"I'm happy to confirm","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to confirm","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to state that one will attend an event.","example":"I'm happy to confirm that I'll be there on Saturday.","plain":"I'm pleased to say that I'll be there on Saturday.","note":"A straightforward and friendly way to confirm attendance.","audio":"audio/2fcbac4fbf60b4c1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-discuss-this-offline","text":"I'm happy to discuss this offline","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to discuss this offline","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Suggests continuing a detailed or sensitive discussion privately after the presentation.","example":"I'm happy to discuss this offline if you'd like to go into more detail about your specific case.","plain":"I can discuss this privately with you later if you'd like to go into more detail about your specific case.","note":"Professional way to manage time or sensitive topics during a public Q&A.","audio":"audio/977956413a1b03ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-elaborate","text":"I'm happy to elaborate","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm happy to elaborate","level":"C2","situations":["presenting","feedback","calls"],"meaning":"Offers to provide more details or a fuller explanation if needed.","example":"If that wasn't clear, I'm happy to elaborate further on the proposal.","plain":"I can give you more details if you need them.","note":"Polite and helpful, showing willingness to clarify. Professional.","audio":"audio/64daee8f43671b50.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-elaborate-further","text":"I'm happy to elaborate further","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to elaborate further","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Offers to provide more details or a deeper explanation if needed.","example":"I'm happy to elaborate further on any of these points during the Q&A.","plain":"I can give more details on any of these points during the Q&A.","note":"A polite and open invitation for more questions or deeper discussion.","audio":"audio/d45c256fea2fc817.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-facilitate","text":"I'm happy to facilitate","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to facilitate","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To offer to help organize or guide a process or discussion.","example":"If the team needs help with the brainstorming session, I'm happy to facilitate.","plain":"If the team needs help with the brainstorming session, I am willing to guide it.","note":"Collaborative and proactive, used to offer support in a leadership role.","audio":"audio/2efb94a47ec47d0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-help-out-where-i-can","text":"I'm happy to help out where I can","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to help out where I can","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An offer to assist as much as possible, acknowledging potential limitations.","example":"I can't commit to everything, but I'm happy to help out where I can.","plain":"I can't commit to everything, but I'm willing to help as much as possible.","note":"A polite and flexible offer of assistance, often used when one cannot fully commit.","audio":"audio/1c3b4a97cf634aee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-join","text":"I'm happy to join","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to join","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses willingness and pleasure to participate in an activity.","example":"I'm happy to join you for the concert if there are tickets left.","plain":"I'd be pleased to join you for the concert if there are tickets left.","note":"A straightforward and friendly way to accept an invitation.","audio":"audio/64113a89345083bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-join-in","text":"I'm happy to join in","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to join in","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To express willingness to participate in an activity.","example":"If you're playing board games, I'm happy to join in!","plain":"If you're playing board games, I'm willing to participate!","note":"A common, informal way to express enthusiasm for joining an activity.","audio":"audio/2594f04a518b7a57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-lend-a-hand","text":"I'm happy to lend a hand","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to lend a hand","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An offer to help someone with a task or problem.","example":"If you need help moving furniture, I'm happy to lend a hand.","plain":"If you need help moving furniture, I'm willing to help.","note":"A common, informal offer of assistance. Very friendly and helpful.","audio":"audio/05d7fcfc9da64da9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-make-time","text":"I'm happy to make time","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to make time","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To express willingness to adjust one's schedule to accommodate something.","example":"Even though I'm busy, I'm happy to make time for your birthday celebration.","plain":"Even though I'm busy, I'm willing to find time for your birthday celebration.","note":"A polite and accommodating way to show willingness to meet despite a busy schedule.","audio":"audio/00bea68b9076d6e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-oblige","text":"I'm happy to oblige","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to oblige","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing willingness to do what someone has asked or requested.","example":"If you need me to send those documents, I'm happy to oblige.","plain":"If you need me to send those documents, I will gladly do so.","note":"Slightly more formal than \"happy to help\", implies fulfilling a request.","audio":"audio/fc723ed4fe156f3e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-pitch-in","text":"I'm happy to pitch in","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm happy to pitch in","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To contribute effort or help with a task, often as part of a group.","example":"If you need help setting up for the party, I'm happy to pitch in.","plain":"If you need help setting up for the party, I'm happy to help.","note":"Similar to 'chip in', but often implies contributing effort rather than just money. Informal.","audio":"audio/1d5cf29b0b0953eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-provide","text":"I'm happy to provide","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to provide","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To offer to supply something, such as information, resources, or assistance.","example":"If you need more data, I'm happy to provide a detailed report by tomorrow.","plain":"If you need more data, I am willing to give you a detailed report by tomorrow.","note":"Collaborative and helpful, used to offer support or information.","audio":"audio/e35413fbb3ab58ae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-report-that","text":"I'm happy to report that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to report that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces positive news or a favorable outcome","example":"I'm happy to report that the project has been completed on time.","plain":"I'm pleased to announce that the project has been completed on time.","note":"A formal and positive way to deliver good news. Often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/a4548f51fa6ad758.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-share","text":"I'm happy to share","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to share","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To offer to provide information, data, or a presentation.","example":"I'm happy to share the latest market research findings with the team.","plain":"I am willing to provide the latest market research findings with the team.","note":"Collaborative and proactive, used to offer relevant information.","audio":"audio/5a264446b141d119.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-happy-to-take-a-few-more","text":"I'm happy to take a few more","kind":"frame","form":"I'm happy to take a few more","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates willingness to answer a limited number of additional questions.","example":"I'm happy to take a few more questions if anyone has them.","plain":"I can answer a few more questions if anyone has them.","note":"Polite and sets an expectation for the number of questions.","audio":"audio/1876934a9a1e6d20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-a-slight-issue","text":"I'm having a slight issue","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm having a slight issue","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To politely report a minor problem or difficulty.","example":"I'm having a slight issue with the Wi-Fi connection in my room; it keeps dropping out.","plain":"The Wi-Fi connection in my room is not working well; it keeps dropping out.","note":"A polite and understated way to report a problem, implying it's not a major crisis.","audio":"audio/af963cee5d3519ea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-having-some-difficulty","text":"I'm having some difficulty","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm having some difficulty","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To politely state that you are experiencing a problem or challenge.","example":"I'm having some difficulty connecting to the hotel's Wi-Fi network.","plain":"I'm finding it hard to connect to the hotel's Wi-Fi network.","note":"A polite and common way to report a problem without being overly demanding.","audio":"audio/4473bb442d470954.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-honoured-to","text":"I'm honoured to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm honoured to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a feeling of privilege or special recognition.","example":"I'm honoured to be invited to such a special event.","plain":"I feel privileged to be invited to such a special event.","note":"Used for significant or exclusive invitations, conveying deep appreciation.","audio":"audio/e52b1ad25d93faee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-honoured-to-be","text":"I'm honoured to be","kind":"frame","form":"I'm honoured to be","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a feeling of privilege or special recognition for being invited.","example":"I'm honoured to be invited to such a special event.","plain":"I feel privileged to be invited to such a special event.","note":"Used for significant or exclusive invitations, conveying deep appreciation.","audio":"audio/e52b1ad25d93faee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hopeful-that","text":"I'm hopeful that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hopeful that","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Expresses optimism and a positive expectation about a future outcome.","example":"I'm hopeful that by implementing these changes, we'll see a significant improvement in team morale.","plain":"I hope that by making these changes, our team's happiness will greatly improve.","note":"Conveys a positive outlook, often used to encourage others.","audio":"audio/3275ebee57b5c801.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hoping-to","text":"I'm hoping to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hoping to","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To express a desire or plan, often with a hint of uncertainty.","example":"I'm hoping to catch the early train to avoid the rush hour.","plain":"I want to catch the early train to avoid the rush hour.","note":"Softer than \"I want to,\" implies a wish or plan.","audio":"audio/6b6f8ef354c0af7e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hoping-to-arrange","text":"I'm hoping to arrange","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hoping to arrange","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To express a desire to organize or schedule something.","example":"I'm hoping to arrange a taxi for an early morning departure tomorrow.","plain":"I want to organize a taxi for an early morning departure tomorrow.","note":"A polite and slightly indirect way to state a request for an arrangement.","audio":"audio/114a0b229bbdd69e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hoping-we-can","text":"I'm hoping we can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hoping we can","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire for a shared activity or outcome.","example":"I'm hoping we can finally get together next week.","plain":"I hope we can finally meet up next week.","note":"A gentle way to suggest making plans, often after previous failed attempts.","audio":"audio/fb3981a3f8481f29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hoping-we-can-make","text":"I'm hoping we can make","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hoping we can make","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a desire for a plan to materialize, often after previous attempts.","example":"I'm hoping we can make that coffee date happen soon.","plain":"I hope we can have that coffee date soon.","note":"Conveys a gentle push to finalize plans, implying it's been pending.","audio":"audio/6f265af189ffab93.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-hoping-you-can-help","text":"I'm hoping you can help","kind":"frame","form":"I'm hoping you can help","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and direct way to ask for assistance.","example":"I'm hoping you can help me find a pharmacy that's open late tonight.","plain":"Can you please help me find a pharmacy that's open late tonight?","note":"A common and polite way to request assistance.","audio":"audio/b4add20be2125ce1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-inclined-to-say","text":"I'm inclined to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm inclined to say","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To express a tendency or preference towards a particular opinion or decision.","example":"I'm inclined to say we should accept their revised offer, as it addresses most of our concerns.","plain":"I tend to think we should accept their revised offer, as it addresses most of our concerns.","note":"Indicates a leaning towards a decision, but leaves room for further discussion.","audio":"audio/8786b3ef64219f5a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-inclined-to-suggest","text":"I'm inclined to suggest","kind":"frame","form":"I'm inclined to suggest","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"I am somewhat disposed to propose or recommend something.","example":"I'm inclined to suggest we re-evaluate our approach to market entry.","plain":"I am somewhat disposed to propose we re-evaluate our approach to market entry.","note":"Polite, slightly hesitant way to offer a suggestion.","audio":"audio/33d1381a2baa244b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-intrigued-by-that","text":"I'm intrigued by that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm intrigued by that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses curiosity or interest in something new or unusual.","example":"You mentioned a new approach to problem-solving; I'm intrigued by that.","plain":"You mentioned a new approach to problem-solving; I am curious about it.","note":"A polite and intellectual way to express interest.","audio":"audio/37ea5900b69c0fc4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-calling-to-arrange","text":"I'm just calling to arrange","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just calling to arrange","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A common opening for a phone call, stating the purpose of making plans.","example":"Hi, I'm just calling to arrange a time for us to meet next week.","plain":"Hi, I'm calling to plan a time for us to meet next week.","note":"A standard, polite way to begin a phone call by stating the intention to schedule something.","audio":"audio/23ac9fd82f00f080.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-calling-to-catch-up","text":"I'm just calling to catch up","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just calling to catch up","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A common opening for a phone call, stating the purpose of reconnecting and sharing news.","example":"Hi, I'm just calling to catch up and see how you're doing.","plain":"Hi, I'm calling to reconnect and see how you're doing.","note":"A standard, informal way to begin a phone call with a friend to share updates.","audio":"audio/67143e8c58b895ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-calling-to-confirm","text":"I'm just calling to confirm","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just calling to confirm","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A common opening for a phone call, stating the purpose of verifying information.","example":"Hi, I'm just calling to confirm our meeting for tomorrow morning.","plain":"Hi, I'm calling to confirm our meeting for tomorrow morning.","note":"A standard, polite way to begin a phone call by stating the intention to verify details.","audio":"audio/f577fe78b0c31df0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-calling-to-follow-up","text":"I'm just calling to follow up","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just calling to follow up","level":"C2","situations":["socialising","calls"],"meaning":"Explains the purpose of a call as a check-in or reminder about a previous interaction.","example":"Hi, I'm just calling to follow up on the email I sent last week.","plain":"I am phoning you to check on the status of something we discussed earlier.","note":"A common and polite opening for a follow-up call. Professional.","audio":"audio/5b8a7d16fde21c28.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-checking-if","text":"I'm just checking if","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just checking if","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A casual and polite way to verify information.","example":"I'm just checking if my room reservation includes breakfast.","plain":"Does my room reservation include breakfast?","note":"Informal and common, used for quick verification.","audio":"audio/9030221022bec772.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-checking-in-on","text":"I'm just checking in on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just checking in on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To follow up on the status of something or to see how things are progressing.","example":"I'm just checking in on the status of my luggage, which hasn't arrived yet.","plain":"I'm checking the status of my luggage, which hasn't arrived yet.","note":"Informal and friendly, used to politely follow up.","audio":"audio/ac32fa8383f9fbe9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-confirming-that","text":"I'm just confirming that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just confirming that","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To verify a piece of information or an agreement.","example":"I'm just confirming that our flight is still scheduled to depart on time.","plain":"I'm verifying that our flight is still scheduled to depart on time.","note":"A common and polite way to double-check details.","audio":"audio/c27ec11ee991ee6e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-curious-about","text":"I'm just curious about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just curious about","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and engaging way to express interest in learning more about a specific topic.","example":"I'm just curious about your experience working abroad.","plain":"I'd like to know more about your experience working abroad.","note":"Shows genuine interest and encourages the other person to share details.","audio":"audio/eb890df4a86aad3f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-dropping-you-a-line","text":"I'm just dropping you a line","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just dropping you a line","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A casual and friendly way to explain that you are sending a brief message.","example":"I'm just dropping you a line to say hello and see how you're doing.","plain":"I'm just sending you a quick message to say hello and see how you're doing.","note":"More common in written communication (email/text) but can be used verbally to refer to it.","audio":"audio/3e700792ca266bc7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-giving-you-a-heads-up","text":"I'm just giving you a heads-up","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just giving you a heads-up","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A common, slightly informal way to explain that you are providing advance notice or a warning.","example":"I'm just giving you a heads-up that the meeting time has changed.","plain":"I'm just informing you in advance that the meeting time has changed.","note":"A friendly and efficient way to share important, time-sensitive information.","audio":"audio/e34f7dd9f233b485.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-playing-devils-advocate","text":"I'm just playing devil's advocate","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just playing devil's advocate","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that you are arguing a point you may not believe in, to stimulate discussion.","example":"I'm just playing devil's advocate, but what if we considered the worst-case scenario?","plain":"I'm just arguing the opposite side for discussion, but what if we considered the worst-case scenario?","note":"A useful phrase for exploring ideas thoroughly without committing to a position.","audio":"audio/11c3b66ba034edb5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-popping-by","text":"I'm just popping by","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just popping by","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To make a quick, informal visit to a place or person.","example":"I'm just popping by to drop off this book you lent me.","plain":"I'm just visiting briefly to drop off this book you lent me.","note":"Similar to 'popping in', but often implies a very brief stop, perhaps without staying long.","audio":"audio/ceb44e05b6ce7552.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-popping-out-for","text":"I'm just popping out for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just popping out for","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To leave briefly to get or do something specific.","example":"I'm just popping out for some milk, I'll be right back.","plain":"I'm just going out briefly to get some milk, I'll be right back.","note":"A common, informal phrase to explain a short, temporary departure for a specific purpose.","audio":"audio/fdbe721b6b5158e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-reaching-out","text":"I'm just reaching out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just reaching out","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A common, slightly informal way to explain that you are making contact, often for the first time or after a break.","example":"I'm just reaching out to introduce myself and offer my assistance.","plain":"I'm just contacting you to introduce myself and offer my assistance.","note":"A modern and friendly way to initiate communication, especially in professional settings.","audio":"audio/f81f08be344de0a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-saying-that","text":"I'm just saying that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just saying that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces a suggestion or a mild opinion without being overly assertive","example":"I'm just saying that we might want to consider other options.","plain":"I'm merely suggesting that we might want to consider other options.","note":"A conversational hedge to soften a suggestion or criticism. Informal.","audio":"audio/2a1777088082d94d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-thinking-aloud","text":"I'm just thinking aloud","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just thinking aloud","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Indicating that you are verbalizing thoughts as they occur, not necessarily fully formed ideas.","example":"I'm just thinking aloud here, but perhaps we could try a different strategy.","plain":"I'm just saying what I'm thinking, but perhaps we could try a different strategy.","note":"Useful for brainstorming or exploring ideas without committing to them.","audio":"audio/7cf5b1fb796625db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-touching-base","text":"I'm just touching base","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm just touching base","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","calls"],"meaning":"Explains the call is a brief check-in, not requiring a lengthy discussion.","example":"Hi Sarah, I'm just touching base about the project's progress.","plain":"I am calling for a quick update or check-in.","note":"Informal but professional, used for brief, non-urgent check-ins. Common.","audio":"audio/4dca37f924d0b8b3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-articulate","text":"I'm just trying to articulate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to articulate","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Explaining that you are attempting to express a thought or idea clearly.","example":"I'm just trying to articulate my concerns about the project timeline.","plain":"I'm attempting to express my concerns about the project timeline clearly.","note":"Used when formulating complex thoughts, signals a process of expression.","audio":"audio/e14064d6027d0c15.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-ascertain","text":"I'm just trying to ascertain","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to ascertain","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to find out or confirm information precisely.","example":"I'm just trying to ascertain the exact number of attendees.","plain":"I'm trying to find out the exact number of attendees.","note":"A more formal synonym for 'find out,' often used in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/7a364ce1f1674d93.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-brief","text":"I'm just trying to be brief","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be brief","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to be concise and to the point","example":"I'm just trying to be brief, so I'll summarize the key findings.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be concise, so I'll summarize the key findings.","note":"Similar to 'I'll keep it brief', emphasizing the effort to be short.","audio":"audio/7ba15fb84ca173dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-clear","text":"I'm just trying to be clear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be clear","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"emphasizes the speaker's intention to communicate unambiguously","example":"I'm just trying to be clear about the expectations for this project.","plain":"I'm simply trying to communicate clearly about the expectations for this project.","note":"Used to ensure understanding, often when explaining complex information.","audio":"audio/565f7eed57bf2881.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-clear-about","text":"I'm just trying to be clear about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be clear about","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"emphasizes the speaker's intention to communicate unambiguously regarding a specific topic","example":"I'm just trying to be clear about the expectations for this project.","plain":"I'm simply trying to communicate clearly about the expectations for this project.","note":"Specifies the subject of the clarification.","audio":"audio/565f7eed57bf2881.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-clear-and-concise","text":"I'm just trying to be clear and concise","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be clear and concise","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to communicate both unambiguously and briefly","example":"I'm just trying to be clear and concise with my instructions.","plain":"I'm simply trying to communicate my instructions unambiguously and briefly.","note":"Combines clarity and brevity, often used in professional communication.","audio":"audio/cee2b2769193805b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-constructive","text":"I'm just trying to be constructive","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be constructive","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to offer helpful criticism or suggestions","example":"I'm just trying to be constructive with my feedback, not critical.","plain":"I'm simply trying to offer helpful feedback, not critical comments.","note":"Used to frame criticism positively, indicating a desire for improvement.","audio":"audio/c274b811955271a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-fair","text":"I'm just trying to be fair","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be fair","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to be impartial and just in a situation","example":"I'm just trying to be fair to everyone involved in the decision-making process.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be impartial to everyone involved in the decision-making process.","note":"Used to justify a balanced approach or decision. Diplomatic.","audio":"audio/dae61acbfcd74e9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-helpful","text":"I'm just trying to be helpful","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to be helpful","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Explaining that your intention is to assist, often when offering unsolicited advice or information.","example":"I'm just trying to be helpful by suggesting an alternative approach.","plain":"My intention is to help by suggesting an alternative approach.","note":"Can soften advice, but sometimes used defensively.","audio":"audio/9a613d4302e6236b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-helpful-and-constructive","text":"I'm just trying to be helpful and constructive","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be helpful and constructive","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to offer assistance and useful suggestions","example":"I'm just trying to be helpful and constructive with my feedback on your draft.","plain":"I'm simply trying to offer assistance and useful suggestions for your draft.","note":"Combines the desire to help with the aim of providing useful criticism.","audio":"audio/a88c6606543f31c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-objective","text":"I'm just trying to be objective","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be objective","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to be impartial and base judgments on facts","example":"I'm just trying to be objective when evaluating the different proposals.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be impartial when evaluating the different proposals.","note":"Used to emphasize fairness and a fact-based approach. Professional.","audio":"audio/3d2da9e68185a3f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-polite","text":"I'm just trying to be polite","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to be polite","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Explaining that your actions or words are motivated by good manners.","example":"I'm just trying to be polite by offering you the last piece of cake.","plain":"I'm simply trying to show good manners by offering you the last piece of cake.","note":"Used to explain a gesture or remark, sometimes with a hint of irony.","audio":"audio/118bfd55b2586645.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-pragmatic","text":"I'm just trying to be pragmatic","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be pragmatic","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to be practical and realistic in approach","example":"I'm just trying to be pragmatic about the resources we have available.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be practical about the resources we have available.","note":"Used to emphasize a practical, results-oriented approach. Professional.","audio":"audio/9b15dae57db1b740.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-realistic","text":"I'm just trying to be realistic","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be realistic","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces a pragmatic or cautious perspective, often in contrast to optimism","example":"I'm just trying to be realistic about the challenges we face.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be pragmatic about the challenges we face.","note":"Used to temper expectations or introduce a dose of pragmatism. Diplomatic.","audio":"audio/677c983b5b51acaa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-realistic-about","text":"I'm just trying to be realistic about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be realistic about","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces a pragmatic or cautious perspective, often in contrast to optimism","example":"I'm just trying to be realistic about the project's timeline.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be pragmatic about the project's timeline.","note":"Specifies the subject of the realistic assessment.","audio":"audio/91892e16681a910c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-respectful","text":"I'm just trying to be respectful","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be respectful","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to show consideration and politeness","example":"I'm just trying to be respectful of everyone's time during this meeting.","plain":"I'm simply trying to show consideration for everyone's time during this meeting.","note":"Used to preface a statement, emphasizing politeness and consideration.","audio":"audio/777ffee4baf56c3b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-be-transparent","text":"I'm just trying to be transparent","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to be transparent","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses an intention to be open and honest about information","example":"I'm just trying to be transparent about the challenges we're facing.","plain":"I'm simply trying to be open and honest about the challenges we're facing.","note":"Used to emphasize openness and honesty, especially in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/3af0bd7068987e3a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-confirm-details","text":"I'm just trying to confirm details","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to confirm details","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to verify specific pieces of information.","example":"I'm just trying to confirm details for the travel arrangements.","plain":"I'm trying to verify specific pieces of information for the travel arrangements.","note":"A professional and polite way to ensure accuracy in planning.","audio":"audio/4c15cb4ecc010496.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-coordinate","text":"I'm just trying to coordinate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to coordinate","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to organize different elements or people to work together.","example":"I'm just trying to coordinate schedules for the team meeting.","plain":"I'm trying to organize schedules for the team meeting.","note":"A common, professional phrase for managing logistics and teamwork.","audio":"audio/e4f8098807ab31d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-determine","text":"I'm just trying to determine","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to determine","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to decide or conclude something after investigation.","example":"I'm just trying to determine the best course of action.","plain":"I'm trying to decide the best course of action.","note":"A formal way to express the process of making a decision based on facts.","audio":"audio/ee19ced015bb975a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-establish","text":"I'm just trying to establish","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to establish","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to determine or prove a fact or situation.","example":"I'm just trying to establish the root cause of the technical issue.","plain":"I'm trying to determine the root cause of the technical issue.","note":"A more formal way to express the goal of finding out something definite.","audio":"audio/b4c28584e34eccfd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-gauge","text":"I'm just trying to gauge","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to gauge","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to assess or estimate something.","example":"I'm just trying to gauge the level of interest in the upcoming workshop.","plain":"I'm trying to assess the level of interest in the upcoming workshop.","note":"A polite way to explain why you're asking questions or observing reactions.","audio":"audio/3fcc40ab7103cf32.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-get-a-clearer-picture","text":"I'm just trying to get a clearer picture","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to get a clearer picture","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to gain a better understanding of a situation.","example":"I'm just trying to get a clearer picture of the project's overall scope.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the project's overall scope better.","note":"A common idiom for seeking improved understanding or more details.","audio":"audio/8483f8d368fc460a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-get-a-read-on","text":"I'm just trying to get a read on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to get a read on","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to understand someone's intentions or a situation's dynamics.","example":"I'm just trying to get a read on how the new manager prefers things to be done.","plain":"I'm trying to understand how the new manager prefers things to be done.","note":"A common idiom for assessing a person's mood, intentions, or a situation.","audio":"audio/6ab96991a2defa69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-get-organized","text":"I'm just trying to get organized","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to get organized","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to arrange your tasks or thoughts systematically.","example":"With all these new projects, I'm just trying to get organized.","plain":"With all these new projects, I'm trying to arrange my tasks systematically.","note":"A common, relatable phrase for managing workload or information.","audio":"audio/36dc6f72038f6944.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-help","text":"I'm just trying to help","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to help","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses a benevolent intention, often when offering unsolicited advice","example":"I'm just trying to help, but perhaps you should review the data again.","plain":"I'm simply trying to assist, but perhaps you should review the data again.","note":"A polite way to offer assistance or advice, sometimes used defensively.","audio":"audio/43a98a54ee6946fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-illustrate","text":"I'm just trying to illustrate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to illustrate","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Explains that the speaker is providing an example to clarify or demonstrate a point.","example":"I'm just trying to illustrate how quickly things can go wrong without proper planning.","plain":"I'm just trying to show how quickly things can go wrong without proper planning.","note":"Clarifies the purpose of an example or anecdote.","audio":"audio/327cf866ca0e172f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-piece-together","text":"I'm just trying to piece together","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to piece together","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to understand a situation by combining various bits of information.","example":"I'm just trying to piece together what happened during the outage.","plain":"I'm trying to understand what happened during the outage by combining information.","note":"A common idiom for reconstructing events from fragments of information.","audio":"audio/18b4e60aa308a7a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-plan-ahead","text":"I'm just trying to plan ahead","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm just trying to plan ahead","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to make arrangements for the future.","example":"I'm just trying to plan ahead for the upcoming holiday season.","plain":"I'm trying to make arrangements for the upcoming holiday season.","note":"A common, practical phrase for future preparation.","audio":"audio/daa306f2ebaed297.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-prioritize","text":"I'm just trying to prioritize","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to prioritize","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to decide which tasks or issues are most important.","example":"I'm just trying to prioritize my tasks for the week.","plain":"I'm trying to decide which tasks are most important for the week.","note":"A common, professional phrase for managing workload effectively.","audio":"audio/a68f27b059cdd02c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-verify","text":"I'm just trying to verify","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to verify","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are attempting to check the truth or accuracy of something.","example":"I'm just trying to verify the figures in the report.","plain":"I'm trying to check the figures in the report for accuracy.","note":"A professional and polite way to ensure information is correct.","audio":"audio/88c47f2fbb99b435.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-trying-to-wrap-my-head-around","text":"I'm just trying to wrap my head around","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just trying to wrap my head around","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to explain that you are trying to understand something complex or difficult.","example":"I'm just trying to wrap my head around the new tax regulations.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the new tax regulations.","note":"A common, slightly informal idiom for grappling with a complex idea.","audio":"audio/7a9f76d6acff151e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-just-wondering-about","text":"I'm just wondering about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm just wondering about","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","travel"],"meaning":"A gentle way to introduce a question or express curiosity about a topic.","example":"I'm just wondering about the logistics for the upcoming event.","plain":"I'm curious about the logistics for the upcoming event.","note":"Softens the inquiry, making it less direct and more conversational.","audio":"audio/68bf8f6cc2c96327.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-for","text":"I'm keen for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen for","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong desire or enthusiasm for something.","example":"I'm keen for a proper catch-up soon.","plain":"I really want a proper catch-up soon.","note":"Common in British English, indicating strong interest or desire.","audio":"audio/1ca04ef305fa9c87.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to","text":"I'm keen to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen to","level":"C2","situations":["travel","feedback"],"meaning":"To express strong eagerness or enthusiasm to do something.","example":"I'm keen to learn more about how I can improve in this area.","plain":"I'm eager to learn more about how I can improve in this area.","note":"Common in British English, shows enthusiasm and a proactive attitude.","audio":"audio/8ee20995435c2487.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-arrange","text":"I'm keen to arrange","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm keen to arrange","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong desire to organize or set up a meeting or event.","example":"I'm keen to arrange a proper catch-up with you soon.","plain":"I really want to organize a proper catch-up with you soon.","note":"Indicates enthusiasm and initiative. 'Keen' is more common in British English.","audio":"audio/8dbb530a80e080f6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-hear-all-about","text":"I'm keen to hear all about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen to hear all about","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong and eager interest in learning details about something.","example":"I'm keen to hear all about your new job and how it's going.","plain":"I really want to hear all about your new job and how it's going.","note":"A friendly and enthusiastic way to invite someone to share their news.","audio":"audio/b531905b1c8df84f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-learn-more","text":"I'm keen to learn more","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm keen to learn more","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong interest and eagerness to acquire additional information or knowledge.","example":"I'm keen to learn more about the team's current projects and challenges.","plain":"I'm very interested in acquiring more information about the team's current projects and challenges.","note":"Shows enthusiasm and a proactive attitude. Common in British English, also used in American English.","audio":"audio/8cee7cab88fae9c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-keen-to-understand","text":"I'm keen to understand","kind":"frame","form":"I'm keen to understand","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses strong interest and eagerness to comprehend something fully.","example":"I'm keen to understand the specific metrics used to measure success in this department.","plain":"I'm very interested in fully comprehending the specific metrics used to measure success in this department.","note":"Shows intellectual curiosity and a desire for clarity. Professional and proactive.","audio":"audio/57c6781006b8522b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-leaning-towards","text":"I'm leaning towards","kind":"frame","form":"I'm leaning towards","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"I am tending to favor a particular option or decision.","example":"I'm leaning towards the option that offers long-term sustainability, despite higher initial costs.","plain":"I am tending to favor the option that offers long-term sustainability, despite higher initial costs.","note":"Expresses a preference without being definitive, inviting further discussion.","audio":"audio/0ac1bb1e45d27bb5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-company-that","text":"I'm looking for a company that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a company that","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to describe the ideal characteristics or values they seek in an employer.","example":"I'm looking for a company that values innovation and invests in employee development.","plain":"I want an employer that values innovation and invests in employee development.","note":"Helps candidates articulate their preferences for company culture and values.","audio":"audio/7386cddb205c3b24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-culture-where","text":"I'm looking for a culture where","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a culture where","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to describe the ideal workplace environment they seek.","example":"I'm looking for a culture where innovation is encouraged and mistakes are seen as learning opportunities.","plain":"I want a workplace environment where innovation is encouraged and mistakes are seen as learning opportunities.","note":"Helps candidates articulate their preferences for company values and dynamics.","audio":"audio/fc3983841221c0d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-mentor-who","text":"I'm looking for a mentor who","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a mentor who","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to express their desire for guidance and support from an experienced person.","example":"I'm looking for a mentor who can guide me through complex leadership challenges.","plain":"I want guidance and support from an experienced person who can guide me through complex leadership challenges.","note":"Shows a desire for continuous learning and professional development.","audio":"audio/5e0b8124e43eafdb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-role-where","text":"I'm looking for a role where","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a role where","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to describe the ideal characteristics or opportunities they seek in a job.","example":"I'm looking for a role where I can apply my leadership skills and mentor junior colleagues.","plain":"I want a job where I can apply my leadership skills and mentor junior colleagues.","note":"Helps candidates articulate their career aspirations and what motivates them.","audio":"audio/27c3df45cbbb194b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-role-with","text":"I'm looking for a role with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a role with","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to describe specific features or benefits they seek in a job position.","example":"I'm looking for a role with greater autonomy and direct impact on strategic decisions.","plain":"I want a job position that has greater autonomy and direct impact on strategic decisions.","note":"Helps candidates specify desired job characteristics beyond basic duties.","audio":"audio/29be378ea731bbcc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-a-team-that","text":"I'm looking for a team that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for a team that","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to describe the ideal characteristics or dynamics they seek in a workgroup.","example":"I'm looking for a team that fosters open communication and mutual support.","plain":"I want a workgroup that fosters open communication and mutual support.","note":"Helps candidates articulate their preferences for team culture and collaboration.","audio":"audio/47f1c4d8aefd0d6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-opportunities-to","text":"I'm looking for opportunities to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for opportunities to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to express their desire for specific chances for growth or contribution.","example":"I'm looking for opportunities to take on more strategic planning responsibilities.","plain":"I want chances to take on more strategic planning responsibilities.","note":"Highlights ambition and a proactive approach to career development.","audio":"audio/d589ff343dccf51f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-for-ways-to","text":"I'm looking for ways to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking for ways to","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express a proactive search for methods to achieve improvement.","example":"I'm looking for ways to enhance our team's productivity and collaboration.","plain":"I'm searching for methods to enhance our team's productivity and collaboration.","note":"Indicates a proactive and improvement-oriented mindset.","audio":"audio/86869fc5cd68474d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-forward-to-it-immensely","text":"I'm looking forward to it immensely","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm looking forward to it immensely","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a very strong degree of anticipation and excitement.","example":"I'm looking forward to it immensely, it sounds like a wonderful evening.","plain":"I'm really looking forward to it, it sounds like a wonderful evening.","note":"A more emphatic and sophisticated way to express excitement than 'very much'.","audio":"audio/28e5b137f2b63716.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-to","text":"I'm looking to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking to","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To express your intention or goal.","example":"I'm looking to book a guided tour of the city for next Tuesday.","plain":"I want to book a guided tour of the city for next Tuesday.","note":"Common for expressing plans or desires, especially for services.","audio":"audio/645374302e196226.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-looking-to-transition-into","text":"I'm looking to transition into","kind":"frame","form":"I'm looking to transition into","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used by a candidate to explain their desire to move into a different type of role or industry.","example":"I'm looking to transition into a more strategic role with greater leadership responsibilities.","plain":"I want to move into a more strategic role with greater leadership responsibilities.","note":"Explains career change aspirations clearly and professionally.","audio":"audio/eb2ed11b09bf1ce1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-making-progress-on","text":"I'm making progress on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm making progress on","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To report that one is advancing in a task or area of development.","example":"I'm making progress on the new client report, and it's nearly complete.","plain":"I'm advancing on the new client report, and it's nearly complete.","note":"Provides an update on ongoing work or development, often in response to a check-in.","audio":"audio/f32aba091253a856.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-at-liberty-to-say","text":"I'm not at liberty to say","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not at liberty to say","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","storytelling"],"meaning":"Politely declining to provide information because you are not authorized to do so.","example":"I'm not at liberty to say who will be taking over the department.","plain":"I am not allowed to say who will be taking over the department.","note":"Formal and professional, used when information is confidential.","audio":"audio/d97a37d98c7fbc64.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-best-pleased","text":"I'm not best pleased","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not best pleased","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"An understated way to express significant displeasure or annoyance.","example":"After waiting an hour for the delivery, I'm not best pleased.","plain":"After waiting an hour for the delivery, I am quite annoyed.","note":"A common, understated British English expression for strong annoyance.","audio":"audio/4d0aa03079afe93f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-its-feasible","text":"I'm not convinced it's feasible","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced it's feasible","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the practicality or possibility of an idea or plan.","example":"I'm not convinced it's feasible to implement all these changes within the given timeframe.","plain":"I don't believe it's possible to implement all these changes within the given timeframe.","note":"Focuses on the practical aspects of a proposal.","audio":"audio/b9733a116f912f8e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-thats-wise","text":"I'm not convinced that's wise","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced that's wise","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite skepticism about the prudence of a decision or action.","example":"While it's an option, I'm not convinced that's wise in the long run.","plain":"While it's an option, I doubt that is a good idea in the long run.","note":"A diplomatic way to express doubt about the wisdom of a choice.","audio":"audio/7a517995a787c613.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-this-is-the-right-path","text":"I'm not convinced this is the right path","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced this is the right path","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the overall direction or strategy being proposed.","example":"I'm not convinced this is the right path for our company's long-term growth.","plain":"I don't believe this is the correct direction for our company's long-term growth.","note":"Challenges the fundamental strategy rather than just a detail.","audio":"audio/8ce22550a5dbec6f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-convinced-were-there-yet","text":"I'm not convinced we're there yet","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not convinced we're there yet","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express doubt that a goal or agreement has been fully reached, implying further discussion is needed.","example":"I appreciate the progress, but I'm not convinced we're there yet regarding the final budget approval.","plain":"I appreciate the progress, but I don't think we've reached the final budget approval yet.","note":"A diplomatic way to indicate that more work or discussion is required before full agreement.","audio":"audio/dfd87674e5152276.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-comfortable","text":"I'm not entirely comfortable","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely comfortable","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses unease or disagreement with a situation or proposal without being confrontational.","example":"I'm not entirely comfortable with the idea of making such a significant change without consulting everyone.","plain":"I feel uneasy about making such a significant change without consulting everyone.","note":"Signals a personal objection or concern, often implying ethical or practical issues.","audio":"audio/27d27f9d43973f31.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-comfortable-with","text":"I'm not entirely comfortable with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely comfortable with","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express slight disapproval or unease about a situation or proposal.","example":"I'm not entirely comfortable with the idea of proceeding without a clear risk assessment.","plain":"I feel uneasy about the idea of proceeding without a clear risk assessment.","note":"A diplomatic way to voice reservations or concerns, suggesting a need for caution.","audio":"audio/ec8630096d035deb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-convinced-by","text":"I'm not entirely convinced by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely convinced by","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite skepticism or mild disagreement with an argument.","example":"I'm not entirely convinced by their arguments for the new policy.","plain":"I am somewhat skeptical of their arguments for the new policy.","note":"A polite way to express doubt or partial disagreement with an argument.","audio":"audio/bd95dbbc40930185.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-convinced-by-that","text":"I'm not entirely convinced by that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely convinced by that","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of full belief or agreement, suggesting more evidence or discussion is needed.","example":"I'm not entirely convinced by that argument, as it seems to overlook key demographic shifts.","plain":"I don't fully believe that argument, as it seems to overlook key demographic shifts.","note":"Similar to \"I'm not entirely convinced,\" but focuses on the argument itself.","audio":"audio/8749173d1486ae46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-convinced-by-that-argument","text":"I'm not entirely convinced by that argument","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely convinced by that argument","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a lack of full belief or agreement, suggesting more evidence or discussion is needed.","example":"I'm not entirely convinced by that argument, as it seems to overlook key demographic shifts.","plain":"I don't fully believe that argument, as it seems to overlook key demographic shifts.","note":"Similar to \"I'm not entirely convinced,\" but focuses on the argument itself.","audio":"audio/8749173d1486ae46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-convinced-thats-the-case","text":"I'm not entirely convinced that's the case","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely convinced that's the case","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the accuracy or truth of a statement.","example":"I'm not entirely convinced that's the case, as our internal data suggests otherwise.","plain":"I don't fully believe that is true, as our internal data suggests otherwise.","note":"A sophisticated and polite way to dispute a factual claim.","audio":"audio/d6900a1e90f751f3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-opposed-to","text":"I'm not entirely opposed to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely opposed to","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to indicate mild agreement or a willingness to consider something, despite initial hesitation.","example":"I'm not entirely opposed to extending the deadline if it means a higher quality deliverable.","plain":"I'm not completely against extending the deadline if it means a higher quality deliverable.","note":"A diplomatic way to express conditional acceptance or a lack of strong objection.","audio":"audio/d8ebeeaf7ce6e55b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-persuaded-by-that","text":"I'm not entirely persuaded by that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not entirely persuaded by that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite skepticism or a lack of full belief in an argument or statement.","example":"While the data is compelling, I'm not entirely persuaded by that conclusion.","plain":"While the data is compelling, I don't completely believe that conclusion.","note":"A formal and diplomatic way to express doubt or disagreement.","audio":"audio/365ae961ec0092c1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-ruling-out","text":"I'm not entirely ruling out","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely ruling out","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that a possibility is still open for consideration, despite reservations.","example":"I'm not entirely ruling out the option of outsourcing, but we need to explore internal solutions first.","plain":"I'm not completely dismissing the option of outsourcing, but we need to explore internal solutions first.","note":"Suggests flexibility and openness to ideas, even if not fully committed yet.","audio":"audio/f9a3a64f4700dc0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-satisfied-with","text":"I'm not entirely satisfied with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely satisfied with","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite dissatisfaction or mild disappointment with something.","example":"While the report is thorough, I'm not entirely satisfied with the conclusions drawn.","plain":"While the report is thorough, I am not completely happy with the conclusions drawn.","note":"A diplomatic way to express mild criticism or disappointment, often in professional settings.","audio":"audio/fef932a6dd57a007.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-sure-about-that","text":"I'm not entirely sure about that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not entirely sure about that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite uncertainty or mild skepticism regarding a statement or idea.","example":"You say it's a simple fix, but I'm not entirely sure about that.","plain":"You say it's a simple fix, but I am not completely certain about that.","note":"A common, diplomatic way to express doubt or hesitation.","audio":"audio/9d7fa062e5c3176e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-sure-how","text":"I'm not entirely sure how","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely sure how","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express uncertainty about the method or process for achieving something.","example":"I'm not entirely sure how we can implement this new policy without disrupting current operations.","plain":"I don't know exactly how we can implement this new policy without disrupting current operations.","note":"Indicates a need for more planning or clarification on the practical steps.","audio":"audio/770bc068b8c3ee75.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-sure-if","text":"I'm not entirely sure if","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely sure if","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express uncertainty about a condition or the truth of a statement.","example":"I'm not entirely sure if the budget allows for such a significant upgrade at this time.","plain":"I don't know if the budget allows for such a significant upgrade at this time.","note":"A polite way to express doubt about a possibility or a factual constraint.","audio":"audio/b2d8174693b7fd7e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-sure-what","text":"I'm not entirely sure what","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely sure what","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express uncertainty about specific details or the nature of something.","example":"I'm not entirely sure what the client expects from us regarding the final report format.","plain":"I don't know exactly what the client expects from us regarding the final report format.","note":"A common way to admit a lack of precise information, often preceding a question.","audio":"audio/d9261121e4aff583.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-entirely-sure-why","text":"I'm not entirely sure why","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not entirely sure why","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to express uncertainty about the reason or justification for something.","example":"I'm not entirely sure why the previous team decided to use this outdated software.","plain":"I don't know exactly why the previous team decided to use this outdated software.","note":"Often used when questioning past decisions or seeking a deeper understanding of motives.","audio":"audio/5b12819d2f03e5c1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-feeling-100","text":"I'm not feeling 100%","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not feeling 100%","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A common idiom meaning to feel slightly unwell or not at one's best.","example":"I'm not feeling 100% today, so I'll have to take it easy.","plain":"I'm not feeling completely well today, so I'll have to take it easy.","note":"A casual and widely understood way to indicate mild illness or fatigue.","audio":"audio/6af8ab958d8ef155.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-feeling-entirely-myself","text":"I'm not feeling entirely myself","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not feeling entirely myself","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and understated way to say one is feeling unwell or not in good spirits.","example":"I'm not feeling entirely myself today, so I think I'll stay in.","plain":"I'm not feeling very well today, so I think I'll stay in.","note":"A subtle way to indicate mild illness or low energy without oversharing.","audio":"audio/df1fd1e5a7ab77b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-feeling-my-best","text":"I'm not feeling my best","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not feeling my best","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to say one is feeling unwell or not in top form.","example":"I'm not feeling my best, so I think I'll stay home tonight.","plain":"I'm not feeling very well, so I think I'll stay home tonight.","note":"A gentle and common way to explain why one might decline social plans.","audio":"audio/0847fea7bec6a5c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-feeling-up-to-par","text":"I'm not feeling up to par","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not feeling up to par","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates that one is not feeling as well or energetic as usual.","example":"I'm not feeling up to par, so I'll have to miss the gathering.","plain":"I'm not feeling as well as usual, so I'll have to miss the gathering.","note":"A slightly more formal way to express feeling unwell, implying a standard of health.","audio":"audio/3870a22eb0a5ad8d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-feeling-well-enough-to","text":"I'm not feeling well enough to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not feeling well enough to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Explains that one's health prevents participation in an activity.","example":"I'm not feeling well enough to be good company tonight.","plain":"I'm too unwell to be good company tonight.","note":"A polite and specific way to decline, focusing on the impact of illness.","audio":"audio/fab15e8534180b70.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-going-to-lie-to-you","text":"I'm not going to lie to you","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not going to lie to you","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces a frank or honest admission, often about a negative feeling","example":"I'm not going to lie to you, the feedback was quite harsh.","plain":"Honestly, the feedback was quite harsh.","note":"Similar to 'I'm not going to lie', adding 'to you' makes it more direct and personal.","audio":"audio/b3ccf6e52d061ae2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-going-to-sugarcoat-it","text":"I'm not going to sugarcoat it","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not going to sugarcoat it","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"indicates that the speaker will be direct and honest, even if the truth is unpleasant","example":"I'm not going to sugarcoat it; the project is facing significant delays.","plain":"I'm going to be direct; the project is facing significant delays.","note":"A direct and informal way to deliver bad news without softening the impact.","audio":"audio/bad3b064aa208b23.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-argue","text":"I'm not one to argue","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to argue","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Introducing a point of disagreement by stating that you generally avoid arguments.","example":"I'm not one to argue, but I think there's a different way to look at this.","plain":"I don't usually argue, but I think there's a different way to look at this.","note":"A polite way to introduce a counter-argument.","audio":"audio/6cc283b1d7c29b8e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-boast","text":"I'm not one to boast","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to boast","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Introducing a statement about your own achievements or qualities, while disclaiming pride.","example":"I'm not one to boast, but I did manage to finish the report ahead of schedule.","plain":"I don't usually brag, but I did manage to finish the report ahead of schedule.","note":"A humble way to share a personal success.","audio":"audio/654c15314a9b8b63.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-complain","text":"I'm not one to complain","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to complain","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Introducing a complaint or criticism by first stating that you don't usually complain.","example":"I'm not one to complain, but the air conditioning in here is quite strong.","plain":"I don't usually complain, but the air conditioning in here is quite strong.","note":"Softens a complaint, making it sound more reasonable or exceptional.","audio":"audio/0d826244f3e823e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-gossip","text":"I'm not one to gossip","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to gossip","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Introducing a piece of information that might be considered gossip, while disclaiming the act of gossiping.","example":"I'm not one to gossip, but I heard they're reorganizing the team.","plain":"I don't usually spread rumors, but I heard they're reorganizing the team.","note":"A common conversational hedge before sharing potentially sensitive information.","audio":"audio/b3aa9ed336e509cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-judge","text":"I'm not one to judge","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to judge","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Introducing a comment about someone's actions or choices, while disclaiming any moral judgment.","example":"I'm not one to judge, but that decision seems a bit risky.","plain":"I don't usually criticize others, but that decision seems a bit risky.","note":"Used to offer an opinion without appearing judgmental.","audio":"audio/81b9380f1348641e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-make-a-fuss","text":"I'm not one to make a fuss","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to make a fuss","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Explaining that you prefer to avoid causing trouble or drawing attention.","example":"I'm not one to make a fuss, but this coffee is cold.","plain":"I don't usually cause trouble, but this coffee is cold.","note":"Used to soften a complaint or request, implying it's a rare occurrence.","audio":"audio/177e1d01cc4efef1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-pry","text":"I'm not one to pry","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to pry","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Introducing a question that might be considered personal, while assuring you don't intend to intrude.","example":"I'm not one to pry, but how was your weekend trip?","plain":"I don't usually ask personal questions, but how was your weekend trip?","note":"Softens a potentially intrusive question.","audio":"audio/206917b6be517c10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-one-to-rush-things","text":"I'm not one to rush things","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not one to rush things","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Explaining that you prefer to take your time and not hurry.","example":"I'm not one to rush things, so I'll take my time reviewing the proposal.","plain":"I don't usually hurry, so I'll take my time reviewing the proposal.","note":"Used to justify a slower pace or careful approach.","audio":"audio/f29c6c234288c5e9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-quite-clear","text":"I'm not quite clear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not quite clear","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To politely state that one does not fully understand something.","example":"I'm not quite clear on the distinction between these two options.","plain":"I don't fully understand the distinction between these two options.","note":"Polite and understated way to admit confusion.","audio":"audio/b057a76ecf5a2964.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-quite-following","text":"I'm not quite following","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not quite following","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Politely indicating that you are having difficulty understanding the current point.","example":"I'm not quite following your logic on that last point, could you elaborate?","plain":"I don't fully understand your logic on that last point, could you elaborate?","note":"A polite way to ask for clarification without sounding critical.","audio":"audio/03832b9e397a6572.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-quite-free-on","text":"I'm not quite free on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not quite free on","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly hesitant way to indicate unavailability on a specific date.","example":"I'm not quite free on the date you suggested, sadly.","plain":"I'm not available on the date you suggested, unfortunately.","note":"The 'quite' softens the refusal, making it sound less abrupt.","audio":"audio/03893d5203e14c35.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-really-up-for-it","text":"I'm not really up for it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not really up for it","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To not feel like doing something or lack enthusiasm for it.","example":"A big party tonight? I'm not really up for it, I'm quite tired.","plain":"A big party tonight? I don't feel like it, I'm quite tired.","note":"An informal way to decline, indicating a lack of energy or desire. Can sound a bit dismissive if not softened.","audio":"audio/19f080b063ef0c2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-suggesting-that","text":"I'm not suggesting that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not suggesting that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"clarifies what the speaker is not implying, often to avoid misunderstanding","example":"I'm not suggesting that you're wrong, but rather that there's another perspective.","plain":"I'm not implying that you're wrong, but rather that there's another perspective.","note":"A diplomatic way to offer an alternative view without directly contradicting.","audio":"audio/c29da1b6b7078165.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-how-else-to-put-it","text":"I'm not sure how else to put it","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure how else to put it","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses difficulty in finding the right words to describe a situation, often implying it's unusual or bad.","example":"I'm not sure how else to put it, but the meeting was a complete disaster from start to finish.","plain":"I don't know another way to say it, but the meeting was a complete disaster from start to finish.","note":"Used when struggling to articulate something, often negative.","audio":"audio/d502bca8021fb524.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-how-to-feel-about-that","text":"I'm not sure how to feel about that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure how to feel about that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mixed feelings, uncertainty, or mild confusion about news.","example":"They've changed the entire project scope; I'm not sure how to feel about that.","plain":"They've changed the entire project scope; I have mixed feelings about it.","note":"A common way to express ambivalence or uncertainty about a development.","audio":"audio/4ae4cb338ee84ba2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-how-to-interpret-that","text":"I'm not sure how to interpret that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure how to interpret that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses uncertainty about the meaning or implications of a statement or event.","example":"His sudden change of tone, I'm not sure how to interpret that.","plain":"His sudden change of tone, I don't know what it means.","note":"Used when something is ambiguous and you're seeking clarity or trying to understand motives.","audio":"audio/5e4bd05df48ceff1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-how-to-process-that","text":"I'm not sure how to process that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure how to process that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses uncertainty or difficulty in understanding and reacting to new information.","example":"The news about the company restructuring, I'm not sure how to process that right now.","plain":"The news about the company restructuring, I don't know how to understand or react to it right now.","note":"Used when information is complex or emotionally impactful, requiring time to comprehend.","audio":"audio/8952078abc862619.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-articulated-that-well","text":"I'm not sure I articulated that well","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I articulated that well","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the clarity or effectiveness of one's explanation.","example":"I'm not sure I articulated that well, so let me try a different analogy.","plain":"I don't think I explained that clearly, so let me try a different analogy.","note":"A humble way to acknowledge potential lack of clarity and offer a re-explanation.","audio":"audio/9ae9fac6670e5243.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-can-fully-agree","text":"I'm not sure I can fully agree","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I can fully agree","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses a personal inability to completely concur with a statement or proposal.","example":"On that particular point, I'm not sure I can fully agree with your assessment.","plain":"On that particular point, I doubt I can completely agree with your assessment.","note":"A polite way to state personal disagreement, often implying a matter of principle.","audio":"audio/87efc1cf5cbf7959.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-entirely-agree","text":"I'm not sure I entirely agree","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I entirely agree","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite disagreement, often softening the directness of a refusal or counter-argument.","example":"While I see your point, I'm not sure I entirely agree with that approach.","plain":"While I see your point, I don't completely agree with that approach.","note":"A diplomatic and common way to express partial disagreement without being confrontational.","audio":"audio/e78511943b2bd2d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-follow-your-logic","text":"I'm not sure I follow your logic","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I follow your logic","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates that one doesn't fully understand someone's reasoning.","example":"Could you explain that again? I'm not sure I follow your logic.","plain":"Could you explain that again? I don't understand your reasoning.","note":"A polite way to ask for clarification of someone's argument.","audio":"audio/a00298363468e8dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-follow-your-reasoning","text":"I'm not sure I follow your reasoning","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I follow your reasoning","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite confusion or disagreement with someone's logic, requesting clarification.","example":"Given the data, I'm not sure I follow your reasoning for increasing the budget.","plain":"Given the data, I don't completely understand your logic for increasing the budget.","note":"A diplomatic way to challenge someone's argument or request more explanation.","audio":"audio/136266f7eef43086.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-have-the-answer","text":"I'm not sure I have the answer","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure I have the answer","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A polite and honest way to admit you don't know the answer to a question.","example":"I'm not sure I have the answer to that specific technical query right now.","plain":"I don't think I know the answer to that specific technical query right now.","note":"Professional and transparent, often followed by an offer to find out.","audio":"audio/1fe6fd9c5bae186a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-quite-follow","text":"I'm not sure I quite follow","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I quite follow","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite confusion or a request for clarification, implying mild skepticism or difficulty understanding.","example":"You mentioned a new metric, but I'm not sure I quite follow how it relates to our current goals.","plain":"You mentioned a new metric, but I don't completely understand how it relates to our current goals.","note":"A polite way to indicate a lack of understanding or to subtly question a point.","audio":"audio/f0ee38a317d254b2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-i-see-it","text":"I'm not sure I see it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure I see it","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses that you don't understand or agree with a particular perspective or point.","example":"I'm not sure I see it that way; the data suggests a different conclusion.","plain":"I don't understand or agree with that perspective; the data suggests a different conclusion.","note":"A polite and common way to indicate a differing viewpoint or lack of understanding.","audio":"audio/7fd1e02bff95bfa8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-that-fully-accounts-for","text":"I'm not sure that fully accounts for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that fully accounts for","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To express doubt that a statement or plan considers all relevant factors, implying incompleteness.","example":"Your analysis is thorough, but I'm not sure that fully accounts for the recent market shifts.","plain":"Your analysis is thorough, but I don't think it completely considers the recent market shifts.","note":"Used to point out a potential oversight or missing element in an argument or plan.","audio":"audio/fc617895c315b517.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-a-certainty","text":"I'm not sure that's a certainty","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's a certainty","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the absolute truth or guaranteed outcome of a statement or prediction.","example":"I'm not sure that's a certainty, as external factors could still influence the results significantly.","plain":"I doubt that's guaranteed, as external factors could still influence the results significantly.","note":"A polite way to introduce an element of doubt or contingency.","audio":"audio/42c32d4441c7f201.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-a-fair-assessment","text":"I'm not sure that's a fair assessment","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's a fair assessment","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the impartiality or accuracy of an evaluation or judgment.","example":"I'm not sure that's a fair assessment of the team's performance given the circumstances.","plain":"I doubt that is an impartial evaluation of the team's performance given the circumstances.","note":"Challenges the objectivity of an opinion or judgment.","audio":"audio/95a0d141c8764af3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-a-given","text":"I'm not sure that's a given","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's a given","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about something being a certainty or an established fact.","example":"I'm not sure that's a given; we still need to secure external funding for the project.","plain":"I doubt that is a certainty; we still need to secure external funding for the project.","note":"Challenges an assumption that something is automatically true or will happen.","audio":"audio/43040f93fb718553.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-accurate","text":"I'm not sure that's accurate","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's accurate","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the correctness or precision of a statement or information.","example":"I'm not sure that's accurate; my records show a slightly different figure for expenses.","plain":"I doubt that's correct; my records show a slightly different figure for expenses.","note":"A polite way to question factual information without being confrontational.","audio":"audio/debc0a70e9e00e33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-accurate-to-say","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate to say","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses polite doubt about the complete correctness of a statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate to say, as the market has shifted considerably.","plain":"I doubt that is completely correct to say, as the market has shifted considerably.","note":"A slightly longer, more emphatic version of questioning accuracy.","audio":"audio/ab756bae65242268.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-accurate-to-state","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate to state","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate to state","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses polite doubt about the complete correctness of a statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely accurate to state, as the market has shifted considerably.","plain":"I doubt that is completely correct to state, as the market has shifted considerably.","note":"A slightly longer, more emphatic version of questioning accuracy.","audio":"audio/1c2e2cd9d882542e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-appropriate","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses polite doubt about the suitability or correctness of an action or statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate for a formal business presentation.","plain":"I doubt that is completely suitable for a formal business presentation.","note":"Challenges the decorum or fit of something in a given context.","audio":"audio/1c7b6fcd5f815495.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-appropriate-to-say","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate to say","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses polite doubt about the complete suitability or correctness of a statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate to say in a public forum.","plain":"I doubt that is completely suitable to say in a public forum.","note":"A slightly longer, more emphatic version of questioning appropriateness.","audio":"audio/40f9793ace9133e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-appropriate-to-suggest","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate to suggest","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate to suggest","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses polite doubt about the complete suitability or correctness of a statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely appropriate to suggest in a formal business meeting.","plain":"I doubt that is completely suitable to suggest in a formal business meeting.","note":"A slightly longer, more emphatic version of questioning appropriateness.","audio":"audio/4c693989843005d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-fair","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely fair","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely fair","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing","reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete fairness or justice of a statement or action.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely fair to attribute all the success solely to one department.","plain":"I doubt that is completely fair to attribute all the success solely to one department.","note":"A slightly stronger version of \"I'm not sure that's fair.\"","audio":"audio/75714466ef26cd9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-fair-to-say","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely fair to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely fair to say","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete fairness or justification of a particular statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely fair to say, considering all the hard work the team put in.","plain":"I doubt that is completely fair to say, considering all the hard work the team put in.","note":"A slightly longer, more emphatic version of questioning fairness.","audio":"audio/f07593b776e691d9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-justified","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely justified","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely justified","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete validity or reasonableness of an action or statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely justified to cut funding for such a vital community program.","plain":"I doubt that is completely reasonable to cut funding for such a vital community program.","note":"Challenges the rationale or fairness of a decision.","audio":"audio/220b7205a0da38b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-justified-to-say","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely justified to say","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely justified to say","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete validity or reasonableness of a particular statement.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely justified to say, considering all the effort involved.","plain":"I doubt that is completely reasonable to say, considering all the effort involved.","note":"A slightly longer, more emphatic version of questioning justification.","audio":"audio/190d31967cb14b4a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-practical","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely practical","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely practical","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the feasibility or realism of an idea or plan.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely practical given our current resource limitations.","plain":"I doubt that is completely practical given our current resource limitations.","note":"Focuses on the real-world applicability and constraints.","audio":"audio/ce90960122a642ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-practical-to-implement","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely practical to implement","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely practical to implement","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete feasibility or realism of an idea or plan.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely practical to implement given our current resource limitations.","plain":"I doubt that is completely practical to implement given our current resource limitations.","note":"Focuses on the real-world applicability and constraints of implementation.","audio":"audio/900fb6cf950723dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-reasonable","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely reasonable","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely reasonable","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete rationality or fairness of a statement or action.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely reasonable to expect such a quick turnaround on this complex task.","plain":"I doubt that is completely reasonable to expect such a quick turnaround on this complex task.","note":"Challenges the logic or fairness of an expectation or demand.","audio":"audio/3d4dc6f861c6c914.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-reasonable-to-suggest","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely reasonable to suggest","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely reasonable to suggest","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the complete rationality or fairness of a statement or action.","example":"I'm not sure that's entirely reasonable to suggest such a drastic change without consultation.","plain":"I doubt that is completely reasonable to suggest such a drastic change without consultation.","note":"Challenges the logic or fairness of an expectation or demand.","audio":"audio/69b6f4cd5a889b2b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-entirely-what-i-meant","text":"I'm not sure that's entirely what I meant","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's entirely what I meant","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To gently correct someone's interpretation of your words, implying a misunderstanding or misrepresentation.","example":"I appreciate your summary, but I'm not sure that's entirely what I meant regarding the project's scope.","plain":"I appreciate your summary, but I think you misunderstood what I meant regarding the project's scope.","note":"A polite way to clarify a point, often used when someone has drawn a conclusion you don't agree with.","audio":"audio/37c24292fc49ccce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-quite-fair","text":"I'm not sure that's quite fair","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's quite fair","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses polite disagreement or mild objection to a statement or situation perceived as unjust.","example":"To blame the entire team for one person's mistake, I'm not sure that's quite fair.","plain":"To blame the entire team for one person's mistake, I don't think that is completely just.","note":"A diplomatic way to challenge a statement or action that seems unjust.","audio":"audio/75ccf25c433dfe5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-allocation","text":"I'm not sure that's the best allocation","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best allocation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether resources (time, money, staff) are being distributed most effectively.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best allocation of our marketing budget for the upcoming campaign.","plain":"I doubt that's the most effective distribution of our marketing budget for the upcoming campaign.","note":"A polite way to question resource management decisions.","audio":"audio/0ff30e2906efbe9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-approach","text":"I'm not sure that's the best approach","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the best approach","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the effectiveness or suitability of a proposed method or strategy.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best approach for engaging with such a diverse group of stakeholders.","plain":"I doubt that is the best approach for engaging with such a diverse group of stakeholders.","note":"Challenges the chosen method without rejecting the overall goal.","audio":"audio/7bad1be5c84c6884.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-direction","text":"I'm not sure that's the best direction","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best direction","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the overall course of action or strategic path being taken.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best direction for our company in the current economic climate.","plain":"I doubt that's the best course of action for our company in the current economic climate.","note":"A polite way to question the strategic wisdom of a plan.","audio":"audio/2d05d4c838fc48ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-use","text":"I'm not sure that's the best use","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the best use","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the optimal allocation of resources or effort.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best use of our limited marketing budget right now.","plain":"I doubt that is the best way to use our limited marketing budget right now.","note":"Challenges the efficiency or strategic value of a proposed action.","audio":"audio/b0079f156a8c572e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-best-way-forward","text":"I'm not sure that's the best way forward","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the best way forward","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the most effective or appropriate future course of action.","example":"I'm not sure that's the best way forward for the project, given the recent changes in requirements.","plain":"I doubt that's the most effective future course of action for the project, given the recent changes.","note":"A polite way to express reservations about a proposed future plan.","audio":"audio/508249ae6e798ae2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-correct-path","text":"I'm not sure that's the correct path","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the correct path","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether a chosen course of action is the right one to achieve a goal.","example":"I'm not sure that's the correct path to achieve our sustainability targets within the given timeframe.","plain":"I doubt that's the right way to achieve our sustainability targets within the given timeframe.","note":"A polite way to question the suitability of a chosen method or strategy.","audio":"audio/fc1bf72fe799544c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-full-picture","text":"I'm not sure that's the full picture","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the full picture","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt that all relevant information or aspects of a situation have been considered.","example":"I'm not sure that's the full picture; we haven't discussed the long-term implications yet.","plain":"I doubt that includes all the information; we haven't discussed the long-term implications yet.","note":"A polite way to suggest that more context or information is needed.","audio":"audio/0450a3c721871500.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-logical-conclusion","text":"I'm not sure that's the logical conclusion","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the logical conclusion","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether a statement logically follows from the preceding information.","example":"I'm not sure that's the logical conclusion based on the data we've reviewed so far.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct conclusion based on the data we've reviewed so far.","note":"A polite way to challenge the reasoning or inference made.","audio":"audio/54de14e8df9e29bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-main-point","text":"I'm not sure that's the main point","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the main point","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether the most crucial aspect of a discussion or argument has been identified.","example":"I'm not sure that's the main point we should be focusing on in this strategic review.","plain":"I doubt that's the most crucial aspect we should be focusing on in this strategic review.","note":"A polite way to redirect focus or challenge a perceived emphasis.","audio":"audio/7ea7f36503c33cce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-effective-strategy","text":"I'm not sure that's the most effective strategy","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the most effective strategy","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether a particular plan of action will achieve the desired results.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most effective strategy for increasing our market share in this competitive environment.","plain":"I doubt that's the best plan of action for increasing our market share in this competitive environment.","note":"A polite way to challenge the efficacy of a strategic plan.","audio":"audio/7960930d1e62f997.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-most-efficient-way","text":"I'm not sure that's the most efficient way","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the most efficient way","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the efficiency or success of a proposed method or tool.","example":"I'm not sure that's the most efficient way to communicate complex information to a broad audience.","plain":"I doubt that is the most efficient way to communicate complex information to a broad audience.","note":"Challenges the method's ability to achieve its goal.","audio":"audio/89403ae91ed194f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-element","text":"I'm not sure that's the only element","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the only element","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt that a single factor is solely responsible, implying other contributing factors.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only element contributing to the team's low morale; other issues might exist.","plain":"I doubt that's the only factor contributing to the team's low morale; other issues might exist.","note":"A polite way to broaden the scope of discussion or analysis.","audio":"audio/56877f8f81cd1dd5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-possibility","text":"I'm not sure that's the only possibility","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the only possibility","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt that there are no other potential outcomes or solutions.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only possibility; we might find other ways to mitigate the risk.","plain":"I doubt that's the only potential outcome; we might find other ways to mitigate the risk.","note":"A polite way to encourage further brainstorming or consideration of alternatives.","audio":"audio/186b4ced72a5be54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-only-way","text":"I'm not sure that's the only way","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the only way","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Suggests that there are alternative solutions or approaches to a problem.","example":"I'm not sure that's the only way to achieve our goals; perhaps we could explore other options.","plain":"I doubt that is the sole method to achieve our goals; perhaps we could explore other options.","note":"Opens up the discussion to alternative solutions.","audio":"audio/79f31dccd1dfb185.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-assessment","text":"I'm not sure that's the right assessment","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right assessment","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the accuracy or fairness of an evaluation or judgment.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right assessment of the market's potential for our new product.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct evaluation of the market's potential for our new product.","note":"A polite way to challenge an evaluation or judgment.","audio":"audio/99ee52fd0f35a5b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-balance","text":"I'm not sure that's the right balance","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right balance","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether different elements are appropriately proportioned or weighted.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right balance between cost-cutting and maintaining service quality.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct proportion between cost-cutting and maintaining service quality.","note":"A polite way to question the equilibrium between competing factors.","audio":"audio/d88fb77bd836d1dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-focus","text":"I'm not sure that's the right focus","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right focus","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether attention is being directed towards the most important aspect.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right focus for our next team meeting; we have other pressing matters.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct area of attention for our next team meeting; we have other pressing matters.","note":"A polite way to suggest shifting attention to a different area.","audio":"audio/1402623d5e603f19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-interpretation","text":"I'm not sure that's the right interpretation","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right interpretation","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether a particular explanation or understanding of something is correct.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right interpretation of the survey results; there could be other factors.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct explanation of the survey results; there could be other factors.","note":"A polite way to challenge an understanding or analysis.","audio":"audio/1be0f1a66694023b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-message","text":"I'm not sure that's the right message","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right message","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether a communication conveys the intended meaning or is appropriate.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right message to send to our stakeholders at this sensitive time.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct communication to send to our stakeholders at this sensitive time.","note":"A polite way to question the content or tone of a communication.","audio":"audio/a47177db68ffa595.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-person","text":"I'm not sure that's the right person","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right person","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the suitability or qualifications of an individual for a role or task.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right person to lead this complex project, given their limited experience.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct individual to lead this complex project, given their limited experience.","note":"A polite way to question a personnel choice.","audio":"audio/6a734c08e711d3ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-place","text":"I'm not sure that's the right place","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right place","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the suitability of a location or context for something.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right place to discuss sensitive personnel matters.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct location to discuss sensitive personnel matters.","note":"A polite way to suggest a different setting or context is more appropriate.","audio":"audio/8e3a6fdda0cadc9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-solution","text":"I'm not sure that's the right solution","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right solution","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether a proposed answer or fix will effectively solve a problem.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right solution for our long-standing technical issues.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct answer for our long-standing technical issues.","note":"A polite way to question the effectiveness of a proposed fix.","audio":"audio/de8681769af5465d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-takeaway","text":"I'm not sure that's the right takeaway","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right takeaway","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether the most important point or conclusion has been correctly identified.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right takeaway from the meeting; I thought we agreed on something different.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct main point from the meeting; I thought we agreed on something different.","note":"A polite way to question the main conclusion drawn from a discussion.","audio":"audio/a8f389d33c8be5e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-term","text":"I'm not sure that's the right term","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right term","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the accuracy or appropriateness of a specific word or phrase used.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right term to describe our current market position; 'challenging' might be better.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct word to describe our current market position; 'challenging' might be better.","note":"A polite way to correct vocabulary or suggest a more precise word.","audio":"audio/2cb6d30a0ff0a7d7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-time","text":"I'm not sure that's the right time","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right time","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the appropriateness of the timing for an action or event.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right time to introduce a new product line during a recession.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct moment to introduce a new product line during a recession.","note":"A polite way to question the timing of a proposed action.","audio":"audio/583939cd98ddb42b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-tone","text":"I'm not sure that's the right tone","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right tone","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the appropriateness of the emotional quality or style of communication.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right tone for a formal apology; it sounds a bit dismissive.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct style for a formal apology; it sounds a bit dismissive.","note":"A polite way to critique the manner of expression.","audio":"audio/cc13d1f6386f5fcf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-understanding","text":"I'm not sure that's the right understanding","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right understanding","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about whether someone has correctly grasped a concept or situation.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right understanding of the new policy's implications for our team.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct grasp of the new policy's implications for our team.","note":"A polite way to clarify or correct someone's comprehension.","audio":"audio/1b66cd0c691e1dfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-way","text":"I'm not sure that's the right way","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the right way","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt about the correctness or appropriateness of a method or manner of doing something.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right way to motivate the team; positive reinforcement might be better.","plain":"I doubt that's the correct method to motivate the team; positive reinforcement might be better.","note":"A polite way to question a method or approach.","audio":"audio/e1c6da2572b2c183.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-right-word","text":"I'm not sure that's the right word","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure that's the right word","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Politely questions the choice of vocabulary, implying a more accurate term is needed.","example":"I'm not sure that's the right word to describe the situation; \"challenging\" might be better.","plain":"I doubt that is the correct word to describe the situation; \"challenging\" might be better.","note":"A subtle way to correct or refine someone's language.","audio":"audio/0b1f59dc324af415.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-thats-the-sole-option","text":"I'm not sure that's the sole option","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure that's the sole option","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"Expresses doubt that only one choice or possibility exists, implying there are others.","example":"I'm not sure that's the sole option we have; perhaps we should explore other avenues.","plain":"I doubt that's the only choice we have; perhaps we should explore other avenues.","note":"A polite way to open up discussion for alternative solutions.","audio":"audio/ae8453e83ef297dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-what-to-make-of-that","text":"I'm not sure what to make of that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not sure what to make of that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses confusion or uncertainty about how to interpret something.","example":"The CEO's cryptic announcement, I'm not sure what to make of that.","plain":"The CEO's cryptic announcement, I don't know how to interpret it.","note":"A common way to express bewilderment or uncertainty about meaning.","audio":"audio/cab0fb0f5dec541c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-sure-where-i-stand","text":"I'm not sure where I stand","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm not sure where I stand","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to express uncertainty about your opinion or position on a particular issue.","example":"On the issue of remote work, I'm not sure where I stand yet.","plain":"On the issue of remote work, I haven't formed a clear opinion yet.","note":"A common, honest way to express indecision or a lack of a firm opinion.","audio":"audio/026d4c371d36376b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-trying-to-be","text":"I'm not trying to be","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not trying to be","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"clarifies the speaker's intention, often to soften a potentially critical remark","example":"I'm not trying to be difficult, but I do have some serious concerns.","plain":"I don't intend to be difficult, but I do have some serious concerns.","note":"A polite way to preface a critical or challenging statement, showing good intent.","audio":"audio/7d8a41085b987b20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-not-trying-to-be-rude","text":"I'm not trying to be rude","kind":"frame","form":"I'm not trying to be rude","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"prefaces a potentially impolite or critical remark to soften its impact","example":"I'm not trying to be rude, but I think you've misunderstood the instructions.","plain":"I don't mean to be impolite, but I think you've misunderstood the instructions.","note":"A polite hedge used before delivering criticism or correction. Very common.","audio":"audio/09018147e19fc4f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-on-board-with","text":"I'm on board with","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm on board with","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To agree with or support an idea or plan.","example":"I'm on board with the new strategy, provided we have adequate resources.","plain":"I agree with the new strategy, provided we have adequate resources.","note":"Informal but very common in professional settings. Expresses support.","audio":"audio/c77e18c27ae6192a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-open-to-suggestions-for","text":"I'm open to suggestions for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm open to suggestions for","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates willingness to consider other ideas or options for a plan.","example":"I'm open to suggestions for a good place to eat downtown.","plain":"I'll consider any ideas for a good place to eat downtown.","note":"Shows flexibility and encourages input from others when making plans.","audio":"audio/c9994b50fcf988b1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-over-the-moon","text":"I'm over the moon","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm over the moon","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness or excitement.","example":"My daughter got into her dream university; I'm over the moon!","plain":"My daughter got into her dream university; I am extremely happy!","note":"A very common, informal expression of intense joy.","audio":"audio/3237434d82bef926.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-particularly-drawn-to","text":"I'm particularly drawn to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm particularly drawn to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong attraction or inclination towards something specific.","example":"I'm particularly drawn to your company's innovative approach to product development.","plain":"I have a strong attraction to your company's innovative approach to product development.","note":"Highlights specific aspects of the company or role that appeal to the candidate.","audio":"audio/2d7f4324833e51c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-pleased-to","text":"I'm pleased to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm pleased to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses satisfaction or happiness about something.","example":"I'm pleased to confirm my attendance at your gathering.","plain":"I'm happy to confirm my attendance at your gathering.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to express satisfaction or confirm something.","audio":"audio/d0fe8337667a162f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-pleased-to-confirm","text":"I'm pleased to confirm","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm pleased to confirm","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal way to state that one will attend an event.","example":"I'm pleased to confirm my attendance at your gathering.","plain":"I'm happy to say I will be at your gathering.","note":"Often used in written communication or for more formal events.","audio":"audio/d0fe8337667a162f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-positive-that","text":"I'm positive that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm positive that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express absolute certainty about something.","example":"I'm positive that we can find a solution that satisfies everyone.","plain":"I'm absolutely certain that we can find a solution that satisfies everyone.","note":"Conveys strong conviction and reassurance.","audio":"audio/c89b7deb7debaa4a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-excited-about","text":"I'm quite excited about","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite excited about","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate to strong feeling of anticipation and pleasure.","example":"I'm quite excited about the prospect of seeing everyone again.","plain":"I'm looking forward to seeing everyone again.","note":"A polite and genuine way to convey enthusiasm without being overly effusive.","audio":"audio/732be95dcf21d75b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-happy-with","text":"I'm quite happy with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite happy with","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express a good level of satisfaction, often with a hint of understatement.","example":"I'm quite happy with the initial results, though there's always room for refinement.","plain":"I'm fairly satisfied with the initial results, though there's always room for refinement.","note":"Polite and slightly understated, suggesting satisfaction without being overly effusive.","audio":"audio/d01dfae0b73b2a55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-keen-on","text":"I'm quite keen on","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite keen on","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate to strong interest in something.","example":"I'm quite keen on seeing that new exhibition.","plain":"I'm quite interested in seeing that new exhibition.","note":"A polite and genuine way to express interest, common in British English.","audio":"audio/66b1aab756f68e16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-keen-to","text":"I'm quite keen to","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite keen to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong interest or desire to do something.","example":"I'm quite keen to hear all about your recent travels.","plain":"I really want to hear all about your recent travels.","note":"A polite and enthusiastic way to show interest. 'Quite' softens it slightly.","audio":"audio/f09eef03a12f2e07.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-surprised-to-hear-that","text":"I'm quite surprised to hear that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm quite surprised to hear that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate level of unexpectedness about information.","example":"I'm quite surprised to hear that they're closing the branch office.","plain":"I am moderately surprised to hear that they're closing the branch office.","note":"A polite and common way to express surprise.","audio":"audio/5b11ef740f545654.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-quite-taken-aback","text":"I'm quite taken aback","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm quite taken aback","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a strong sense of surprise, often accompanied by slight shock or bewilderment.","example":"Her sudden outburst in the meeting, I'm quite taken aback by it.","plain":"Her sudden outburst in the meeting, I am very surprised by it.","note":"A polite but strong way to express surprise, often implying mild disapproval.","audio":"audio/b302c6182ec79469.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-annoyed-by","text":"I'm rather annoyed by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm rather annoyed by","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild to moderate irritation or displeasure.","example":"I'm rather annoyed by the constant interruptions during our meetings.","plain":"I am somewhat irritated by the constant interruptions during our meetings.","note":"A polite way to express annoyance, often used in British English.","audio":"audio/786465a1f3fe5bf5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-disappointed-that","text":"I'm rather disappointed that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm rather disappointed that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate degree of sadness or regret about an outcome or situation.","example":"I'm rather disappointed that the funding for the project was cut.","plain":"I am somewhat sad that the funding for the project was cut.","note":"A polite and common way to express mild disappointment.","audio":"audio/b29f0bf64f972cb6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-keen-to","text":"I'm rather keen to","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm rather keen to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses a polite but strong desire to do something.","example":"I'm rather keen to hear about your new project.","plain":"I'm quite eager to hear about your new project.","note":"A slightly more formal or understated way to express strong interest.","audio":"audio/1d28a83a85f12d44.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-looking-forward-to","text":"I'm rather looking forward to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm rather looking forward to","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate degree of anticipation and pleasure about a future event.","example":"I'm rather looking forward to the team retreat next month.","plain":"I am somewhat anticipating the team retreat next month with pleasure.","note":"A polite and common way to express positive anticipation, often in British English.","audio":"audio/6f714b66fa320eb3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-relieved-to-hear","text":"I'm rather relieved to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm rather relieved to hear","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate degree of comfort and ease upon receiving news.","example":"I'm rather relieved to hear that the meeting has been rescheduled for next week.","plain":"I am somewhat comforted to hear that the meeting has been rescheduled for next week.","note":"A polite, understated way to express relief, common in British English.","audio":"audio/5b541f28c2bfde1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-taken-aback","text":"I'm rather taken aback","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm rather taken aback","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild surprise and slight discomfort or disapproval.","example":"When they announced the sudden policy change, I'm rather taken aback.","plain":"When they announced the sudden policy change, I was quite surprised and a little unsettled.","note":"A polite, understated way to express surprise that borders on disapproval or discomfort.","audio":"audio/5d09be9d43224030.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-rather-tied-up-with","text":"I'm rather tied up with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm rather tied up with","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates that one is very busy or occupied with a specific task or commitment.","example":"I'm rather tied up with a project deadline, unfortunately.","plain":"I'm very busy with a project deadline, unfortunately.","note":"A polite and slightly understated way to explain being unavailable due to work.","audio":"audio/1025bcbc35b81f17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-ready-to","text":"I'm ready to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm ready to","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express preparedness to take action.","example":"I'm ready to implement the changes you suggested immediately.","plain":"I'm prepared to implement the changes you suggested immediately.","note":"Conveys readiness and willingness to act on feedback.","audio":"audio/e663d5607d71f41d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-really-sorry-to-miss-out","text":"I'm really sorry to miss out","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm really sorry to miss out","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An expression of regret at not being able to participate in an enjoyable event.","example":"I'm really sorry to miss out on the concert, but I'm out of town.","plain":"I'm very sad I can't go to the concert, but I'm out of town.","note":"A polite and sincere way to express disappointment when declining an invitation.","audio":"audio/4ab7634ba86b1d1b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-relieved-to-hear-that","text":"I'm relieved to hear that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm relieved to hear that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses comfort or reassurance upon receiving specific news.","example":"I'm relieved to hear that the project deadline has been extended.","plain":"I am comforted to hear that the project deadline has been extended.","note":"A common and sincere way to express relief about a situation.","audio":"audio/e17f823486194548.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-returning-your-call-about","text":"I'm returning your call about","kind":"frame","form":"I'm returning your call about","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States the purpose of a callback, referring to the original message.","example":"Hi, this is David. I'm returning your call about the invoice query.","plain":"I am phoning you back regarding the topic you mentioned in your message.","note":"A standard and professional opening for a return call. Clear and efficient.","audio":"audio/8158c13ef9e91a3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-seeing-a-red-flag","text":"I'm seeing a red flag","kind":"collocation","form":"I'm seeing a red flag","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"I am noticing a warning sign that indicates a potential problem or danger.","example":"I'm seeing a red flag with the supplier's inconsistent delivery times; we should investigate.","plain":"I am noticing a warning sign with the supplier's inconsistent delivery times; we should investigate.","note":"Used to alert others to a potential issue that requires attention.","audio":"audio/6cd9d0f4af8cc333.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-glad-you-could-make-it","text":"I'm so glad you could make it","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm so glad you could make it","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses genuine pleasure that someone was able to attend an event.","example":"I'm so glad you could make it, we haven't seen you in ages!","plain":"I'm very happy you were able to come, we haven't seen you in ages!","note":"A warm and welcoming phrase from a host to a guest, especially if they haven't met in a while.","audio":"audio/7de10a0b337d3ea5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-so-relieved-to-hear","text":"I'm so relieved to hear","kind":"frame","form":"I'm so relieved to hear","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses great comfort or reassurance upon receiving specific news.","example":"I'm so relieved to hear that your surgery went well.","plain":"I am very comforted to hear that your surgery went well.","note":"A common and sincere way to express relief about someone's situation.","audio":"audio/d76070f760abcbb1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-still-on-the-fence","text":"I'm still on the fence","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm still on the fence","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to indicate that you are undecided about something and haven't made a choice yet.","example":"I'm still on the fence about whether to take the new role or stay where I am.","plain":"I haven't decided yet whether to take the new role or stay where I am.","note":"A common, informal idiom for expressing indecision.","audio":"audio/677e3bdf9d7c2e28.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-sure-that","text":"I'm sure that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm sure that","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express certainty about a fact or outcome.","example":"I'm sure that with a bit more effort, we'll achieve our targets.","plain":"I'm certain that with a bit more effort, we'll achieve our targets.","note":"Conveys conviction and reassurance, often used to motivate.","audio":"audio/b8a9ff48d5e03957.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-sure-we-can","text":"I'm sure we can","kind":"frame","form":"I'm sure we can","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express certainty about a collective ability to achieve something.","example":"I'm sure we can resolve this misunderstanding with open communication.","plain":"I'm certain we can resolve this misunderstanding with open communication.","note":"Reassuring and collaborative, expressing confidence in team problem-solving.","audio":"audio/8af85de4be62206f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-swamped-with","text":"I'm swamped with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm swamped with","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To be overwhelmed with a large amount of work or tasks.","example":"I'm swamped with deadlines this week, so I can't make it to the pub.","plain":"I have too many deadlines this week, so I can't make it to the pub.","note":"An informal way to express being extremely busy, often implying stress. More intense than 'tied up'.","audio":"audio/e55c0a868a3619b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-taken-aback-by","text":"I'm taken aback by","kind":"frame","form":"I'm taken aback by","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses surprise or shock, often with a hint of disapproval.","example":"I'm taken aback by the sudden change in company policy.","plain":"I am surprised by the sudden change in company policy.","note":"A common expression for surprise or momentary shock, slightly more formal than \"a little taken aback.\"","audio":"audio/165fd8e9dee35905.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-terribly-sorry-but","text":"I'm terribly sorry but","kind":"frame","form":"I'm terribly sorry but","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A very polite and apologetic way to decline an invitation or deliver bad news.","example":"I'm terribly sorry but I already have a prior engagement.","plain":"I'm very sorry but I already have another plan.","note":"A sincere and empathetic way to express regret when declining.","audio":"audio/c09ef35b2bf52388.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-thankful-for","text":"I'm thankful for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm thankful for","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express appreciation for something received or experienced.","example":"I'm thankful for the opportunity to discuss these points with you.","plain":"I appreciate the opportunity to discuss these points with you.","note":"A common and polite way to express gratitude.","audio":"audio/7680035b434479c0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-thinking-of-hosting","text":"I'm thinking of hosting","kind":"frame","form":"I'm thinking of hosting","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Indicates an intention to organize and host a social event.","example":"I'm thinking of hosting a small gathering at my place next month.","plain":"I might host a small gathering at my place next month.","note":"A casual way to float an idea for an event without a firm commitment yet.","audio":"audio/ad01f371d37dc5d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-thrilled-to","text":"I'm thrilled to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm thrilled to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses extreme happiness or excitement about an opportunity.","example":"I'm thrilled to be able to join you for the celebration.","plain":"I'm extremely happy to be able to join you for the celebration.","note":"A strong and genuine expression of joy, often for a special occasion.","audio":"audio/77217e4a9a156dc8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-arrange-for","text":"I'm trying to arrange for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to arrange for","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to organize or make preparations for something.","example":"I'm trying to arrange for a late check-out, as my flight isn't until evening.","plain":"I'm trying to organize a late check-out, as my flight isn't until evening.","note":"A polite way to state your intention to make a specific arrangement.","audio":"audio/68b121a53601425a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-ascertain","text":"I'm trying to ascertain","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to ascertain","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to find out or confirm information, often with some effort.","example":"I'm trying to ascertain the best route to the city centre during rush hour.","plain":"I'm trying to find out the best route to the city centre during rush hour.","note":"Formal and precise, implies a careful search for accurate information.","audio":"audio/cc72a534d36258a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-gauge","text":"I'm trying to gauge","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to gauge","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Explains that the speaker is attempting to assess or understand the extent of something, like opinions or reactions.","example":"I'm trying to gauge the team's reaction to the recent organizational changes.","plain":"I'm trying to assess the team's reaction to the recent organizational changes.","note":"Used when seeking to understand or measure a response.","audio":"audio/21de2477e8b75551.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-a-clear-picture-of","text":"I'm trying to get a clear picture of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get a clear picture of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to obtain a complete and accurate understanding of a situation.","example":"I'm trying to get a clear picture of the itinerary for the guided tour tomorrow.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the itinerary for the guided tour tomorrow completely and accurately.","note":"Used when seeking comprehensive and unambiguous information.","audio":"audio/c1d1c31b62b5fe86.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-a-feel-for","text":"I'm trying to get a feel for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get a feel for","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to develop an intuitive understanding or impression of something.","example":"I'm trying to get a feel for the local culture by visiting markets and cafes.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the local culture intuitively by visiting markets and cafes.","note":"Implies a more experiential and less analytical understanding.","audio":"audio/725b823cc0c36a2f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-a-grasp-of","text":"I'm trying to get a grasp of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get a grasp of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to understand something thoroughly, especially a complex idea or system.","example":"I'm trying to get a grasp of the local public transport system before I venture out.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the local public transport system thoroughly before I venture out.","note":"Implies a deeper, more comprehensive understanding.","audio":"audio/1bdd5327531afe04.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-a-sense-of-proportion","text":"I'm trying to get a sense of proportion","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get a sense of proportion","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to understand the relative importance of different things in a situation.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense of proportion about this minor delay; it's not the end of the world.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the relative importance of this minor delay; it's not the end of the world.","note":"Used to put a problem into perspective, often to downplay its severity.","audio":"audio/803df7abd4f32fa1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-a-sense-of-urgency","text":"I'm trying to get a sense of urgency","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get a sense of urgency","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to convey or understand the immediate importance of a situation.","example":"I'm trying to get a sense of urgency across to the airline about my missed connection.","plain":"I'm trying to make the airline understand the immediate importance of my missed connection.","note":"Used when emphasizing the need for prompt action.","audio":"audio/9064864aea43e810.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-accustomed-to","text":"I'm trying to get accustomed to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get accustomed to","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to become familiar with and adapt to new conditions or surroundings.","example":"I'm trying to get accustomed to the time difference, but jet lag is hitting hard.","plain":"I'm trying to adapt to the time difference, but jet lag is hitting hard.","note":"Used when adjusting to new environments or routines.","audio":"audio/292846228b4078a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-an-idea-of","text":"I'm trying to get an idea of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get an idea of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to form a general understanding or estimate of something.","example":"I'm trying to get an idea of how long the journey will take by public transport.","plain":"I'm trying to understand roughly how long the journey will take by public transport.","note":"Similar to \"get a sense of\" but focuses more on estimation or general understanding.","audio":"audio/453a02d9f08058be.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-an-update-on","text":"I'm trying to get an update on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get an update on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to receive the latest information about a situation or process.","example":"I'm trying to get an update on the status of my lost luggage claim.","plain":"I'm trying to receive the latest information about my lost luggage claim.","note":"Common when waiting for news or progress on an ongoing issue.","audio":"audio/00118cae34f1d810.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-clarity-on","text":"I'm trying to get clarity on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get clarity on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to obtain a clear understanding or explanation of something.","example":"I'm trying to get clarity on the visa requirements for my next destination.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the visa requirements for my next destination clearly.","note":"Formal and precise, used when seeking unambiguous information.","audio":"audio/f7c1cdc9a42ad956.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-hold-of","text":"I'm trying to get hold of","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get hold of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to contact or find someone or something.","example":"I'm trying to get hold of the hotel manager to discuss my booking issue.","plain":"I'm trying to contact the hotel manager to discuss my booking issue.","note":"Informal and common, often implies some difficulty in reaching someone.","audio":"audio/8d58802b33082dda.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-my-bearings","text":"I'm trying to get my bearings","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get my bearings","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to understand one's position or situation relative to surroundings.","example":"I'm trying to get my bearings after arriving in this new city; everything feels unfamiliar.","plain":"I'm trying to understand where I am after arriving in this new city; everything feels unfamiliar.","note":"Common when feeling disoriented in a new place.","audio":"audio/82a85edf61255c6b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-get-through-to","text":"I'm trying to get through to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to get through to","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to contact someone by phone, often with difficulty.","example":"I'm trying to get through to the airline's customer service, but the line is always busy.","plain":"I'm trying to call the airline's customer service, but the line is always busy.","note":"Common when experiencing difficulty reaching someone by phone.","audio":"audio/a0315b2edcb8eaaf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-navigate","text":"I'm trying to navigate","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to navigate","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to find one's way through a complex or unfamiliar area or situation.","example":"I'm trying to navigate the local public transport system, which is quite extensive.","plain":"I'm trying to find my way through the local public transport system, which is quite extensive.","note":"Implies dealing with complexity, not just simple directions.","audio":"audio/aa5caed75804cc0f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-resolve","text":"I'm trying to resolve","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to resolve","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state your intention to find a solution to a problem or issue.","example":"I'm trying to resolve the issue with my hotel key card, which isn't working.","plain":"I'm trying to fix the problem with my hotel key card, which isn't working.","note":"A professional and polite way to indicate you are working on a solution.","audio":"audio/69048c6052b61f54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-trying-to-sort-out","text":"I'm trying to sort out","kind":"frame","form":"I'm trying to sort out","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To attempt to organize, resolve, or deal with a problem or situation.","example":"I'm trying to sort out my travel insurance claim after my bag was delayed.","plain":"I'm trying to deal with my travel insurance claim after my bag was delayed.","note":"Informal and common, implies dealing with a complex or messy situation.","audio":"audio/9a63b66d6a2c0d41.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-unable-to-commit-to","text":"I'm unable to commit to","kind":"frame","form":"I'm unable to commit to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"States that one cannot make a firm promise or decision about something.","example":"I'm unable to commit to that date, as my schedule is uncertain.","plain":"I can't promise to be available on that date, as my schedule is uncertain.","note":"A clear and honest way to express uncertainty about future availability.","audio":"audio/5f51e3f36489a32d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-under-the-impression-that","text":"I'm under the impression that","kind":"frame","form":"I'm under the impression that","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state what you believe to be true, often based on what you've heard or observed.","example":"I'm under the impression that breakfast is included in our room rate.","plain":"I think breakfast is included in our room rate.","note":"A slightly formal way to state a belief, inviting confirmation or correction.","audio":"audio/c6301aadd56b857a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-up-for","text":"I'm up for","kind":"frame","form":"I'm up for","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses willingness or enthusiasm to participate in an activity.","example":"I'm up for anything, just let me know the details.","plain":"I'm willing to do anything, just let me know the details.","note":"A casual and enthusiastic way to agree to a suggestion.","audio":"audio/d73d6a2640b47c1f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-very-pleased-with","text":"I'm very pleased with","kind":"frame","form":"I'm very pleased with","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express strong satisfaction with something.","example":"I'm very pleased with the outcome of the negotiation; well done.","plain":"I'm very satisfied with the outcome of the negotiation; well done.","note":"Direct and clear expression of satisfaction, often for results.","audio":"audio/adda750d64769db6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-very-relieved-to-hear-that","text":"I'm very relieved to hear that","kind":"chunk","form":"I'm very relieved to hear that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong comfort and ease upon receiving positive news.","example":"I'm very relieved to hear that your surgery went well and you're recovering.","plain":"I am very comforted to hear that your surgery went well and you're recovering.","note":"A sincere and common expression of relief, slightly less intense than \"so relieved.\"","audio":"audio/e6b8421f37d00058.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.im-working-on","text":"I'm working on","kind":"frame","form":"I'm working on","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To indicate that one is currently engaged in improving or developing something.","example":"I'm working on improving my presentation skills, as you suggested.","plain":"I'm currently developing my presentation skills, as you suggested.","note":"Shows that feedback has been acted upon and progress is underway.","audio":"audio/5d31819d2e318ac3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ive-gone-off-on-a-tangent","text":"I've gone off on a tangent","kind":"chunk","form":"I've gone off on a tangent","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To admit that one has strayed from the main topic of discussion.","example":"Apologies, I've gone off on a tangent; let's get back to the main agenda.","plain":"Sorry, I've strayed from the main topic; let's return to the main agenda.","note":"A self-deprecating but honest way to acknowledge a digression and redirect.","audio":"audio/757d80567f86352c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ive-really-enjoyed-our-conversation","text":"I've really enjoyed our conversation","kind":"chunk","form":"I've really enjoyed our conversation","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A sincere and polite way to express genuine pleasure from a discussion.","example":"I've really enjoyed our conversation about sustainable living; it's given me much to think about.","plain":"I genuinely liked talking to you about sustainable living; it's given me much to think about.","note":"A warm and appreciative closing, suitable for engaging discussions.","audio":"audio/f764f3b5323c459c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ideally-speaking","text":"Ideally speaking","kind":"chunk","form":"Ideally speaking","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Refers to the best possible outcome or situation, often contrasted with reality.","example":"Ideally speaking, we'd prefer a shorter contract, but we're flexible.","plain":"The best outcome would be a shorter contract, but we are flexible.","note":"A slightly more formal variant of 'in an ideal world', used to state a preference.","audio":"audio/e639344daa114726.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-could-just","text":"If I could just","kind":"frame","form":"If I could just","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite way to interrupt or interject, often to ask a question or make a point.","example":"If I could just ask, what time does the last bus leave for the city center?","plain":"What time does the last bus leave for the city center?","note":"Used to politely gain attention or interject.","audio":"audio/81157bd7e3399697.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-could-just-add-a-quick-point","text":"If I could just add a quick point","kind":"frame","form":"If I could just add a quick point","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for permission to insert a brief additional comment.","example":"If I could just add a quick point, the client feedback was overwhelmingly positive.","plain":"Can I quickly add that the client's feedback was very good?","note":"A common and polite way to interject briefly without disrupting the flow too much.","audio":"audio/a5c741267a65ffd9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-could-just-interject","text":"if I could just interject","kind":"frame","form":"If I could just interject","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A formal and polite way to ask for a moment to interrupt and make a point.","example":"If I could just interject for a moment, I have a slightly different perspective.","plain":"May I interrupt briefly to share my thoughts?","note":"More formal than 'jump in'. Used to politely assert one's turn in a discussion.","audio":"audio/6ff8bbfb86511b54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-i-may-offer-a-thought","text":"If I may offer a thought","kind":"frame","form":"If I may offer a thought","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for permission to share an idea or opinion.","example":"If I may offer a thought, perhaps we should consider an alternative approach.","plain":"Can I share an idea? Maybe we should think about a different way.","note":"A very polite and slightly formal way to introduce a contribution.","audio":"audio/ab58c8b17156cbdf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.if-youre-willing-to","text":"If you're willing to","kind":"frame","form":"If you're willing to","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Introduces a conditional offer based on the other party's readiness to act.","example":"If you're willing to commit to a longer contract, we can offer a better rate.","plain":"We can offer a better rate if you commit to a longer contract.","note":"A polite and common way to present a conditional offer, inviting cooperation.","audio":"audio/9ca7fa61ed785789.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.imagine-a-scenario-where","text":"Imagine a scenario where","kind":"frame","form":"Imagine a scenario where","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to ask the listener to visualize a hypothetical situation to understand a concept.","example":"Imagine a scenario where our main competitor suddenly goes out of business.","plain":"Consider what would happen if our main competitor suddenly goes out of business.","note":"Engages the listener by inviting them to think through a hypothetical situation.","audio":"audio/10d8d847b60224e6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.immerse-oneself-in","text":"immerse oneself in","kind":"collocation","form":"immerse myself in","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To fully engage with and experience a new culture or environment.","example":"I try to immerse myself in the local culture whenever I travel.","plain":"I try to fully experience the local culture when I travel.","note":"Suggests a deep engagement, often used for cultural experiences.","audio":"audio/9f53c6660b5c0db6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-an-ideal-world","text":"In an ideal world","kind":"chunk","form":"In an ideal world","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Describes a perfect but often unrealistic or unlikely scenario.","example":"In an ideal world, we'd have unlimited budget, but we must work within constraints.","plain":"Ideally, we'd have an unlimited budget, but we have to work within limits.","note":"Used to acknowledge a desired outcome while recognizing practical limitations.","audio":"audio/0d23ffb8ae4a0fd7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-comparison-with","text":"In comparison with","kind":"chunk","form":"In comparison with","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a point by contrasting it with another.","example":"In comparison with the control group, the intervention group showed significant improvements.","plain":"The intervention group showed significant improvements when compared to the control group.","note":"A standard, formal phrase for making comparisons.","audio":"audio/b07a26ebf0c112e6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-conclusion-it-appears-that","text":"In conclusion, it appears that","kind":"frame","form":"In conclusion, it appears that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce the final summary of findings, often with a degree of hedging.","example":"In conclusion, it appears that the intervention had a modest but significant positive effect.","plain":"In conclusion, the intervention seems to have had a modest but significant positive effect.","note":"A formal way to present a final finding, often with a cautious tone.","audio":"audio/7c6f7690b14a58e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-contrast-to-x","text":"In contrast to X","kind":"frame","form":"In contrast to previous studies","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights a difference or opposition between the current point and a previously mentioned one.","example":"In contrast to previous studies, our research indicates a significant positive correlation.","plain":"Unlike previous studies, our research indicates a significant positive correlation.","note":"Used to introduce a contrasting idea or finding, often when comparing research.","audio":"audio/f5a412971388f925.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-line-with-xs-argument","text":"In line with X's argument","kind":"frame","form":"In line with Professor Smith's argument","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that a point is consistent with or supported by another person's reasoning.","example":"In line with Professor Smith's argument, we find that social capital plays a crucial role.","plain":"Our findings are consistent with Professor Smith's argument that social capital plays a crucial role.","note":"Used to show agreement or support from an external source.","audio":"audio/f34a06d4178c76f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-line-with-xs-findings","text":"In line with X's findings","kind":"chunk","form":"In line with Dr. Smith's findings","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To state that something is consistent with or supported by the research of another person.","example":"In line with Dr. Smith's findings, our study also suggests a correlation between these variables.","plain":"Our study also suggests a correlation between these variables, similar to Dr. Smith's findings.","note":"A formal way to cite or refer to another's work, showing agreement.","audio":"audio/d75dd3988f58eee0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-reference-to-x","text":"In reference to X","kind":"frame","form":"In reference to the seminal paper","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a point by indicating it relates to a specific source or topic.","example":"In reference to the seminal paper, our methodology diverges slightly in data collection.","plain":"Regarding the seminal paper, our methodology differs slightly in data collection.","note":"A formal way to introduce a point by linking it to a specific source.","audio":"audio/0b176f491c6f5c8f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-retrospect","text":"In retrospect","kind":"chunk","form":"In retrospect","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reflects on past events with the benefit of hindsight or current knowledge.","example":"In retrospect, I should have asked more questions before agreeing to the terms of the contract.","plain":"Looking back, I should have asked more questions before agreeing to the terms of the contract.","note":"Used for thoughtful reflection on past decisions or events.","audio":"audio/750c8492b6aa711e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.in-stark-contrast-to","text":"In stark contrast to","kind":"chunk","form":"In stark contrast to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights a very strong difference between two things.","example":"In stark contrast to earlier theories, our findings suggest a more dynamic interaction.","plain":"Our findings suggest a more dynamic interaction, which is very different from earlier theories.","note":"Emphasizes a significant difference; quite strong.","audio":"audio/7c045c467d51896e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.is-that-making-sense","text":"Is that making sense","kind":"frame","form":"Is that making sense","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To check if the explanation is clear and comprehensible to the listener.","example":"We're implementing a new agile workflow; is that making sense so far?","plain":"We're implementing a new agile workflow; is that clear and comprehensible so far?","note":"A common, slightly more active way to check understanding than \"Does that make sense?\".","audio":"audio/6a849e4a2b9f2981.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-bears-repeating-that","text":"It bears repeating that","kind":"frame","form":"It bears repeating that","level":"C2","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"Emphasizes a point by stating it is important enough to be mentioned again.","example":"It bears repeating that these results are preliminary and should be interpreted with caution.","plain":"We should repeat that these results are preliminary and should be interpreted with caution.","note":"Used to underscore the importance of a particular point, often when summarizing or reinforcing.","audio":"audio/bfcb9de321c87b77.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-certainly-makes-you-think","text":"It certainly makes you think","kind":"frame","form":"It certainly makes you think","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that something prompts careful consideration or reflection.","example":"The implications of AI on employment, it certainly makes you think.","plain":"The implications of AI on employment, that definitely prompts reflection.","note":"A common phrase for acknowledging a thought-provoking topic.","audio":"audio/87815bbe16a822dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-could-be-argued","text":"It could be argued","kind":"frame","form":"It could be argued","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a possible interpretation or claim, often to present an alternative view or soften a statement.","example":"It could be argued that the data, while compelling, doesn't fully account for external variables.","plain":"One might say the data is compelling but doesn't fully account for external variables.","note":"A common academic phrase to introduce a point without fully committing to it.","audio":"audio/a58632687da98d82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-dawned-on-me-that","text":"it dawned on me that","kind":"frame","form":"it dawned on me that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes a sudden realization or understanding","example":"After reviewing the data, it dawned on me that we had overlooked a key factor.","plain":"After reviewing the data, I suddenly realized that we had overlooked a key factor.","note":"Conveys a moment of insight or epiphany. Common in reflective storytelling.","audio":"audio/4b33f736fc065759.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-depends-on","text":"it depends on","kind":"frame","form":"It depends on","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Indicates that the answer or outcome is conditional and varies based on certain factors.","example":"It depends on the specific project requirements, but I'm flexible with my working hours.","plain":"The answer varies based on the specific project requirements, but I'm flexible with my working hours.","note":"Useful for giving nuanced answers, avoiding overly simplistic or definitive statements.","audio":"audio/2c0c17883eb93cc0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-is-important-to-stress","text":"It is important to stress","kind":"frame","form":"It is important to stress","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes a particular point or aspect as significant.","example":"It is important to stress that these results are preliminary and require replication.","plain":"We need to emphasize that these results are preliminary and must be replicated.","note":"A direct way to highlight the significance of a specific detail.","audio":"audio/7aa73f1995f22da8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-is-often-the-case","text":"It is often the case","kind":"frame","form":"It is often the case","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States a general tendency or common occurrence, acknowledging exceptions.","example":"It is often the case that early career researchers face significant funding challenges.","plain":"Early career researchers often face significant funding challenges.","note":"Useful for making generalizations while implicitly allowing for exceptions.","audio":"audio/9171fe185bfaa281.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-looks-like-youre","text":"It looks like you're","kind":"frame","form":"It looks like you're","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to make a polite observation about someone's current activity or state, often to open a conversation.","example":"It looks like you're enjoying the exhibition; which piece is your favourite?","plain":"You seem to be enjoying the exhibition; which piece is your favourite?","note":"A non-intrusive way to comment on someone's actions or appearance, inviting a response.","audio":"audio/744098505a2f4f8c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-might-be-prudent","text":"it might be prudent","kind":"chunk","form":"it might be prudent","level":"C2","situations":["meetings","feedback"],"meaning":"It would be wise or sensible to do something.","example":"Given the current market, it might be prudent to delay the launch.","plain":"Given the current market, it would be wise to delay the launch.","note":"Formal and cautious suggestion.","audio":"audio/f9b49a8daaadc765.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-might-be-worth-considering","text":"it might be worth considering","kind":"chunk","form":"it might be worth considering","level":"C2","situations":["feedback","persuading"],"meaning":"Suggests that something is a good idea to think about.","example":"It might be worth considering alternative suppliers for this component.","plain":"It could be a good idea to think about alternative suppliers for this component.","note":"Gentle suggestion, inviting discussion.","audio":"audio/30cb56636530052c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-presents-a-practical-challenge","text":"It presents a practical challenge","kind":"collocation","form":"It presents a practical challenge","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Indicates that something is difficult to implement or achieve in reality.","example":"While the idea is good, implementing it presents a practical challenge due to resource limitations.","plain":"The idea is good, but doing it is hard because we don't have enough resources.","note":"Professional and realistic, used to acknowledge difficulties in execution.","audio":"audio/a25e6d2cfa9b3fd6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-requires-careful-navigation","text":"It requires careful navigation","kind":"collocation","form":"It requires careful navigation","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Suggests that a situation is delicate or complex and needs sensitive handling.","example":"This negotiation involves multiple stakeholders, so it requires careful navigation.","plain":"This negotiation has many people involved, so we need to handle it very carefully.","note":"Implies a need for skill and diplomacy in a tricky situation.","audio":"audio/2102fd0e42dcc172.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-sounds-like-a-lot","text":"It sounds like a lot","kind":"frame","form":"It sounds like a lot","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that a situation or task is overwhelming or demanding.","example":"You're managing three different teams? It sounds like a lot to handle.","plain":"You're managing three different teams? That seems like a very demanding task.","note":"An empathetic way to acknowledge someone's heavy workload or stress.","audio":"audio/05260c40aa3d51da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-sounds-like-youre-underwater","text":"it sounds like you're underwater","kind":"frame","form":"It sounds like you're underwater","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describes distorted audio that is muffled and unclear, as if speaking from a distance.","example":"I'm afraid it sounds like you're underwater; the audio is very distorted.","plain":"Your voice is muffled and unclear, making it hard to understand.","note":"An idiomatic and vivid description of poor audio quality. Informal but widely understood.","audio":"audio/7001ad8d01bca3e8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-sounds-rather-challenging","text":"It sounds rather challenging","kind":"chunk","form":"It sounds rather challenging","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild sympathy or understanding that a situation is difficult, often an understatement.","example":"Having to manage two major projects simultaneously, it sounds rather challenging.","plain":"Having to manage two major projects simultaneously sounds very difficult.","note":"A polite, understated way to acknowledge difficulty, suitable for professional contexts.","audio":"audio/632ef5f9b130b86c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-warms-my-heart","text":"It warms my heart","kind":"chunk","form":"It warms my heart","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something evokes feelings of happiness, comfort, or tenderness.","example":"Seeing the community come together like that, it warms my heart.","plain":"Seeing the community come together like that, it makes me feel happy.","note":"A common, heartfelt expression of positive emotion.","audio":"audio/a6797198be5705ae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-classic-case-of","text":"It was a classic case of","kind":"frame","form":"It was a classic case of","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Categorizes a situation as a typical or archetypal example of a particular problem or phenomenon.","example":"It was a classic case of miscommunication leading to a completely avoidable error.","plain":"It was a typical example of miscommunication leading to a completely avoidable error.","note":"Used to generalize from a specific instance to a broader pattern.","audio":"audio/ebcf6836bd0b30de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-delightful-evening","text":"It was a delightful evening","kind":"chunk","form":"It was a delightful evening","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and appreciative way to describe an enjoyable social event.","example":"It was a delightful evening, thank you so much for hosting.","plain":"It was a very pleasant evening, thank you so much for hosting.","note":"A slightly more formal or elegant way to express enjoyment than 'lovely'.","audio":"audio/c97c553ab770710d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-lovely-evening","text":"it was a lovely evening","kind":"chunk","form":"It was a lovely evening","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and appreciative closing remark after a social gathering.","example":"Thank you so much for having us, it was a lovely evening.","plain":"Thank you so much for having us, it was a very nice evening.","note":"A common, polite way for a guest to thank their host at the end of an event.","audio":"audio/dbe398be5ec35f27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-moment-of-truth","text":"It was a moment of truth","kind":"chunk","form":"It was a moment of truth","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a decisive or revealing point in a situation where a crucial test or decision occurs.","example":"It was a moment of truth when we had to present our findings to the board.","plain":"It was a critical moment when we had to present our findings to the board.","note":"Highlights a pivotal, often challenging, point in a story.","audio":"audio/bb1d985463fe22ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-moment-when","text":"it was a moment when","kind":"frame","form":"it was a moment when","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"highlights a specific, significant point in time within a narrative","example":"It was a moment when I realized that my priorities had completely shifted.","plain":"At that specific time, I realized that my priorities had completely shifted.","note":"Draws attention to a turning point or a profound realization in a story.","audio":"audio/1d37e1fc5ed52d2a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-productive-call","text":"It was a productive call","kind":"chunk","form":"It was a productive call","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describes the conversation as having achieved good results or progress.","example":"It was a productive call; we managed to resolve several key issues.","plain":"The call was useful; we solved many important problems.","note":"A common and positive way to summarize the outcome of a conversation.","audio":"audio/db50c0adf7260d7e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-real-pleasure-having-you","text":"It was a real pleasure having you","kind":"chunk","form":"It was a real pleasure having you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A sincere expression of enjoyment from a host after a guest's visit.","example":"It was a real pleasure having you over for dinner last night.","plain":"I truly enjoyed having you over for dinner last night.","note":"A warm and genuine way for a host to thank a guest for their company.","audio":"audio/ba3a34d3bd467124.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-a-wonderful-evening","text":"It was a wonderful evening","kind":"chunk","form":"It was a wonderful evening","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A warm and appreciative way to describe a very enjoyable social event.","example":"It was a wonderful evening, thank you again for everything.","plain":"It was a fantastic evening, thank you again for everything.","note":"A sincere and positive closing remark after a pleasant social gathering.","audio":"audio/5f6d2943e9f4d419.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-good-to-connect","text":"it was good to connect","kind":"collocation","form":"It was good to connect","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses satisfaction at having made contact or spoken with someone.","example":"Thank you for the productive discussion; it was good to connect.","plain":"I enjoyed our conversation and making contact with you.","note":"A professional and polite closing, especially for initial contacts or business calls.","audio":"audio/b15349e534c1af29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-good-to-touch-base","text":"It was good to touch base","kind":"chunk","form":"It was good to touch base","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses that it was productive or pleasant to briefly connect and discuss things.","example":"It was good to touch base about the project updates; I feel much clearer now.","plain":"It was helpful to talk briefly about the project updates; I understand better now.","note":"A common and friendly way to acknowledge a useful short conversation.","audio":"audio/2eee1f96c9b7da81.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-great-catching-up","text":"It was great catching up","kind":"chunk","form":"It was great catching up","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A friendly and common way to express enjoyment of updating each other on recent events.","example":"It was great catching up with you after all this time; let's not leave it so long.","plain":"It was good to hear your news after all this time; let's not leave it so long.","note":"Informal and warm, suitable for friends or colleagues you haven't seen in a while.","audio":"audio/f4385c224c687e37.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-great-to-reconnect","text":"It was great to reconnect","kind":"collocation","form":"It was great to reconnect","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses pleasure at renewing a relationship or friendship after a period apart.","example":"It was great to reconnect with you after all these years.","plain":"It was wonderful to get back in touch with you after all these years.","note":"A sincere and appreciative way to acknowledge renewing an old acquaintance.","audio":"audio/caf6b1fa45d7de92.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-implied-that","text":"It was implied that","kind":"frame","form":"It was implied that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports an indirect suggestion or hint, rather than a direct statement.","example":"It was implied that if we didn't meet the deadline, there would be consequences.","plain":"It was hinted that if we didn't meet the deadline, there would be consequences.","note":"Useful for conveying subtle communication or unspoken threats.","audio":"audio/ad399580b685d323.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-lovely-talking-to-you","text":"It was lovely talking to you","kind":"chunk","form":"It was lovely talking to you","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and warm way to express enjoyment of a conversation when parting.","example":"It was lovely talking to you about your travels; perhaps we'll chat again soon.","plain":"I enjoyed talking to you about your travels; perhaps we'll chat again soon.","note":"A standard, friendly closing, suitable for both casual and professional settings.","audio":"audio/c8ad2f68b8e44d7a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-one-of-those","text":"it was one of those","kind":"frame","form":"it was one of those","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces a situation that is typical of a certain type or category","example":"It was one of those meetings where everyone talks but nothing gets decided.","plain":"It was a typical meeting where everyone talks but nothing gets decided.","note":"Sets a relatable scene by categorizing an experience. Very conversational.","audio":"audio/4d40678751734ed1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-one-of-those-moments","text":"It was one of those moments","kind":"chunk","form":"It was one of those moments","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a memorable or significant point in time, often with a particular feeling or realization.","example":"It was one of those moments where you just knew things would never be the same again.","plain":"It was a significant moment where you just knew things would never be the same again.","note":"Evokes a sense of shared experience or understanding.","audio":"audio/47f3a3be97b2c4cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-quite-a-revelation","text":"It was quite a revelation","kind":"chunk","form":"It was quite a revelation","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a significant or surprising discovery or realization.","example":"It was quite a revelation to discover the true reason behind their sudden departure.","plain":"It was a significant discovery to find the true reason behind their sudden departure.","note":"Used for impactful, often unexpected, insights.","audio":"audio/a681da8203158f4a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-was-quite-a-scene","text":"It was quite a scene","kind":"chunk","form":"It was quite a scene","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes an event that was remarkable, dramatic, or memorable.","example":"It was quite a scene when the CEO walked into the meeting wearing a Hawaiian shirt.","plain":"It was a memorable event when the CEO walked into the meeting wearing a Hawaiian shirt.","note":"Used to describe something visually striking or noteworthy.","audio":"audio/09ffc2d8d67cf48d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.it-would-appear-that","text":"It would appear that","kind":"frame","form":"It would appear that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests an observation or conclusion based on available evidence, but with a degree of caution.","example":"It would appear that the initial findings support our hypothesis, though further testing is needed.","plain":"The initial findings seem to support our hypothesis, but more testing is needed.","note":"A polite, formal way to present a tentative conclusion.","audio":"audio/1fcca93cff4be46a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-balancing-act-between","text":"it's a balancing act between","kind":"frame","form":"it's a balancing act between","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes a situation requiring careful management of competing demands","example":"It's a balancing act between innovation and maintaining stability.","plain":"It requires careful management to balance innovation and maintaining stability.","note":"Expands on 'a balancing act' to specify the elements being balanced.","audio":"audio/71424aefe909a86e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-bit-difficult-to-make-out","text":"It's a bit difficult to make out","kind":"frame","form":"It's a bit difficult to make out","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses that the speaker is having trouble understanding what is being said due to poor audio.","example":"It's a bit difficult to make out what you're saying with all the background noise.","plain":"I can't understand you well because of the noise in the background.","note":"A polite and slightly indirect way to indicate poor comprehension.","audio":"audio/666c835295b67a1a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-bit-much-to-ask","text":"It's a bit much to ask","kind":"frame","form":"It's a bit much to ask","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that a request is unreasonable or excessive.","example":"Expecting us to work unpaid overtime, it's a bit much to ask.","plain":"Expecting us to work unpaid overtime, that is an unreasonable request.","note":"A common, informal way to express disapproval of an excessive request.","audio":"audio/5c7bdcfdc3aa6dfc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-bit-of-a-nuisance","text":"It's a bit of a nuisance","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a bit of a nuisance","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes something as a minor annoyance or inconvenience.","example":"Having to redo the entire report due to a formatting error, it's a bit of a nuisance.","plain":"Having to redo the entire report due to a formatting error is a minor annoyance.","note":"A common, understated way to express mild irritation or inconvenience.","audio":"audio/f37b475ced17713e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-challenging-situation","text":"It's a challenging situation","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a challenging situation","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes a circumstance that presents significant difficulties or requires considerable effort.","example":"Navigating the new market regulations, it's a challenging situation for many businesses.","plain":"Navigating the new market regulations is a difficult situation for many businesses.","note":"A neutral, professional way to describe a difficult circumstance.","audio":"audio/293b5aea1999b915.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-common-misconception","text":"It's a common misconception","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a common misconception","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","presenting"],"meaning":"To state that a widely held belief is actually incorrect.","example":"It's a common misconception that innovation always requires a huge budget.","plain":"Many people wrongly believe that innovation always requires a huge budget.","note":"Useful for correcting popular but inaccurate ideas, often used to introduce a counter-argument.","audio":"audio/0bb6f828684985f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-common-pitfall","text":"It's a common pitfall","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a common pitfall","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a frequent or typical mistake or difficulty that people encounter.","example":"It's a common pitfall to underestimate the time required for complex tasks.","plain":"It's a frequent mistake to underestimate the time required for complex tasks.","note":"Used to identify a recurring error or challenge.","audio":"audio/108c8dc78530a452.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-complex-issue","text":"It's a complex issue","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a complex issue","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to acknowledge that a topic or problem is intricate and not easily resolved.","example":"It's a complex issue with many interconnected factors, so there's no simple solution.","plain":"This issue has many interconnected factors, so there's no simple solution.","note":"Used to manage expectations about the ease of understanding or solving a problem.","audio":"audio/f9a8f9ce7d689937.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-crying-shame","text":"It's a crying shame","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a crying shame","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong regret or disappointment about something unfortunate.","example":"It's a crying shame that such a talented artist never got recognition.","plain":"It is very regrettable that such a talented artist never got recognition.","note":"A common, slightly informal expression of strong regret or pity.","audio":"audio/2634d114850a9356.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-date","text":"it's a date","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a date","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An informal and enthusiastic way to confirm a social arrangement.","example":"Dinner on Friday? Sounds good, it's a date!","plain":"Dinner on Friday? Sounds good, we have a plan!","note":"A playful and informal way to confirm plans, often used for one-on-one meetings.","audio":"audio/c914f51c1211c532.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-delicate-balance","text":"It's a delicate balance","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a delicate balance","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To describe a situation requiring careful management to satisfy conflicting requirements or interests.","example":"It's a delicate balance between keeping costs down and ensuring the highest quality product.","plain":"It requires careful management to keep costs down while ensuring the highest quality product.","note":"Highlights the complexity and sensitivity of a situation.","audio":"audio/57664bfcf697e5a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-double-edged-sword","text":"it's a double-edged sword","kind":"collocation","form":"it's a double-edged sword","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes something that has both positive and negative consequences","example":"Working from home offers flexibility, but it's a double-edged sword for team cohesion.","plain":"Working from home offers flexibility, but it also has negative consequences for team cohesion.","note":"A common idiom to describe a situation with inherent trade-offs.","audio":"audio/2c672674096543c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-fine-line","text":"It's a fine line","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a fine line","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to describe a situation where two things are very similar, and it's hard to distinguish them.","example":"It's a fine line between being assertive and being aggressive in negotiations.","plain":"It's hard to distinguish between being assertive and being aggressive in negotiations.","note":"Used when discussing a delicate balance or distinction.","audio":"audio/bbd53550f102701f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-grey-area","text":"it's a grey area","kind":"collocation","form":"it's a grey area","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes a situation that is unclear, undefined, or open to interpretation","example":"The company policy doesn't explicitly cover this, so it's a grey area.","plain":"The company policy doesn't explicitly cover this, so it's an unclear situation.","note":"A common idiom for situations lacking clear rules or definitions.","audio":"audio/326d961b33c5d81d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-huge-relief-to-know","text":"It's a huge relief to know","kind":"frame","form":"It's a huge relief to know","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses great comfort and ease upon learning specific positive information.","example":"After waiting for weeks, it's a huge relief to know the funding has been approved.","plain":"After waiting for weeks, I feel much better knowing the funding has been approved.","note":"A common and strong expression of relief, focusing on the knowledge gained.","audio":"audio/82ddbdddf82f90d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-little-frustrating-when","text":"It's a little frustrating when","kind":"frame","form":"It's a little frustrating when","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses mild irritation or annoyance about a recurring or specific situation.","example":"It's a little frustrating when you spend hours on a task only for it to be cancelled.","plain":"It is slightly annoying when you spend hours on a task only for it to be cancelled.","note":"A common, understated way to express mild annoyance or irritation.","audio":"audio/b5067def572bda9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-long-story","text":"it's a long story","kind":"chunk","form":"it's a long story","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"indicates that a full explanation would be too lengthy or complicated","example":"Why did I leave early? Oh, it's a long story, I'll tell you later.","plain":"Why did I leave early? It's a complicated story, I'll tell you later.","note":"Used to defer a detailed explanation, often implying complexity or drama.","audio":"audio/31b018a2fa38573b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-lot-to-take-on","text":"It's a lot to take on","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a lot to take on","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a task or responsibility is substantial and potentially overwhelming.","example":"Managing the entire conference logistics single-handedly, it's a lot to take on.","plain":"Managing the entire conference logistics single-handedly is a huge responsibility.","note":"Used to express empathy for someone facing a significant workload or challenge.","audio":"audio/f3db7677874faf63.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-matter-of","text":"It's a matter of","kind":"frame","form":"It's a matter of","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Indicates the core issue, subject, or determining factor.","example":"Ultimately, it's a matter of trust and building a strong working relationship.","plain":"Ultimately, the core issue is trust and building a strong working relationship.","note":"Used to highlight the fundamental aspect of a situation or decision.","audio":"audio/254292fcb74be09b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-matter-of-perspective","text":"It's a matter of perspective","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a matter of perspective","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Highlights that different people may view the same situation in different ways, depending on their viewpoint.","example":"It's a matter of perspective whether you see this challenge as a burden or an opportunity.","plain":"It depends on your viewpoint whether you see this challenge as a burden or an opportunity.","note":"Used to acknowledge subjectivity in interpretation.","audio":"audio/c5ae4f129faf64dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-matter-of-principle","text":"it's a matter of principle","kind":"collocation","form":"it's a matter of principle","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes a decision or action based on a fundamental belief or value","example":"I refused to compromise on quality; for me, it's a matter of principle.","plain":"I refused to compromise on quality; for me, it's based on a fundamental belief.","note":"Indicates a strong, non-negotiable stance based on personal values.","audio":"audio/49fe3e86fa3c2972.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-matter-of-record","text":"it's a matter of record","kind":"collocation","form":"it's a matter of record","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes information that has been officially documented","example":"The decision was approved by the board, and it's a matter of record.","plain":"The decision was approved by the board, and it has been officially documented.","note":"Similar to 'public record' but doesn't necessarily imply public accessibility.","audio":"audio/76273f8eec47b929.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-moot-point","text":"It's a moot point","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a moot point","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"States that a particular issue or argument is no longer relevant or worth discussing because it's already decided.","example":"It's a moot point now, as the deadline for submissions has already passed.","plain":"It's an irrelevant point now, as the deadline for submissions has already passed.","note":"Used to dismiss a discussion as no longer pertinent.","audio":"audio/99041964d35e51b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-multifaceted-problem","text":"It's a multifaceted problem","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a multifaceted problem","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Describes a problem with many different aspects or dimensions.","example":"It's a multifaceted problem, involving technology, human resources, and budget constraints.","plain":"It's a problem with many parts, including technology, staff, and money limits.","note":"Sophisticated way to describe complexity, common in analytical discussions.","audio":"audio/f7f1a5ee3262ab1f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-nuanced-discussion","text":"It's a nuanced discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a nuanced discussion","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to describe a conversation that involves subtle distinctions and careful consideration.","example":"It's a nuanced discussion, and we need to consider all perspectives before deciding.","plain":"This discussion involves subtle distinctions, and we need to consider all perspectives before deciding.","note":"Highlights the need for careful attention to subtle differences.","audio":"audio/aacc58c1b611492a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-real-joy-to-hear","text":"It's a real joy to hear","kind":"frame","form":"It's a real joy to hear","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses genuine happiness and pleasure upon receiving good news.","example":"After all the uncertainty, it's a real joy to hear that your family is safe.","plain":"After all the uncertainty, I am truly happy to hear that your family is safe.","note":"A warm and heartfelt expression of happiness, especially for news about loved ones.","audio":"audio/38717726b1a76da1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-real-pleasure","text":"It's a real pleasure","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a real pleasure","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses genuine enjoyment or satisfaction from an experience.","example":"Working with such a dedicated team, it's a real pleasure.","plain":"Working with such a dedicated team, that is genuinely enjoyable.","note":"A common and sincere expression of enjoyment or satisfaction.","audio":"audio/fd6dd79480ec7e48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-relief-to-hear","text":"It's a relief to hear","kind":"frame","form":"It's a relief to hear","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses comfort and ease upon receiving good news or a positive update.","example":"After all the uncertainty, it's a relief to hear that the flight wasn't cancelled.","plain":"After all the uncertainty, I feel much better knowing that the flight wasn't cancelled.","note":"A common and natural way to express relief about news.","audio":"audio/8ad2145b1c2d1183.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-slippery-slope","text":"It's a slippery slope","kind":"chunk","form":"It's a slippery slope","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Warns that a seemingly small or harmless action could lead to a series of increasingly negative consequences.","example":"It's a slippery slope if we start making exceptions for every individual case.","plain":"It's a dangerous path if we start making exceptions for every individual case.","note":"Used to caution against a course of action due to potential negative progression.","audio":"audio/54f7cdc50dcbfce4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-subtle-distinction","text":"It's a subtle distinction","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a subtle distinction","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Refers to a small but important difference that is not immediately obvious.","example":"It's a subtle distinction, but \"effective\" isn't quite the same as \"efficient.\"","plain":"There's a small but important difference, \"effective\" isn't exactly \"efficient.\"","note":"Used to highlight fine points of meaning or difference, often in academic or precise contexts.","audio":"audio/7322493b4c5f65ca.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-testament-to","text":"It's a testament to","kind":"frame","form":"It's a testament to","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Highlights something as strong evidence or proof of a particular quality, effort, or fact.","example":"It's a testament to her dedication that she finished the project on time despite all obstacles.","plain":"It shows her dedication that she finished the project on time despite all obstacles.","note":"Used to praise or acknowledge significant achievement.","audio":"audio/a3b1f8264bfbcbbe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-testament-to-the-fact-that","text":"It's a testament to the fact that","kind":"frame","form":"It's a testament to the fact that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Highlights something as strong evidence or proof of a general truth or principle.","example":"It's a testament to the fact that hard work pays off in the long run.","plain":"It shows that hard work pays off in the long run.","note":"Used to emphasize a general truth demonstrated by a specific event.","audio":"audio/0da3770731e67910.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-tough-call","text":"it's a tough call","kind":"collocation","form":"it's a tough call","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"It is a difficult decision to make.","example":"Choosing between these two excellent candidates, it's a tough call.","plain":"Choosing between these two excellent candidates, it is a difficult decision to make.","note":"Informal but common, acknowledging the difficulty of a choice.","audio":"audio/4a9f7980f464e028.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-tough-pill-to-swallow","text":"It's a tough pill to swallow","kind":"collocation","form":"It's a tough pill to swallow","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes something as a difficult or unpleasant truth to accept.","example":"Admitting the project failed, it's a tough pill to swallow for the team.","plain":"Admitting the project failed, that is a difficult truth for the team to accept.","note":"A common, informal idiom for accepting an unpleasant reality.","audio":"audio/0f38f81bd7b77b8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-a-vicious-cycle","text":"it's a vicious cycle","kind":"collocation","form":"it's a vicious cycle","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"describes a recurring sequence of events where one problem leads to another","example":"Lack of sleep leads to poor concentration, which then causes more stress; it's a vicious cycle.","plain":"Lack of sleep leads to poor concentration, which then causes more stress; it's a recurring problem.","note":"A common idiom to describe a self-perpetuating negative pattern.","audio":"audio/5018188605904581.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-an-interesting-question","text":"it's an interesting question","kind":"chunk","form":"It's an interesting question","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A polite way to acknowledge a question, often used to buy a moment to think before answering.","example":"It's an interesting question; I'd say my biggest challenge was managing conflicting priorities.","plain":"That's a good question; I'd say my biggest challenge was managing conflicting priorities.","note":"A common filler phrase that sounds thoughtful and buys a few seconds for reflection.","audio":"audio/ce0d8587159923b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-fruitful-discussion","text":"It's been a fruitful discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a fruitful discussion","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Expressing that the conversation was productive and yielded good results or insights.","example":"Thank you for your time, it's been a fruitful discussion.","plain":"Thank you for your time, this discussion has been very productive.","note":"Formal and positive, suitable for professional or academic settings.","audio":"audio/fa46b05b08091a9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-pleasure-getting-to-know-you","text":"it's been a pleasure getting to know you","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a pleasure getting to know you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite closing remark after developing a new acquaintance.","example":"After our project, it's been a pleasure getting to know you better.","plain":"After our project, it was nice getting to know you better.","note":"A warm and polite farewell, often used after spending some time with a new acquaintance.","audio":"audio/3f4e176035bf21a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-pleasure-having-you-over","text":"it's been a pleasure having you over","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a pleasure having you over","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and warm closing remark from a host to a guest who has visited their home.","example":"Thank you for coming, it's been a pleasure having you over.","plain":"Thank you for coming, it was nice having you visit our home.","note":"A standard, polite farewell from a host after guests have visited their home.","audio":"audio/c9b1b1cc001372f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-pleasure-meeting-all-of-you","text":"it's been a pleasure meeting all of you","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a pleasure meeting all of you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite closing remark when introduced to a group of people for the first time.","example":"Thank you for the lovely evening, it's been a pleasure meeting all of you.","plain":"Thank you for the lovely evening, it was nice meeting all of you.","note":"A standard, polite farewell when leaving a group after initial introductions.","audio":"audio/0a20e68018cad372.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-pleasure-spending-time-with-you","text":"it's been a pleasure spending time with you","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a pleasure spending time with you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and warm closing remark after a period of social interaction.","example":"Thank you for the weekend, it's been a pleasure spending time with you.","plain":"Thank you for the weekend, it was nice spending time with you.","note":"A standard, polite farewell after a social occasion, expressing enjoyment of company.","audio":"audio/0f737bb505622711.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-real-pleasure","text":"It's been a real pleasure","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a real pleasure","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A slightly more emphatic and sincere version of 'It's been a pleasure'.","example":"Thank you for inviting me; it's been a real pleasure attending your event.","plain":"Thank you for inviting me; I truly enjoyed attending your event.","note":"Conveys stronger appreciation and enjoyment than the simpler version.","audio":"audio/a551b2c03529448b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-a-real-treat","text":"It's been a real treat","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been a real treat","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk","socialising"],"meaning":"Expressing that an experience or conversation was particularly enjoyable or special.","example":"Meeting you and hearing your stories, it's been a real treat.","plain":"Meeting you and hearing your stories has been very enjoyable.","note":"More emphatic and personal than \"a pleasure\", often used for a memorable interaction.","audio":"audio/df1f1f46c1eeeabc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-good-catching-up","text":"it's been good catching up","kind":"chunk","form":"It's been good catching up","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses pleasure at having spoken to someone, especially after a period of time.","example":"Thanks for the call, it's been good catching up with you.","plain":"I enjoyed talking to you and hearing your news.","note":"A warm and friendly closing, suitable for both personal and professional calls with acquaintances.","audio":"audio/fe9bb6f5908dd86c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-been-productive","text":"It's been productive","kind":"collocation","form":"It's been productive","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A concise and professional way to describe a meeting or discussion that yielded good results.","example":"Thank you for your time; it's been productive to discuss these matters.","plain":"Thank you for your time; discussing these matters has been fruitful.","note":"A professional and efficient way to conclude a work-related conversation.","audio":"audio/68d6bc64736b4308.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-certainly-a-bold-move","text":"It's certainly a bold move","kind":"collocation","form":"It's certainly a bold move","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes an action as courageous or daring, often with implied risk.","example":"Launching a new product in this saturated market, it's certainly a bold move.","plain":"Launching a new product in this saturated market, that is a very daring action.","note":"A common phrase to describe a risky or audacious decision.","audio":"audio/c24d1b7fa9cc061c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-certainly-a-thought","text":"It's certainly a thought","kind":"chunk","form":"It's certainly a thought","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges an idea or suggestion, often implying it's worth considering but not necessarily agreeing.","example":"Moving the entire department to a new city, it's certainly a thought, but perhaps not practical.","plain":"Moving the entire department to a new city is an idea, but perhaps not practical.","note":"A neutral, often understated way to acknowledge an idea, sometimes with implied reservation.","audio":"audio/eb9be3c2fbf1b6de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-certainly-an-interesting-perspective","text":"It's certainly an interesting perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"It's certainly an interesting perspective","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges an opinion or viewpoint as thought-provoking, often without fully agreeing.","example":"While I hadn't considered it, it's certainly an interesting perspective on the issue.","plain":"While I hadn't considered it, that is definitely a thought-provoking way of looking at the issue.","note":"A diplomatic way to acknowledge a viewpoint, often used as a polite hedge.","audio":"audio/b736624314d51b57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-certainly-something-to-consider","text":"It's certainly something to consider","kind":"frame","form":"It's certainly something to consider","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges an idea or proposal as worthy of thought, often non-committally.","example":"Your suggestion for a new marketing strategy, it's certainly something to consider.","plain":"Your suggestion for a new marketing strategy, it is definitely worth thinking about.","note":"A polite, often diplomatic way to acknowledge an idea without fully endorsing it.","audio":"audio/c807ad2d3e76893a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-come-to-my-attention-that","text":"it's come to my attention that","kind":"frame","form":"It's come to my attention that","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A formal way to introduce information you have recently learned, often about a problem.","example":"It's come to my attention that some deadlines are being consistently missed.","plain":"I've recently learned that some deadlines are being consistently missed.","note":"Formal and often used when addressing an issue that needs to be resolved.","audio":"audio/3c49cf3d159a05b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-crucial-to-recognize","text":"It's crucial to recognize","kind":"frame","form":"It's crucial to recognize","level":"C2","situations":["academic","persuading"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the vital importance of understanding or acknowledging something.","example":"It's crucial to recognize the historical context that shaped these social structures.","plain":"We must recognize the historical context that shaped these social structures.","note":"A strong, formal way to highlight a necessary understanding.","audio":"audio/12bbae537729c858.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-crucial-to-remember","text":"It's crucial to remember","kind":"chunk","form":"It's crucial to remember","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the importance of retaining a particular piece of information.","example":"It's crucial to remember that client satisfaction is our top priority.","plain":"It's very important to keep in mind that making clients happy is our main goal.","note":"Strong emphasis, used to highlight a key point that should not be forgotten.","audio":"audio/ab1448b3b6d3b64d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-definitely-worth-considering","text":"It's definitely worth considering","kind":"chunk","form":"It's definitely worth considering","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that an idea or proposal merits serious thought and evaluation.","example":"Your suggestion for a new marketing strategy, it's definitely worth considering.","plain":"Your suggestion for a new marketing strategy should definitely be thought about seriously.","note":"A polite and encouraging way to acknowledge an idea's value.","audio":"audio/d7ae4f1a14925e83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-difficult-to-ascertain","text":"It's difficult to ascertain","kind":"collocation","form":"It's difficult to ascertain","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To state that it is hard to find out or determine something with certainty.","example":"It's difficult to ascertain the precise impact of these policy changes without more data.","plain":"It's hard to determine the exact impact of these policy changes without more data.","note":"Used to express uncertainty or the challenge in obtaining clear information.","audio":"audio/f142ae7c62f00958.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-early-days-yet","text":"It's early days yet","kind":"chunk","form":"It's early days yet","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Used to suggest that it's too soon to draw conclusions or make definitive judgments about something.","example":"We've only just started the project, so it's early days yet to predict its success.","plain":"We've only just started the project, so it's too soon to predict its success.","note":"A common, understated British English phrase for 'it's too soon to tell'.","audio":"audio/3c0e58d16c7835af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-easier-said-than-done","text":"It's easier said than done","kind":"chunk","form":"It's easier said than done","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to acknowledge that a proposed solution or task is more difficult in practice than in theory.","example":"Increasing productivity by 20% sounds good, but it's easier said than done.","plain":"Increasing productivity by 20% sounds good, but it's difficult to achieve.","note":"A common idiom to express practical difficulty.","audio":"audio/b9901cc87c8687a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-easy-to-forget-that","text":"It's easy to forget that","kind":"frame","form":"It's easy to forget that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Points out a fact or detail that is often overlooked or not immediately apparent.","example":"It's easy to forget that they've only been in business for a year, given their rapid growth.","plain":"People often forget that they've only been in business for a year, given their rapid growth.","note":"Used to bring an important, often overlooked, detail to attention.","audio":"audio/d2569e1db6649270.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-essential-to-grasp","text":"It's essential to grasp","kind":"frame","form":"It's essential to grasp","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Stresses the absolute necessity of understanding something fully.","example":"It's essential to grasp the underlying reasons for customer dissatisfaction before proposing solutions.","plain":"It's absolutely necessary to fully understand why customers are unhappy before suggesting answers.","note":"Highlights the critical need for deep comprehension.","audio":"audio/6be6d026718b5522.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-acknowledge-the-complexity","text":"It's important to acknowledge the complexity","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to acknowledge the complexity","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the need to recognize that a situation or issue is intricate and multi-faceted.","example":"It's important to acknowledge the complexity of human behavior in any social science research.","plain":"We must recognize that human behavior is complex in any social science research.","note":"A common academic phrase to show awareness of intricate factors.","audio":"audio/5b19cb7fd49240f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-generalizations","text":"It's important to avoid generalizations","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to avoid generalizations","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Advises against making broad statements that might not be universally true.","example":"It's important to avoid generalizations when discussing diverse populations.","plain":"We must not make broad statements when discussing diverse populations.","note":"A common academic caution against oversimplification.","audio":"audio/63d971780e93095a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-avoid-overstating","text":"It's important to avoid overstating","kind":"collocation","form":"It's important to avoid overstating","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To advise against exaggerating the importance or extent of something.","example":"It's important to avoid overstating the significance of these preliminary findings at this stage.","plain":"We should not exaggerate the significance of these preliminary findings at this stage.","note":"A common academic caution against making claims that are too strong.","audio":"audio/8975222ee657eecf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-clarify-that","text":"It's important to clarify that","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to clarify that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement intended to make something clearer or more precise.","example":"It's important to clarify that our study focused on a specific age demographic.","plain":"We need to make it clear that our study focused on a specific age demographic.","note":"Used to add precision or correct potential misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/33f3c6b045e8f3e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-consider-the-ethical-dimensions","text":"It's important to consider the ethical dimensions","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to consider the ethical dimensions","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the necessity of examining the moral aspects of an issue.","example":"It's important to consider the ethical dimensions of using AI in medical diagnostics.","plain":"We must consider the moral aspects of using AI in medical diagnostics.","note":"A common academic phrase when discussing responsible research or application.","audio":"audio/e2370660c3954e16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-differentiate-between","text":"It's important to differentiate between","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to differentiate between","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Stresses the necessity of recognizing distinct differences between two or more things.","example":"It's important to differentiate between correlation and causation in these statistical analyses.","plain":"We must distinguish between correlation and causation in these statistical analyses.","note":"Used to clarify concepts and prevent confusion.","audio":"audio/42739dfd4ecb1efb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-distinguish","text":"It's important to distinguish","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to distinguish","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to emphasize the need to differentiate between two similar but distinct concepts.","example":"It's important to distinguish between correlation and causation in our data analysis.","plain":"We must differentiate between correlation and causation in our data analysis.","note":"Used to prevent confusion between closely related ideas.","audio":"audio/5a6982678df95f2b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-emphasize","text":"It's important to emphasize","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to emphasize","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To highlight the necessity of stressing a particular point.","example":"It's important to emphasize the long-term benefits of this investment.","plain":"We need to stress the long-term benefits of this investment.","note":"A strong way to draw attention to a crucial aspect.","audio":"audio/fd054b008136c174.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-keep-in-mind","text":"It's important to keep in mind","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to keep in mind","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of remembering a particular fact or consideration.","example":"It's important to keep in mind that resources are limited for this phase.","plain":"We must remember that resources are limited for this phase.","note":"A common way to introduce a crucial reminder.","audio":"audio/cdb65cb8fef08368.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-remain-open-to","text":"It's important to remain open to","kind":"collocation","form":"It's important to remain open to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To emphasize the need to be receptive to new ideas, evidence, or possibilities.","example":"It's important to remain open to alternative interpretations of the data as new evidence emerges.","plain":"We should be open to alternative interpretations of the data as new evidence emerges.","note":"Encourages intellectual flexibility and a non-dogmatic approach.","audio":"audio/504846226d52159a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-set-the-stage","text":"It's important to set the stage","kind":"collocation","form":"It's important to set the stage","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To provide necessary background information or context before presenting the main topic.","example":"Before discussing the results, it's important to set the stage by outlining the historical context.","plain":"Before discussing the results, we need to provide the historical context.","note":"Used to prepare the audience for the main discussion by giving background.","audio":"audio/e082640a58cf7224.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-important-to-underscore","text":"It's important to underscore","kind":"frame","form":"It's important to underscore","level":"C2","situations":["academic","persuading"],"meaning":"Emphasizes a point as particularly significant or crucial.","example":"It's important to underscore the ethical considerations involved in data collection.","plain":"We need to emphasize the ethical considerations involved in data collection.","note":"A formal way to highlight the importance of a specific aspect.","audio":"audio/1b7acd75c3fab9b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-no-exaggeration-to-say","text":"It's no exaggeration to say","kind":"frame","form":"It's no exaggeration to say","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Emphasizes that a statement is true and not overstated, despite how strong it may sound.","example":"It's no exaggeration to say that the presentation was a resounding success for the team.","plain":"It's true to say that the presentation was a resounding success for the team.","note":"Used to affirm the truth of a strong statement.","audio":"audio/d195db59eefc8834.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-a-simple-answer","text":"It's not a simple answer","kind":"chunk","form":"It's not a simple answer","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"States that the response to a question is nuanced and cannot be given briefly.","example":"It's not a simple answer, as several variables influence the outcome.","plain":"The answer is not simple, as several variables influence the outcome.","note":"Prepares the audience for a more detailed explanation or a qualified response.","audio":"audio/599dcec4a090cb5a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-entirely-accurate-to-say","text":"It's not entirely accurate to say","kind":"frame","form":"It's not entirely accurate to say","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Gently corrects or qualifies a statement, suggesting it's not fully correct.","example":"It's not entirely accurate to say that all participants showed improvement; some regressed.","plain":"It's not completely correct to say that all participants showed improvement; some regressed.","note":"A polite and diplomatic way to express disagreement or provide a correction.","audio":"audio/b2c8fb01e93651a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-entirely-clear-whether","text":"It's not entirely clear whether","kind":"frame","form":"It's not entirely clear whether","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Expresses uncertainty or ambiguity about a particular situation or outcome.","example":"It's not entirely clear whether the observed effects are directly attributable to the intervention.","plain":"We are not completely sure if the observed effects are directly attributable to the intervention.","note":"A useful phrase for hedging when there is insufficient evidence or conflicting data.","audio":"audio/6ab444fd7fabe7a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-quite-adding-up","text":"it's not quite adding up","kind":"chunk","form":"it's not quite adding up","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Something doesn't seem logical or consistent, suggesting a discrepancy.","example":"The numbers in the report are conflicting; it's not quite adding up.","plain":"The numbers in the report are conflicting; it doesn't seem logical.","note":"Used when something feels wrong or inconsistent, prompting further investigation.","audio":"audio/7e6776d61ee41e06.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-quite-as-simple","text":"It's not quite as simple","kind":"frame","form":"It's not quite as simple","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To suggest that a situation or idea is more complex than presented, implying a gentle disagreement.","example":"While the solution seems straightforward, it's not quite as simple when you consider the regulatory hurdles.","plain":"While the solution seems straightforward, it's more complex when you consider the regulatory hurdles.","note":"A polite way to introduce complexity or nuance, often used to push back on an oversimplified view.","audio":"audio/85aa10081bdb5071.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-not-unreasonable-to-suggest","text":"It's not unreasonable to suggest","kind":"chunk","form":"It's not unreasonable to suggest","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To propose an idea or conclusion that is logical and acceptable, often as a mild hedge.","example":"It's not unreasonable to suggest that environmental factors play a role in this phenomenon.","plain":"It is reasonable to suggest that environmental factors play a role in this phenomenon.","note":"A polite, understated way to introduce a plausible idea, often hedging slightly.","audio":"audio/e3389c8e061f1878.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-perfectly-understandable","text":"It's perfectly understandable","kind":"chunk","form":"It's perfectly understandable","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"States that a feeling or reaction is entirely reasonable and justified.","example":"After working overtime all week, it's perfectly understandable that you'd want to rest.","plain":"After working overtime all week, it is completely reasonable that you'd want to rest.","note":"A reassuring phrase, validating someone's feelings or actions.","audio":"audio/c385c17c687a2a0b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-quite-pleasant-out","text":"It's quite pleasant out","kind":"chunk","form":"It's quite pleasant out","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite, understated way to comment on good weather, often used to start a conversation.","example":"It's quite pleasant out today, isn't it? Much better than yesterday.","plain":"The weather is nice today, isn't it? Much better than yesterday.","note":"A common, gentle opener for small talk, suitable for any casual setting.","audio":"audio/03266b6e8c71e7d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-reassuring-to-know-that","text":"It's reassuring to know that","kind":"frame","form":"It's reassuring to know that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses comfort and a reduction of anxiety upon learning something positive or certain.","example":"After the system crash, it's reassuring to know that all our data was backed up.","plain":"After the system crash, I feel calmer knowing that all our data was backed up.","note":"A common phrase to express that information has reduced worry or uncertainty.","audio":"audio/7aa6bce28281b891.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-safe-to-say-that","text":"it's safe to say that","kind":"frame","form":"it's safe to say that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"introduces a conclusion that is generally accepted or not controversial","example":"After all that effort, it's safe to say that we've learned a great deal.","plain":"After all that effort, we can confidently say that we've learned a great deal.","note":"Used to state an obvious or widely agreed-upon conclusion. Diplomatic.","audio":"audio/3f23cdd918aa8439.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-tempting-to-conclude-that","text":"It's tempting to conclude that","kind":"frame","form":"It's tempting to conclude that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Acknowledges a seemingly obvious conclusion while subtly suggesting it might be an oversimplification or incorrect.","example":"It's tempting to conclude that the intervention was successful, but we lack a robust control group.","plain":"One might want to conclude the intervention was successful, but we lack a robust control group.","note":"Used to introduce a conclusion that appears logical but requires further scrutiny or qualification.","audio":"audio/4ad23f9f55e9cfad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-understandable-of-course","text":"It's understandable, of course","kind":"chunk","form":"It's understandable, of course","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a reaction or situation is completely logical and expected.","example":"After working so many extra hours, it's understandable, of course, that you'd be tired.","plain":"After working so many extra hours, it is completely logical that you would be tired.","note":"A polite and empathetic way to validate someone's feelings or situation.","audio":"audio/e2ffdea674857524.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-vital-to-ensure","text":"It's vital to ensure","kind":"frame","form":"It's vital to ensure","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the extreme importance of making certain that something happens or is true.","example":"It's vital to ensure that all team members understand their roles and responsibilities clearly.","plain":"It's extremely important to make sure every team member clearly knows their job and duties.","note":"A strong statement of necessity, often used when setting critical objectives.","audio":"audio/d63e8abf1b022013.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-vital-to-understand","text":"It's vital to understand","kind":"frame","form":"It's vital to understand","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Stresses the absolute necessity of comprehending a particular concept or fact.","example":"It's vital to understand the cultural nuances when expanding into new markets.","plain":"It's absolutely necessary to understand the cultural nuances when expanding into new markets.","note":"Conveys a strong sense of importance for comprehension.","audio":"audio/d5fbab57f3d668fe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-wonderful-to-know","text":"It's wonderful to know","kind":"frame","form":"It's wonderful to know","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses happiness and satisfaction upon learning positive information.","example":"After all the hard work, it's wonderful to know that the new system is fully operational.","plain":"After all the hard work, I am happy to know that the new system is fully operational.","note":"A warm and appreciative way to acknowledge good news or a positive development.","audio":"audio/6bc238cb4f95f215.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-all","text":"It's worth considering all","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth considering all","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest a comprehensive review of available options or factors.","example":"It's worth considering all the potential risks before making a final decision.","plain":"We should think about all the potential risks before making a final decision.","note":"Promotes thoroughness in decision-making.","audio":"audio/3c4b34391a107261.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-how","text":"It's worth considering how","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth considering how","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest exploring the methods or processes for a particular outcome.","example":"It's worth considering how we can better integrate these two systems.","plain":"We should think about how we can better integrate these two systems.","note":"Promotes analytical thought about implementation.","audio":"audio/28cf353eb9560d73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-the-fact","text":"It's worth considering the fact","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth considering the fact","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce a specific piece of information that should be taken into account.","example":"It's worth considering the fact that our main competitor just released a similar product.","plain":"We should think about the fact that our main competitor just released a similar product.","note":"A more formal way to introduce a relevant piece of information.","audio":"audio/40860acf95c6e9e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-the-impact","text":"It's worth considering the impact","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth considering the impact","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest evaluating the effects or consequences of something.","example":"It's worth considering the impact of this decision on our brand reputation.","plain":"We should think about the effect of this decision on our brand reputation.","note":"Promotes a thorough assessment of consequences.","audio":"audio/a4446b26c6607807.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-the-long-term","text":"It's worth considering the long-term","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth considering the long-term","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest evaluating the future consequences of a decision.","example":"It's worth considering the long-term impact of this policy on employee retention.","plain":"We should think about the future impact of this policy on employee retention.","note":"Encourages strategic, forward-thinking analysis.","audio":"audio/1c511d4903475e10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-the-possibility","text":"It's worth considering the possibility","kind":"chunk","form":"It's worth considering the possibility","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To suggest that a particular outcome or idea should be taken into account.","example":"It's worth considering the possibility that external factors influenced the observed results.","plain":"We should consider that external factors might have influenced the observed results.","note":"A polite way to introduce an alternative explanation or a potential scenario.","audio":"audio/e20098adb69afc5e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-considering-xs-perspective","text":"It's worth considering X's perspective","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth considering Dr. Smith's perspective","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular person's viewpoint is valuable and should be taken into account.","example":"It's worth considering Dr. Smith's perspective on the ethical implications of gene editing.","plain":"We should consider Dr. Smith's perspective on the ethical implications of gene editing.","note":"A polite and academic way to introduce an alternative or important viewpoint for discussion.","audio":"audio/60a94eff756f8d48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-emphasizing","text":"It's worth emphasizing","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth emphasizing","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To suggest that a particular point is important enough to be highlighted.","example":"It's worth emphasizing that our team achieved these results with limited resources.","plain":"It is important to highlight that our team achieved these results with limited resources.","note":"A polite but firm way to draw attention to a significant achievement or factor.","audio":"audio/2ab62e1e5fb804d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-emphasizing-that","text":"It's worth emphasizing that","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth emphasizing that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights a point as particularly important and deserving of attention.","example":"It's worth emphasizing that these findings are specific to the cultural context studied.","plain":"We should emphasize that these findings are specific to the cultural context studied.","note":"A common way to draw attention to a crucial detail.","audio":"audio/755feb5255bed05a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-noting-the-caveat","text":"It's worth noting the caveat","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth noting the caveat","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Advises paying attention to a specific limitation or condition.","example":"It's worth noting the caveat that our study relied solely on self-reported measures.","plain":"We should note the warning that our study relied solely on self-reported measures.","note":"A formal way to highlight an important limitation or condition.","audio":"audio/7081852b2bb75a58.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-reflecting-on","text":"It's worth reflecting on","kind":"frame","form":"It's worth reflecting on","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular idea or situation deserves careful thought and consideration.","example":"It's worth reflecting on how these historical events continue to shape contemporary society.","plain":"We should think carefully about how these historical events continue to shape contemporary society.","note":"Encourages deeper, contemplative thought about a topic.","audio":"audio/93ebd07cac6f0cb4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.its-worth-revisiting-the-idea","text":"It's worth revisiting the idea","kind":"collocation","form":"It's worth revisiting the idea","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To suggest that a previously discussed or considered concept should be looked at again.","example":"Given the new evidence, it's worth revisiting the idea of a causal link between these variables.","plain":"Given the new evidence, we should look again at the idea of a causal link between these variables.","note":"Used to suggest a re-evaluation of a concept in light of new information.","audio":"audio/0eac1e7bbfd22a0c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-a-moment-to-think","text":"Just a moment to think","kind":"chunk","form":"Just a moment to think","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Asks for a brief pause to organize one's ideas before speaking.","example":"Just a moment to think before I respond to that complex question.","plain":"Please give me a moment to organize my ideas before I respond to that complex question.","note":"A polite way to ask for a brief pause, showing thoughtfulness.","audio":"audio/5c3045b872e19714.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-a-quick-thought","text":"Just a quick thought","kind":"chunk","form":"Just a quick thought","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a brief idea or comment without taking up much time.","example":"Just a quick thought: have we considered the impact on our existing customer base?","plain":"I have a quick idea: have we considered the impact on our existing customer base?","note":"Informal and concise, used to offer a brief contribution without dominating the discussion.","audio":"audio/e0be00eecc2fc7ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-be-on-the-safe-side","text":"just to be on the safe side","kind":"chunk","form":"just to be on the safe side","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To take extra precautions to avoid potential problems.","example":"I packed an extra adapter, just to be on the safe side, in case one breaks.","plain":"I packed an extra adapter as a precaution.","note":"A common, informal way to explain a cautious action.","audio":"audio/ee29b58489ff1e46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-be-sure","text":"Just to be sure","kind":"chunk","form":"Just to be sure","level":"C2","situations":["meetings","explaining"],"meaning":"to reconfirm understanding or clarify a point to avoid mistakes.","example":"Just to be sure, you're confirming the meeting is at 10 AM tomorrow, right?","plain":"You're confirming the meeting is at 10 AM tomorrow, right?","note":"Used when seeking confirmation or double-checking information.","audio":"audio/169f08fa283fd3e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-give-you-an-idea","text":"Just to give you an idea","kind":"frame","form":"Just to give you an idea","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Prepares the listener for a descriptive detail that helps them visualize or understand the scale.","example":"Just to give you an idea, the queue for the concert stretched for over two blocks.","plain":"To help you understand, the queue for the concert stretched for over two blocks.","note":"Used to provide a vivid detail or example.","audio":"audio/8663bbb90f4dc839.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-make-sure-im-following","text":"Just to make sure I'm following","kind":"frame","form":"Just to make sure I'm following","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates a need to confirm understanding of the discussion so far.","example":"Just to make sure I'm following, are we still on track for the original deadline?","plain":"To confirm I understand, is the original deadline still valid?","note":"A polite and collaborative way to seek clarification during a conversation.","audio":"audio/6007653c46176cba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-pick-up-on-that","text":"just to pick up on that","kind":"frame","form":"Just to pick up on that","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introduces a comment that builds upon or refers back to something previously said.","example":"Just to pick up on that point about the budget, I think we have some flexibility.","plain":"Referring back to what you just said, I have a related comment.","note":"A smooth way to link one's contribution to a previous speaker's point. Professional.","audio":"audio/e69ba71a5cfa74d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.just-to-verify","text":"Just to verify","kind":"chunk","form":"Just to verify","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To confirm information or understanding, often before proceeding.","example":"Just to verify, the deadline for submitting the proposal is Friday, correct?","plain":"To confirm, the proposal is due on Friday, right?","note":"A polite way to double-check facts or instructions.","audio":"audio/44917a3745d5dd0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.keep-a-low-profile","text":"keep a low profile","kind":"collocation","form":"keep a low profile","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To avoid attracting attention or publicity.","example":"When traveling in crowded areas, it's sometimes best to keep a low profile.","plain":"When traveling in crowded areas, it's sometimes best to avoid attracting attention.","note":"Suggests discretion or caution, often for safety or privacy.","audio":"audio/d38118f9ccaef3c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.keep-abreast-of","text":"keep abreast of","kind":"collocation","form":"keep abreast of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To stay informed about the latest news or developments.","example":"It's important to keep abreast of the local travel advisories.","plain":"It's important to stay informed about the local travel advisories.","note":"Slightly formal, suggests staying current with information.","audio":"audio/82645ba679b06add.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.keep-us-on-track","text":"keep us on track","kind":"chunk","form":"keep us on track","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To ensure the discussion stays focused on the main topic.","example":"I'll try to keep us on track so we can cover all agenda items within the hour.","plain":"I'll try to keep us focused so we can cover all agenda items within the hour.","note":"A polite way for the chair to manage time and focus.","audio":"audio/869e2b746cddd573.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lay-out-the-ground-rules","text":"lay out the ground rules","kind":"collocation","form":"lay out the ground rules","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To explain the basic rules or principles for how something will be done.","example":"Before we begin, I'd like to lay out the ground rules for our discussion today.","plain":"Before we begin, I'd like to explain the basic rules for our discussion today.","note":"Used by a facilitator to ensure a productive and respectful discussion.","audio":"audio/fc93dad289f756e1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lend-weight-to","text":"lend weight to","kind":"collocation","form":"lend weight to","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To provide support or credibility to an argument, idea, or claim.","example":"The recent market research findings certainly lend weight to our proposal for product diversification.","plain":"The latest market research definitely supports our idea to offer more varied products.","note":"A formal but common expression, suggesting that evidence strengthens an argument.","audio":"audio/0eaff2b616c6575b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-elaborate-on-that","text":"Let me elaborate on that","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me elaborate on that","level":"C2","situations":["academic","presenting"],"meaning":"To offer to provide more details or a fuller explanation of a previous statement.","example":"That's a good question; let me elaborate on that point regarding the data collection process.","plain":"That's a good question; I'll give more details about the data collection process.","note":"A polite and common way to offer further explanation.","audio":"audio/424436061ab49180.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-just-clarify","text":"let me just clarify","kind":"frame","form":"Let me just clarify","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Introduces an attempt to make something clearer or correct a misunderstanding.","example":"Before we proceed, let me just clarify the exact terms of the agreement.","plain":"I want to make sure this point is clear and understood correctly.","note":"A common phrase for ensuring accuracy and avoiding misinterpretation. Professional.","audio":"audio/6c39cf529d670e2f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-just-gather-my-thoughts","text":"Let me just gather my thoughts","kind":"chunk","form":"let me just gather my thoughts","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To ask for a brief pause to organize one's ideas before speaking.","example":"That's a complex question; let me just gather my thoughts before I answer.","plain":"That's a complex question; I need a moment to organize my ideas before I answer.","note":"A natural way to buy time to formulate a coherent response, especially to a difficult question.","audio":"audio/44bc6da218e6c547.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-phrase-it-differently","text":"Let me phrase it differently","kind":"frame","form":"Let me phrase it differently","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to rephrase something for clarity after an initial explanation wasn't clear.","example":"Let me phrase it differently: our primary goal is to enhance user experience, not just add features.","plain":"To explain it differently, our primary goal is to enhance user experience, not just add features.","note":"Useful for offering an alternative explanation.","audio":"audio/f76d24f6ab2be6aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-put-that-differently","text":"Let me put that differently","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me put that differently","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To rephrase a statement to make it clearer or more accurate.","example":"Let me put that differently: our focus is on long-term growth, not just quarterly gains.","plain":"I will say that in another way: our focus is on long-term growth, not just quarterly gains.","note":"Similar to \"let me rephrase that,\" but often implies a more significant change in wording.","audio":"audio/a608cdf4b3011d7a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-rephrase","text":"let me rephrase","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me rephrase","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to say something again using different words, often for clarity.","example":"Let me rephrase: the project timeline is flexible, but the core deliverables are not.","plain":"The project timeline is flexible, but the core deliverables are not.","note":"Polite and direct way to indicate you're going to try explaining again.","audio":"audio/5589cf2f22482e36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-rephrase-that-slightly","text":"Let me rephrase that slightly","kind":"frame","form":"Let me rephrase that slightly","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to express something again in a slightly different way for better clarity.","example":"Let me rephrase that slightly: our goal is to optimize, not just automate.","plain":"Let me say that again in a slightly different way: our goal is to optimize, not just automate.","note":"A polite way to correct or improve an earlier statement.","audio":"audio/9dd73293d3957a9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-try-that-again","text":"Let me try that again","kind":"chunk","form":"Let me try that again","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates a desire to re-explain something after an initial attempt was unclear or incorrect.","example":"Let me try that again, focusing on the practical applications rather than the theory.","plain":"I'll try to explain that once more, focusing on the practical applications rather than the theory.","note":"A straightforward way to signal a re-attempt at explanation.","audio":"audio/9cc7cde6ac32623a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.let-me-try-to-explain","text":"Let me try to explain","kind":"frame","form":"Let me try to explain","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Offers to provide an explanation, often after a question or perceived confusion.","example":"Let me try to explain the rationale behind our decision to pivot.","plain":"I will try to explain the reasons behind our decision to pivot.","note":"A straightforward way to introduce an explanation.","audio":"audio/a1dc9a95d8989089.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-assume-for-a-moment","text":"Let's assume for a moment","kind":"frame","form":"Let's assume for a moment","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a hypothetical scenario for discussion or analysis.","example":"Let's assume for a moment that we secure the additional funding; how would that change our plans?","plain":"If we imagine we secure the additional funding, how would that change our plans?","note":"Helpful for exploring possibilities and contingency planning. Professional.","audio":"audio/aaefb60099d35a55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-briefly-touch-on","text":"Let's briefly touch on","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's briefly touch on","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To quickly mention a topic without going into extensive detail.","example":"Let's briefly touch on the feedback we received from the pilot group.","plain":"Let's quickly mention the feedback we received from the pilot group.","note":"A common and polite way to introduce a minor point or a topic that won't be fully explored.","audio":"audio/dd3d8222a7bd62ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-confirm-the-actions","text":"Let's confirm the actions","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's confirm the actions","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request to review and finalize the specific tasks or steps to be taken.","example":"Before we close, let's confirm the actions and who is responsible for each.","plain":"Before we close, let's review the tasks and who is responsible for each.","note":"Essential for ensuring accountability and clear next steps after a meeting.","audio":"audio/5b0eda3c358ef450.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-consider-the-implications","text":"Let's consider the implications","kind":"frame","form":"Let's consider the implications","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To invite a discussion about the potential consequences of a decision or action.","example":"Let's consider the implications of delaying this launch for another month.","plain":"We should think about the consequences of delaying this launch for another month.","note":"Encourages forward-thinking and risk assessment.","audio":"audio/f9b88095b4452049.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-consider-this-from","text":"Let's consider this from","kind":"frame","form":"Let's consider this from","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Invites others to view a situation or problem from a different perspective.","example":"Let's consider this from the client's perspective; what are their primary needs and expectations?","plain":"Let's think about this from the client's point of view; what do they mainly need and expect?","note":"Encourages empathy and a broader understanding of the situation.","audio":"audio/e22e8c8f9658fc25.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-delve-into","text":"Let's delve into","kind":"frame","form":"Let's delve into","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Suggests a deeper exploration or detailed examination of a topic.","example":"Let's delve into the specifics of the new regulatory framework.","plain":"Let's examine the specifics of the new regulatory framework in detail.","note":"Implies a thorough and in-depth discussion.","audio":"audio/49065a388eff64fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-dive-right-in","text":"Let's dive right in","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's dive right in","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"An informal way to suggest starting the discussion immediately without delay.","example":"We have a lot to cover, so let's dive right in with the first item.","plain":"We have much to discuss, so let's start immediately with the first item.","note":"Informal and energetic, often used by the chair to encourage prompt engagement.","audio":"audio/37b657d21858cb99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-ensure-were-aligned","text":"Let's ensure we're aligned","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's ensure we're aligned","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request to confirm that everyone shares the same understanding and goals.","example":"Before we proceed, let's ensure we're aligned on the project's ultimate vision.","plain":"Before we proceed, let's make sure we all agree on the project's ultimate vision.","note":"Professional and collaborative, used to build consensus and prevent future conflicts.","audio":"audio/f5c3ffc717b94d30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-explore-alternatives","text":"Let's explore alternatives","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's explore alternatives","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To suggest looking for other options or solutions when the current ones are not working.","example":"If that deadline is too tight, let's explore alternatives for phasing the project rollout.","plain":"If that deadline is too tight, let's look for other options for phasing the project rollout.","note":"Collaborative and constructive, indicating a willingness to find a workable solution.","audio":"audio/f03b89e2f37b13ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-explore-how-we-can","text":"Let's explore how we can","kind":"frame","form":"Let's explore how we can","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Invites collaborative investigation into methods for achieving a goal.","example":"Let's explore how we can bridge the gap between our current capabilities and future market demands.","plain":"Let's investigate ways to connect what we can do now with what the market will need later.","note":"Encourages creative problem-solving and strategic thinking.","audio":"audio/19c7bf1283db9479.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-explore-that-further","text":"Let's explore that further","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's explore that further","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A suggestion to delve deeper into a particular idea or topic.","example":"That's an interesting concept; let's explore that further in a separate session.","plain":"That's an interesting concept; let's discuss it in more detail in a separate session.","note":"Polite and collaborative, used to acknowledge an idea while deferring detailed discussion.","audio":"audio/29418df7ee41fdd4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-explore-the-ramifications","text":"Let's explore the ramifications","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's explore the ramifications","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Invites the audience to think about the potential negative consequences or effects of something.","example":"Let's explore the ramifications of these policy changes for our international operations.","plain":"Let's think about the potential negative effects of these policy changes for our international operations.","note":"Slightly more formal, often used when discussing potential negative outcomes.","audio":"audio/93080afee9c2fc52.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-finalize-the-actions","text":"let's finalize the actions","kind":"frame","form":"let's finalize the actions","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To confirm and agree upon the specific tasks to be done.","example":"Before we adjourn, let's finalize the actions and assign owners for each.","plain":"Before we adjourn, let's confirm the tasks and assign owners for each.","note":"Action-oriented phrase for concluding a meeting.","audio":"audio/92524634fe486736.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-get-started","text":"Let's get started","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's get started","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A common way to begin a meeting or activity, signaling it's time to begin.","example":"Alright team, let's get started, we have a packed agenda today.","plain":"Okay team, we should begin now, as we have a lot to cover.","note":"Informal and direct, often used by the meeting chair to signal the start.","audio":"audio/ae62593838a614a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-get-your-input-on","text":"Let's get your input on","kind":"frame","form":"Let's get your input on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"An invitation for specific individuals or the group to share their opinions or ideas.","example":"Now, let's get your input on the proposed changes to the project scope.","plain":"Now, please share your opinions on the proposed changes to the project scope.","note":"Inclusive and direct, used to actively solicit contributions from attendees.","audio":"audio/889ad68ed65a3455.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-keep-an-eye-on","text":"Let's keep an eye on","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's keep an eye on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A suggestion to monitor or pay close attention to a particular issue or development.","example":"Let's keep an eye on the competitor's new product launch and its market impact.","plain":"Let's monitor the competitor's new product launch and its market impact.","note":"Practical and proactive, used to assign ongoing vigilance for a situation.","audio":"audio/0da469dfebde94e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-keep-in-touch","text":"let's keep in touch","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's keep in touch","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A suggestion to maintain communication with someone.","example":"It was great seeing you, let's keep in touch!","plain":"It was great seeing you, let's stay in contact!","note":"A common, friendly farewell, especially when you don't have immediate plans to meet again.","audio":"audio/19b88580a047c8fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-keep-it-productive","text":"Let's keep it productive","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's keep it productive","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request to maintain focus on achieving goals and avoid unproductive discussions.","example":"While discussing challenges, let's keep it productive and focus on solutions.","plain":"While discussing challenges, let's stay productive and focus on solutions.","note":"Encouraging and practical, used to steer discussions towards constructive outcomes.","audio":"audio/de96eecaccd99120.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-keep-this-concise","text":"Let's keep this concise","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's keep this concise","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request for speakers to be brief and to the point.","example":"Given our limited time, let's keep this concise and focus on key decisions.","plain":"Since we have little time, let's be brief and focus on key decisions.","note":"Direct and practical, used to encourage efficiency in discussions.","audio":"audio/d961e212efc2aa33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-kick-off-by","text":"Let's kick off by","kind":"frame","form":"Let's kick off by","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"An informal but professional way to start a presentation or a new section.","example":"Let's kick off by reviewing the market research findings from last quarter.","plain":"We'll start by reviewing the market research findings from last quarter.","note":"Slightly informal, but perfectly acceptable in a professional setting, especially in British English.","audio":"audio/675548ce540a36ab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-look-at-this","text":"Let's look at this","kind":"frame","form":"Let's look at this","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To invite others to consider a situation or problem from a particular angle.","example":"Let's look at this from the client's perspective to understand their hesitation.","plain":"We should consider this from the client's perspective to understand their hesitation.","note":"Encourages a shared analytical approach; inclusive.","audio":"audio/6390861a76535942.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-make-a-call","text":"let's make a call","kind":"chunk","form":"let's make a call","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Let's make a decision.","example":"We've discussed this enough; let's make a call on the best way forward.","plain":"We've discussed this enough; let's make a decision on the best way forward.","note":"Informal but common, used to prompt a decision after discussion.","audio":"audio/cae398017e17825f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-make-it-happen","text":"let's make it happen","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's make it happen","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An enthusiastic agreement to proceed with a plan or idea.","example":"You want to go hiking next month? Sounds great, let's make it happen!","plain":"You want to go hiking next month? Sounds great, let's do it!","note":"Expresses eagerness and commitment to turn a plan into reality. Informal and positive.","audio":"audio/92b8fd382a698853.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-make-sure-we-capture","text":"Let's make sure we capture","kind":"frame","form":"Let's make sure we capture","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request to ensure that important information, ideas, or decisions are recorded.","example":"Let's make sure we capture all the action items and assign owners before we finish.","plain":"Let's ensure we record all the action items and assign owners before we finish.","note":"Professional and practical, used to ensure thorough documentation of meeting outcomes.","audio":"audio/545af2fee8e5e87a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-move-forward-to","text":"Let's move forward to","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's move forward to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To transition to the next topic or stage of the presentation.","example":"Let's move forward to the implementation phase of our new strategy.","plain":"Now we will discuss the implementation phase of our new strategy.","note":"A common and direct way to signal progression in the presentation's agenda.","audio":"audio/1df650406d3d50eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-move-forward-with-this","text":"let's move forward with this","kind":"chunk","form":"let's move forward with this","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Let's proceed with this plan or decision.","example":"We've discussed it thoroughly, so let's move forward with this approach.","plain":"We've discussed it thoroughly, so let's proceed with this approach.","note":"Used to signal the end of discussion and the start of action.","audio":"audio/05776bff0ec92a59.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-not-get-bogged-down","text":"Let's not get bogged down","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's not get bogged down","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A suggestion to avoid getting stuck on minor details and to keep the discussion moving.","example":"Let's not get bogged down in the minutiae; we need to focus on the strategic overview.","plain":"Let's not get stuck on small details; we need to focus on the strategic overview.","note":"Informal but effective, used to maintain momentum and focus on important issues.","audio":"audio/6ccdab09dc518401.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-put-a-date-in","text":"Let's put a date in","kind":"collocation","form":"put a date in","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggests scheduling a specific time for an event or meeting.","example":"We should really put a date in the diary for that dinner we discussed.","plain":"We should schedule that dinner we discussed.","note":"Common in British English, often followed by 'the diary' or 'the calendar'.","audio":"audio/40ad5ef6aef2f9f0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-reconvene-next-week","text":"let's reconvene next week","kind":"chunk","form":"let's reconvene next week","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggesting that the group or individuals meet again the following week.","example":"We've made good progress, so let's reconvene next week to finalize the plan.","plain":"We've done well, so let's meet again next week to finish the plan.","note":"A formal and efficient way to schedule the next meeting.","audio":"audio/985a004a11fbc360.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-reconvene-on","text":"Let's reconvene on","kind":"frame","form":"Let's reconvene on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A suggestion to meet again at a specified future date or time.","example":"We still have some open items, so let's reconvene on Friday to finalize them.","plain":"We still have some open items, so let's meet again on Friday to finalize them.","note":"Professional and efficient, used to schedule follow-up meetings.","audio":"audio/0d2dd77873ea6842.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-reschedule","text":"let's reschedule","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's reschedule","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A suggestion to arrange a new time for a cancelled or postponed meeting.","example":"I can't make it today, let's reschedule for next week.","plain":"I can't make it today, let's arrange a new time for next week.","note":"A common and practical suggestion when a plan needs to be changed.","audio":"audio/c29000972f35176c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-say-for-example","text":"Let's say for example","kind":"frame","form":"Let's say for example","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to introduce a hypothetical situation or specific instance as an example.","example":"Let's say for example you're managing a project with a very tight deadline.","plain":"Imagine you're managing a project with a very tight deadline.","note":"A common and conversational way to introduce a hypothetical example.","audio":"audio/375515b134d3519c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-set-a-deadline","text":"Let's set a deadline","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's set a deadline","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A suggestion to establish a specific date or time for a task to be completed.","example":"For this action item, let's set a deadline of next Tuesday to ensure timely completion.","plain":"For this task, let's decide on a completion date of next Tuesday.","note":"Direct and practical, used to ensure tasks are completed within a timeframe.","audio":"audio/6af237023a643deb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-stay-in-touch","text":"Let's stay in touch","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's stay in touch","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A friendly suggestion to maintain communication in the future.","example":"Let's stay in touch and not let so much time pass.","plain":"Let's keep communicating and not let so much time pass.","note":"A common and warm way to end a conversation, encouraging future contact.","audio":"audio/f3b74592a3eefb5a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-switch-to-a-phone-call","text":"let's switch to a phone call","kind":"chunk","form":"let's switch to a phone call","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggesting moving from a video call to an audio-only call due to technical issues.","example":"Given the persistent video issues, perhaps let's switch to a phone call instead.","plain":"The video problems are ongoing, so maybe we should just use the phone.","note":"A practical suggestion when video quality is poor, implying better audio.","audio":"audio/5568149a06ba60ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-table-this-discussion","text":"Let's table this discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's table this discussion","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To postpone a discussion until a later time or meeting.","example":"Given the complexity, let's table this discussion until we have more data.","plain":"Given the complexity, let's postpone this discussion until we have more data.","note":"Formal and professional, common in American English to mean 'postpone'.","audio":"audio/5d4326f403fa5fcd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-take-a-moment-to","text":"Let's take a moment to","kind":"frame","form":"Let's take a moment to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To suggest a brief pause for reflection or to perform a quick action.","example":"Let's take a moment to review the key performance indicators on this slide.","plain":"We should briefly review the key performance indicators on this slide.","note":"A collaborative way to guide the audience through a short, focused activity.","audio":"audio/2f4a4c1b6ef97649.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-take-that-offline","text":"Let's take that offline","kind":"chunk","form":"Let's take that offline","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To suggest discussing a specific topic outside of the current meeting, usually with relevant individuals.","example":"This is a detailed technical discussion, so let's take that offline with the engineering team.","plain":"This is a detailed technical discussion, so let's discuss it separately with the engineering team.","note":"Professional and efficient, used to keep meetings focused on broader issues.","audio":"audio/08191e7280477bb9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-touch-base-again-soon","text":"let's touch base again soon","kind":"collocation","form":"Let's touch base again soon","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggests having another brief contact or meeting in the near future.","example":"We've made good progress, so let's touch base again soon to review.","plain":"Let's talk again briefly sometime in the near future.","note":"A common professional phrase for scheduling a follow-up. Friendly and proactive.","audio":"audio/1f78d3f52c29daaa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-try-and-carve-out","text":"Let's try and carve out","kind":"collocation","form":"let's try and carve out","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Suggests making a special effort to find time for something despite a busy schedule.","example":"We're both so busy, but let's try and carve out some time to meet.","plain":"We're both so busy, but let's try to find some time to meet.","note":"Implies that time is scarce and requires effort to find, often used with 'time'.","audio":"audio/72dc31fb409d6169.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lets-try-to-keep-to","text":"Let's try to keep to","kind":"frame","form":"Let's try to keep to","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A request to adhere to a schedule, topic, or set of guidelines.","example":"Let's try to keep to our allotted time for each agenda item.","plain":"Let's try to stay within our allotted time for each agenda item.","note":"Polite and practical, used to manage time and maintain focus.","audio":"audio/cf5ca8166593f9ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.long-term-benefits","text":"long-term benefits","kind":"collocation","form":"long-term benefits","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Advantages or positive outcomes that will be realized over an extended period.","example":"We should prioritize investments that offer long-term benefits, even if initial costs are higher.","plain":"We should focus on investments that will help us for a long time, even if they cost more at first.","note":"Emphasizes future gains over immediate returns, often in strategic planning.","audio":"audio/5d1cc50ea9e60549.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.looking-forward-to-hearing-from-you","text":"looking forward to hearing from you","kind":"chunk","form":"looking forward to hearing from you","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Expresses anticipation for a response or update from the interviewer.","example":"I'm looking forward to hearing from you regarding the next steps in the process.","plain":"I anticipate receiving an update from you regarding the next steps in the process.","note":"A standard and polite closing statement for candidates after an interview.","audio":"audio/a996374df77c0ac8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.looking-forward-to-our-next-chat","text":"looking forward to our next chat","kind":"chunk","form":"Looking forward to our next chat","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses anticipation for a future conversation with the other person.","example":"This was helpful; looking forward to our next chat about the project.","plain":"I am eager to speak with you again soon.","note":"A friendly and positive closing, suitable for ongoing professional or personal relationships.","audio":"audio/445fa8a3aae38230.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lose-track-of","text":"lose track of","kind":"collocation","form":"lose track of","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To no longer know where something is or what is happening.","example":"I often lose track of time when I'm exploring a new city.","plain":"I often forget what time it is when I'm exploring a new city.","note":"Common for describing a lapse in awareness.","audio":"audio/4fd39231574fca11.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.lovely-weather-for-it","text":"Lovely weather for it","kind":"chunk","form":"Lovely weather for it","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to comment on good weather, often when doing an outdoor activity.","example":"Lovely weather for it, isn't it? Perfect for a stroll in the park.","plain":"The weather is nice, isn't it? It's perfect for a stroll in the park.","note":"Common British English opener, often used when both parties are experiencing the same weather.","audio":"audio/d23e841d197778d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.make-a-mental-note","text":"make a mental note","kind":"collocation","form":"make a mental note","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To remember something important without writing it down.","example":"I'll make a mental note to bring a warmer jacket next time we visit this region.","plain":"I'll remember to bring a warmer jacket next time we visit this region.","note":"Common for remembering small but important details.","audio":"audio/67ac06ac10dfcdd4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.make-arrangements","text":"make arrangements","kind":"collocation","form":"make arrangements","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To plan and prepare for something.","example":"We need to make arrangements for our accommodation in advance.","plain":"We need to plan our accommodation in advance.","note":"Common for planning events or travel.","audio":"audio/07d534a9fc204e97.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.make-sense-of","text":"make sense of","kind":"collocation","form":"make sense of","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to understand something that is confusing or difficult.","example":"It took me a while to make sense of the new tax regulations, but I think I've got it now.","plain":"It took me a while to understand the new tax regulations, but I think I've got it now.","note":"Often used when someone is struggling with comprehension.","audio":"audio/5e69f616f22c1623.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.may-i-just-jump-in-here","text":"may I just jump in here","kind":"frame","form":"May I just jump in here","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for permission to interrupt and contribute to the conversation.","example":"May I just jump in here with a quick point about the budget?","plain":"Could I interrupt briefly to add something?","note":"Polite and slightly informal, used to interject without being rude. Common in meetings.","audio":"audio/ba16ee946b5c4b7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.might-be-worth-exploring","text":"might be worth exploring","kind":"frame","form":"might be worth exploring","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Suggesting that an idea or option should be investigated further.","example":"It might be worth exploring alternative marketing channels for this product.","plain":"We should probably investigate alternative marketing channels for this product.","note":"Tentative and open-ended, encouraging further thought and research.","audio":"audio/0b7e7b9364e5883e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.might-want-to-revisit","text":"might want to revisit","kind":"frame","form":"might want to revisit","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To suggest going back to reconsider or discuss something again.","example":"We might want to revisit the budget allocations before finalizing the plan.","plain":"We should reconsider the budget allocations before finalizing the plan.","note":"A gentle suggestion to re-evaluate a decision or topic.","audio":"audio/1e5d32bd68542701.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.miss-out-on","text":"miss out on","kind":"collocation","form":"miss out on","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To lose an opportunity to experience something good.","example":"I don't want to miss out on seeing the sunset from the castle.","plain":"I want to see the sunset from the castle and not miss it.","note":"Common for expressing regret or a desire not to miss an experience.","audio":"audio/c3614bb31d759a63.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.monitor-the-progress","text":"monitor the progress","kind":"collocation","form":"monitor the progress","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To regularly check how well something is developing.","example":"We need to monitor the progress of the pilot project closely over the next few weeks.","plain":"We need to regularly check how well the pilot project is developing over the next few weeks.","note":"Professional phrase for overseeing development.","audio":"audio/e9276dd62559c7ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.move-on-swiftly","text":"move on swiftly","kind":"collocation","form":"move on swiftly","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To proceed quickly to the next item.","example":"Given the time, I suggest we move on swiftly to the next agenda item.","plain":"Given the time, I suggest we proceed quickly to the next agenda item.","note":"Used by the chair to maintain pace. Professional and efficient.","audio":"audio/9157b3cb2f7e571a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.moving-forward-well","text":"Moving forward, we'll","kind":"frame","form":"Moving forward, we'll","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates a transition to the next topic or stage in the presentation.","example":"Moving forward, we'll examine the proposed solutions and their potential impact.","plain":"Next, we'll examine the proposed solutions and their potential impact.","note":"A standard signposting phrase, useful for guiding the audience through the agenda.","audio":"audio/06811004687171f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.moving-swiftly-on","text":"Moving swiftly on","kind":"chunk","form":"Moving swiftly on","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To indicate a quick transition to the next topic, often to save time.","example":"Moving swiftly on, let's look at the projected budget for the next fiscal year.","plain":"Let's quickly move to the projected budget for the next fiscal year.","note":"Common in British English, signals a desire to keep the pace of the presentation moving.","audio":"audio/a7ab4ebdb964b9b4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.muffled-voice","text":"muffled voice","kind":"collocation","form":"muffled voice","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Speech that is unclear and difficult to hear, as if blocked.","example":"I'm afraid I'm hearing a muffled voice; could you speak closer to the microphone?","plain":"Your speech is unclear; please speak closer to the microphone.","note":"A polite way to describe unclear speech, suggesting a microphone adjustment.","audio":"audio/d16840d96f7a4d24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-apologies-i-misspoke","text":"My apologies, I misspoke","kind":"chunk","form":"My apologies, I misspoke","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely correct oneself after saying something incorrectly or unintentionally.","example":"My apologies, I misspoke; the deadline is next Tuesday, not this Friday.","plain":"I'm sorry, I said that incorrectly; the deadline is next Tuesday, not this Friday.","note":"A polite and professional way to acknowledge and correct a verbal error.","audio":"audio/c5599950118c09d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-apologies-im-not-available","text":"My apologies, I'm not available","kind":"chunk","form":"My apologies, I'm not available","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and slightly formal way to express regret for unavailability.","example":"My apologies, I'm not available on that particular evening.","plain":"I'm sorry, I'm not free on that particular evening.","note":"A courteous way to decline, often used in more formal or professional contexts.","audio":"audio/1c456f85591c351b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-apologies-that-came-out-wrong","text":"My apologies, that came out wrong","kind":"chunk","form":"My apologies, that came out wrong","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Expresses regret for an unintended or poorly worded statement.","example":"My apologies, that came out wrong; I didn't mean to suggest any blame.","plain":"I'm sorry, I said that badly; I didn't mean to suggest any blame.","note":"A polite and immediate apology for a verbal slip.","audio":"audio/14945eab44e61528.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-audio-is-cutting-out","text":"my audio is cutting out","kind":"collocation","form":"my audio is cutting out","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Informs the other person that one's own voice is intermittently disappearing.","example":"Please bear with me if my audio is cutting out; I'm having some technical issues.","plain":"My sound is intermittently stopping, so you might miss some words.","note":"Useful for self-diagnosis of connection problems. Professional and clear.","audio":"audio/c3698725109e1327.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-concern-is-that","text":"My concern is that","kind":"frame","form":"My concern is that","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to state a specific worry or apprehension about a situation or proposed action.","example":"My concern is that the new timeline might not be realistic given our current resources.","plain":"I'm worried that the new timeline might not be realistic given our current resources.","note":"Directly expresses a worry in a professional and clear manner, inviting discussion.","audio":"audio/5a1c7db1182ac3c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-condolences","text":"My condolences","kind":"chunk","form":"My condolences","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses formal sympathy for someone's loss or grief.","example":"Please accept my condolences on the passing of your grandmother.","plain":"Please accept my sympathy on the passing of your grandmother.","note":"A formal and traditional expression of sympathy for a death.","audio":"audio/0a807aa8290e5549.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-core-competencies-include","text":"my core competencies include","kind":"frame","form":"My core competencies include","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to list one's fundamental and most important skills or abilities.","example":"My core competencies include strategic planning, team leadership, and stakeholder management.","plain":"My fundamental and most important skills include strategic planning, team leadership, and stakeholder management.","note":"Formal and professional, ideal for summarizing key qualifications.","audio":"audio/8f1a0f1688fb9afd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-greatest-strength-lies-in","text":"my greatest strength lies in","kind":"frame","form":"My greatest strength lies in","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to introduce and highlight one's most significant positive attribute or skill.","example":"My greatest strength lies in my ability to simplify complex technical information.","plain":"My most significant positive attribute is my ability to simplify complex technical information.","note":"A formal and direct way to state a key strength during an interview.","audio":"audio/351611cecbe62bf9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-initial-thought-is-to","text":"my initial thought is to","kind":"frame","form":"My initial thought is to","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to share a first idea or preliminary plan.","example":"My initial thought is to prioritize the critical bugs and then address the minor ones.","plain":"My first idea is to prioritize the critical bugs and then address the minor ones.","note":"Indicates a starting point for discussion, not a final decision.","audio":"audio/647ae2e446425998.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-key-takeaway-was","text":"my key takeaway was","kind":"frame","form":"My key takeaway was","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to state the most important lesson or conclusion drawn from an experience or discussion.","example":"My key takeaway was the importance of proactive communication in managing client expectations.","plain":"The most important lesson I learned was the importance of proactive communication in managing client expectations.","note":"A common phrase in professional settings for summarizing learning points.","audio":"audio/fd6a130f04257277.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-long-term-goal-is","text":"my long-term goal is","kind":"frame","form":"My long-term goal is","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to state one's primary objective or aspiration for the distant future.","example":"My long-term goal is to become a recognized expert in sustainable energy solutions.","plain":"My primary objective for the distant future is to become a recognized expert in sustainable energy solutions.","note":"Shows ambition and strategic thinking. Important for career planning discussions.","audio":"audio/fb130b466609c531.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-only-reservation","text":"my only reservation","kind":"collocation","form":"my only reservation","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"The single doubt or concern one has about something.","example":"My only reservation is whether we have the bandwidth to execute this plan effectively.","plain":"My only concern is whether we have the bandwidth to execute this plan effectively.","note":"Polite way to express a specific concern without outright rejecting an idea.","audio":"audio/1229c6a55296a3f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-primary-concern-is","text":"My primary concern is","kind":"frame","form":"My primary concern is","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"States the most important worry or issue that you have regarding a situation.","example":"My primary concern is ensuring that any new policy doesn't negatively impact employee well-being.","plain":"My main worry is to make sure that no new policy harms how well employees are doing.","note":"Clearly articulates a key reservation, often used to guide discussion or decision-making.","audio":"audio/4e77aef156dcf6fc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-primary-motivation-is","text":"my primary motivation is","kind":"frame","form":"My primary motivation is","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to state the main reason or driving force behind one's actions or career choices.","example":"My primary motivation is to work for a company that makes a positive societal impact.","plain":"The main reason I do things is to work for a company that makes a positive societal impact.","note":"Helps candidates articulate their core drivers and align them with the company's mission.","audio":"audio/2319b1f83f2a14fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-screen-is-frozen","text":"My screen is frozen","kind":"chunk","form":"My screen is frozen","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describes a situation where the visual display on the speaker's device is stuck.","example":"My screen is frozen, so I can't see the presentation slides anymore.","plain":"The image on my computer is stuck, and I can't see the slides.","note":"A common and direct way to describe a visual technical issue.","audio":"audio/39026df84d9c4e9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-understanding-of-the-company","text":"my understanding of the company","kind":"frame","form":"My understanding of the company","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to state one's interpretation of the organization's mission, values, or operations.","example":"My understanding of the company is that it prioritizes innovation and customer satisfaction.","plain":"My interpretation of the company is that it prioritizes innovation and customer satisfaction.","note":"Allows a candidate to demonstrate research and alignment with the company.","audio":"audio/d7076beb38b267de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.my-understanding-of-the-role","text":"my understanding of the role","kind":"frame","form":"My understanding of the role","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to state one's interpretation of the job responsibilities and expectations.","example":"My understanding of the role is that it involves leading a small team and managing client accounts.","plain":"My interpretation of the job responsibilities is that it involves leading a small team and managing client accounts.","note":"Allows a candidate to confirm their grasp of the position's requirements.","audio":"audio/02b1c44c4fce1a47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.navigate-complex-situations","text":"navigate complex situations","kind":"collocation","form":"navigate complex situations","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To successfully manage and find solutions in difficult or intricate circumstances.","example":"My previous role required me to navigate complex situations involving multiple stakeholders.","plain":"My previous role required me to successfully manage difficult situations involving multiple stakeholders.","note":"Suggests problem-solving skills and adaptability. Useful for describing challenging past experiences.","audio":"audio/9d755c4db10f8c29.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.needs-a-bit-of-tweaking","text":"needs a bit of tweaking","kind":"chunk","form":"needs a bit of tweaking","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Requires some small adjustments or fine-tuning to improve it.","example":"The presentation is good, but it needs a bit of tweaking to fit the new branding guidelines.","plain":"The presentation is good, but it needs some small adjustments to fit the new branding guidelines.","note":"Informal and gentle way to suggest minor improvements.","audio":"audio/d634424c8881e45d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.next-on-the-agenda","text":"Next on the agenda","kind":"chunk","form":"Next on the agenda","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to introduce the subsequent item or topic that will be discussed.","example":"Next on the agenda, we'll discuss the budget allocation for the upcoming fiscal year.","plain":"The next topic we'll discuss is the budget allocation for the upcoming fiscal year.","note":"Clear and direct, often used when following a structured agenda.","audio":"audio/b4f879ac27b7585e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.not-entirely-convinced","text":"not entirely convinced","kind":"chunk","form":"not entirely convinced","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To have some doubts or reservations about something, not fully believing it.","example":"I'm not entirely convinced that the new policy will be effective in practice.","plain":"I have some doubts that the new policy will be effective in practice.","note":"A polite and understated way to express disagreement or skepticism.","audio":"audio/8f9651238f477dfd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.noticeable-delay","text":"noticeable delay","kind":"collocation","form":"noticeable delay","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A clear lag between speaking and hearing, causing awkward pauses.","example":"There's a noticeable delay, so let's try to pause briefly after each speaker.","plain":"There's a lag, so let's pause after each speaker.","note":"A gentle way to acknowledge latency, suggesting a solution for turn-taking.","audio":"audio/d79eae718d59dbe6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.offer-a-different-perspective","text":"offer a different perspective","kind":"frame","form":"offer a different perspective","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To present an alternative viewpoint or way of looking at a situation.","example":"I'd like to offer a different perspective on how we might approach this problem.","plain":"I'd like to present an alternative viewpoint on how we might approach this problem.","note":"Collaborative and non-confrontational, inviting consideration of other ideas.","audio":"audio/0d623e3476203781.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-reflection","text":"on reflection","kind":"chunk","form":"On reflection","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling","interviews","feedback"],"meaning":"After careful thought or consideration.","example":"On reflection, I think we should have allocated more time for the testing phase.","plain":"After careful thought, I think we should have allocated more time for the testing phase.","note":"Introduces a revised opinion or realization after further consideration.","audio":"audio/1977609bf1451f11.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-that-specific-point","text":"on that specific point","kind":"chunk","form":"on that specific point","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To refer to a particular detail just mentioned.","example":"On that specific point, I think we need to consider the legal implications.","plain":"Regarding that particular detail, I think we need to consider the legal implications.","note":"Helps to focus a contribution on a precise aspect of the discussion.","audio":"audio/ed4ffa4149d16a79.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-the-contrary","text":"On the contrary","kind":"chunk","form":"On the contrary","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement that is the opposite of what has just been said or implied.","example":"On the contrary, the evidence suggests a more complex interplay of factors than initially thought.","plain":"Actually, the evidence suggests a more complex interplay of factors than initially thought.","note":"Used to strongly contradict a previous statement or assumption.","audio":"audio/6532fe0552e395fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.on-the-face-of-it","text":"On the face of it","kind":"chunk","form":"On the face of it","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Based on initial appearance or superficial examination, before deeper consideration.","example":"On the face of it, their proposal looks attractive, but we need to examine the hidden costs.","plain":"Initially, their proposal looks attractive, but we need to examine the hidden costs.","note":"Suggests that a deeper look might reveal complexities or problems.","audio":"audio/5a1be000d3138f5e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-area-for-development","text":"one area for development","kind":"collocation","form":"one area for development","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A specific aspect or skill that could be improved or enhanced.","example":"I think one area for development could be your public speaking skills.","plain":"I think your public speaking skills could be improved.","note":"A common, polite, and professional euphemism for a weakness or flaw.","audio":"audio/128367e54b641c3e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-could-make-the-case-that","text":"One could make the case that","kind":"frame","form":"One could make the case that","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular argument or viewpoint is plausible or defensible, without fully committing to it.","example":"One could make the case that remote work offers greater flexibility and boosts employee morale.","plain":"It's possible to argue that working remotely provides more flexibility and improves staff happiness.","note":"A sophisticated way to introduce an argument, often used to explore possibilities or anticipate others' views.","audio":"audio/8f51ccf0bc5c8564.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-might-also-consider","text":"One might also consider","kind":"frame","form":"One might also consider","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional point or perspective for consideration.","example":"One might also consider the ethical implications of such a widespread data collection strategy.","plain":"We could also think about the ethical implications of such a widespread data collection strategy.","note":"A formal and impersonal way to suggest another angle or factor.","audio":"audio/e2fd2dc0f09a05bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-might-contend-that","text":"One might contend that","kind":"frame","form":"One might contend that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a possible argument or claim, often one that is debatable or not universally accepted.","example":"One might contend that the current policy overlooks the long-term socio-economic implications.","plain":"Someone could argue that the current policy overlooks the long-term socio-economic implications.","note":"A formal and academic way to present a potential argument, often used when discussing differing viewpoints.","audio":"audio/83932efc91fe4f55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-option-would-be-to","text":"one option would be to","kind":"frame","form":"One option would be to","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a possible course of action or solution.","example":"One option would be to reallocate resources from a less critical project.","plain":"A possible course of action would be to reallocate resources from a less critical project.","note":"A common and polite way to suggest a solution.","audio":"audio/27c46c7aa0bc7d11.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.one-thing-ive-noticed-is","text":"One thing I've noticed is","kind":"frame","form":"One thing I've noticed is","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To introduce an observation or comment about something.","example":"One thing I've noticed is how friendly everyone is here.","plain":"I've observed that everyone here is very friendly.","note":"A natural way to share an observation or insight.","audio":"audio/8abdddf3c4942c6e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-agenda-for-today","text":"Our agenda for today","kind":"frame","form":"Our agenda for today","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to introduce the list of topics to be discussed during the meeting.","example":"Our agenda for today includes reviewing last quarter's performance and planning for the next.","plain":"Today, we will discuss last quarter's performance and plan for the next.","note":"Standard and professional, used by the chair to outline the meeting structure.","audio":"audio/775faf7ddb9e1fea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-baseline-expectation","text":"Our baseline expectation","kind":"collocation","form":"Our baseline expectation","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"The minimum or fundamental level of what is anticipated or required.","example":"Our baseline expectation for this project is a 10% return on investment within the first year.","plain":"Our minimum expectation for this project is a 10% return on investment within the first year.","note":"Professional language for stating a fundamental requirement or starting point.","audio":"audio/1fdcd2047503aae9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-course-of-action","text":"our course of action","kind":"collocation","form":"our course of action","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"The plan or method chosen to deal with a situation.","example":"Our course of action will be to conduct a thorough review before making any changes.","plain":"Our plan will be to conduct a thorough review before making any changes.","note":"Professional and formal way to describe future plans.","audio":"audio/ff26d0c37d960ab4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.our-efforts-are-aligned","text":"our efforts are aligned","kind":"collocation","form":"our efforts are aligned","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Our actions are coordinated and consistent with a common goal or strategy.","example":"We need to ensure that our efforts are aligned with the company's overall strategic objectives.","plain":"We must make sure that what we do matches the company's main strategic goals.","note":"Emphasizes coordination and consistency in achieving objectives.","audio":"audio/05e49f8659901b0d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.over-to-you","text":"over to you","kind":"chunk","form":"Over to you","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A concise way to indicate that it is now the other person's turn to speak.","example":"I've finished my update, so over to you for the next section.","plain":"I am done speaking, so you may begin.","note":"More informal than 'I'll hand it over to you', but still professional. Direct.","audio":"audio/09fdadd1230a9f83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.overcome-these-challenges","text":"overcome these challenges","kind":"collocation","form":"overcome these challenges","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To successfully deal with or surmount difficulties.","example":"I'm confident that together we can overcome these challenges and achieve our goals.","plain":"I believe that working together, we can conquer these difficulties and reach our goals.","note":"Expresses optimism and a belief in collective problem-solving.","audio":"audio/f57e10b113750e66.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.particularly-adept-at","text":"particularly adept at","kind":"collocation","form":"particularly adept at","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"especially skilled or proficient in something.","example":"You are particularly adept at mediating conflicts, which is a great asset.","plain":"You are especially skilled at mediating conflicts, which is a great asset.","note":"High-level praise for a specific skill.","audio":"audio/635db438b34140db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.particularly-effective","text":"particularly effective","kind":"collocation","form":"particularly effective","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Especially successful in producing a desired or intended result.","example":"Your strategy for engaging the new clients was particularly effective.","plain":"Your strategy for engaging the new clients was very successful.","note":"Specific praise, highlighting a successful method or approach.","audio":"audio/2efcca505249a203.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.pencil-you-in","text":"pencil you in","kind":"chunk","form":"pencil you in","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To make a tentative booking or arrangement, subject to confirmation.","example":"I can pencil you in for Tuesday evening, but I'll confirm the exact time tomorrow.","plain":"I can tentatively book you for Tuesday evening, but I'll confirm the exact time tomorrow.","note":"Informal and flexible, suggesting the plan isn't fully fixed yet. Often used when scheduling.","audio":"audio/d6791c5f0eb316fa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.perhaps-i-could-offer-a-perspective","text":"perhaps I could offer a perspective","kind":"frame","form":"Perhaps I could offer a perspective","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A very polite and humble way to introduce one's opinion or viewpoint.","example":"Perhaps I could offer a perspective on how this might impact our international team.","plain":"May I share my view on this matter?","note":"Highly diplomatic and formal, used to introduce a potentially differing opinion gently.","audio":"audio/73ddb48ae9986428.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.perhaps-we-can-discuss-that","text":"Perhaps we can discuss that","kind":"chunk","form":"Perhaps we can discuss that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To suggest deferring a detailed discussion to a more appropriate time.","example":"Perhaps we can discuss that in more detail offline, as it's quite specific.","plain":"We can discuss that in more detail offline, as it's quite specific.","note":"A polite and diplomatic way to manage time or avoid getting sidetracked.","audio":"audio/50396f8121c8d9c3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.perhaps-we-should-consider","text":"perhaps we should consider","kind":"frame","form":"Perhaps we should consider","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A polite and tentative way to suggest a course of action or idea.","example":"Perhaps we should consider the long-term implications before making a hasty decision.","plain":"Maybe we should think about the long-term implications before making a hasty decision.","note":"Softens a suggestion, making it less direct and more open to discussion.","audio":"audio/e1aa5cd88b1d2dff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.point-me-in-the-right-direction","text":"point me in the right direction","kind":"collocation","form":"point me in the right direction","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To give someone instructions or advice on how to find something or proceed.","example":"Could you point me in the right direction for the nearest metro station?","plain":"Could you tell me how to get to the nearest metro station?","note":"A common, friendly way to ask for directions or guidance.","audio":"audio/180c84f3cf81c72a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.poor-audio-quality","text":"poor audio quality","kind":"collocation","form":"poor audio quality","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"The sound is not clear or good, making it hard to hear or be heard.","example":"We're experiencing poor audio quality on this call; can you hear me clearly?","plain":"The sound on this call is bad; can you hear me?","note":"A common way to describe sound problems, suitable for professional settings.","audio":"audio/9a992bb47719d2d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.pop-round","text":"pop round","kind":"collocation","form":"pop round","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To visit someone's house for a short, informal period.","example":"I'll pop round later this evening to drop off the book.","plain":"I'll visit your house later this evening to drop off the book.","note":"Similar to 'pop over', common in British English for a quick, casual visit.","audio":"audio/70c1989febb4ac83.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.potential-pitfalls","text":"potential pitfalls","kind":"collocation","form":"potential pitfalls","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Possible hidden dangers or difficulties.","example":"We should discuss the potential pitfalls of launching this product without further testing.","plain":"We should discuss the possible hidden dangers of launching this product without further testing.","note":"Useful for risk assessment and planning.","audio":"audio/c7dca046e325fa18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.potential-resistance","text":"potential resistance","kind":"collocation","form":"potential resistance","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Possible opposition or unwillingness to accept something new or different.","example":"It's important to address potential resistance from employees early in the change management process.","plain":"We must deal with possible opposition from staff early when managing changes.","note":"Highlights the need to anticipate and manage objections or reluctance.","audio":"audio/1c6acd92abb7a4c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.provided-that","text":"Provided that","kind":"frame","form":"Provided that","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Introduces a condition that must be met for something else to happen.","example":"Provided that we receive the final designs by Friday, we can start production next week.","plain":"If we receive the final designs by Friday, we can start production next week.","note":"Formal and precise; used to state a clear prerequisite.","audio":"audio/b8ac1dcca6b05949.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-off","text":"put off","kind":"collocation","form":"put off","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To postpone or delay doing something.","example":"We had to put off our trip until next month due to unforeseen circumstances.","plain":"We had to delay our trip until next month.","note":"Very common phrasal verb, informal.","audio":"audio/addd3f14667515cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-ones-mind-at-ease","text":"put one's mind at ease","kind":"collocation","form":"put my mind at ease","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To relieve someone's worry or anxiety.","example":"The hotel manager's quick response really put my mind at ease about the lost key.","plain":"The hotel manager's quick response made me stop worrying about the lost key.","note":"Used when someone's concerns are alleviated.","audio":"audio/7c4a44bfce2ae5b0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-our-heads-together","text":"put our heads together","kind":"collocation","form":"put our heads together","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To collaborate with others to solve a problem or come up with an idea.","example":"Let's put our heads together and figure out a good date for the reunion.","plain":"Let's work together and figure out a good date for the reunion.","note":"Suggests a collaborative effort, often for planning or problem-solving. Informal and friendly.","audio":"audio/23c2091a2141ac44.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.put-things-into-perspective","text":"put things into perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"put things into perspective","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To help someone understand the true importance of something.","example":"Seeing the ancient ruins really put things into perspective about human history.","plain":"Seeing the ancient ruins helped me understand human history's true importance.","note":"Common for gaining a broader understanding or context.","audio":"audio/1ae1c0fdb640120e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.reach-a-compromise","text":"reach a compromise","kind":"collocation","form":"reach a compromise","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To come to an agreement where both sides give up something.","example":"We had to reach a compromise on our travel dates due to conflicting schedules.","plain":"We had to agree on travel dates by both giving in a little.","note":"Used when negotiating or resolving differences.","audio":"audio/e93c4e178ddc150c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.really-appreciate","text":"really appreciate","kind":"chunk","form":"really appreciate","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To feel grateful or thankful for something.","example":"I really appreciate you staying late to help finish the proposal.","plain":"I am very grateful that you stayed late to help finish the proposal.","note":"Sincere and common way to express gratitude for effort or help.","audio":"audio/17eb65ff67bd5e1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.regrettably-i-wont-be-able-to","text":"Regrettably, I won't be able to","kind":"frame","form":"Regrettably, I won't be able to","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A formal and apologetic way to state inability to attend or participate.","example":"Regrettably, I won't be able to attend your event.","plain":"Unfortunately, I won't be able to attend your event.","note":"A more formal and slightly understated way to decline, conveying sincere regret.","audio":"audio/9a37dc200e1dd280.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.revisit-a-point","text":"revisit a point","kind":"collocation","form":"revisit a point","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"To refer back to something previously said by another person and discuss it further.","example":"I wanted to revisit a point you made earlier about the project timeline.","plain":"I want to discuss your earlier comment about the project timeline.","note":"A polite way to bring a previous point back into discussion.","audio":"audio/8f9cc4ba6a473b9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.revisit-this-point","text":"revisit this point","kind":"collocation","form":"revisit this point","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To discuss a topic again at a later time.","example":"We'll need to revisit this point once we have more data from the market research.","plain":"We'll need to discuss this point again once we have more data from the market research.","note":"Professional way to defer a discussion.","audio":"audio/0cb71d4defa08b93.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.room-to-grow","text":"room to grow","kind":"chunk","form":"room to grow","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"areas where improvement or development is possible.","example":"While the project was successful, there's always room to grow in terms of efficiency.","plain":"While the project was successful, there's always a chance to improve efficiency.","note":"Positive framing for areas needing improvement.","audio":"audio/713c4258f9bca231.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.run-into-someone","text":"run into someone","kind":"collocation","form":"ran into an old friend","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"To meet someone unexpectedly, often by chance.","example":"I ran into an old friend at the supermarket yesterday, which was a pleasant surprise.","plain":"I met an old friend unexpectedly at the supermarket yesterday, which was a pleasant surprise.","note":"Implies a chance encounter, not a planned meeting. Can be positive or neutral.","audio":"audio/a1c48708525db2df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.run-into-trouble","text":"run into trouble","kind":"collocation","form":"run into trouble","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To encounter difficulties or problems.","example":"We might run into trouble if we don't have our visas sorted out before we leave.","plain":"We might have problems if we don't have our visas sorted out.","note":"Informal and common, implies unexpected difficulties.","audio":"audio/ea94f8bb75b6537b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.secure-approval","text":"secure approval","kind":"collocation","form":"secure approval","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To obtain official permission or agreement for something.","example":"Our next step is to secure approval from the board for the proposed expansion plan.","plain":"Our next action is to get the board's permission for the suggested growth plan.","note":"Common in business and formal contexts when seeking official consent.","audio":"audio/39b90fb1b413294c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.secure-buy-in","text":"secure buy-in","kind":"collocation","form":"secure buy-in","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To gain agreement and commitment from others for a plan or idea.","example":"We need to present a clear strategy to secure buy-in from all key stakeholders.","plain":"We must show a clear plan to get agreement and commitment from all important people involved.","note":"Crucial for successful implementation, as it implies gaining support and acceptance.","audio":"audio/09a38ad933d773fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.seize-the-opportunity","text":"seize the opportunity","kind":"collocation","form":"seize the opportunity","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To take advantage of a chance quickly and eagerly.","example":"We decided to seize the opportunity to visit the ancient ruins while we were nearby.","plain":"We decided to take the chance to visit the ancient ruins while we were nearby.","note":"Suggests enthusiasm and quick action.","audio":"audio/a724ce4f8832c053.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.set-a-positive-example","text":"set a positive example","kind":"collocation","form":"sets a positive example","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"to behave in a way that others should imitate.","example":"Your proactive approach to problem-solving really sets a positive example for the whole department.","plain":"Your proactive approach to problem-solving is a good model for the whole department.","note":"Praises leadership or influential behavior.","audio":"audio/af7b920842df3b2d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.set-a-timeline","text":"set a timeline","kind":"collocation","form":"set a timeline","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To establish a schedule with deadlines for a project.","example":"We need to set a timeline for the implementation phase to ensure we meet our targets.","plain":"We need to establish a schedule for the implementation phase to ensure we meet our targets.","note":"Essential for project management.","audio":"audio/395028b4cdc7364c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.set-the-tone","text":"set the tone","kind":"collocation","form":"set the tone","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Establish the general mood or atmosphere for the discussion.","example":"I'd like to set the tone for this meeting by focusing on collaborative problem-solving.","plain":"I want to establish the mood for this meeting by focusing on working together to solve problems.","note":"Useful for a facilitator to guide the group's approach. Professional and positive.","audio":"audio/33c430e6e4527c9e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.shall-we-make-a-start","text":"Shall we make a start","kind":"chunk","form":"Shall we make a start","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A polite question to suggest beginning the meeting.","example":"Everyone seems to be here, so shall we make a start on the agenda?","plain":"Everyone is here, so should we begin discussing the agenda?","note":"Polite and slightly formal, common in British English. Used by the chair.","audio":"audio/929534c91f6b0049.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.small-world-isnt-it","text":"Small world, isn't it","kind":"chunk","form":"Small world, isn't it","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Expressed when you discover an unexpected connection or coincidence with someone.","example":"You know John from university? Small world, isn't it! He was my flatmate.","plain":"You know John from university? That's a coincidence! He was my flatmate.","note":"A friendly, informal expression to acknowledge a surprising shared connection.","audio":"audio/2cd974093a9257e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.so-to-recap","text":"So, to recap","kind":"chunk","form":"So, to recap","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to briefly review the main points or decisions made so far.","example":"So, to recap, we've decided to proceed with the pilot project in Q4.","plain":"So, to summarize, we've decided to proceed with the pilot project in Q4.","note":"A common and effective way to ensure everyone is aligned before moving on.","audio":"audio/e53db1abdf97aebe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.something-to-keep-an-eye-on","text":"something to keep an eye on","kind":"chunk","form":"something to keep an eye on","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"An issue or situation that requires continuous monitoring or attention.","example":"The fluctuating market trends are something to keep an eye on for our next investment.","plain":"We should continuously monitor the fluctuating market trends for our next investment.","note":"A subtle warning or reminder to remain vigilant about a particular matter.","audio":"audio/be6f168e2fb7ed97.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.somethings-come-up","text":"something's come up","kind":"chunk","form":"something's come up","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"An unexpected event or issue has occurred, preventing one from doing something.","example":"I have to cancel our plans for tonight, something's come up at work.","plain":"I have to cancel our plans for tonight, an unexpected issue has occurred at work.","note":"A common, slightly vague way to explain a cancellation without going into detail. Polite.","audio":"audio/44a8572dd5c981ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.sorry-to-interrupt-but-2","text":"sorry to interrupt, but","kind":"frame","form":"Sorry to interrupt, but","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite apology before breaking into someone else's speech to make a point.","example":"Sorry to interrupt, but we're running short on time for this agenda item.","plain":"I apologize for breaking in, however, I need to say something.","note":"A standard polite interruption. Essential for maintaining good conversational etiquette.","audio":"audio/da600a99f09f22e9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.struck-a-chord","text":"struck a chord","kind":"collocation","form":"struck a chord","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To evoke a strong emotional response or resonate with someone's feelings or beliefs.","example":"Your opening remarks in the presentation really struck a chord with the audience.","plain":"Your opening remarks in the presentation really resonated with the audience.","note":"Implies a deep connection or understanding, often used for impactful communication.","audio":"audio/21652d65bade1a89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.subject-to","text":"Subject to","kind":"frame","form":"Subject to","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Introduces a condition or requirement that must be met.","example":"The agreement is subject to final review by our legal department.","plain":"Our legal department must give final approval for the agreement to be valid.","note":"Formal and concise; often used in legal or business contexts to state dependencies.","audio":"audio/b9b4902e75624849.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-chance","text":"take a chance","kind":"collocation","form":"take a chance","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To do something risky, hoping for a positive outcome.","example":"We decided to take a chance and try the small local restaurant without a reservation.","plain":"We decided to risk it and try the local restaurant without a reservation.","note":"Informal and common, implies a willingness to risk.","audio":"audio/dce1f40f32ef964e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-detour","text":"take a detour","kind":"collocation","form":"take a detour","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To take a longer, indirect route to a destination.","example":"We had to take a detour because the main road was closed for repairs.","plain":"We had to go a different, longer way because the main road was closed.","note":"Common in travel contexts, literal or figurative.","audio":"audio/adef56c88fa33f7c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-a-mental-break","text":"take a mental break","kind":"collocation","form":"take a mental break","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To pause from mental effort or stress to relax one's mind.","example":"After a long day of sightseeing, I needed to take a mental break.","plain":"After a long day of sightseeing, I needed to relax my mind.","note":"Common for self-care and managing stress.","audio":"audio/8f656945d0b7421b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.take-for-example","text":"Take for example","kind":"frame","form":"Take for example","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to present a specific case as an illustration of a general point.","example":"Take for example the recent changes in consumer behavior due to online shopping.","plain":"Consider the recent changes in consumer behavior due to online shopping.","note":"Invites the listener to consider a particular case.","audio":"audio/02e0f5d884e3227a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thank-goodness-for-that","text":"Thank goodness for that","kind":"chunk","form":"Thank goodness for that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong relief or gratitude for a positive outcome.","example":"The train arrived just in time; thank goodness for that!","plain":"The train arrived just in time; I am very grateful for that!","note":"A common, slightly informal expression of relief.","audio":"audio/cea4681fb2588a16.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thank-heavens-for-that","text":"Thank heavens for that","kind":"chunk","form":"Thank heavens for that","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong relief and gratitude for a positive outcome or averted problem.","example":"The train was delayed, but we made it to the airport just in time, thank heavens for that!","plain":"The train was delayed, but we made it to the airport just in time, which is a great relief!","note":"A slightly more formal or traditional alternative to \"thank goodness,\" expressing strong relief.","audio":"audio/dd6abea6b2d828c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thank-you-for-the-feedback","text":"thank you for the feedback","kind":"chunk","form":"Thank you for the feedback","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"A standard and polite way to express gratitude for receiving comments or criticism.","example":"Thank you for the feedback on my performance review; I found it very helpful.","plain":"I appreciate the comments on my performance review; I found them very helpful.","note":"Essential for showing professionalism and receptiveness to input.","audio":"audio/8de7c2e81890bfce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thank-you-for-your-time","text":"thank you for your time","kind":"chunk","form":"Thank you for your time","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"A polite expression of gratitude for the time and attention given by the interviewer.","example":"Thank you for your time today; I really enjoyed learning more about this opportunity.","plain":"I appreciate the time you spent with me today; I really enjoyed learning more about this opportunity.","note":"Essential closing phrase for candidates, showing politeness and professionalism.","audio":"audio/30dea708354c5bc7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thanks-for-getting-back-to-me","text":"thanks for getting back to me","kind":"chunk","form":"Thanks for getting back to me","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Expresses gratitude for someone returning a call or message.","example":"Thanks for getting back to me so quickly regarding my request.","plain":"I appreciate you contacting me again in response to my message.","note":"A polite and common expression of appreciation for a timely response. Neutral.","audio":"audio/f7b2f145fc6bf4c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-brings-me-great-comfort","text":"That brings me great comfort","kind":"chunk","form":"That brings me great comfort","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that a piece of news or a situation provides significant emotional ease and reassurance.","example":"Knowing that my parents are settling in well in their new home, that brings me great comfort.","plain":"Knowing that my parents are settling in well in their new home makes me feel much better.","note":"A slightly more formal or heartfelt way to express deep relief and reassurance.","audio":"audio/140c2bb0f1c48406.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-certainly-gives-one-pause","text":"That certainly gives one pause","kind":"chunk","form":"That certainly gives one pause","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates that a piece of information or an event causes one to stop and think carefully.","example":"The unexpected market downturn, that certainly gives one pause before making new investments.","plain":"The unexpected market downturn certainly makes one stop and think carefully before making new investments.","note":"A slightly formal way to say something makes you think twice or reconsider.","audio":"audio/3d6cb20bf888fb53.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-might-be-an-oversimplification","text":"That might be an oversimplification","kind":"frame","form":"That might be an oversimplification","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To suggest that an explanation or idea is too basic and lacks necessary detail or nuance.","example":"While the concept is clear, that might be an oversimplification of the complex issues involved.","plain":"While the concept is clear, that might be too simple an explanation for the complex issues involved.","note":"A polite, intellectual way to challenge a statement by suggesting it lacks depth or nuance.","audio":"audio/0e6571943363df82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-must-be-quite-something","text":"That must be quite something","kind":"chunk","form":"That must be quite something","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges an experience as significant or challenging, often with a hint of empathy.","example":"Living abroad for five years without speaking the language first, that must be quite something.","plain":"Living abroad for five years without speaking the language first, that must have been a significant challenge.","note":"Used to acknowledge a remarkable or difficult experience, often with a sense of awe or empathy.","audio":"audio/703664f37de1d8c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-said","text":"That said","kind":"chunk","form":"That said","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Despite what has just been mentioned; used to introduce a contrasting or qualifying statement.","example":"Their offer is quite generous. That said, we still have concerns about the long-term commitment.","plain":"Their offer is quite generous. However, we still have concerns about the long-term commitment.","note":"A concise way to introduce a counterpoint or nuance.","audio":"audio/60cb6b181fe9518d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-sounds-absolutely-lovely","text":"That sounds absolutely lovely","kind":"chunk","form":"That sounds absolutely lovely","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Expresses warm approval and pleasure regarding a suggestion or plan.","example":"A quiet dinner at your place? That sounds absolutely lovely.","plain":"A quiet dinner at your place? That sounds wonderful.","note":"A very positive and appreciative response, common in British English.","audio":"audio/3b840899ac9810d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-sounds-like-a-burden","text":"That sounds like a burden","kind":"chunk","form":"That sounds like a burden","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sympathy by identifying a situation or responsibility as heavy or oppressive.","example":"Having to care for an elderly parent while working full-time, that sounds like a burden.","plain":"Having to care for an elderly parent while working full-time sounds very difficult and heavy.","note":"A direct but empathetic way to acknowledge someone's heavy responsibilities.","audio":"audio/9dfe5d7c21979eda.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.that-wasnt-quite-what-i-intended","text":"That wasn't quite what I intended","kind":"chunk","form":"That wasn't quite what I intended","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To indicate that a statement or outcome was not exactly what was meant or desired.","example":"That wasn't quite what I intended to convey about the project's timeline.","plain":"That's not exactly what I meant to say about the project's timeline.","note":"A gentle way to correct a misunderstanding or a poorly phrased statement.","audio":"audio/5e6f18f560b9880f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-bit-much","text":"That's a bit much","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a bit much","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something is excessive, unreasonable, or overwhelming.","example":"Expecting us to work through the weekend without extra pay, that's a bit much.","plain":"Expecting us to work through the weekend without extra pay is unreasonable.","note":"An informal but common phrase to express mild indignation or that something is unfair.","audio":"audio/f0305e2d51bd1aa0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-bit-much-to-ask-for","text":"That's a bit much to ask for","kind":"frame","form":"That's a bit much to ask for","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that a request is unreasonable or excessive.","example":"Expecting us to work all weekend, that's a bit much to ask for.","plain":"Expecting us to work all weekend, that is an unreasonable request.","note":"A common, informal way to express disapproval of an excessive request.","audio":"audio/10b71d8b66b032d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-bit-much-to-take-in","text":"That's a bit much to take in","kind":"frame","form":"That's a bit much to take in","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that information is overwhelming or difficult to process.","example":"All these new regulations at once, that's a bit much to take in.","plain":"All these new regulations at once, that is overwhelming to process.","note":"A common expression for feeling overwhelmed by too much information.","audio":"audio/bda8cd520d682c8d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-bit-of-a-stretch","text":"That's a bit of a stretch","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a bit of a stretch","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Suggests that something is an exaggeration or unlikely to be true.","example":"Calling that a success, I think that's a bit of a stretch.","plain":"Calling that a success, I think that is an exaggeration.","note":"A common, informal way to express skepticism about a claim.","audio":"audio/e20641b1ebd662ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-complex-issue","text":"That's a complex issue","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a complex issue","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a question or topic is intricate and requires careful consideration.","example":"That's a complex issue, and one that requires a multi-faceted approach.","plain":"That's an intricate problem, and one that requires a multi-faceted approach.","note":"Used to indicate that a simple answer is not possible, often to buy time or defer.","audio":"audio/4526e4527a741265.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-delightful-piece-of-news","text":"That's a delightful piece of news","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a delightful piece of news","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong pleasure and happiness about a specific item of good news.","example":"Your promotion to senior management, that's a delightful piece of news!","plain":"Your promotion to senior management is very happy news!","note":"A slightly more formal or elegant way to express joy about good news.","audio":"audio/9f20b9fbcddb41d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-difficult-position","text":"That's a difficult position","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a difficult position","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that someone is in a tough or awkward situation.","example":"Having to choose between two good job offers, that's a difficult position to be in.","plain":"Having to choose between two good job offers is a tough situation.","note":"Used to express empathy for someone facing a hard choice or circumstance.","audio":"audio/90bc8326704a90d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-fantastic-outcome","text":"That's a fantastic outcome","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a fantastic outcome","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses strong approval and happiness about a very positive result.","example":"Despite the initial setbacks, achieving all your targets, that's a fantastic outcome.","plain":"Despite the initial setbacks, achieving all your targets is a very positive result.","note":"A common and enthusiastic way to praise a successful result.","audio":"audio/c0c1f868a65e2f96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-given","text":"That's a given","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a given","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Stating that something is an accepted fact or a foregone conclusion.","example":"That's a given; we always prioritize client satisfaction above all else.","plain":"That is an accepted fact; we always prioritize client satisfaction above all else.","note":"Informal but common, implies something is universally understood.","audio":"audio/99c7dc6815708417.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-great-relief","text":"That's a great relief","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a great relief","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses significant comfort and ease after a period of worry or stress.","example":"Hearing that the test results came back negative, that's a great relief.","plain":"Hearing that the test results came back negative makes me feel much better.","note":"A common and sincere expression of relief, slightly less emphatic than \"huge.\"","audio":"audio/087b90855b6ad19e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-huge-relief","text":"That's a huge relief","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a huge relief","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates great comfort or reassurance after a period of worry or stress.","example":"The test results came back negative, so that's a huge relief for everyone involved.","plain":"The test results came back negative, which makes everyone feel much better.","note":"Used to express significant comfort after a stressful situation. Very common.","audio":"audio/c4f9b1611a0fd489.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-huge-relief-to-everyone","text":"That's a huge relief to everyone","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a huge relief to everyone","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates widespread comfort or reassurance after a period of worry.","example":"The successful audit, that's a huge relief to everyone in the department.","plain":"The successful audit, that makes everyone in the department feel much better.","note":"Used to express significant comfort shared by a group.","audio":"audio/a60b2b5e4b5f0497.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-huge-weight-lifted","text":"That's a huge weight lifted","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a huge weight lifted","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses significant relief from a burden of worry or responsibility.","example":"Finishing the final exam, that's a huge weight lifted from my shoulders.","plain":"Finishing the final exam, I feel much less worried and burdened.","note":"A common and strong expression of relief, similar to \"off my mind.\"","audio":"audio/d4c49392ae59d42a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-huge-weight-off-my-mind","text":"That's a huge weight off my mind","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a huge weight off my mind","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses significant relief from a burden of worry or responsibility.","example":"Knowing the children are safe, that's a huge weight off my mind.","plain":"Knowing the children are safe, I feel much less worried.","note":"A very common and strong expression of relief.","audio":"audio/44febb76725297d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-load-off-my-mind","text":"That's a load off my mind","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a load off my mind","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses great relief that a worry or responsibility has been removed.","example":"Finally submitting my tax return, that's a load off my mind.","plain":"Finally submitting my tax return makes me feel much less worried.","note":"An idiomatic expression for significant relief from a burden.","audio":"audio/317180937053bef3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-pleasant-turn","text":"That's a pleasant turn","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a pleasant turn","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses positive surprise at an unexpected and welcome change in events.","example":"After all the delays, getting the visa approved so quickly, that's a pleasant turn.","plain":"After all the delays, getting the visa approved so quickly is a welcome and unexpected change.","note":"Used to acknowledge a positive and unexpected development in a situation.","audio":"audio/f7ac3373028054fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-real-blow","text":"That's a real blow","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a real blow","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Describes an event as a significant setback or disappointment.","example":"Losing the contract at the last minute, that's a real blow to the team.","plain":"Losing the contract at the last minute, that is a significant disappointment to the team.","note":"A common, informal expression for a major disappointment or setback.","audio":"audio/c7102b57ad0b3204.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-real-shame","text":"That's a real shame","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a real shame","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses disappointment or regret about an unfortunate situation.","example":"Oh, the concert was cancelled? That's a real shame, I was looking forward to it.","plain":"Oh, the concert was cancelled? That is very disappointing, I was looking forward to it.","note":"A common, informal expression of disappointment or mild sympathy.","audio":"audio/62096c68779ba24f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-tough-break","text":"That's a tough break","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a tough break","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sympathy for someone experiencing bad luck or misfortune.","example":"Missing out on the promotion by one vote, that's a tough break.","plain":"Missing out on the promotion by one vote, that is unfortunate.","note":"A common, informal expression of sympathy for bad luck.","audio":"audio/e7b3f3a73bb601da.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-tough-call","text":"That's a tough call","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a tough call","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges that a decision is difficult and requires careful consideration.","example":"Choosing between two equally qualified candidates, that's a tough call for the hiring committee.","plain":"Choosing between two equally qualified candidates is a difficult decision for the hiring committee.","note":"A common, informal but appropriate phrase for a hard decision.","audio":"audio/448ff2192ab6144f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-valid-point-however","text":"That's a valid point, however","kind":"frame","form":"That's a valid point, however","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To acknowledge the strength of an argument before introducing a contrasting or dissenting idea.","example":"That's a valid point, however, the implementation costs might outweigh the benefits.","plain":"Your point is valid, but the implementation costs might outweigh the benefits.","note":"A common, polite way to transition from agreement to disagreement, often used in formal discussions.","audio":"audio/0087d47deb3f34b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-weight-off-everyones-shoulders","text":"That's a weight off everyone's shoulders","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a weight off everyone's shoulders","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that a shared burden or worry has been removed, bringing relief to a group.","example":"The successful completion of the audit, that's a weight off everyone's shoulders.","plain":"The successful completion of the audit has relieved everyone of a great worry.","note":"An idiomatic expression for shared relief from a collective burden.","audio":"audio/af0f15f3597b9edf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-welcome-development","text":"That's a welcome development","kind":"collocation","form":"That's a welcome development","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses pleasure and approval for a new and positive change or event.","example":"The new policy on flexible working, that's a welcome development for many employees.","plain":"The new policy on flexible working is a positive and appreciated change for many employees.","note":"A formal and positive way to acknowledge a beneficial change.","audio":"audio/73a999306fe11c3f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-a-whole-other-story","text":"That's a whole other story","kind":"chunk","form":"That's a whole other story","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Indicates that a topic is separate and too complex or lengthy to discuss at the current moment.","example":"That's a whole other story, and one I don't have time to get into right now.","plain":"That's a completely different topic, and one I don't have time to discuss now.","note":"Used to politely defer a tangential discussion.","audio":"audio/ab73077a9138011d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-an-excellent-question","text":"That's an excellent question","kind":"chunk","form":"That's an excellent question","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Praises a question for being particularly good, relevant, or thought-provoking.","example":"That's an excellent question, and one I'm happy to address in detail.","plain":"That's a very good question, and one I'm happy to address in detail.","note":"A common and polite way to acknowledge a good question.","audio":"audio/f2533024d3dcdb04.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-an-interesting-development","text":"That's an interesting development","kind":"collocation","form":"That's an interesting development","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"A neutral or slightly understated reaction to new information or a change.","example":"The new competitor entering the market? That's an interesting development.","plain":"The new competitor entering the market? That is a noteworthy change.","note":"A polite and often diplomatic way to acknowledge new information, sometimes with implied caution.","audio":"audio/0101eeea00d7fe45.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-an-interesting-perspective","text":"That's an interesting perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"That's an interesting perspective","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges an idea or viewpoint as noteworthy, often without full agreement.","example":"Your take on the market shift, that's an interesting perspective.","plain":"Your take on the market shift, that is a noteworthy viewpoint.","note":"A polite and often diplomatic way to acknowledge a different opinion.","audio":"audio/363c7c48404777c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-an-interesting-thought","text":"That's an interesting thought","kind":"chunk","form":"That's an interesting thought","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to acknowledge an idea, often when you need time to process it or don't fully agree.","example":"That's an interesting thought, I'll have to give it some more consideration.","plain":"That's an interesting idea, I'll have to think about it more.","note":"Can be a genuine compliment or a polite deferral.","audio":"audio/d12ee26b91aca334.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-an-intriguing-development","text":"That's an intriguing development","kind":"collocation","form":"That's an intriguing development","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses curiosity and mild surprise about a new and potentially significant event.","example":"The competitor launching a similar product, that's an intriguing development.","plain":"The competitor launching a similar product is a surprising and interesting new event.","note":"A sophisticated way to acknowledge a new situation that sparks curiosity.","audio":"audio/a1bb6232cb6e47cf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-as-far-as-we-can-go","text":"That's as far as we can go","kind":"chunk","form":"That's as far as we can go","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"We have reached the limit of what we are able to offer or concede.","example":"We've reviewed our budget, and that's as far as we can go on the project funding.","plain":"We've checked our budget, and that is the maximum funding we can provide for the project.","note":"Indicates a firm limit, often after some prior movement or discussion.","audio":"audio/22b3a4b1eaf9e0b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-beside-the-point","text":"That's beside the point","kind":"chunk","form":"That's beside the point","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"States that a particular comment or detail is irrelevant to the main topic being discussed.","example":"That's beside the point; the main issue of project delays is still unresolved.","plain":"That's not relevant; the main issue of project delays is still unresolved.","note":"Used to redirect a conversation back to the core topic.","audio":"audio/297490e14e25d9bd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-beyond-the-scope-of","text":"That's beyond the scope of","kind":"chunk","form":"that's beyond the scope of","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To indicate that a question or topic falls outside the boundaries of the current discussion.","example":"While interesting, that's beyond the scope of today's presentation on project timelines.","plain":"That topic is outside the boundaries of today's presentation on project timelines.","note":"A professional way to politely decline to answer a question that is off-topic.","audio":"audio/e39aa36fc45e9742.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-certainly-one-way-of-looking-at-it","text":"That's certainly one way of looking at it","kind":"frame","form":"That's certainly one way of looking at it","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"A diplomatic way to acknowledge a perspective without fully agreeing with it.","example":"You think the new policy is revolutionary? That's certainly one way of looking at it.","plain":"You think the new policy is revolutionary? That is one possible perspective.","note":"A common, often subtly ironic or skeptical, way to acknowledge a differing opinion.","audio":"audio/f601704cb55d0911.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-certainly-unexpected","text":"That's certainly unexpected","kind":"chunk","form":"That's certainly unexpected","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a definite sense of surprise at something unforeseen.","example":"The sudden resignation of the director, that's certainly unexpected.","plain":"The sudden resignation of the director is definitely surprising.","note":"A common and direct way to express surprise.","audio":"audio/38953d5208bd318d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-easier-said-than-done","text":"That's easier said than done","kind":"chunk","form":"That's easier said than done","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Expresses skepticism about the ease of a proposed action, implying it is more difficult in practice.","example":"That's easier said than done when you're dealing with multiple stakeholders and conflicting priorities.","plain":"That's difficult to do when you're dealing with multiple stakeholders and conflicting priorities.","note":"A common response to an overly simplistic suggestion.","audio":"audio/3d66bfd7d6a4411f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-food-for-thought","text":"That's food for thought","kind":"collocation","form":"That's food for thought","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Indicates that something provides material for careful consideration.","example":"Your perspective on the market trends, that's food for thought.","plain":"Your perspective on the market trends, that gives me something to think about.","note":"A common phrase for acknowledging a valuable or thought-provoking idea.","audio":"audio/0f4d86ad0009ba78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-genuinely-delightful","text":"That's genuinely delightful","kind":"chunk","form":"That's genuinely delightful","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses sincere pleasure or happiness about something.","example":"Hearing that your research was published in such a prestigious journal, that's genuinely delightful.","plain":"Hearing that your research was published in such a prestigious journal makes me very happy.","note":"A sophisticated and warm expression of happiness, often for someone else's success.","audio":"audio/16e147e018a95a33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-not-quite-right","text":"that's not quite right","kind":"chunk","form":"that's not quite right","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"A gentle and indirect way to correct someone or indicate that their statement is inaccurate.","example":"While I appreciate the effort, that's not quite right regarding the project's original scope.","plain":"While I appreciate the effort, that's inaccurate regarding the project's original scope.","note":"Softens a direct correction, making it less confrontational.","audio":"audio/7a1ff04c3e82e67e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-not-what-i-meant","text":"That's not what I meant","kind":"chunk","form":"That's not what I meant","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Explains that your previous statement did not accurately convey your meaning.","example":"That's not what I meant to say about the project's scope.","plain":"That's not exactly what I intended to say about the project's scope.","note":"A polite way to indicate a miscommunication or poor phrasing.","audio":"audio/8dd13388e42158cf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-one-way-of-looking-at-it","text":"That's one way of looking at it","kind":"chunk","form":"That's one way of looking at it","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To acknowledge a perspective without fully agreeing, implying there are other valid viewpoints.","example":"That's one way of looking at it, but I think we need to consider the long-term implications.","plain":"I understand your perspective, but I think we need to consider the long-term implications.","note":"A polite, slightly indirect way to signal disagreement or to introduce an alternative view.","audio":"audio/5a38d89d17dcecff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-our-final-offer","text":"That's our final offer","kind":"chunk","form":"that's our final offer","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To state that the current proposal is the absolute best one can do and no further changes will be made.","example":"We've stretched our budget as far as it can go, so that's our final offer for the software license.","plain":"We've stretched our budget as far as it can go, so this is the best offer we can make for the software license.","note":"A very firm statement, often used at the end of a negotiation to signal an ultimatum.","audio":"audio/8be94da35603cfba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-a-stretch","text":"That's quite a stretch","kind":"chunk","form":"That's quite a stretch","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that a statement or claim is exaggerated, improbable, or difficult to believe.","example":"To say we'll double our profits this quarter, that's quite a stretch.","plain":"To say we'll double our profits this quarter is a significant exaggeration.","note":"An informal but common phrase to express skepticism about an ambitious claim.","audio":"audio/63983c2e41959161.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-a-turn-of-events","text":"That's quite a turn of events","kind":"collocation","form":"That's quite a turn of events","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses surprise or astonishment at an unexpected change in circumstances.","example":"The sudden resignation of the entire board? That's quite a turn of events.","plain":"The sudden resignation of the entire board? That is a very surprising change.","note":"A common expression for a significant and unexpected change in a situation.","audio":"audio/2afc52244e81edf3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-a-twist","text":"That's quite a twist","kind":"chunk","form":"That's quite a twist","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses surprise at a sudden and unexpected change or complication in a situation.","example":"After all the planning, for the venue to cancel last minute, that's quite a twist.","plain":"After all the planning, for the venue to cancel last minute is a very unexpected complication.","note":"Used for a surprising and often problematic development, often with a hint of irony.","audio":"audio/99b26f5b5e728aea.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-inspiring","text":"That's quite inspiring","kind":"chunk","form":"That's quite inspiring","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something is very motivating or uplifting.","example":"Hearing about her journey from intern to CEO, that's quite inspiring.","plain":"Hearing about her journey from intern to CEO is very motivating.","note":"A slightly understated but sincere way to express admiration and motivation.","audio":"audio/5cf64b42a4b1cc06.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-something","text":"that's quite something","kind":"chunk","form":"that's quite something","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses admiration or surprise at an impressive or unusual event","example":"You managed to finish the entire report in one night? Well, that's quite something!","plain":"You managed to finish the entire report in one night? That's very impressive!","note":"A polite, understated way to show admiration or surprise. Can be slightly ironic.","audio":"audio/358b91a2ede53b8a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-quite-unexpected","text":"That's quite unexpected","kind":"chunk","form":"That's quite unexpected","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate degree of surprise at something unforeseen.","example":"To see him at the conference after he said he couldn't make it, that's quite unexpected.","plain":"To see him at the conference after he said he couldn't make it is very surprising.","note":"A common, slightly understated way to express surprise.","audio":"audio/1871d25080015531.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-rather-surprising","text":"That's rather surprising","kind":"collocation","form":"That's rather surprising","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"A polite and understated way to say something is unexpected.","example":"The sudden policy reversal, that's rather surprising given previous statements.","plain":"The sudden policy reversal, that is quite unexpected given previous statements.","note":"A polite, slightly formal understatement for an unexpected situation.","audio":"audio/d8a8a17812150e87.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-rather-unfortunate","text":"That's rather unfortunate","kind":"collocation","form":"That's rather unfortunate","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"A polite and understated way to say something is regrettable or bad.","example":"The delay in the project timeline, that's rather unfortunate.","plain":"The delay in the project timeline, that is quite regrettable.","note":"A polite, slightly formal understatement for a negative situation.","audio":"audio/4bb8e71ac4ad5429.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-somewhat-surprising","text":"That's somewhat surprising","kind":"chunk","form":"That's somewhat surprising","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses a moderate degree of unexpectedness or mild astonishment.","example":"To hear that they're moving to another country so soon, that's somewhat surprising.","plain":"To hear that they're moving to another country so soon is a little surprising.","note":"A polite, understated way to express mild surprise.","audio":"audio/dec0fb1612e2fe13.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-truly-excellent","text":"That's truly excellent","kind":"chunk","form":"That's truly excellent","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the high quality or positive nature of something.","example":"Your presentation was so clear and insightful; that's truly excellent work.","plain":"Your presentation was so clear and insightful; that is genuinely superb work.","note":"A slightly more formal or emphatic way to praise or react positively.","audio":"audio/987a10f7fed8d140.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-truly-heartening","text":"That's truly heartening","kind":"chunk","form":"That's truly heartening","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something is genuinely encouraging and uplifting.","example":"Hearing about the community's support for the new initiative, that's truly heartening.","plain":"Hearing about the community's support for the new initiative is genuinely encouraging.","note":"A warm and sincere expression of finding something uplifting.","audio":"audio/06318e6a6dfae496.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-truly-inspiring","text":"That's truly inspiring","kind":"collocation","form":"That's truly inspiring","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses that something is genuinely motivating or uplifting.","example":"Her dedication to her work, despite challenges, that's truly inspiring.","plain":"Her dedication to her work, despite challenges, that is genuinely motivating.","note":"A common phrase to express admiration and motivation.","audio":"audio/5d77e016f93da9d4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.thats-very-true","text":"That's very true","kind":"chunk","form":"That's very true","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Emphatically agreeing with someone's statement or observation.","example":"\"It's been a long week.\" \"That's very true, I'm looking forward to the weekend.\"","plain":"\"It's been a long week.\" \"That is absolutely correct, I'm looking forward to the weekend.\"","note":"A common and natural way to show strong agreement.","audio":"audio/db08b2ad35004345.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-audio-is-cutting-in-and-out","text":"The audio is cutting in and out","kind":"chunk","form":"The audio is cutting in and out","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describes sound that is not continuous, repeatedly stopping and starting.","example":"The audio is cutting in and out, so I'm missing parts of what you're saying.","plain":"The sound keeps stopping and starting, so I'm not hearing everything.","note":"A very common and direct way to describe an unstable audio connection.","audio":"audio/9ab8a05d9a176702.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-bigger-picture","text":"the bigger picture","kind":"collocation","form":"the bigger picture","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"The overall perspective or context of a situation, rather than just the details.","example":"While individual tasks are important, we need to consider the bigger picture of how they contribute to our mission.","plain":"Individual tasks matter, but we must also think about the overall context of how they help our mission.","note":"Encourages a broader, more strategic view.","audio":"audio/eabb8b3056dca77d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-caveat-here-is","text":"The caveat here is","kind":"frame","form":"The caveat here is","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to introduce a warning or condition that limits the scope of a statement.","example":"The caveat here is that these projections are based on current market stability.","plain":"These projections are based on current market stability.","note":"Highlights a specific limitation or condition.","audio":"audio/216f6abdae794939.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-central-argument-is-that","text":"The central argument is that","kind":"frame","form":"The central argument is that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States the main point or thesis being presented.","example":"The central argument is that policy interventions must be culturally sensitive to be effective.","plain":"The main point is that policy interventions must be culturally sensitive to be effective.","note":"Used to clearly state the core thesis of a discussion or paper.","audio":"audio/b5de0b8ffc3f5982.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-central-point-is","text":"The central point is","kind":"frame","form":"The central point is","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Introduces the most important idea or argument being made.","example":"The central point is that our current strategy isn't yielding the desired results.","plain":"The main idea is that our current strategy isn't working as we hoped.","note":"Direct and assertive, used to focus attention on the core message.","audio":"audio/24f9de67e4d8ca91.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-central-premise-is-that","text":"The central premise is that","kind":"collocation","form":"The central premise is that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To state the main underlying assumption or argument of a theory, discussion, or work.","example":"The central premise is that human behavior is largely driven by unconscious motivations.","plain":"The main idea is that human behavior is largely driven by unconscious motivations.","note":"A formal way to introduce the core argument of a theory or paper.","audio":"audio/92b5599cb0265fd0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-connection-is-a-bit-patchy","text":"the connection is a bit patchy","kind":"collocation","form":"the connection is a bit patchy","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggests that the internet or phone signal is inconsistent and unreliable.","example":"I apologize if I cut out; the connection is a bit patchy where I am today.","plain":"My internet signal is not very strong or consistent right now.","note":"A polite and slightly understated way to describe a poor connection. Common in professional settings.","audio":"audio/283b2af68d18b985.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-consensus-is","text":"the consensus is","kind":"frame","form":"the consensus is","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To state the general agreement reached by the group.","example":"So, the consensus is that we proceed with the pilot project next month.","plain":"So, the general agreement is that we proceed with the pilot project next month.","note":"Professional way to state a collective decision.","audio":"audio/3de8e8da3190f181.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-core-challenge","text":"The core challenge","kind":"collocation","form":"The core challenge","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The most significant or fundamental difficulty that needs to be addressed.","example":"The core challenge for us is integrating the new software with our legacy systems.","plain":"The main difficulty for us is connecting the new software with our old systems.","note":"Highlights the primary obstacle; common in business and project discussions.","audio":"audio/3474ff1b22b0b067.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-core-idea","text":"The core idea","kind":"collocation","form":"The core idea","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The central or most important concept or principle.","example":"The core idea behind this initiative is to empower local communities.","plain":"The central concept behind this initiative is to empower local communities.","note":"A straightforward way to identify the fundamental concept of a plan or argument.","audio":"audio/00ead40edd0a36a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-core-message-here-is","text":"The core message here is","kind":"frame","form":"The core message here is","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Introduces the central, most important idea or meaning of the discussion.","example":"The core message here is that collaboration across departments is key to innovation.","plain":"The central idea here is that collaboration across departments is key to innovation.","note":"Helps to distil complex information into its most important point.","audio":"audio/b50e02883698114f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-crux-of-the-argument","text":"The crux of the argument","kind":"collocation","form":"The crux of the argument","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"the most important or central point of a discussion or debate.","example":"The crux of the argument is whether the benefits outweigh the implementation costs.","plain":"The most important point is whether the benefits outweigh the implementation costs.","note":"Highlights the central, often contentious, point of a debate.","audio":"audio/b3f7df473b11fd44.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-crux-of-the-matter","text":"The crux of the matter","kind":"chunk","form":"The crux of the matter","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","explaining"],"meaning":"the most important or difficult part of a problem or issue.","example":"The crux of the matter is how we can scale our operations without compromising quality.","plain":"The most important part is how we can scale our operations without compromising quality.","note":"Formal. Highlights the central, often challenging, aspect of a situation.","audio":"audio/a3b34022963d945c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-crux-of-the-matter-is","text":"The crux of the matter is","kind":"frame","form":"The crux of the matter is","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Identifies the most essential or central point of an issue.","example":"The crux of the matter is whether these interventions are sustainable in the long term.","plain":"The main point is whether these interventions are sustainable in the long term.","note":"A slightly informal but common phrase to get to the core issue.","audio":"audio/79f4230d10bfdc0c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-deciding-factor-is","text":"the deciding factor is","kind":"collocation","form":"The deciding factor is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"The most important element that influences a choice or outcome.","example":"The deciding factor is often the cost, even if other options seem better.","plain":"The most important element is often the cost, even if other options seem better.","note":"Highlights the critical element that determines a decision.","audio":"audio/16d61a2386060d78.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-distinction-between-x-and-y","text":"The distinction between X and Y","kind":"frame","form":"The distinction between explicit and implicit knowledge","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Refers to the difference between two concepts or categories.","example":"The distinction between explicit and implicit knowledge is crucial for understanding learning processes.","plain":"The difference between explicit and implicit knowledge is crucial for understanding learning processes.","note":"A formal way to refer to a conceptual difference.","audio":"audio/b57031fd4566ae30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-elephant-in-the-room","text":"The elephant in the room","kind":"collocation","form":"the elephant in the room","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"An obvious and important issue that everyone is aware of but no one wants to discuss.","example":"Let's address the elephant in the room: the significant budget overrun from the previous phase.","plain":"Let's address the obvious and important issue that everyone is aware of but no one wants to discuss: the significant budget overrun from the previous phase.","note":"Informal but widely understood. Used to bring up an uncomfortable but necessary topic.","audio":"audio/1d8f69fdc496f87c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-essential-point-is","text":"The essential point is","kind":"frame","form":"The essential point is","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"Introduces the most crucial or fundamental idea.","example":"The essential point is that we must adapt our marketing strategy to new demographics.","plain":"The main idea is that we need to change our marketing for new groups of people.","note":"Emphatic and direct, similar to \"the central point is.\"","audio":"audio/e98b47e28583fde1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-fact-of-the-matter-is","text":"The fact of the matter is","kind":"frame","form":"The fact of the matter is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to state the actual facts, often to emphasize a point or correct a misconception.","example":"The fact of the matter is, our competitors are already implementing similar solutions.","plain":"Actually, our competitors are already implementing similar solutions.","note":"Similar to 'the truth of the matter is', used to underscore a factual point.","audio":"audio/88aa9d6aeef0dd7d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-foundational-concept","text":"The foundational concept","kind":"collocation","form":"The foundational concept","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The basic or underlying idea upon which something else is built.","example":"Understanding the foundational concept of supply and demand is key to economics.","plain":"Knowing the basic idea of supply and demand is crucial for economics.","note":"Academic or professional, refers to a core idea.","audio":"audio/003f485e029e9a2f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-fundamental-issue","text":"The fundamental issue","kind":"collocation","form":"The fundamental issue","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The basic or underlying problem that needs to be addressed.","example":"The fundamental issue is a lack of communication between departments.","plain":"The main problem is that departments aren't talking to each other enough.","note":"Highlights the root cause; often used in problem-solving discussions.","audio":"audio/e1494bf5e95617e9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-fundamental-issue-here-is","text":"The fundamental issue here is","kind":"collocation","form":"The fundamental issue here is","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To identify the most basic or underlying problem or question in a discussion.","example":"The fundamental issue here is not just funding, but the lack of inter-departmental collaboration.","plain":"The main problem here is not just funding, but the lack of inter-departmental collaboration.","note":"Used to pinpoint the core problem or disagreement.","audio":"audio/b2955ea5a836a51b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-fundamental-nature","text":"The fundamental nature","kind":"collocation","form":"The fundamental nature","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The basic and inherent characteristics or qualities of something.","example":"Understanding the fundamental nature of the problem is the first step to solving it.","plain":"Knowing the basic qualities of the problem is the first step to fixing it.","note":"Academic or analytical, refers to the core identity of something.","audio":"audio/74da2a888d2b1af1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-fundamental-principle","text":"The fundamental principle","kind":"collocation","form":"The fundamental principle","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The basic, essential truth or rule that forms the foundation of something.","example":"The fundamental principle of our approach is user-centric design.","plain":"The basic rule of our approach is user-centric design.","note":"Used for explaining the foundational beliefs or rules guiding a system, philosophy, or method.","audio":"audio/7909bd043b05ca1d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-general-consensus-was","text":"The general consensus was","kind":"frame","form":"The general consensus was","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports the widely held opinion or agreement among a group of people.","example":"The general consensus was that the new policy would be beneficial for everyone involved.","plain":"Most people agreed that the new policy would be beneficial for everyone involved.","note":"Useful for summarizing group opinion or a collective decision.","audio":"audio/0c211b2eab47bb6c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-gist-of-it","text":"the gist of it","kind":"chunk","form":"the gist of it","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"the main point or general sense of something, without the details.","example":"The gist of it is that we need to adapt quickly to changing market conditions.","plain":"The main point is that we need to adapt quickly to changing market conditions.","note":"Implies a summary that captures the essence without getting bogged down in specifics.","audio":"audio/e47e641fa7e9f279.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-gist-of-the-argument","text":"The gist of the argument","kind":"collocation","form":"The gist of the argument","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"the main point or general sense of a discussion or debate.","example":"The gist of the argument is that we need to adapt to changing consumer habits.","plain":"The main point is that we need to adapt to changing consumer habits.","note":"Informal. Refers to the essential meaning of a discussion.","audio":"audio/c2919907d8e37437.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-implications-are-far-reaching","text":"The implications are far-reaching","kind":"collocation","form":"The implications are far-reaching","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that the consequences or effects of something are extensive and significant.","example":"The implications are far-reaching, affecting not only individuals but also entire communities.","plain":"The consequences are extensive, affecting not only individuals but also entire communities.","note":"A strong phrase to emphasize the broad impact of an issue.","audio":"audio/70960e56c0445a54.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-implications-of-this-are","text":"The implications of this are","kind":"collocation","form":"The implications of this are","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To discuss the possible future effects or consequences of a particular finding or situation.","example":"The implications of this are far-reaching for public health policy and resource allocation.","plain":"The consequences of this are far-reaching for public health policy and resource allocation.","note":"Used to discuss the broader significance or future impact of a result.","audio":"audio/60d7bae81c73d1c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-key-takeaway-here-is","text":"The key takeaway here is","kind":"frame","form":"The key takeaway here is","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To highlight the most important piece of information or conclusion.","example":"The key takeaway here is that customer satisfaction directly impacts retention rates.","plain":"The most important point is that customer satisfaction directly impacts retention rates.","note":"Very common in business presentations to summarise the main message.","audio":"audio/22ce0778ad55de67.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-long-and-short-of-it-is","text":"The long and short of it is","kind":"frame","form":"The long and short of it is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to summarize the main facts of a situation concisely, omitting details.","example":"The long and short of it is, we missed a critical deadline due to unforeseen technical issues.","plain":"In summary, we missed a critical deadline due to unforeseen technical issues.","note":"An informal but common way to quickly get to the main point, often used in explanations.","audio":"audio/013898db619f3de0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-main-insight","text":"The main insight","kind":"collocation","form":"The main insight","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The most important or profound understanding gained from a situation or analysis.","example":"The main insight from our research is the shift in consumer preferences.","plain":"The most important thing we learned from our research is that what customers like has changed.","note":"Highlights a key discovery or realization.","audio":"audio/876fb84ab1c957bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-message-i-want-to-impart","text":"The message I want to impart","kind":"collocation","form":"The message I want to impart","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The specific idea or information the speaker wishes to communicate.","example":"The message I want to impart is the importance of teamwork in achieving our goals.","plain":"I want to tell you that teamwork is very important for reaching our goals.","note":"Formal and intentional, emphasizes the speaker's purpose.","audio":"audio/f9500efe2f0b2ad6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-moral-of-the-story","text":"the moral of the story","kind":"chunk","form":"the moral of the story","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"states the lesson or main takeaway from an experience","example":"I learned a lot from that mistake; the moral of the story is to always double-check.","plain":"I learned a lot from that mistake; the lesson is to always double-check.","note":"Used to explicitly state the wisdom gained from an anecdote. Slightly formal.","audio":"audio/1977eb31d474311c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-next-point-i-want-to-make-is","text":"The next point I want to make is","kind":"frame","form":"The next point I want to make is","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce the subsequent main idea or argument.","example":"The next point I want to make is about the potential risks involved in this venture.","plain":"My next point is about the potential risks involved in this venture.","note":"A straightforward and clear way to signpost the progression of ideas.","audio":"audio/1cc6fcf85fe2a812.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-next-steps-are","text":"The next steps are","kind":"frame","form":"The next steps are","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to outline the actions or stages that will follow the current discussion or decision.","example":"The next steps are to draft the proposal, get internal approval, and then present to the client.","plain":"The following actions are to draft the proposal, get internal approval, and then present to the client.","note":"Clear and action-oriented, provides a roadmap for moving forward.","audio":"audio/0e1d727279a4e188.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-optimal-solution","text":"the optimal solution","kind":"collocation","form":"the optimal solution","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"The best or most favorable answer to a problem.","example":"Finding the optimal solution for resource allocation will save us significant time.","plain":"Finding the best answer for resource allocation will save us significant time.","note":"A formal term for the most effective or efficient resolution.","audio":"audio/33cca8a714cff779.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-overarching-concept","text":"The overarching concept","kind":"collocation","form":"The overarching concept","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The main or dominant idea that encompasses all other related ideas.","example":"The overarching concept of this initiative is to foster a culture of innovation.","plain":"The main idea of this project is to create a culture where new ideas are encouraged.","note":"Academic or strategic, refers to a broad, unifying idea.","audio":"audio/53c8737c2a516aa7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-practical-implications","text":"the practical implications","kind":"collocation","form":"the practical implications","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"The real-world consequences or effects of a decision or action.","example":"We need to be pragmatic about the practical implications of implementing such a large-scale change.","plain":"We must be realistic about the real-world effects of making such a big change.","note":"Focuses on the tangible effects and feasibility of a plan.","audio":"audio/b713b57190edf7b9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-prevailing-view-is-that","text":"The prevailing view is that","kind":"frame","form":"The prevailing view is that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces the most widely accepted or common opinion or belief on a particular subject.","example":"The prevailing view is that climate change is primarily driven by anthropogenic factors.","plain":"Most people believe that climate change is primarily driven by anthropogenic factors.","note":"Used to state the generally accepted opinion, often as a starting point for discussion or contrast.","audio":"audio/d1d621fc90941aa8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-primary-concern-is","text":"The primary concern is","kind":"frame","form":"The primary concern is","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Identifies the most important issue or worry.","example":"The primary concern is the generalizability of these findings to broader populations.","plain":"The main worry is whether these findings can be applied to broader populations.","note":"Used to highlight the most significant problem or consideration.","audio":"audio/d8bc1fd6cfcdf791.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-problem-is-that","text":"The problem is that","kind":"frame","form":"The problem is that","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To introduce the main difficulty or obstacle.","example":"The problem is that we don't have enough time to visit all the museums.","plain":"We don't have enough time to visit all the museums.","note":"Direct and clear, used to state a specific issue.","audio":"audio/0f11198e618a4d1a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-prospects-for-success","text":"the prospects for success","kind":"collocation","form":"the prospects for success","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"The likelihood or chances of achieving a favorable outcome.","example":"I'm optimistic about the prospects for success of our new product launch in the Asian market.","plain":"I feel positive about the chances that our new product launch in Asia will do well.","note":"Refers to the likelihood of a positive result, often used in business contexts.","audio":"audio/5e0a675a68351211.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-purpose-of-this-meeting","text":"The purpose of this meeting","kind":"frame","form":"The purpose of this meeting","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to clearly state the main objective or reason for the gathering.","example":"The purpose of this meeting is to finalize the project timeline and assign responsibilities.","plain":"We are meeting to finalize the project timeline and assign responsibilities.","note":"Clear and direct, often used by the chair to set expectations at the outset.","audio":"audio/696e928e121dfadf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-question-is","text":"The question is","kind":"frame","form":"The question is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a key query or the central problem that needs to be addressed.","example":"The question is, can we realistically meet these aggressive targets without compromising quality?","plain":"The main problem is, can we realistically meet these aggressive targets without compromising quality?","note":"Frames the core dilemma or decision point, inviting discussion or a solution.","audio":"audio/b7fbc5c943271bf0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-rationale-behind-this","text":"The rationale behind this","kind":"collocation","form":"The rationale behind this","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"The fundamental reason or logical basis for a decision, action, or belief.","example":"The rationale behind this policy change is to improve employee well-being.","plain":"The reason for this policy change is to make employees happier and healthier.","note":"Formal and analytical, used to explain the reasoning.","audio":"audio/0ccd80c98da020d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-reality-of-the-situation-is","text":"The reality of the situation is","kind":"frame","form":"The reality of the situation is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to state the true facts of a situation, often contrasting with assumptions or hopes.","example":"The reality of the situation is, we don't have the resources to take on another major client right now.","plain":"The truth is, we don't have the resources to take on another major client right now.","note":"Used to bring a dose of realism to a discussion, often when facing difficult truths.","audio":"audio/3826af9c2b352ad7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-reason-being","text":"The reason being","kind":"frame","form":"The reason being","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to introduce the explanation or justification for a previous statement.","example":"We decided to postpone the launch, the reason being that we need more testing time.","plain":"We decided to postpone the launch because we need more testing time.","note":"Slightly formal. Provides a direct explanation for a decision or situation.","audio":"audio/cbdc810ad769771b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-right-approach","text":"the right approach","kind":"collocation","form":"the right approach","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"The most suitable or correct method or way of dealing with something.","example":"I believe that a phased implementation is the right approach for integrating the new software.","plain":"I think that putting the new software in place step-by-step is the best method.","note":"Suggests a well-considered and optimal strategy.","audio":"audio/fb93cf02bfc7c433.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-signal-is-weak","text":"the signal is weak","kind":"collocation","form":"the signal is weak","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Explains that the wireless or cellular reception is poor, causing connection issues.","example":"I'm in an area where the signal is weak, so I might drop off unexpectedly.","plain":"The phone or internet reception here is not strong, so the call might end.","note":"A common explanation for poor call quality, especially on mobile phones. Neutral.","audio":"audio/2386146ce82933de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-sound-is-breaking-up","text":"the sound is breaking up","kind":"collocation","form":"the sound is breaking up","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Describes audio that is intermittent and unclear, making speech difficult to understand.","example":"I'm sorry, but the sound is breaking up quite badly on your end.","plain":"Your audio is not clear and keeps cutting out.","note":"A common and clear way to describe poor audio quality. Neutral tone.","audio":"audio/653ab7b3c9063918.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-takeaway-here-is","text":"The takeaway here is","kind":"frame","form":"The takeaway here is","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to highlight the most important lesson or conclusion from a discussion.","example":"The takeaway here is that adaptability is crucial for navigating market changes.","plain":"Adaptability is crucial for navigating market changes.","note":"Informal but widely used. Emphasizes the main point to remember.","audio":"audio/4b3159e4c1939671.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-thing-about-it-was","text":"The thing about it was","kind":"frame","form":"The thing about it was","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Highlights a crucial or defining aspect of a situation or event.","example":"The thing about it was, nobody had expected such a sudden change in management.","plain":"The important point was, nobody had expected such a sudden change in management.","note":"Draws attention to a key element of the story.","audio":"audio/c7dbfa1609f4bbf4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-truth-of-the-matter-is","text":"The truth of the matter is","kind":"frame","form":"The truth of the matter is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to state the actual facts, often to clarify or correct a misunderstanding.","example":"The truth of the matter is, the budget was approved, but the funds haven't been released yet.","plain":"Actually, the budget was approved, but the funds haven't been released yet.","note":"Emphasizes the factual basis of a statement, often used to set the record straight.","audio":"audio/d10026b8392910e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-underlying-assumption-is","text":"The underlying assumption is","kind":"frame","form":"The underlying assumption is","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Identifies a fundamental belief or premise that forms the basis of an argument or theory.","example":"The underlying assumption is that participants will provide honest and accurate self-reports.","plain":"The basic belief is that participants will provide honest and accurate self-reports.","note":"Used to expose or discuss the foundational beliefs of a concept.","audio":"audio/b79fde5dbda758b4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-underlying-principle","text":"The underlying principle","kind":"collocation","form":"The underlying principle","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"the fundamental rule or idea that forms the basis of something.","example":"The underlying principle of this new software is to simplify complex data analysis.","plain":"The fundamental idea of this new software is to simplify complex data analysis.","note":"Refers to the core concept or foundation.","audio":"audio/da0fe2cce6a4b2b2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-underlying-reasons","text":"the underlying reasons","kind":"collocation","form":"the underlying reasons","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"The fundamental or hidden causes of a situation or problem.","example":"Before we react, we need to investigate the underlying reasons for the unexpected project delay.","plain":"Before we respond, we must look into the basic causes of the project's unexpected delay.","note":"Implies a need for deeper analysis beyond surface-level symptoms.","audio":"audio/58b876f72b6bf18e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-upshot-is","text":"The upshot is","kind":"frame","form":"The upshot is","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"the final result or outcome of a series of events or discussions.","example":"The upshot is, we'll need to reallocate resources to meet the new targets.","plain":"We'll need to reallocate resources to meet the new targets.","note":"Informal. Refers to the ultimate consequence or main result.","audio":"audio/b11aab7b3697c5ef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-video-is-lagging-a-bit","text":"The video is lagging a bit","kind":"chunk","form":"The video is lagging a bit","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Explains that the video feed is not smooth and is experiencing delays.","example":"The video is lagging a bit, so your movements appear jerky on my screen.","plain":"Your video is delayed and not moving smoothly on my screen.","note":"A common, informal way to describe video synchronization issues.","audio":"audio/f22630504c9971af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.the-way-i-see-things","text":"The way I see things","kind":"frame","form":"The way I see things","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to introduce one's personal opinion or interpretation of a situation.","example":"The way I see things, we need to prioritize long-term sustainability over quick profits.","plain":"I think we need to prioritize long-term sustainability over quick profits.","note":"Similar to \"the way I see it,\" but slightly more general.","audio":"audio/73e33962c5a7fba7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.their-point-of-view","text":"their point of view","kind":"collocation","form":"their point of view","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"The way someone else sees or understands a situation.","example":"Let's try to see things from their point of view to better understand their concerns.","plain":"Let's try to understand their perspective to better grasp what worries them.","note":"Encourages empathy and understanding of others' positions.","audio":"audio/f2ad8d081140db6a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.there-is-a-strong-case-for","text":"There is a strong case for","kind":"collocation","form":"There is a strong case for","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To suggest that there are compelling reasons or evidence supporting a particular argument or action.","example":"There is a strong case for implementing stricter ethical guidelines in AI development.","plain":"There are good reasons to implement stricter ethical guidelines in AI development.","note":"Used to introduce a well-supported argument or recommendation.","audio":"audio/f7232d68ef00471c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.there-is-a-strong-indication-that","text":"There is a strong indication that","kind":"frame","form":"There is a strong indication that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that evidence points clearly towards a particular conclusion, though not definitively.","example":"There is a strong indication that early childhood education positively impacts long-term outcomes.","plain":"The evidence strongly suggests that early childhood education positively impacts long-term outcomes.","note":"A formal way to present a likely conclusion based on robust evidence.","audio":"audio/86f943595436fb96.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.there-seems-to-be-an-issue","text":"there seems to be an issue","kind":"chunk","form":"there seems to be an issue","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A polite and indirect way to state that a problem exists.","example":"There seems to be an issue with the air conditioning in my room.","plain":"The air conditioning in my room isn't working.","note":"Softens a complaint, making it less confrontational.","audio":"audio/f9c3199fb2afded9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-bit-of-a-disconnect","text":"there's a bit of a disconnect","kind":"chunk","form":"there's a bit of a disconnect","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"There is a lack of connection or understanding between two things or people.","example":"It seems there's a bit of a disconnect between what the client expects and what we can deliver.","plain":"It seems there's a lack of connection between what the client expects and what we can deliver.","note":"A polite way to point out a gap in understanding or alignment.","audio":"audio/30c546cbdc61824e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-fine-line","text":"There's a fine line","kind":"chunk","form":"There's a fine line","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Describes a subtle and often difficult distinction between two similar things.","example":"There's a fine line between offering helpful advice and interfering in someone's choices.","plain":"There's a subtle difference between offering helpful advice and interfering in someone's choices.","note":"Highlights a delicate balance or distinction.","audio":"audio/1b4de88f01cf5d3b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-slight-complication","text":"There's a slight complication","kind":"collocation","form":"There's a slight complication","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To gently introduce a problem or difficulty that challenges a proposed idea or plan.","example":"That sounds like a good plan, but there's a slight complication with the venue availability.","plain":"That sounds like a good plan, but the venue availability is a small problem.","note":"A mild, understated way to express a potential issue or objection.","audio":"audio/a138450d23616daa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.theres-a-slight-nuance","text":"There's a slight nuance","kind":"chunk","form":"There's a slight nuance","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To indicate that a situation or idea is more complex than it appears, implying a subtle disagreement.","example":"While the data seems clear, there's a slight nuance in how it applies to our specific market.","plain":"While the data seems clear, there's a subtle difference in how it applies to our specific market.","note":"Used to introduce a subtle distinction or complexity that challenges a straightforward interpretation.","audio":"audio/f4ab18fd277542e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.they-suggested-that","text":"They suggested that","kind":"frame","form":"They suggested that","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Reports a recommendation or idea put forward by others.","example":"They suggested that we consider alternative solutions before making a final decision.","plain":"They recommended that we consider alternative solutions before making a final decision.","note":"Used to convey advice or a proposal from others.","audio":"audio/9448085a4b9404c4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.think-of-it-this-way","text":"Think of it this way","kind":"frame","form":"Think of it this way","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To offer an alternative, often simpler, perspective or analogy to explain something.","example":"Think of it this way: each team member is a gear, and we all need to turn together.","plain":"Each team member is a gear, and we all need to turn together; consider it from this perspective.","note":"A common, conversational phrase used to simplify a complex idea with a relatable comparison.","audio":"audio/97b42e038d74cc3c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-aligns-with","text":"This aligns with","kind":"collocation","form":"This aligns with","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that a finding or idea is consistent or in agreement with something else.","example":"This aligns with the theoretical predictions outlined in the foundational literature on the subject.","plain":"This is consistent with the theoretical predictions outlined in the foundational literature on the subject.","note":"A formal and academic way to express agreement or consistency between different pieces of information.","audio":"audio/8371c9d513d868c6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-me-to-my-final-point","text":"This brings me to my final point","kind":"chunk","form":"This brings me to my final point","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To signal the introduction of the last main idea or argument.","example":"This brings me to my final point about the importance of continuous learning.","plain":"This leads me to my last point about the importance of continuous learning.","note":"A clear way to signpost the nearing end of the main content of the presentation.","audio":"audio/8167377359713f26.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-me-to-the-end-of","text":"This brings me to the end of","kind":"frame","form":"This brings me to the end of","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To signal the conclusion of a section or the entire presentation.","example":"This brings me to the end of my overview of the project's progress.","plain":"This concludes my overview of the project's progress.","note":"A formal and clear way to indicate that a section or the talk is finishing.","audio":"audio/d7aa6e26bee810a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-us-back-to","text":"This brings us back to","kind":"frame","form":"This brings us back to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To redirect the discussion to a previously mentioned point or a core theme.","example":"This brings us back to the fundamental question of how we define intelligence in artificial systems.","plain":"This reminds us of the fundamental question of how we define intelligence in artificial systems.","note":"Useful for maintaining coherence in a discussion or argument.","audio":"audio/5f602c6ff341a48f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-brings-us-to-the-point","text":"This brings us to the point","kind":"frame","form":"This brings us to the point","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Transitions to a specific idea or argument that follows logically from the previous discussion.","example":"This brings us to the point that interdisciplinary collaboration is increasingly vital.","plain":"This leads us to the idea that interdisciplinary collaboration is increasingly vital.","note":"A smooth way to introduce a new, related argument.","audio":"audio/b857b69505c0c7db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-contrasts-sharply-with","text":"This contrasts sharply with","kind":"frame","form":"This contrasts sharply with","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Points out a very clear and noticeable difference.","example":"This contrasts sharply with the findings from the urban population, suggesting regional variations.","plain":"This is very different from the findings from the urban population, suggesting regional variations.","note":"A strong phrase to highlight a significant difference.","audio":"audio/c620b7b890144cd6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-could-be-problematic","text":"this could be problematic","kind":"chunk","form":"this could be problematic","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"This has the potential to cause difficulties or issues.","example":"If we don't secure the necessary permits, this could be problematic for the construction schedule.","plain":"If we don't secure the necessary permits, this has the potential to cause difficulties for the construction schedule.","note":"A polite and slightly understated way to express concern about a potential issue.","audio":"audio/fc38169996de6dae.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-diverges-from","text":"This diverges from","kind":"collocation","form":"This diverges from","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that a finding or idea is different or inconsistent with something else.","example":"This diverges from the expected outcomes, suggesting a need for further investigation.","plain":"This is different from the expected outcomes, suggesting a need for further investigation.","note":"A formal and academic way to express disagreement or inconsistency between different pieces of information.","audio":"audio/e693003583849a0c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-feels-like-the-right-direction","text":"this feels like the right direction","kind":"chunk","form":"this feels like the right direction","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"This seems to be the correct or most appropriate course of action.","example":"Based on the feedback, this feels like the right direction for the project.","plain":"Based on the feedback, this seems to be the correct course of action for the project.","note":"Expresses a strong intuition or general agreement about a path.","audio":"audio/0ab44e41e61e36c7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-highlights-the-fact-that","text":"This highlights the fact that","kind":"frame","form":"This highlights the fact that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To draw attention to a specific truth or reality revealed by previous information.","example":"This highlights the fact that customer feedback is invaluable for product development.","plain":"This shows that customer feedback is invaluable for product development.","note":"A formal way to emphasize a conclusion drawn from presented data or arguments.","audio":"audio/d48c6c04df699b06.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-a-critical-aspect","text":"This is a critical aspect","kind":"chunk","form":"This is a critical aspect","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Identifies a particular part or feature as being extremely important.","example":"This is a critical aspect of our new security protocol that requires careful attention.","plain":"This is an extremely important part of our new security protocol that requires careful attention.","note":"Emphasizes the importance of a specific component or detail.","audio":"audio/d661a408764d8916.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-a-key-distinction","text":"This is a key distinction","kind":"chunk","form":"This is a key distinction","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Points out a crucial difference between two things that might otherwise be confused.","example":"This is a key distinction between our product and that of our main competitor.","plain":"This is a crucial difference between our product and that of our main competitor.","note":"Useful for clarifying nuances and avoiding misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/8cc3e415c09aaaf9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-a-nuanced-issue","text":"This is a nuanced issue","kind":"collocation","form":"This is a nuanced issue","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that a topic is complex and involves subtle distinctions.","example":"This is a nuanced issue, requiring careful consideration of multiple interacting factors.","plain":"This is a complex issue, requiring careful consideration of multiple interacting factors.","note":"Used to describe a topic that is not straightforward and has many subtle aspects.","audio":"audio/5381e5889f49bf30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-fundamental-to","text":"This is fundamental to","kind":"frame","form":"This is fundamental to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Explains that a particular point forms the basic foundation or core of something.","example":"This is fundamental to our understanding of customer behavior.","plain":"This is basic to our understanding of customer behavior.","note":"Used to highlight foundational principles or essential elements.","audio":"audio/1f0ee8b03de9e5d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-largely-due-to","text":"This is largely due to","kind":"frame","form":"This is largely due to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Explains the primary reason for something, while allowing for other minor contributing factors.","example":"This is largely due to the methodological constraints inherent in longitudinal studies.","plain":"This is mostly because of the methodological limitations in longitudinal studies.","note":"A common way to attribute cause, with a slight hedge.","audio":"audio/9fe3f1411a2cdf8e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-merely-a-hypothesis","text":"This is merely a hypothesis","kind":"collocation","form":"This is merely a hypothesis","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that a proposed explanation is only a theory, not yet proven or confirmed.","example":"This is merely a hypothesis at this stage, requiring extensive empirical testing for validation.","plain":"This is only a theory at this stage, requiring extensive empirical testing for validation.","note":"Used to downplay the certainty of a claim, emphasizing its speculative nature.","audio":"audio/512357532050887f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-merely-an-observation","text":"This is merely an observation","kind":"chunk","form":"This is merely an observation","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Presents a point as a simple remark rather than a definitive conclusion or strong claim.","example":"This is merely an observation, not a definitive statement about causality.","plain":"This is just an observation, not a definitive statement about causality.","note":"Used to downplay the strength or certainty of a statement.","audio":"audio/808afaeaacef0d38.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-diminish","text":"This is not to diminish","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to diminish","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To clarify that a preceding statement is not intended to lessen the importance of something else.","example":"This is not to diminish the efforts of the previous team, but rather to highlight new challenges.","plain":"I don't mean to lessen the previous team's efforts, but to highlight new challenges.","note":"Used to prevent misinterpretation, ensuring respect while introducing a different perspective.","audio":"audio/aa09da747ed29c36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-imply","text":"This is not to imply","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to imply","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Clarifies that a previous statement should not be taken to mean something specific.","example":"This is not to imply that qualitative methods are inferior, merely that they serve different purposes.","plain":"I don't mean to suggest that qualitative methods are inferior, just that they serve different purposes.","note":"Used to prevent misinterpretation or to refine a previous point.","audio":"audio/a92d0b48064be563.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-say-that","text":"This is not to say that","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to say that","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Clarifies a previous statement by explicitly ruling out a potential misinterpretation or extreme conclusion.","example":"This is not to say that the previous research was flawed, but rather that our understanding has evolved.","plain":"I'm not saying the previous research was flawed; instead, our understanding has evolved.","note":"A useful phrase for nuance, preventing misinterpretation and clarifying one's position in a discussion.","audio":"audio/376d0edc604690fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-not-to-suggest","text":"This is not to suggest","kind":"frame","form":"This is not to suggest","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Clarifies that a previous statement should not be interpreted in a particular way.","example":"This is not to suggest that the previous model was entirely flawed, but rather incomplete.","plain":"I don't mean to imply the previous model was completely wrong, just incomplete.","note":"Used to prevent misinterpretation or to refine a previous point.","audio":"audio/8161e2ccd0bd6dfe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-particularly-relevant-because","text":"This is particularly relevant because","kind":"frame","form":"This is particularly relevant because","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Explains why a specific point or piece of information is especially important in the current context.","example":"This is particularly relevant because our target demographic is shifting rapidly.","plain":"This is especially important because our target demographic is shifting rapidly.","note":"Connects a detail to the broader picture, justifying its inclusion.","audio":"audio/e2e57983ce2de7a5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-particularly-relevant-for","text":"This is particularly relevant for","kind":"frame","form":"This is particularly relevant for","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights the specific applicability or importance of a point to a certain area or group.","example":"This is particularly relevant for policymakers designing public health interventions.","plain":"This is especially important for policymakers designing public health interventions.","note":"Used to emphasize the specific impact or significance for a target audience.","audio":"audio/047496408373e753.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-predicated-on-the-idea","text":"This is predicated on the idea","kind":"frame","form":"This is predicated on the idea","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that something is based on or depends on a particular concept or belief.","example":"This is predicated on the idea that individuals act rationally in economic decision-making.","plain":"This is based on the idea that individuals act rationally in economic decision-making.","note":"A formal way to explain the foundational concept of an argument.","audio":"audio/533c5333c8543bbd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-is-subject-to-change","text":"This is subject to change","kind":"chunk","form":"This is subject to change","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States that something is not fixed and may be altered in the future.","example":"This is subject to change as more data becomes available and further analysis is conducted.","plain":"This might change as more data becomes available and further analysis is conducted.","note":"A common phrase to indicate provisionality, especially for plans or preliminary findings.","audio":"audio/9dff89e738aa9a55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-leads-me-to-conclude","text":"This leads me to conclude","kind":"frame","form":"This leads me to conclude","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a conclusion derived from the preceding discussion or evidence.","example":"This leads me to conclude that a multi-faceted approach is necessary for effective policy.","plain":"Based on this, I conclude that a multi-faceted approach is necessary for effective policy.","note":"A formal way to present a conclusion based on reasoning.","audio":"audio/6342147225f2c2ad.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-leads-me-to-conclude-that","text":"This leads me to conclude that","kind":"frame","form":"This leads me to conclude that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To state the logical inference or final judgment based on the presented information.","example":"This leads me to conclude that our current strategy needs significant adjustments.","plain":"Based on this, I conclude that our current strategy needs significant adjustments.","note":"A formal way to present a conclusion that logically follows from the evidence.","audio":"audio/20ba593e0ea51bbb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-leads-us-to-consider","text":"This leads us to consider","kind":"frame","form":"This leads us to consider","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce a logical next step in an argument or a new point for discussion.","example":"This leads us to consider the ethical implications of using AI in medical diagnostics.","plain":"This makes us think about the ethical implications of using AI in medical diagnostics.","note":"A formal way to transition to a new, related topic or question.","audio":"audio/79c8881d8e491262.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-naturally-leads-to","text":"This naturally leads to","kind":"frame","form":"This naturally leads to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Connects the current point to the next logical topic, showing a smooth progression.","example":"This naturally leads to our discussion on how to implement these new guidelines effectively.","plain":"This connects to our discussion on how to implement these new guidelines effectively.","note":"Creates a sense of logical flow, making transitions feel seamless.","audio":"audio/80d7ae95cbfdde17.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-opens-up-new-avenues-for","text":"This opens up new avenues for","kind":"frame","form":"This opens up new avenues for","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that a development or finding creates new possibilities for research or action.","example":"This opens up new avenues for interdisciplinary research into climate change adaptation.","plain":"This creates new possibilities for interdisciplinary research into climate change adaptation.","note":"A positive phrase to highlight future research directions.","audio":"audio/4ded05fceec01ffd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-perspective-is-echoed-by","text":"This perspective is echoed by","kind":"collocation","form":"This perspective is echoed by","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To state that a particular viewpoint is also expressed or supported by other sources or people.","example":"This perspective is echoed by several leading scholars in the field of cognitive psychology.","plain":"Several leading scholars in cognitive psychology also express this perspective.","note":"A formal way to show that an idea has broader support.","audio":"audio/750ed7e342dfd83b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-raises-an-important-point","text":"This raises an important point","kind":"collocation","form":"This raises an important point","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that a previous statement or observation brings up a significant issue for discussion.","example":"This raises an important point about the ethical responsibilities of researchers in data sharing.","plain":"This brings up a significant issue about the ethical responsibilities of researchers in data sharing.","note":"A common phrase to transition to a new, related discussion point.","audio":"audio/79c54ce1c2c2832b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-really-boils-down-to","text":"This really boils down to","kind":"frame","form":"This really boils down to","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To explain the fundamental essence or core issue of a complex situation.","example":"This really boils down to a question of resource allocation and strategic priorities.","plain":"The fundamental issue here is resource allocation and strategic priorities.","note":"Helps to simplify complex discussions by identifying the core elements.","audio":"audio/6ba3cc607b6ee3f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-seems-like-the-most-sensible-approach","text":"this seems like the most sensible approach","kind":"chunk","form":"this seems like the most sensible approach","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"This appears to be the most reasonable and practical way to deal with something.","example":"Given our current constraints, this seems like the most sensible approach.","plain":"Given our current constraints, this appears to be the most reasonable and practical way.","note":"A polite way to express a preference for a particular solution.","audio":"audio/6ea1f86e2e5af046.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-serves-to-highlight","text":"This serves to highlight","kind":"frame","form":"This serves to highlight","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To show that a previous point or example draws attention to something important.","example":"This serves to highlight the critical role of data analytics in modern business.","plain":"This helps to show the critical role of data analytics in modern business.","note":"A formal way to connect an example or piece of data to a significant observation.","audio":"audio/841d82e2afe7c5ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-serves-to-illustrate","text":"This serves to illustrate","kind":"frame","form":"This serves to illustrate","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To show that a previous point or example helps to explain a concept.","example":"This serves to illustrate the importance of proactive risk management.","plain":"This helps to explain the importance of proactive risk management.","note":"A formal way to connect an example or piece of data to a broader principle.","audio":"audio/aad1e13180bac899.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-stands-apart-from","text":"This stands apart from","kind":"frame","form":"This stands apart from","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that something is distinct or different from something else.","example":"This stands apart from traditional approaches by integrating qualitative and quantitative methods.","plain":"This is different from traditional approaches because it integrates qualitative and quantitative methods.","note":"Emphasizes uniqueness or a significant departure.","audio":"audio/967188211efa6e68.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-stands-in-stark-contrast-to","text":"This stands in stark contrast to","kind":"chunk","form":"This stands in stark contrast to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To emphasize a very clear and striking difference between two things.","example":"This stands in stark contrast to the previous administration's approach to environmental policy.","plain":"This is very different from the previous administration's approach to environmental policy.","note":"A strong way to highlight a significant difference, often used for emphasis.","audio":"audio/f1705be23c3130e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-suggests-a-potential-link","text":"This suggests a potential link","kind":"collocation","form":"This suggests a potential link","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Indicates that there might be a connection, but without stating it as a certainty.","example":"This suggests a potential link between early exposure and later developmental outcomes.","plain":"This indicates there might be a connection between early exposure and later developmental outcomes.","note":"A common way to cautiously propose a relationship in research.","audio":"audio/4faa33a7c5f0932f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.this-underscores-the-necessity-of","text":"This underscores the necessity of","kind":"frame","form":"This underscores the necessity of","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the vital need for a particular action or factor.","example":"This underscores the necessity of clear communication in remote teams.","plain":"This emphasizes the vital need for clear communication in remote teams.","note":"A strong way to emphasize a key takeaway or lesson, slightly more formal.","audio":"audio/11cf782e7f33fa5f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-acknowledge-a-concern","text":"to acknowledge a concern","kind":"collocation","form":"acknowledge a concern","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To recognize and show that you understand someone's worry or issue.","example":"I want to acknowledge a concern raised by the team about the increased workload.","plain":"I want to recognize and show that I understand the worry raised by the team about the increased workload.","note":"A diplomatic way to show empathy and validate another's point.","audio":"audio/74040cc3e5ac3981.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-add-a-bit-more-detail","text":"To add a bit more detail","kind":"chunk","form":"To add a bit more detail","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide additional specific information about a topic.","example":"To add a bit more detail, the project timeline has been extended by two weeks.","plain":"To give you more information, the project will now take two weeks longer.","note":"Informal and conversational, a common way to elaborate.","audio":"audio/65ca4dfd647d317d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-add-a-layer-of-complexity","text":"To add a layer of complexity","kind":"collocation","form":"To add a layer of complexity","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional factor or detail that makes a situation more complicated.","example":"To add a layer of complexity, we also need to consider the regulatory changes coming next year.","plain":"We also need to consider the regulatory changes coming next year, which makes the situation more complicated.","note":"Used when acknowledging or introducing additional challenges or nuances to an explanation.","audio":"audio/abc6f9daebf55348.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-the-core-issue","text":"To address the core issue","kind":"collocation","form":"to address the core issue","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To deal with the central or most important problem.","example":"We need to address the core issue of resource allocation before we can move forward.","plain":"We must deal with the central problem of resource allocation before progressing.","note":"A professional phrase emphasizing the need to tackle the fundamental problem.","audio":"audio/41a5bc8afaceb632.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-the-issue","text":"to address the issue","kind":"collocation","form":"address the issue","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To deal with a problem or concern directly and effectively.","example":"We need to address the issue of low team morale before it impacts productivity further.","plain":"We need to deal with the problem of low team morale before it impacts productivity further.","note":"A direct and professional way to state the need for action.","audio":"audio/f34b69daf79649d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-address-the-issue-head-on","text":"to address the issue head-on","kind":"chunk","form":"address the issue head-on","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To confront a problem directly and without avoidance.","example":"This conflict won't resolve itself; we need to address the issue head-on.","plain":"This conflict won't resolve itself; we need to confront the problem directly.","note":"Suggests courage and a direct approach to difficult problems.","audio":"audio/94e23c4dde5bea7e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-align-our-interests","text":"To align our interests","kind":"collocation","form":"to align our interests","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To ensure that the goals and objectives of different parties are in harmony.","example":"Our priority is to align our interests with yours to ensure a successful long-term partnership.","plain":"Our priority is to ensure that our goals and objectives are in harmony with yours to ensure a successful long-term partnership.","note":"Professional and strategic, focusing on shared goals.","audio":"audio/a3dc0ef9216874ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-align-with-the-companys-objectives","text":"to align with the company's objectives","kind":"collocation","form":"align with the company's objectives","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be consistent with or match the goals and aims of the organization.","example":"My career aspirations truly align with the company's objectives for growth and innovation.","plain":"My career aspirations truly match the company's goals for growth and innovation.","note":"Shows strategic thinking and understanding of the company's mission.","audio":"audio/d5546116d128988b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-answer-your-question-directly","text":"To answer your question directly","kind":"frame","form":"To answer your question directly","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Signals that you are about to give a straightforward and concise response.","example":"To answer your question directly, no, we do not anticipate any further delays.","plain":"To give you a direct answer, no, we do not anticipate any further delays.","note":"Useful for cutting through ambiguity and providing a clear response.","audio":"audio/0b0e44d820ce2d89.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-articulate-complex-ideas","text":"to articulate complex ideas","kind":"collocation","form":"articulate complex ideas","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To explain difficult or intricate concepts clearly and effectively.","example":"My role often required me to articulate complex ideas to non-technical stakeholders.","plain":"My role often required me to explain difficult concepts clearly to non-technical stakeholders.","note":"Highlights strong communication skills, especially for technical or specialized roles.","audio":"audio/2e75e688b41c8104.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-articulate-it-differently","text":"To articulate it differently","kind":"chunk","form":"To articulate it differently","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To express the same idea using different words for better understanding.","example":"To articulate it differently, we need to prioritize long-term sustainability over short-term gains.","plain":"To say it another way, we should focus on long-term sustainability, not short-term gains.","note":"Formal and precise, used when rephrasing for clarity or emphasis.","audio":"audio/486927dfd7260254.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-articulate-my-vision","text":"to articulate my vision","kind":"collocation","form":"to articulate my vision","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To express one's ideas and goals clearly and persuasively.","example":"I'm eager to articulate my vision for how this department can achieve its strategic objectives.","plain":"I want to clearly explain my ideas for how this department can reach its goals.","note":"Used by candidates to show leadership and strategic thinking.","audio":"audio/0b8022392eeb934a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-avoid-any-misunderstanding","text":"To avoid any misunderstanding","kind":"collocation","form":"To avoid any misunderstanding","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To clarify a point proactively to prevent misinterpretation or confusion.","example":"To avoid any misunderstanding, I'm referring to the first quarter of next fiscal year.","plain":"I'm referring to the first quarter of next fiscal year, so there's no confusion.","note":"Used when you want to be very precise and leave no room for error in interpretation.","audio":"audio/d3f35d52f4226219.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-avoid-setting-a-precedent","text":"To avoid setting a precedent","kind":"collocation","form":"to avoid setting a precedent","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To prevent establishing a rule or example that might be disadvantageous in future situations.","example":"We need to avoid setting a precedent that could lead to similar demands from other clients.","plain":"We need to prevent establishing an example that could lead to similar demands from other clients.","note":"Often used when refusing a request that could have wider implications.","audio":"audio/57c090f5ef89042c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-bottleneck","text":"to be a bottleneck","kind":"collocation","form":"a bottleneck","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A point in a process where progress slows down or stops, causing delays.","example":"The approval process is currently a bottleneck, delaying all subsequent project phases.","plain":"The approval process is currently causing delays, slowing all subsequent project phases.","note":"Identifies a specific constraint that impedes efficiency or flow.","audio":"audio/0b8e8ffd58d888db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-catalyst-for","text":"to be a catalyst for","kind":"chunk","form":"was a catalyst for","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be a person or thing that causes a significant change or event to happen.","example":"I was a catalyst for implementing new digital tools, significantly improving team productivity.","plain":"I caused the implementation of new digital tools, significantly improving team productivity.","note":"Strong, impactful phrase; implies driving innovation and progress.","audio":"audio/17fc0fa5a78c911f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-cause-for-concern","text":"to be a cause for concern","kind":"collocation","form":"a cause for concern","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Something that gives reason to worry or be anxious.","example":"The unexpected drop in server performance is definitely a cause for concern.","plain":"The unexpected drop in server performance is definitely something to worry about.","note":"A formal way to state that something is worrying, often used in reports or meetings.","audio":"audio/72df9efaaa3ca416.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-champion-of","text":"to be a champion of","kind":"chunk","form":"am a champion of","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who strongly supports or defends a particular cause, idea, or person.","example":"I am a champion of customer-centric design, always prioritizing user experience in product development.","plain":"I strongly support customer-centric design, always prioritizing user experience in product development.","note":"Stronger than \"proponent\"; implies active advocacy and belief.","audio":"audio/abf5ee5a81efee37.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-clear-communicator","text":"to be a clear communicator","kind":"chunk","form":"am a clear communicator","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who expresses ideas and information effectively and understandably.","example":"I am a clear communicator, ensuring that complex project updates are easily understood by all.","plain":"I express ideas well, ensuring that complex project updates are easily understood by all.","note":"Essential for any professional role; emphasizes effective information exchange.","audio":"audio/7cac9390c6041d1f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-coach-to","text":"to be a coach to","kind":"chunk","form":"was a coach to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To train and instruct someone, helping them improve their performance.","example":"I was a coach to new hires, ensuring they quickly integrated into the team and understood their roles.","plain":"I trained and instructed new hires, ensuring they quickly integrated into the team and understood their roles.","note":"Similar to mentor, but often implies more direct instruction and skill development.","audio":"audio/c3d764380dbcb398.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-confident-speaker","text":"to be a confident speaker","kind":"chunk","form":"am a confident speaker","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who speaks with assurance and self-possession.","example":"I am a confident speaker, comfortable presenting to large groups and senior management.","plain":"I speak with assurance, comfortable presenting to large groups and senior management.","note":"Useful for roles involving presentations, public speaking, or client interaction.","audio":"audio/762b8c93032e3a6f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-contributing-factor","text":"to be a contributing factor","kind":"collocation","form":"a contributing factor","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Something that helps cause a particular outcome or problem.","example":"Lack of clear communication was a contributing factor to the project's delay.","plain":"Lack of clear communication helped cause the project's delay.","note":"Identifies one element among several that led to a specific result.","audio":"audio/7ee3313efc7e9916.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-delicate-matter","text":"to be a delicate matter","kind":"collocation","form":"a delicate matter","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A sensitive issue requiring careful and tactful handling to avoid offense or problems.","example":"Discussing budget cuts with the team is always a delicate matter that needs careful preparation.","plain":"Discussing budget cuts with the team is always a sensitive issue that needs careful preparation.","note":"Implies that an issue is sensitive and requires diplomacy and caution.","audio":"audio/8fdba18ba2d68a99.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-driving-force","text":"to be a driving force","kind":"chunk","form":"was a driving force","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be the main influence or power behind a particular action or development.","example":"I was a driving force behind the successful integration of our new CRM system.","plain":"I was the main influence behind the successful integration of our new CRM system.","note":"Highlights leadership and significant impact on projects.","audio":"audio/cf096385cba03fd0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-facilitator-of","text":"to be a facilitator of","kind":"chunk","form":"am a facilitator of","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who helps a process or discussion happen more easily and effectively.","example":"I am a facilitator of cross-functional collaboration, ensuring smooth communication between departments.","plain":"I make cross-functional collaboration easier, ensuring smooth communication between departments.","note":"Highlights interpersonal skills and ability to bridge gaps.","audio":"audio/773a109da9473c30.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-game-changer","text":"to be a game changer","kind":"collocation","form":"a game changer","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Something that significantly alters a situation, strategy, or outcome.","example":"The new AI integration could be a game changer for our product development process.","plain":"The new AI integration could significantly alter our product development process.","note":"Describes an innovation or event that fundamentally changes the competitive landscape.","audio":"audio/e02c768ed6a34694.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-good-listener","text":"to be a good listener","kind":"chunk","form":"am a good listener","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who pays close attention to what others are saying.","example":"I am a good listener, which helps me understand client needs and build strong relationships.","plain":"I pay close attention to what others say, which helps me understand client needs and build strong relationships.","note":"Important interpersonal skill, especially for client-facing or leadership roles.","audio":"audio/43585b25776a4e2f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-grey-area","text":"to be a grey area","kind":"collocation","form":"a grey area","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Something unclear, undefined, or open to interpretation, lacking clear rules.","example":"The company policy on remote work is still a grey area, causing some confusion.","plain":"The company policy on remote work is still unclear, causing some confusion.","note":"Refers to a situation where rules or definitions are ambiguous, leading to uncertainty.","audio":"audio/0ec6fc83c19ed197.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-key-consideration","text":"to be a key consideration","kind":"collocation","form":"a key consideration","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"An important point or factor that must be thought about carefully.","example":"User experience is always a key consideration when developing new software features.","plain":"User experience is always an important point when developing new software features.","note":"Identifies a crucial factor that influences decisions or outcomes.","audio":"audio/dcb75e7df02d467c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-key-contributor","text":"to be a key contributor","kind":"chunk","form":"was a key contributor","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make an important and significant input to a project or team.","example":"I was a key contributor to the strategic planning process, shaping future company direction.","plain":"I made an important input to the strategic planning process, shaping future company direction.","note":"Modest yet impactful way to describe one's role in a team.","audio":"audio/64723f3f72cc1665.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-mentor-to","text":"to be a mentor to","kind":"chunk","form":"was a mentor to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To guide and advise someone, especially a less experienced person, in their career.","example":"In my previous role, I was a mentor to junior colleagues, helping them develop their skills.","plain":"In my previous role, I guided and advised junior colleagues, helping them develop their skills.","note":"Shows leadership, teaching ability, and a collaborative spirit.","audio":"audio/a7699d05704aab09.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-non-starter","text":"to be a non-starter","kind":"collocation","form":"a non-starter","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"An idea or plan that has no chance of success or is not worth pursuing.","example":"Proposing a complete overhaul of the system right now would be a non-starter.","plain":"Proposing a complete overhaul of the system right now would have no chance of success.","note":"Indicates that an idea is unfeasible or unacceptable from the outset.","audio":"audio/4216ae5c619f5ffe.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-persuasive-speaker","text":"to be a persuasive speaker","kind":"chunk","form":"am a persuasive speaker","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who is good at convincing others to agree with their point of view.","example":"I am a persuasive speaker, often successfully advocating for new strategies within the team.","plain":"I am good at convincing others, often successfully advocating for new strategies within the team.","note":"Highlights influence and negotiation skills.","audio":"audio/fe23a86eff61b6ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-pressing-matter","text":"to be a pressing matter","kind":"collocation","form":"a pressing matter","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"An urgent issue that needs immediate attention or action.","example":"Resolving the client's complaint is a pressing matter that requires our full focus.","plain":"Resolving the client's complaint is an urgent issue that requires our full focus.","note":"Emphasizes the urgency and importance of an issue, demanding prompt resolution.","audio":"audio/61e1edfe899b76bb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-proponent-of","text":"to be a proponent of","kind":"chunk","form":"am a proponent of","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who supports an idea, plan, or way of doing things.","example":"I am a proponent of agile methodologies, believing they lead to more efficient project delivery.","plain":"I support agile methodologies, believing they lead to more efficient project delivery.","note":"Formal way to express support for a particular approach or philosophy.","audio":"audio/38a409204ec530f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-significant-hurdle","text":"to be a significant hurdle","kind":"collocation","form":"a significant hurdle","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A major obstacle or difficulty that must be overcome.","example":"Securing adequate funding remains a significant hurdle for the expansion plan.","plain":"Securing adequate funding remains a major obstacle for the expansion plan.","note":"Highlights a substantial challenge that requires considerable effort to surmount.","audio":"audio/7814793688356ef4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-skilled-negotiator","text":"to be a skilled negotiator","kind":"chunk","form":"am a skilled negotiator","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who is good at discussing things formally to reach an agreement.","example":"I am a skilled negotiator, having successfully closed several high-value contracts.","plain":"I am good at reaching agreements, having successfully closed several high-value contracts.","note":"Important for sales, business development, or management roles.","audio":"audio/2845d28c6e8ba17d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-slippery-slope","text":"to be a slippery slope","kind":"collocation","form":"a slippery slope","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A course of action that will lead to increasingly bad or undesirable results.","example":"Compromising on quality standards could be a slippery slope towards losing customer trust.","plain":"Compromising on quality standards could lead to increasingly bad results towards losing customer trust.","note":"Warns that a seemingly small negative step could lead to a series of worse outcomes.","audio":"audio/f440dc113162d124.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-strategic-planner","text":"to be a strategic planner","kind":"chunk","form":"am a strategic planner","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who plans with a focus on long-term goals and overall objectives.","example":"I am a strategic planner, always considering the broader implications of immediate decisions.","plain":"I plan with long-term goals in mind, always considering the broader implications of immediate decisions.","note":"Highlights foresight and ability to connect actions to larger objectives.","audio":"audio/5fe0d5548532e542.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-strong-leader","text":"to be a strong leader","kind":"chunk","form":"am a strong leader","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be someone who effectively guides, motivates, and inspires a team or organization.","example":"I am a strong leader who empowers team members to take ownership of their projects.","plain":"I guide and inspire effectively, empowering team members to take ownership of their projects.","note":"Direct way to state leadership qualities; confident tone.","audio":"audio/f81f1cdf00944dbc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-stumbling-block","text":"to be a stumbling block","kind":"collocation","form":"a stumbling block","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"An obstacle or difficulty that prevents progress or success.","example":"Lack of clear leadership proved to be a stumbling block for the entire initiative.","plain":"Lack of clear leadership proved to be an obstacle for the entire initiative.","note":"Highlights a specific impediment that hinders advancement.","audio":"audio/71f644d1ac250bdc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-tall-order","text":"to be a tall order","kind":"collocation","form":"a tall order","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A difficult or demanding task or request.","example":"Completing the entire project by Friday is a tall order, but we'll do our best.","plain":"Completing the entire project by Friday is a difficult task, but we'll do our best.","note":"Suggests a challenging request that will require significant effort to fulfill.","audio":"audio/70ef96f58c72de2e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-thorny-issue","text":"to be a thorny issue","kind":"collocation","form":"a thorny issue","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A difficult and often controversial problem that is hard to resolve.","example":"Employee compensation remains a thorny issue that we need to address in the next quarter.","plain":"Employee compensation remains a difficult and controversial problem that we need to address in the next quarter.","note":"Describes a problem that is complex, contentious, and potentially painful to deal with.","audio":"audio/c162b2f15c80cfd0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-tricky-situation","text":"to be a tricky situation","kind":"collocation","form":"a tricky situation","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A difficult or complicated circumstance that requires careful handling.","example":"Dealing with the conflicting demands of two major stakeholders is a tricky situation.","plain":"Dealing with the conflicting demands of two major stakeholders is a difficult circumstance.","note":"Describes a complex scenario that needs skill and diplomacy to navigate.","audio":"audio/1fe808b3611a0b0e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-viable-path","text":"to be a viable path","kind":"collocation","form":"be a viable path","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be a practical and achievable course of action.","example":"Outsourcing this task could be a viable path if we can't hire more staff internally.","plain":"Outsourcing this task could be a practical and achievable course of action if we can't hire more staff internally.","note":"Assesses the feasibility and effectiveness of an option.","audio":"audio/dc859b754d68c5e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-vicious-circle","text":"to be a vicious circle","kind":"collocation","form":"a vicious circle","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A continuing unpleasant situation that causes itself and is difficult to escape.","example":"The lack of training leads to errors, which then require more training; it's a vicious circle.","plain":"The lack of training leads to errors, which then require more training; it's a continuing unpleasant situation.","note":"Describes a self-perpetuating negative cycle, often hard to break.","audio":"audio/f83830560dd5417d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-a-wake-up-call","text":"to be a wake-up call","kind":"collocation","form":"a wake-up call","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"An event that makes one realize a problem or danger that was previously ignored.","example":"The recent security breach was a wake-up call for us to invest more in cybersecurity.","plain":"The recent security breach made us realize we needed to invest more in cybersecurity.","note":"Describes an event that forces a realization of a serious issue, prompting action.","audio":"audio/57c50aa03a9bd267.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-adaptable-to","text":"to be adaptable to","kind":"chunk","form":"am adaptable to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be able to adjust easily to new conditions, environments, or changes.","example":"I am adaptable to new technologies and quickly learn how to utilize them effectively.","plain":"I am able to adjust to new technologies and quickly learn how to utilize them effectively.","note":"Shows flexibility and resilience in dynamic workplaces.","audio":"audio/6fa8f6f7d23c0288.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-adept-at","text":"to be adept at","kind":"chunk","form":"am adept at","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be very skilled or proficient at something.","example":"I am adept at managing multiple projects simultaneously while maintaining high quality.","plain":"I am very skilled at managing multiple projects simultaneously while maintaining high quality.","note":"Formal and precise way to state a skill; sounds confident.","audio":"audio/0cc949ec898731fc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-at-a-crossroads","text":"to be at a crossroads","kind":"chunk","form":"at a crossroads","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be at a point where an important decision must be made, with significant consequences.","example":"The company is at a crossroads, needing to decide whether to expand internationally or focus domestically.","plain":"The company is at a point where an important decision must be made, needing to decide whether to expand internationally or focus domestically.","note":"Implies a critical juncture requiring careful consideration.","audio":"audio/c7b0ae3998335853.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-caught-in-the-crossfire","text":"to be caught in the crossfire","kind":"chunk","form":"caught in the crossfire","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be unintentionally involved in a conflict between other people or groups.","example":"I feel like I'm caught in the crossfire between the two department heads arguing over resources.","plain":"I feel like I'm unintentionally involved in the conflict between the two department heads arguing over resources.","note":"Describes being an innocent party affected by others' disputes.","audio":"audio/aad755f96dd7007c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-committed-to","text":"to be committed to","kind":"chunk","form":"am committed to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be dedicated and loyal to a particular task, cause, or organization.","example":"I am committed to continuous professional development and staying current with industry trends.","plain":"I am dedicated to continuous professional development and staying current with industry trends.","note":"Shows reliability and a strong work ethic.","audio":"audio/7fe5b255f1a5bba6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-detail-oriented","text":"to be detail-oriented","kind":"chunk","form":"am detail-oriented","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To pay close attention to small elements and ensure accuracy.","example":"I am detail-oriented, which is crucial for ensuring accuracy in financial reporting.","plain":"I pay close attention to small things, which is crucial for ensuring accuracy in financial reporting.","note":"Important for roles requiring precision and accuracy.","audio":"audio/2de9c4abe8992b21.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-difficult-to-justify","text":"To be difficult to justify","kind":"chunk","form":"is difficult to justify","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be hard to explain or defend a decision or position.","example":"An additional 15% increase at this stage is difficult to justify to our stakeholders.","plain":"It's hard to explain a 15% increase at this point to our stakeholders.","note":"Used when a request or demand lacks clear reasoning or support.","audio":"audio/8455620512624940.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-eager-to","text":"to be eager to","kind":"frame","form":"am eager to","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be very keen and enthusiastic to do something.","example":"I am eager to apply my skills in a dynamic and forward-thinking environment.","plain":"I am very keen to apply my skills in a dynamic and forward-thinking environment.","note":"Expresses enthusiasm and readiness; versatile frame.","audio":"audio/178653fba3a3f110.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-fair-to-them","text":"to be fair to them","kind":"chunk","form":"To be fair to them","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Introduces a point that acknowledges another party's perspective or mitigates their actions.","example":"To be fair to them, they were working with very limited resources at the time.","plain":"They were working with very limited resources at the time, which is an important point to consider.","note":"Used to offer a balanced view, often when discussing past colleagues or employers.","audio":"audio/2f52c31ef33539de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-flexible-on","text":"To be flexible on","kind":"collocation","form":"to be flexible on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be willing to change or adapt one's position or terms regarding something.","example":"We're prepared to be flexible on the delivery schedule if you can guarantee the quality.","plain":"We're prepared to change the delivery schedule if you can guarantee the quality.","note":"Signals a willingness to adapt, which is crucial in negotiation.","audio":"audio/c42f2081eb67b3bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-hard-to-swallow","text":"To be hard to swallow","kind":"chunk","form":"is hard to swallow","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be difficult to accept or believe, often because it's unfair or unreasonable.","example":"Their demand for immediate payment without any grace period is hard to swallow.","plain":"Their demand for immediate payment with no grace period is difficult to accept.","note":"Informal but common; expresses strong disapproval or disbelief.","audio":"audio/14fdb3fd98d39102.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-in-a-bind","text":"to be in a bind","kind":"chunk","form":"in a bind","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in a difficult or awkward situation.","example":"If we lose that client, we'll really be in a bind financially for the next quarter.","plain":"If we lose that client, we'll really be in a difficult situation financially for the next quarter.","note":"Informal but widely used in professional contexts to describe a predicament.","audio":"audio/d60b72871a9611f7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-in-a-tight-spot","text":"to be in a tight spot","kind":"chunk","form":"in a tight spot","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in a difficult or awkward situation.","example":"If we don't secure that funding, we'll be in a tight spot regarding project completion.","plain":"If we don't secure that funding, we'll be in a difficult situation regarding project completion.","note":"Similar to \"in a bind,\" but perhaps slightly more common in general conversation.","audio":"audio/6a9ee79ca1a20184.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-in-the-firing-line","text":"to be in the firing line","kind":"chunk","form":"in the firing line","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in a position where one is likely to be criticized or blamed.","example":"If this project fails, the whole team will be in the firing line, not just the manager.","plain":"If this project fails, the whole team will be in a position to be criticized, not just the manager.","note":"Suggests vulnerability to criticism or negative consequences.","audio":"audio/ff79c5c1bb53aeef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-in-uncharted-territory","text":"to be in uncharted territory","kind":"collocation","form":"in uncharted territory","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in a new or unfamiliar situation with no previous experience to guide you.","example":"Implementing this new technology means we're in uncharted territory, so we'll learn as we go.","plain":"Implementing this new technology means we're in a new situation, so we'll learn as we go.","note":"Highlights novelty and the need for adaptability.","audio":"audio/7860887f9a96fe8f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-instrumental-in","text":"to be instrumental in","kind":"chunk","form":"was instrumental in","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To play a crucial or essential role in achieving a particular outcome.","example":"I was instrumental in developing the new client onboarding process, which improved efficiency.","plain":"I was very important in developing the new client onboarding process, which improved efficiency.","note":"Strong way to highlight significant contributions; sounds formal and impactful.","audio":"audio/7e40170f1eaea7ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-more-exact","text":"To be more exact","kind":"chunk","form":"To be more exact","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to provide a more precise or accurate piece of information.","example":"To be more exact, the meeting starts at 9:05 AM, not just \"after nine.\"","plain":"The meeting starts at 9:05 AM, not just \"after nine.\"","note":"Used to correct or refine a previous, less precise statement.","audio":"audio/f650490fc8e48780.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-on-thin-ice","text":"to be on thin ice","kind":"chunk","form":"on thin ice","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in a precarious or risky situation, where one wrong move could lead to trouble.","example":"After missing two deadlines, John is really on thin ice with the project manager.","plain":"After missing two deadlines, John is in a precarious situation with the project manager.","note":"Implies a warning or a delicate situation requiring extreme care.","audio":"audio/b87a13e1a4ef1ac6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-open-to-feedback","text":"to be open to feedback","kind":"chunk","form":"am open to feedback","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be willing to accept criticism or suggestions for improvement.","example":"I am open to feedback and always seek opportunities to refine my approach.","plain":"I am willing to accept criticism and always seek opportunities to refine my approach.","note":"Shows humility and a desire for growth; a positive trait.","audio":"audio/63d779c072c0c065.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-passionate-about","text":"to be passionate about","kind":"chunk","form":"am passionate about","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To have very strong feelings or enthusiasm for something.","example":"I am passionate about leveraging technology to solve real-world business problems.","plain":"I have very strong feelings about leveraging technology to solve real-world business problems.","note":"Common in interviews to express genuine interest and motivation.","audio":"audio/b0b02cf0cf15419e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-prepared-to-walk-away","text":"To be prepared to walk away","kind":"collocation","form":"to be prepared to walk away","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be ready to end negotiations if satisfactory terms cannot be reached.","example":"We need to be prepared to walk away if they don't improve their offer on the equity stake.","plain":"We need to be ready to end negotiations if they don't improve their offer on the equity stake.","note":"A strong negotiating stance, indicating a limit to compromise.","audio":"audio/97bcd8e619e3f764.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-proactive","text":"to be proactive","kind":"chunk","form":"be proactive","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To act in anticipation of future problems, needs, or changes.","example":"We need to be proactive in identifying potential risks, rather than just reacting to them.","plain":"We need to act in anticipation of potential risks, rather than just reacting to them.","note":"Emphasizes foresight and taking initiative.","audio":"audio/1f42197a7f87c5b6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-proactive-in","text":"to be proactive in","kind":"chunk","form":"am proactive in","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To take action and anticipate problems before they occur.","example":"I am proactive in identifying potential issues and implementing preventative measures.","plain":"I take action before problems arise in identifying potential issues and implementing preventative measures.","note":"Highly valued trait; shows foresight and responsibility.","audio":"audio/7db8861f730a1280.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-problematic","text":"To be problematic","kind":"chunk","form":"is problematic","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be difficult, causing issues, or creating obstacles.","example":"The clause regarding unlimited revisions is problematic for our fixed-price model.","plain":"The clause about unlimited revisions causes issues for our fixed-price model.","note":"A formal and slightly understated way to describe a difficulty.","audio":"audio/2826c50d884dea62.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-quite-honest","text":"to be quite honest","kind":"chunk","form":"to be quite honest","level":"C2","situations":["interviews","feedback"],"meaning":"used to introduce a frank or direct statement.","example":"To be quite honest, I think we underestimated the complexity of this task.","plain":"Frankly, I think we underestimated the complexity of this task.","note":"Similar to \"to be perfectly honest,\" signals directness.","audio":"audio/aa244d3329c0f302.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-reasonable","text":"To be reasonable","kind":"chunk","form":"to be reasonable","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To act or think in a sensible, fair, and practical way.","example":"We're asking them to be reasonable about the payment schedule, given our current situation.","plain":"We're asking them to be sensible about the payment schedule, considering our situation.","note":"A common appeal for fairness and practicality in demands or expectations.","audio":"audio/1f9f574de67d9446.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-resourceful-in","text":"to be resourceful in","kind":"chunk","form":"am resourceful in","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be good at finding quick and clever ways to overcome difficulties.","example":"When faced with limited resources, I am resourceful in finding alternative solutions.","plain":"When faced with limited resources, I am good at finding clever ways to overcome difficulties.","note":"Implies creativity and problem-solving under constraints.","audio":"audio/04ae4adc7e8d782b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-results-driven","text":"to be results-driven","kind":"chunk","form":"am results-driven","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be focused on achieving specific outcomes and measurable results.","example":"I am results-driven and consistently strive to exceed performance targets.","plain":"I am focused on achieving outcomes and consistently strive to exceed performance targets.","note":"Common in sales, marketing, and management roles; emphasizes effectiveness.","audio":"audio/19f4e649fbba2ce3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-specific","text":"To be specific","kind":"chunk","form":"To be specific","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Introduces a statement that provides exact details rather than generalities.","example":"To be specific, we need five units delivered by Tuesday, not just 'soon'.","plain":"We need five units delivered by Tuesday, to be exact, not just 'soon'.","note":"Similar to 'to be precise', used to add clarity and avoid vagueness.","audio":"audio/7929a4619b7a699b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-stretched-thin","text":"to be stretched thin","kind":"chunk","form":"stretched thin","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To have too much work or too many responsibilities, leading to being overworked.","example":"Everyone on the team is stretched thin, so adding another project right now isn't feasible.","plain":"Everyone on the team has too much work, so adding another project right now isn't feasible.","note":"A common way to describe resource constraints and workload issues.","audio":"audio/437e5f8e79fc16f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-symptomatic-of","text":"to be symptomatic of","kind":"collocation","form":"symptomatic of","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To indicate a larger, underlying issue or trend.","example":"The recent decline in sales is symptomatic of deeper issues within our marketing strategy.","plain":"The recent decline in sales indicates deeper issues within our marketing strategy.","note":"Suggests that a visible problem is merely a sign of a more fundamental, hidden issue.","audio":"audio/0f00c6f5d10ecf1e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-unacceptable","text":"To be unacceptable","kind":"chunk","form":"is unacceptable","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Not satisfactory or allowable; cannot be agreed to.","example":"A delay of more than two weeks is unacceptable for this critical project.","plain":"A delay exceeding two weeks cannot be allowed for this critical project.","note":"A firm and direct statement of rejection or disapproval.","audio":"audio/5ae7874c96418de9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-consideration","text":"to be under consideration","kind":"chunk","form":"under consideration","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be being thought about or discussed.","example":"Your proposal for the new marketing campaign is currently under consideration.","plain":"Your proposal for the new marketing campaign is currently being thought about.","note":"A formal way to indicate that something is being evaluated.","audio":"audio/b53f99f488652113.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-discussion","text":"to be under discussion","kind":"chunk","form":"under discussion","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be the subject of conversation or debate.","example":"The possibility of extending the project deadline is still under discussion.","plain":"The possibility of extending the project deadline is still being talked about.","note":"Indicates that a topic is being actively considered but not yet decided.","audio":"audio/330d0a5dc758604a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-negotiation","text":"to be under negotiation","kind":"chunk","form":"under negotiation","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in the process of being discussed in order to reach an agreement.","example":"The terms of the new contract are still under negotiation with the supplier.","plain":"The terms of the new contract are still being discussed to reach an agreement with the supplier.","note":"Indicates an ongoing process of bargaining and discussion.","audio":"audio/0a41fb87922b66d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-no-illusion","text":"to be under no illusion","kind":"chunk","form":"under no illusion","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To fully understand the reality of a difficult situation, without false hopes.","example":"I'm under no illusion that this will be an easy task; it will require significant effort.","plain":"I fully understand that this will not be an easy task; it will require significant effort.","note":"Expresses a realistic and often sober assessment of a challenge.","audio":"audio/a2660279569172cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-review","text":"to be under review","kind":"chunk","form":"under review","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in the process of being formally examined or evaluated.","example":"The new proposal is currently under review by the executive committee.","plain":"The new proposal is currently being formally examined by the executive committee.","note":"A neutral term for an ongoing evaluation process.","audio":"audio/7488305d90aed960.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-scrutiny","text":"to be under scrutiny","kind":"chunk","form":"under scrutiny","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be subjected to close and careful examination or observation.","example":"Our department is currently under scrutiny due to the recent performance dip.","plain":"Our department is currently being closely examined due to the recent performance dip.","note":"Suggests a period of intense review, often implying potential problems.","audio":"audio/16c39a362e93ce09.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-the-impression","text":"to be under the impression","kind":"chunk","form":"under the impression","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To believe something, often based on indirect information, which may or may not be true.","example":"I was under the impression that the report was due next Friday, not this Friday.","plain":"I believed that the report was due next Friday, not this Friday.","note":"Used to politely correct a misunderstanding or state a belief that might be wrong.","audio":"audio/110a4adb5e0a9c4e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-the-microscope","text":"to be under the microscope","kind":"chunk","form":"under the microscope","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be subjected to extremely close and detailed examination.","example":"After the audit, our financial records are really under the microscope.","plain":"After the audit, our financial records are being extremely closely examined.","note":"Stronger than \"under scrutiny,\" implying intense and detailed inspection.","audio":"audio/1de6509923fcf2dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-way","text":"to be under way","kind":"chunk","form":"under way","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in progress; happening now.","example":"The implementation of the new system is now under way and progressing well.","plain":"The implementation of the new system is now in progress and progressing well.","note":"A formal way to state that an activity has started and is continuing.","audio":"audio/84c49de3801f4ede.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-under-wraps","text":"to be under wraps","kind":"chunk","form":"under wraps","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be kept secret or hidden from public knowledge.","example":"The details of the new product launch are still under wraps until the official announcement.","plain":"The details of the new product launch are still being kept secret until the official announcement.","note":"Used for confidential information or plans.","audio":"audio/f3b4a9e1cdc03f56.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-unrealistic","text":"To be unrealistic","kind":"chunk","form":"is unrealistic","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To have expectations or demands that are not practical or achievable.","example":"Their demand for a 24-hour turnaround on this complex project is unrealistic.","plain":"Their demand for a 24-hour turnaround on this complex project is not practical.","note":"A direct but professional way to state that a demand is unachievable.","audio":"audio/42df49ad287be7fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-untenable","text":"To be untenable","kind":"chunk","form":"is untenable","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Not able to be defended against criticism or attack; unsustainable.","example":"Their current pricing model is untenable in the long term given market competition.","plain":"Their current pricing model cannot be defended long-term due to market competition.","note":"Formal and strong; implies a position or argument cannot be maintained.","audio":"audio/4af69b82e97202f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-unworkable","text":"To be unworkable","kind":"chunk","form":"is unworkable","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"Not practical or able to be implemented successfully.","example":"The proposed solution, while creative, is unworkable due to technical limitations.","plain":"The proposed solution, though creative, cannot be implemented due to technical limits.","note":"A professional way to reject a proposal based on practical feasibility.","audio":"audio/e5c988c3d724395c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-up-against-it","text":"to be up against it","kind":"chunk","form":"up against it","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be facing significant difficulties or strong opposition.","example":"With the tight deadline and limited staff, we're really up against it to finish on time.","plain":"With the tight deadline and limited staff, we're really facing significant difficulties to finish on time.","note":"Conveys a sense of struggle against challenging circumstances.","audio":"audio/94fdbeb71a7c91af.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-well-versed-in","text":"to be well-versed in","kind":"chunk","form":"am well-versed in","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To be very knowledgeable or skilled in a particular subject or area.","example":"I am well-versed in various data analysis techniques, including statistical modeling.","plain":"I am very knowledgeable about various data analysis techniques, including statistical modeling.","note":"A formal way to express expertise; often used in professional contexts.","audio":"audio/c03bb1e64c32cebf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-be-willing-to-budge","text":"To be willing to budge","kind":"collocation","form":"to be willing to budge","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To be willing to change one's position or opinion slightly.","example":"They don't seem to be willing to budge on the delivery timeline, which is a problem for us.","plain":"They don't seem to be willing to change their position slightly on the delivery timeline, which is a problem for us.","note":"Informal but common. Implies a slight movement from an initial stance.","audio":"audio/4ad49db6b3f0420d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bear-in-mind","text":"To bear in mind","kind":"collocation","form":"bear in mind","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To remember and consider a piece of information when making a decision.","example":"Please bear in mind that our team is already at full capacity for the next quarter.","plain":"Please remember that our team is already at full capacity for the next quarter.","note":"Polite way to introduce an important consideration.","audio":"audio/6bf1582c3b4f40ee.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-begin-with-id-like-to","text":"To begin with, I'd like to","kind":"frame","form":"To begin with, I'd like to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce the first point or action in a presentation or discussion.","example":"To begin with, I'd like to briefly outline the scope of our research project.","plain":"First, I want to briefly outline the scope of our research project.","note":"A polite and common way to start a formal presentation or discussion.","audio":"audio/ca3690a5730d8027.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bite-the-bullet","text":"to bite the bullet","kind":"chunk","form":"bite the bullet","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To face a difficult or unpleasant situation with courage and endure it.","example":"We'll just have to bite the bullet and work extra hours to meet this tight deadline.","plain":"We'll just have to face the difficult situation and work extra hours to meet this tight deadline.","note":"Implies accepting an unavoidable hardship, often with a sense of resignation.","audio":"audio/ef232caf70b5c5d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bounce-ideas-off","text":"to bounce ideas off","kind":"chunk","form":"bounce some ideas off","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discuss ideas with someone to get their feedback and perspective.","example":"Could I bounce some ideas off you about the project proposal before I finalize it?","plain":"Could I discuss some ideas with you about the project proposal before I finalize it?","note":"Suggests an informal, exploratory discussion to refine thoughts.","audio":"audio/a8ae9d847866d38e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-brainstorm-some-ideas","text":"to brainstorm some ideas","kind":"collocation","form":"brainstorm some ideas","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To generate a large number of creative ideas quickly, often in a group.","example":"Let's take 15 minutes to brainstorm some ideas for improving team collaboration.","plain":"Let's take 15 minutes to generate a large number of ideas for improving team collaboration.","note":"Focuses on creative, uninhibited idea generation.","audio":"audio/cf623c1d381a1086.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-it-all-together","text":"to bring it all together","kind":"chunk","form":"to bring it all together","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To summarize and synthesize various points into a coherent conclusion.","example":"To bring it all together, our strategy needs to be agile and customer-centric.","plain":"To summarize everything, our strategy needs to be agile and customer-centric.","note":"Useful for synthesizing diverse contributions into a unified message.","audio":"audio/a8c195edc7bed38a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-things-to-a-close","text":"To bring things to a close","kind":"frame","form":"To bring things to a close","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Used to signal that the discussion or meeting is about to end.","example":"To bring things to a close, I think we've made good progress today.","plain":"To finish up, I believe we achieved a lot today.","note":"A formal and clear way to transition to the end of a conversation.","audio":"audio/823f165016274f3d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-bring-us-back","text":"To bring us back","kind":"frame","form":"To bring us back","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to redirect the conversation to the main topic after a digression.","example":"To bring us back to the core issue, what are the immediate next steps for implementation?","plain":"To return to the main issue, what are the immediate next steps for implementation?","note":"Polite and effective for steering the conversation. Often used by the chair.","audio":"audio/6ed79349e8d7497f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-champion-new-ideas","text":"to champion new ideas","kind":"collocation","form":"championed new ideas","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To strongly support and promote new concepts or initiatives.","example":"I consistently championed new ideas for product development, leading to several successful launches.","plain":"I consistently supported and promoted new ideas for product development, leading to several successful launches.","note":"Shows leadership, innovation, and persuasive skills.","audio":"audio/6afb52a256409482.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-check-my-understanding","text":"To check my understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"To check my understanding","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To confirm one's own comprehension of what has been said or explained.","example":"Just to check my understanding, you're proposing a complete overhaul of the system?","plain":"Just to confirm what I understand, you're proposing a complete overhaul of the system?","note":"Used by the listener to ensure they have correctly grasped the speaker's message.","audio":"audio/0b0a3b95cf47e538.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-circle-back-to-your-point","text":"to circle back to your point","kind":"frame","form":"To circle back to your point","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates a return to a previously mentioned topic or idea in the conversation.","example":"To circle back to your point about the timeline, I think we need more flexibility.","plain":"Returning to what you said earlier about the timeline, I have a thought.","note":"Professional and useful for managing discussion flow, ensuring all points are addressed.","audio":"audio/e2a5a31d61df00fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-what-i-mean","text":"To clarify what I mean","kind":"frame","form":"To clarify what I mean","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain a previous statement more clearly, often because it might have been misunderstood.","example":"To clarify what I mean, I'm suggesting a temporary solution, not a permanent one.","plain":"I'm suggesting a temporary solution, not a permanent one, to explain my previous statement more clearly.","note":"Useful for correcting potential misinterpretations or adding precision to a statement.","audio":"audio/295fd59e927f97e5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-what-you-mean","text":"To clarify what you mean","kind":"frame","form":"To clarify what you mean","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To ask the questioner to explain their query more precisely.","example":"To clarify what you mean, are you asking about the short-term or long-term impact?","plain":"Could you explain your question more precisely? Are you asking about the short-term or long-term impact?","note":"Essential for ensuring you understand the question before attempting to answer it.","audio":"audio/6ce6dfcec5fa5bd2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-your-position","text":"To clarify your position","kind":"collocation","form":"to clarify your position","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To explain one's stance or viewpoint more clearly and precisely.","example":"Before we proceed, I'd like you to clarify your position on the intellectual property rights.","plain":"Before we continue, please explain your stance on intellectual property rights more clearly.","note":"A professional request for clear communication, often to avoid misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/615844bf0e635a0f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-clarify-i-meant","text":"To clarify, I meant","kind":"frame","form":"To clarify, I meant","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide a clearer explanation of a previous statement.","example":"To clarify, I meant that the initial phase is complete, not the entire project.","plain":"To make it clearer, I intended to say that the initial phase is complete.","note":"Direct and concise for correcting or adding precision to a statement.","audio":"audio/1747f3d890d28219.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-close-this-meeting","text":"To close this meeting","kind":"frame","form":"To close this meeting","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A formal way to signal the end of the meeting.","example":"To close this meeting, I'd like to thank everyone for their valuable contributions.","plain":"To end this meeting, I'd like to thank everyone for their valuable contributions.","note":"Formal and polite, typically used by the chair to conclude the session.","audio":"audio/cb5378c0e64ddb9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-come-down-to","text":"to come down to","kind":"chunk","form":"comes down to","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be essentially a matter of; to be the most important factor.","example":"Ultimately, the success of this project comes down to effective teamwork and communication.","plain":"Ultimately, the success of this project depends on effective teamwork and communication.","note":"Used to simplify a complex situation to its core element or deciding factor.","audio":"audio/ac928ffeda56e423.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-come-to-a-conclusion","text":"to come to a conclusion","kind":"chunk","form":"come to a conclusion","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To reach a final decision or judgment after consideration.","example":"After reviewing all the data, we've come to a conclusion about the best strategy.","plain":"After reviewing all the data, we've reached a final decision about the best strategy.","note":"Implies a definitive outcome after a process of analysis.","audio":"audio/3bc4e96d8cfb8666.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-come-to-a-decision","text":"To come to a decision","kind":"collocation","form":"to come to a decision","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make a choice or judgment after consideration.","example":"We need to come to a decision on the final project timeline by the end of the day.","plain":"We need to make a choice on the final project timeline by the end of the day.","note":"Standard phrase for making a choice.","audio":"audio/38967c5a38b07369.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-come-to-terms","text":"To come to terms","kind":"chunk","form":"to come to terms","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To reach an agreement or accept a difficult situation.","example":"We need to come to terms on the final deliverables before signing the contract.","plain":"We must agree on the final deliverables before signing the contract.","note":"Can mean 'agree' or 'accept'; in negotiation, it usually means 'agree'.","audio":"audio/ca55b67116528b90.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-come-up-with-a-solution","text":"to come up with a solution","kind":"chunk","form":"come up with a solution","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To think of or create an answer to a problem.","example":"We need to brainstorm quickly and come up with a solution for the unexpected budget cut.","plain":"We need to brainstorm quickly and find an answer for the unexpected budget cut.","note":"Focuses on the act of generating a resolution.","audio":"audio/d488615bb1aba0a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-commit-to-a-course-of-action","text":"to commit to a course of action","kind":"collocation","form":"commit to a course of action","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To firmly decide on a particular plan or strategy.","example":"Once we commit to a course of action, we need to stick to it.","plain":"Once we firmly decide on a particular plan, we need to stick to it.","note":"Emphasizes firm decision-making and follow-through.","audio":"audio/b89a8792faec59de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-concede-a-point","text":"To concede a point","kind":"collocation","form":"to concede a point","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To admit that an argument or demand made by the other party is valid or acceptable.","example":"I'm willing to concede a point on the minor features, but the core functionality is essential.","plain":"I'm willing to admit that your argument on the minor features is valid, but the core functionality is essential.","note":"Formal and precise. Refers to giving in on a specific aspect of the negotiation.","audio":"audio/de3af3a5947d29d0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-conceptualize-it-as","text":"To conceptualize it as","kind":"frame","form":"To conceptualize it as","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To think of or understand something in a particular way, often by comparing it to something else.","example":"To conceptualize it as a journey helps us appreciate the progress we've made.","plain":"Thinking of it like a journey helps us see how much progress we've made.","note":"Intellectual and analytical, used to introduce a specific mental model.","audio":"audio/6e38f7914124e8a6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-conclude-my-remarks","text":"To conclude my remarks","kind":"frame","form":"To conclude my remarks","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"A formal way to signal the end of a presentation or a specific section.","example":"To conclude my remarks, I want to reiterate our commitment to sustainable practices.","plain":"To finish my talk, I want to repeat our commitment to sustainable practices.","note":"Formal and clear, suitable for wrapping up a speech or presentation.","audio":"audio/5bdb4a6494aa3474.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-conclude-this-point","text":"to conclude this point","kind":"frame","form":"to conclude this point","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To bring a specific topic of discussion to an end.","example":"To conclude this point, I think we have a clear understanding of the risks involved.","plain":"To end this topic, I think we have a clear understanding of the risks involved.","note":"Useful for moving on from a specific agenda item.","audio":"audio/3a8c95d2eda632cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-confirm-mutual-understanding","text":"To confirm mutual understanding","kind":"chunk","form":"To confirm mutual understanding","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To ensure that all parties involved comprehend the situation or agreement in the same way.","example":"Let's quickly review the action points to confirm mutual understanding.","plain":"Let's quickly check the action points to make sure we all understand them the same way.","note":"Formal and precise, used in professional contexts to avoid future disagreements.","audio":"audio/4af87f7c4df494de.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-consider-all-angles","text":"to consider all angles","kind":"chunk","form":"consider all angles","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To think about a situation or problem from every possible perspective.","example":"Before we make a final decision, let's consider all angles of this complex issue.","plain":"Before we make a final decision, let's think about every possible perspective of this complex issue.","note":"Emphasizes thoroughness and comprehensive analysis.","audio":"audio/eb12481025ac894b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-consider-alternatives","text":"To consider alternatives","kind":"collocation","form":"to consider alternatives","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To think about other possible solutions or courses of action.","example":"If the current plan isn't feasible, we'll need to consider alternatives for achieving our objectives.","plain":"If the current plan isn't feasible, we'll need to think about other possible solutions for achieving our objectives.","note":"Similar to \"explore options,\" but often implies a more evaluative process.","audio":"audio/6971d7c21fc7851a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-consider-the-implications","text":"to consider the implications","kind":"collocation","form":"consider the implications","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To think about the possible future effects or consequences of an action or decision.","example":"Before launching, we need to carefully consider the implications of this new feature on user privacy.","plain":"Before launching, we need to carefully think about the possible future effects of this new feature on user privacy.","note":"Highlights the importance of foresight and understanding potential outcomes.","audio":"audio/db878ab7c8cf8adc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-contextualize-this","text":"To contextualize this","kind":"chunk","form":"To contextualize this","level":"C2","situations":["academic","explaining"],"meaning":"Introduces an explanation that provides background information to help understand a point.","example":"To contextualize this, we need to consider the historical development of similar policies.","plain":"To provide background for this, we need to consider the historical development of similar policies.","note":"A formal phrase used to provide necessary background information for better understanding.","audio":"audio/f24ec0c7ccf03341.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-contribute-meaningfully","text":"to contribute meaningfully","kind":"collocation","form":"contribute meaningfully","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make a significant and valuable input or impact.","example":"I'm looking for a role where I can contribute meaningfully to the company's mission.","plain":"I'm looking for a role where I can make a significant contribution to the company's mission.","note":"Expresses a desire for impact beyond just completing tasks.","audio":"audio/c2db1da147138c33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-correct-any-inaccuracies","text":"To correct any inaccuracies","kind":"chunk","form":"To correct any inaccuracies","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the correct information where there might be errors or misunderstandings.","example":"I'd like to correct any inaccuracies in the previous report regarding our sales figures.","plain":"I want to fix any mistakes in the last report about our sales numbers.","note":"Professional and direct, used to set the record straight politely.","audio":"audio/de3043d1f8558d6c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-demonstrate-initiative","text":"to demonstrate initiative","kind":"collocation","form":"demonstrated initiative","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To show a willingness to act independently and proactively.","example":"I consistently demonstrated initiative by proposing new solutions to recurring operational issues.","plain":"I consistently showed willingness to act by proposing new solutions to recurring operational issues.","note":"Formal way to describe proactive behavior; often used in performance reviews.","audio":"audio/dce54d54d5a065a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-drill-down","text":"to drill down","kind":"chunk","form":"drill down","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To examine something in more detail or to a deeper level.","example":"The initial report shows an issue, but we need to drill down into the data to understand why.","plain":"The initial report shows an issue, but we need to examine the data in more detail to understand why.","note":"Common in data analysis and problem-solving discussions.","audio":"audio/4f5b4b72a50429ed.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-echo-what-x-said","text":"To echo what X said","kind":"frame","form":"To echo what Sarah said","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To reiterate or support a point made by a previous speaker, often adding your own perspective.","example":"To echo what Sarah said, the team's motivation is crucial for project success.","plain":"As Sarah mentioned, the team's motivation is crucial for project success.","note":"Collaborative and supportive, shows you're building on others' contributions.","audio":"audio/19ca536cfaa2884d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-echo-xs-sentiments","text":"To echo X's sentiments","kind":"frame","form":"To echo Professor Lee's sentiments","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To express agreement with or repeat the feelings or opinions previously stated by someone else.","example":"To echo Professor Lee's sentiments, the interdisciplinary approach is crucial for this research.","plain":"I agree with Professor Lee that the interdisciplinary approach is crucial for this research.","note":"A formal and respectful way to show agreement with an expert or respected figure.","audio":"audio/0e260cca5a4449fd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-elaborate-on-this","text":"To elaborate on this","kind":"chunk","form":"To elaborate on this","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a more detailed explanation or expansion of a previously mentioned point.","example":"To elaborate on this, the statistical model accounts for several confounding variables.","plain":"To explain this in more detail, the statistical model accounts for several confounding variables.","note":"A common phrase used to signal that one is about to provide more details or explanation.","audio":"audio/24cc4047f45e10df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-embrace-change","text":"to embrace change","kind":"collocation","form":"embrace change","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To readily accept and adapt to new situations, ideas, or developments.","example":"In a fast-paced industry, it's essential to embrace change and adapt quickly.","plain":"In a fast-paced industry, it's essential to accept new things and adapt quickly.","note":"Shows flexibility and a positive attitude towards evolving environments.","audio":"audio/940012525156040d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-encounter-resistance","text":"to encounter resistance","kind":"collocation","form":"encountered resistance","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To meet with opposition, unwillingness to cooperate, or difficulty.","example":"We encountered resistance from some departments regarding the new data privacy regulations.","plain":"We met opposition from some departments regarding the new data privacy regulations.","note":"Describes facing opposition or difficulty, often from people or established systems.","audio":"audio/e6646be1b064664c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-ensure-accountability","text":"to ensure accountability","kind":"collocation","form":"ensure accountability","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make certain that individuals or teams are responsible for their actions and outcomes.","example":"We need clearer roles to ensure accountability for each stage of the project.","plain":"We need clearer roles to make certain that individuals are responsible for each stage of the project.","note":"A formal term for establishing responsibility.","audio":"audio/9bccc1d852e4296d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-ensure-smooth-implementation","text":"to ensure smooth implementation","kind":"collocation","form":"ensure smooth implementation","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make certain that a plan or system is put into effect without problems.","example":"We need a detailed checklist to ensure smooth implementation of the new policy.","plain":"We need a detailed checklist to make certain that the new policy is put into effect without problems.","note":"Focuses on the execution phase of a plan.","audio":"audio/6c97d54228b73289.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-ensure-were-aligned","text":"To ensure we're aligned","kind":"collocation","form":"To ensure we're aligned","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To make certain that all parties have the same goals, understanding, and direction.","example":"Let's review the project brief one last time to ensure we're aligned.","plain":"Let's review the project brief one last time to make sure we all agree on the goals.","note":"Formal, often used in business or team settings to confirm shared understanding and purpose.","audio":"audio/edea1b9ec10d9e21.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-ensure-were-on-track","text":"To ensure we're on track","kind":"frame","form":"To ensure we're on track","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to check if the discussion or project is progressing as planned.","example":"To ensure we're on track, let's quickly review the progress against our milestones.","plain":"To make sure we are progressing as planned, let's quickly review the progress against our milestones.","note":"Professional and practical, used to monitor progress and manage expectations.","audio":"audio/019ee0301c682b43.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-err-on-the-side-of-caution","text":"to err on the side of caution","kind":"chunk","form":"err on the side of caution","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To choose the safer or less risky option, even if it means being overly careful.","example":"I think we should err on the side of caution and double-check all the figures before submitting.","plain":"I think we should choose the safer option and double-check all the figures before submitting.","note":"A common idiom for recommending a conservative approach.","audio":"audio/904fac0022aa61f8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-exceed-expectations","text":"To exceed expectations","kind":"collocation","form":"to exceed expectations","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To perform better than what was anticipated or required.","example":"We aim to exceed expectations with our service, even if it means going the extra mile.","plain":"We aim to perform better than what was anticipated with our service, even if it means going the extra mile.","note":"Positive and aspirational. Often used to describe high performance.","audio":"audio/dca38f089f0107eb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-exercise-caution","text":"to exercise caution","kind":"collocation","form":"exercise caution","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be careful and prudent in one's actions or decisions.","example":"We should exercise caution when dealing with this new vendor until we build trust.","plain":"We should be careful when dealing with this new vendor until we build trust.","note":"A formal way to advise prudence.","audio":"audio/62044c21bb143348.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-explore-all-avenues","text":"to explore all avenues","kind":"collocation","form":"explore all avenues","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To investigate every possible method or approach to solve a problem.","example":"We must explore all avenues to find a sustainable energy source for the new facility.","plain":"We must investigate every possible method to find a sustainable energy source for the new facility.","note":"Emphasizes a comprehensive search for solutions, leaving no possibility unexamined.","audio":"audio/e47f728933618ca5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-explore-alternatives","text":"to explore alternatives","kind":"collocation","form":"explore alternatives","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To investigate other possible options or solutions.","example":"If this approach doesn't work, we'll need to explore alternatives quickly.","plain":"If this approach doesn't work, we'll need to investigate other possible options quickly.","note":"Suggests a willingness to consider different paths if the current one fails.","audio":"audio/1a6a00ada1880c0f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-explore-options","text":"To explore options","kind":"collocation","form":"to explore options","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To investigate different possibilities or choices before making a decision.","example":"Before we commit, let's take some time to explore options for alternative suppliers.","plain":"Before we commit, let's take some time to investigate different possibilities for alternative suppliers.","note":"Collaborative and proactive, suggesting a thorough approach to problem-solving.","audio":"audio/6192185281997f24.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-extend-the-deadline","text":"To extend the deadline","kind":"collocation","form":"to extend the deadline","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To postpone the agreed-upon date or time by which something must be completed.","example":"We'd be willing to extend the deadline by two weeks if you can guarantee the quality of the final product.","plain":"We'd be willing to postpone the completion date by two weeks if you can guarantee the quality of the final product.","note":"A common concession or request in project-based negotiations.","audio":"audio/e751e17ebfaae758.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-face-a-challenge","text":"to face a challenge","kind":"collocation","form":"face a challenge","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To confront a difficult situation or task.","example":"Our team will face a challenge in integrating the new software with our legacy systems.","plain":"Our team will confront a difficult situation in integrating the new software with our legacy systems.","note":"A neutral and professional way to describe a difficulty.","audio":"audio/fc4a9b1475fe800e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-face-a-dilemma","text":"to face a dilemma","kind":"collocation","form":"face a dilemma","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in a difficult situation requiring a choice between two undesirable options.","example":"We face a dilemma: either delay the launch or release with known bugs.","plain":"We are in a difficult situation: either delay the launch or release with known bugs.","note":"Highlights a challenging choice with no easy answer.","audio":"audio/78799c4ffcf473ba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-facilitate-discussions","text":"to facilitate discussions","kind":"collocation","form":"facilitated discussions","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make conversations or meetings easier and more productive.","example":"I often facilitated discussions between different departments to ensure project alignment.","plain":"I often helped conversations happen between different departments to ensure project alignment.","note":"Highlights mediation and communication skills, especially in collaborative settings.","audio":"audio/97169deefd2206b5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-factor-in","text":"to factor in","kind":"chunk","form":"factor in","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To include something as a relevant element or consideration.","example":"Did you factor in the increased shipping costs when calculating the project budget?","plain":"Did you include the increased shipping costs when calculating the project budget?","note":"A concise way to ask if a specific element has been considered in calculations or plans.","audio":"audio/9030bccf180ad203.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-fall-short-of","text":"To fall short of","kind":"collocation","form":"falls short of","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To fail to meet an expectation, standard, or goal.","example":"Unfortunately, their initial offer falls short of our minimum acceptable profit margin.","plain":"Unfortunately, their initial offer fails to meet our minimum acceptable profit margin.","note":"Polite way to indicate dissatisfaction or inadequacy.","audio":"audio/89e87852c467ac47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-field-a-question","text":"To field a question","kind":"collocation","form":"to field a question","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To receive and respond to a question, especially in a public setting.","example":"I'm happy to field a question about the financial projections now, if anyone has one.","plain":"I'm happy to answer a question about the financial projections now, if anyone has one.","note":"Common in professional contexts, implies readiness and competence in handling queries.","audio":"audio/4637b63132b978c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-find-a-middle-path","text":"To find a middle path","kind":"collocation","form":"to find a middle path","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To identify a solution that is acceptable to all parties involved.","example":"Let's work together to find a middle path that addresses everyone's concerns.","plain":"Let's collaborate to discover a solution that satisfies everyone's concerns.","note":"Emphasizes fairness and inclusivity in reaching a resolution.","audio":"audio/21590a5414fc38a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-find-a-way-around","text":"To find a way around","kind":"chunk","form":"to find a way around","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To discover a solution to a problem or an obstacle.","example":"We need to find a way around this logistical challenge to meet the deadline.","plain":"We must discover a solution to this logistical challenge to meet the deadline.","note":"Suggests creative problem-solving when faced with a difficulty.","audio":"audio/928e3a77c94650b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-find-common-ground","text":"to find common ground","kind":"collocation","form":"find common ground","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discover shared interests, beliefs, or areas of agreement.","example":"Despite our initial disagreements, I'm confident we can find common ground on the budget.","plain":"Despite our initial disagreements, I'm confident we can discover shared interests on the budget.","note":"Essential for negotiation and reaching consensus.","audio":"audio/5512ad03a4f728df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-fine-tune","text":"to fine-tune","kind":"chunk","form":"fine-tune","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make small adjustments to improve something.","example":"The new process is working well, but we still need to fine-tune a few steps for efficiency.","plain":"The new process is working well, but we still need to make small adjustments to a few steps for efficiency.","note":"Implies making minor, precise improvements.","audio":"audio/8f0b6a58722bd800.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-flesh-out-the-details","text":"To flesh out the details","kind":"collocation","form":"To flesh out the details","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To add more specific information or substance to an idea or plan.","example":"We have the basic concept, now we need to flesh out the details of implementation.","plain":"We have the basic concept, now we need to add more specific information about implementation.","note":"Suggests moving from a general idea to a more concrete and comprehensive plan.","audio":"audio/1390ef17c834875e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-float-an-idea","text":"To float an idea","kind":"collocation","form":"to float an idea","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To suggest an idea informally to see how others react to it.","example":"Before we commit, I wanted to float an idea about extending the trial period.","plain":"I wanted to suggest extending the trial period informally, to see your reaction.","note":"Casual and exploratory; used when testing the waters without full commitment.","audio":"audio/1a7a54f6968c2978.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-leverage","text":"To gain leverage","kind":"collocation","form":"to gain leverage","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To acquire an advantage or influence that can be used in negotiation.","example":"Securing this patent will help us to gain leverage in future licensing discussions.","plain":"Getting this patent will help us acquire influence in future licensing discussions.","note":"Focuses on obtaining an advantage to strengthen one's bargaining power.","audio":"audio/428c0830505aea1a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-some-clarity","text":"to gain some clarity","kind":"collocation","form":"gain some clarity","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To achieve a better understanding of a situation or problem.","example":"I'd like to speak with Sarah to gain some clarity on her team's current workload.","plain":"I'd like to speak with Sarah to better understand her team's current workload.","note":"Implies seeking specific information to resolve ambiguity.","audio":"audio/c13cd50a637f2b4c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-gain-some-perspective","text":"To gain some perspective","kind":"frame","form":"To gain some perspective","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Helps the audience understand the relative importance or scale of a point.","example":"To gain some perspective, this small increase in cost will yield significant long-term benefits.","plain":"To help you understand the relative importance, this small cost increase will yield significant long-term benefits.","note":"Useful for clarifying the true significance of a piece of information.","audio":"audio/94a6a9ba6a85a66a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-better-understanding","text":"to get a better understanding","kind":"collocation","form":"get a better understanding","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To improve one's comprehension or knowledge of something.","example":"Let's review the report again to get a better understanding of the financial projections.","plain":"Let's review the report again to improve our comprehension of the financial projections.","note":"Suggests a need for deeper insight or clarification on a topic.","audio":"audio/d1f77dd0560e2295.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-clear-picture","text":"to get a clear picture","kind":"collocation","form":"get a clear picture","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To understand a situation fully and without confusion.","example":"We need more data to get a clear picture of the market trends before investing.","plain":"We need more data to understand the market trends fully before investing.","note":"Emphasizes the need for comprehensive understanding to make informed decisions.","audio":"audio/733524ca27895022.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-commitment","text":"To get a commitment","kind":"collocation","form":"to get a commitment","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To obtain a firm promise or agreement from someone.","example":"We need to get a commitment from them on the delivery date before we sign.","plain":"We need to obtain a firm promise from them on the delivery date before we sign.","note":"Emphasizes securing a binding promise or assurance.","audio":"audio/7796d5438caecace.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-different-angle","text":"to get a different angle","kind":"collocation","form":"get a different angle","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To consider a situation from an alternative viewpoint or approach.","example":"Perhaps we should talk to the sales team to get a different angle on customer feedback.","plain":"Perhaps we should talk to the sales team to consider customer feedback from an alternative viewpoint.","note":"Similar to 'fresh perspective', but often implies seeking a specific, alternative viewpoint.","audio":"audio/56e8dd798128196f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-fresh-perspective","text":"to get a fresh perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"get a fresh perspective","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To view a situation from a new or different angle, often by seeking outside input.","example":"Inviting an external consultant might help us get a fresh perspective on this long-standing problem.","plain":"Inviting an external consultant might help us view this long-standing problem from a new angle.","note":"Encourages seeking new viewpoints to overcome entrenched thinking.","audio":"audio/00d822f2a0367a90.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-grip-on","text":"to get a grip on","kind":"chunk","form":"get a grip on","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To take control of a situation, often one that is becoming difficult or chaotic.","example":"We need to get a grip on these escalating costs before they impact our profitability.","plain":"We need to take control of these escalating costs before they impact our profitability.","note":"Suggests regaining control or understanding of a challenging situation.","audio":"audio/6c8151b06adadb8b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-new-take-on","text":"to get a new take on","kind":"collocation","form":"get a new take on","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To consider a new interpretation or understanding of a situation or idea.","example":"I'm hoping the workshop will help us get a new take on how to approach client engagement.","plain":"I'm hoping the workshop will help us consider a new interpretation of how to approach client engagement.","note":"Suggests gaining a novel or updated understanding of a subject.","audio":"audio/d433ffae5db0c698.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-a-read-on","text":"to get a read on","kind":"chunk","form":"get a read on","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To understand a situation or a person's intentions or mood.","example":"I'm trying to get a read on the market's reaction to our competitor's recent product launch.","plain":"I'm trying to understand the market's reaction to our competitor's recent product launch.","note":"Informal but common, implies assessing a situation or person's state of mind.","audio":"audio/c1966179b582294c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-ahead-of-the-curve","text":"to get ahead of the curve","kind":"chunk","form":"get ahead of the curve","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be in front of current trends or developments, anticipating future needs.","example":"By investing in new technology now, we can get ahead of the curve in market innovation.","plain":"By investing in new technology now, we can be in front of current trends in market innovation.","note":"Focuses on proactive strategy and competitive advantage.","audio":"audio/52cc26bbdfd1f590.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-back-on-track","text":"To get back on track","kind":"chunk","form":"To get back on track","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to signal a return to the main topic after a digression.","example":"To get back on track, let's review the key performance indicators for this quarter.","plain":"To return to the main topic, let's review the key performance indicators for this quarter.","note":"A common phrase for refocusing the discussion.","audio":"audio/cd89600899c0501c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-back-to-my-story","text":"To get back to my story","kind":"chunk","form":"To get back to my story","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Signals a return to the main narrative after a digression or interruption.","example":"To get back to my story, the flight was eventually cancelled due to heavy fog.","plain":"To continue my story, the flight was eventually cancelled due to heavy fog.","note":"A useful phrase for maintaining narrative flow.","audio":"audio/c67d4f7529a0c0dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-the-best-possible-deal","text":"To get the best possible deal","kind":"collocation","form":"to get the best possible deal","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To achieve the most favorable agreement or terms.","example":"Our priority is to get the best possible deal for our shareholders.","plain":"Our main goal is to achieve the most favorable agreement for our shareholders.","note":"Common in business; emphasizes maximizing benefit for one's own side.","audio":"audio/5318a9240a7294c9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-this-over-the-line","text":"To get this over the line","kind":"collocation","form":"to get this over the line","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To successfully complete a project, deal, or negotiation.","example":"We're so close to an agreement; let's make one final push to get this over the line.","plain":"We're so close to an agreement; let's make one final push to successfully complete this.","note":"Informal but very common in business, especially when nearing completion.","audio":"audio/f8d1d10cd6f1d638.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-to-the-crux-of","text":"to get to the crux of","kind":"collocation","form":"get to the crux of","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To identify the most important or fundamental part of a problem or matter.","example":"We spent too long on minor points; let's try to get to the crux of the matter.","plain":"We spent too long on minor points; let's try to identify the most important part of the matter.","note":"Suggests a desire to focus on the core issue, often after tangential discussions.","audio":"audio/2c90ab9ca996f298.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-get-to-the-heart-of-the-matter","text":"To get to the heart of the matter","kind":"collocation","form":"To get to the heart of the matter","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To focus on the most crucial or central aspect of a complex issue.","example":"To get to the heart of the matter, our budget constraints mean we can't pursue both options.","plain":"Our budget constraints mean we can't pursue both options, which is the main issue.","note":"Used when you want to move past superficial details and address the core problem or idea.","audio":"audio/871032a9f6b5eb9b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-ground-on","text":"To give ground on","kind":"collocation","form":"to give ground on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make concessions or yield on certain demands during a negotiation.","example":"We're willing to give ground on the payment schedule if you can commit to a longer contract.","plain":"We're willing to make concessions on the payment schedule if you can commit to a longer contract.","note":"Indicates flexibility and a readiness to compromise. Similar to \"budge on\".","audio":"audio/3288542988327afb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-it-some-thought","text":"to give it some thought","kind":"chunk","form":"give it some thought","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To consider something carefully and reflect on it.","example":"I'll give it some thought over the weekend and get back to you with my decision.","plain":"I'll consider it over the weekend and get back to you with my decision.","note":"A polite way to indicate that a decision or idea requires careful consideration.","audio":"audio/e4798c27cb3dd5e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-it-the-green-light","text":"to give it the green light","kind":"collocation","form":"give it the green light","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To give official permission for something to proceed.","example":"Once legal reviews it, we can give it the green light to start production.","plain":"Once legal reviews it, we can give official permission to start production.","note":"Informal but widely understood, meaning approval to proceed.","audio":"audio/129aa9ef7c24ffa8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-better-idea","text":"To give you a better idea","kind":"frame","form":"To give you a better idea","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide additional information or an example to improve understanding.","example":"To give you a better idea, imagine a system that automates all routine tasks.","plain":"Imagine a system that automates all routine tasks, so you understand it more clearly.","note":"Often precedes an example or a more detailed explanation.","audio":"audio/f1608c7d9c23bcef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-a-flavour","text":"To give you a flavour","kind":"frame","form":"To give you a flavour","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to provide a general impression or a small sample of something.","example":"To give you a flavour of the event, we'll have keynote speakers and interactive workshops.","plain":"The event will have keynote speakers and interactive workshops.","note":"Informal. Offers a brief taste or impression of something.","audio":"audio/8cd6b77d25ea824e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-an-example","text":"To give you an example","kind":"frame","form":"To give you an example","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to introduce a specific case or illustration to make a point clearer.","example":"To give you an example, consider how our competitors handled a similar market downturn.","plain":"Consider how our competitors handled a similar market downturn.","note":"A common and direct way to offer an illustrative example.","audio":"audio/683ee0f5834a61ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-give-you-the-picture","text":"To give you the picture","kind":"frame","form":"To give you the picture","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to provide a comprehensive overview or understanding of a situation.","example":"To give you the picture, our sales have dropped significantly in the last quarter.","plain":"Our sales have dropped significantly in the last quarter.","note":"Informal. Suggests providing a full, clear understanding of a situation.","audio":"audio/1b4cf76bda9961a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hammer-out-an-agreement","text":"To hammer out an agreement","kind":"collocation","form":"to hammer out an agreement","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To negotiate and finalize an agreement with considerable effort and discussion.","example":"It took several long meetings to hammer out an agreement on the complex licensing terms.","plain":"It took many long meetings to negotiate and finalize the complex licensing terms.","note":"Implies a difficult, detailed, and often lengthy negotiation process.","audio":"audio/66ee6744410035a2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-have-a-slight-hiccup","text":"to have a slight hiccup","kind":"collocation","form":"have a slight hiccup","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To experience a minor, temporary problem or delay.","example":"We might have a slight hiccup with the server migration, but it should be resolved quickly.","plain":"We might experience a minor, temporary problem with the server migration, but it should be resolved quickly.","note":"An understated way to refer to a small, usually manageable issue.","audio":"audio/65de41b8739358bf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-highlight-the-importance","text":"To highlight the importance","kind":"collocation","form":"to highlight the importance","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To draw attention to how crucial something is.","example":"I'd like to highlight the importance of regular feedback in professional development.","plain":"I'd like to draw attention to how crucial regular feedback is in professional development.","note":"A standard phrase for emphasizing the value or necessity of something.","audio":"audio/17251d1f35813881.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hold-firm-on","text":"To hold firm on","kind":"collocation","form":"to hold firm on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To maintain one's position or demand without yielding during a negotiation.","example":"We're prepared to negotiate on other points, but we have to hold firm on the intellectual property rights.","plain":"We're prepared to negotiate on other points, but we have to maintain our position on the intellectual property rights.","note":"Indicates a strong, unwavering stance on a particular issue.","audio":"audio/1eb7d87974cd8a5f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hold-our-position","text":"To hold our position","kind":"collocation","form":"to hold our position","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To maintain one's stance or demands in a negotiation.","example":"It's crucial for us to hold our position regarding intellectual property rights.","plain":"It's vital that we maintain our stance on intellectual property rights.","note":"A more formal way of saying 'stick to our guns'; emphasizes strategic resolve.","audio":"audio/1ce2b4864ee58562.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-hold-the-line","text":"To hold the line","kind":"collocation","form":"to hold the line","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To maintain a firm position or limit, especially on price or terms.","example":"We've been instructed to hold the line on the budget, so no further increases are possible.","plain":"We've been instructed to maintain a firm limit on the budget, so no further increases are possible.","note":"Implies a non-negotiable boundary. Can be used to signal a final position.","audio":"audio/3c4a10ab144c9e7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-illustrate-my-point","text":"to illustrate my point","kind":"chunk","form":"To illustrate my point","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to provide an example or explanation that helps make a statement clearer.","example":"To illustrate my point, consider the success of our competitor's recent marketing campaign.","plain":"Consider the success of our competitor's recent marketing campaign.","note":"Introduces an example or analogy to support an argument.","audio":"audio/dd9b1dfc35c2fd35.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-keep-an-eye-on","text":"to keep an eye on","kind":"chunk","form":"keep an eye on","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To monitor or watch something carefully.","example":"I'll keep an eye on the budget projections to ensure we don't overspend this quarter.","plain":"I'll monitor the budget projections to ensure we don't overspend this quarter.","note":"Implies ongoing vigilance and responsibility.","audio":"audio/ad5e8b34185124ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-keep-an-open-mind","text":"to keep an open mind","kind":"chunk","form":"keep an open mind","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To be willing to consider new ideas or opinions, even if they differ from one's own.","example":"Let's keep an open mind during the brainstorming session; all suggestions are welcome.","plain":"Let's be willing to consider new ideas during the brainstorming session; all suggestions are welcome.","note":"Encourages receptiveness to diverse perspectives, crucial for effective problem-solving.","audio":"audio/8fcef6aeae713338.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-keep-in-mind","text":"to keep in mind","kind":"chunk","form":"keep in mind","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To remember a piece of information or a fact, especially when making decisions.","example":"We need to keep in mind the long-term sustainability of any solution we propose.","plain":"We need to remember the long-term sustainability of any solution we propose.","note":"Similar to 'bear in mind', but slightly less formal and very common.","audio":"audio/e6a1dac73704935e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-keep-lines-of-communication-open","text":"to keep lines of communication open","kind":"collocation","form":"keep lines of communication open","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To ensure that people continue to talk to each other and share information.","example":"It's vital to keep lines of communication open between departments during this transition.","plain":"It's vital to ensure people continue to talk to each other between departments during this transition.","note":"Stresses the importance of ongoing dialogue to prevent issues.","audio":"audio/411d3815c9790fbd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-keep-momentum-going","text":"to keep momentum going","kind":"collocation","form":"keep momentum going","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To maintain the speed or progress of an activity.","example":"We've had a great start to the project; let's keep momentum going with regular updates.","plain":"We've had a great start to the project; let's maintain the progress with regular updates.","note":"Focuses on sustaining positive progress and energy.","audio":"audio/89dca6a755bb4f9d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-keep-our-options-open","text":"to keep our options open","kind":"chunk","form":"keep our options open","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To avoid making a final decision too soon, allowing for flexibility and alternative choices.","example":"For now, let's keep our options open regarding the vendor until we receive all quotes.","plain":"For now, let's avoid making a final decision regarding the vendor until we receive all quotes.","note":"Suggests a strategic delay in commitment to maintain flexibility.","audio":"audio/47f1437ac2a57882.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-lay-out-our-position","text":"To lay out our position","kind":"collocation","form":"to lay out our position","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To clearly explain one's stance, demands, or proposal in a structured way.","example":"Before we proceed, I'd like to lay out our position on the proposed changes to the contract.","plain":"Before we proceed, I'd like to clearly explain our stance on the proposed changes to the contract.","note":"Professional and formal, used for presenting one's case.","audio":"audio/2810cd177dc8dc9a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-lay-the-groundwork","text":"To lay the groundwork","kind":"collocation","form":"to lay the groundwork","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To do preparatory work for future discussions or agreements.","example":"We need to lay the groundwork for a long-term partnership, not just this single deal.","plain":"We should prepare for a long-term partnership, rather than focusing only on this deal.","note":"Suggests strategic planning; often used in formal business contexts.","audio":"audio/d06f322204a56b22.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-learn-from-our-mistakes","text":"to learn from our mistakes","kind":"chunk","form":"learn from our mistakes","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To gain knowledge or improve behavior by reflecting on past errors.","example":"This project didn't go as planned, but we must learn from our mistakes for next time.","plain":"This project didn't go as planned, but we must gain knowledge from our past errors for next time.","note":"A common phrase for promoting growth and continuous improvement.","audio":"audio/84e673b333d44ccf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-learn-from-setbacks","text":"to learn from setbacks","kind":"collocation","form":"learned from setbacks","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To gain knowledge or experience from failures or temporary difficulties.","example":"Even when projects didn't go as planned, I always learned from setbacks and applied those lessons.","plain":"Even when projects didn't go as planned, I always gained knowledge from failures and applied those lessons.","note":"Shows a growth mindset and ability to reflect constructively.","audio":"audio/fb40d6c69e8e2368.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-leave-no-stone-unturned","text":"to leave no stone unturned","kind":"chunk","form":"leave no stone unturned","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To do everything possible to find a solution or gather information.","example":"We need to leave no stone unturned in our investigation to understand the system failure.","plain":"We need to do everything possible in our investigation to understand the system failure.","note":"Emphasizes thoroughness and persistence in problem-solving or searching.","audio":"audio/f0887d573c0d04e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-leave-room-for","text":"To leave room for","kind":"chunk","form":"to leave room for","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To allow for future adjustments, changes, or negotiation.","example":"We've structured the contract to leave room for future expansion of services.","plain":"We've structured the contract to allow for future expansion of services.","note":"Suggests foresight and strategic planning for flexibility.","audio":"audio/8eea4936fa125002.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-look-at-the-bigger-picture","text":"To look at the bigger picture","kind":"collocation","form":"to look at the bigger picture","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To consider all the important aspects of a situation, not just the immediate details.","example":"While this specific clause is important, we need to look at the bigger picture of the entire partnership.","plain":"While this specific clause is important, we need to consider all the important aspects of the entire partnership.","note":"Encourages a holistic view, often to overcome minor disagreements.","audio":"audio/5b656ab1feafa97a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-call","text":"To make a call","kind":"collocation","form":"to make a call","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make a decision, often a difficult or important one.","example":"Ultimately, it's up to the steering committee to make a call on the budget allocation.","plain":"Ultimately, it's up to the steering committee to make a decision on the budget allocation.","note":"Informal but very common in business. Implies responsibility for the decision.","audio":"audio/ed6084457a327f8f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-concession","text":"To make a concession","kind":"collocation","form":"to make a concession","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To agree to something that was initially resisted, often to facilitate an agreement.","example":"We're prepared to make a concession on the warranty period to finalize the purchase.","plain":"We're prepared to agree to a shorter warranty period to finalize the purchase.","note":"Formal and precise. Highlights a specific point of compromise.","audio":"audio/5d8673db3fba448c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-counter-offer","text":"To make a counter-offer","kind":"collocation","form":"to make a counter-offer","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To respond to an offer with a different proposal, usually with more favorable terms for oneself.","example":"We can't accept your initial price, but we're prepared to make a counter-offer.","plain":"We can't accept your initial price, but we're prepared to respond with a different proposal.","note":"A key part of the negotiation process, indicating a willingness to continue discussions.","audio":"audio/1e3b8e88e37c5523.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-judgment-call","text":"to make a judgment call","kind":"collocation","form":"make a judgment call","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make a decision based on one's own discretion and assessment, often without clear rules.","example":"Ultimately, it's up to the project manager to make a judgment call on the best approach.","plain":"Ultimately, it's up to the project manager to make a decision based on their own assessment on the best approach.","note":"Implies a decision made based on experience and intuition rather than strict guidelines.","audio":"audio/aa2db73e3380434f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-a-strong-case-for","text":"To make a strong case for","kind":"collocation","form":"to make a strong case for","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To present compelling arguments in favor of a particular position or action.","example":"We need to make a strong case for why our pricing is justified given the premium quality.","plain":"We need to present compelling arguments for why our pricing is justified given the premium quality.","note":"Emphasizes the need for persuasive argumentation.","audio":"audio/f981b5bf6e1511a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-an-informed-decision","text":"to make an informed decision","kind":"collocation","form":"make an informed decision","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make a choice based on a thorough understanding of the facts and options.","example":"We need more data before we can make an informed decision about the investment.","plain":"We need more data before we can make a choice based on a thorough understanding about the investment.","note":"Emphasizes the importance of data and knowledge in decision-making.","audio":"audio/8b27a164d662447a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-headway","text":"To make headway","kind":"collocation","form":"to make headway","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make progress, especially when facing difficulties or resistance.","example":"We're finally starting to make headway on the budget discussions after a long debate.","plain":"We're finally starting to make progress on the budget discussions after a long debate.","note":"Implies overcoming obstacles and moving forward.","audio":"audio/fbb05f96203494d8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-some-adjustments","text":"To make some adjustments","kind":"collocation","form":"to make some adjustments","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To modify or change certain aspects of a plan or agreement.","example":"We might need to make some adjustments to the project timeline if we add that feature.","plain":"We might need to change the project timeline if we include that feature.","note":"Suggests minor, practical changes; often used to soften a request for change.","audio":"audio/60323dd3da1d7a43.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-make-sure-were-aligned","text":"to make sure we're aligned","kind":"chunk","form":"make sure we're aligned","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To ensure that everyone involved has the same goals and understanding.","example":"Before we start, let's quickly discuss the objectives to make sure we're aligned.","plain":"Before we start, let's quickly discuss the objectives to ensure everyone has the same goals and understanding.","note":"Crucial for team cohesion and preventing conflicting efforts.","audio":"audio/f1c628e1acd8aee2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-manage-expectations","text":"to manage expectations","kind":"collocation","form":"manage expectations","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To ensure that others have realistic ideas about what is possible or likely.","example":"We need to manage expectations with the client about how quickly we can deliver the new features.","plain":"We need to ensure the client has realistic ideas about how quickly we can deliver the new features.","note":"Prevents disappointment by setting realistic boundaries.","audio":"audio/ec1789f3398ac304.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-map-out-a-strategy","text":"to map out a strategy","kind":"collocation","form":"map out a strategy","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To plan a detailed course of action to achieve a specific goal.","example":"Let's map out a strategy for improving customer retention over the next six months.","plain":"Let's plan a detailed course of action for improving customer retention over the next six months.","note":"Suggests a thorough and systematic approach to planning.","audio":"audio/e4a83f43024babff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-meet-halfway","text":"To meet halfway","kind":"chunk","form":"to meet halfway","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To reach an agreement by each side making concessions.","example":"If you can adjust the scope slightly, we're prepared to meet halfway on the price.","plain":"If you can adjust the scope, we're willing to compromise on the price.","note":"Implies a fair and balanced approach to negotiation.","audio":"audio/b35780161d99c41a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-mitigate-the-risk","text":"to mitigate the risk","kind":"collocation","form":"mitigate the risk","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To make a potential problem or danger less severe or serious.","example":"We've implemented new security protocols to mitigate the risk of data breaches.","plain":"We've implemented new security protocols to make the risk of data breaches less severe.","note":"A key term in project management and strategic planning.","audio":"audio/8b4840fcdec88854.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-monitor-progress","text":"to monitor progress","kind":"collocation","form":"monitor progress","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To regularly check the development or improvement of something.","example":"We need to monitor progress closely on the new software rollout to catch any issues early.","plain":"We need to regularly check the development closely on the new software rollout to catch any issues early.","note":"Essential for project management and ensuring goals are met.","audio":"audio/02e5821dc93e5041.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-move-past-this","text":"to move past this","kind":"chunk","form":"move past this","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To overcome a problem or difficult situation and continue forward.","example":"We've spent enough time dwelling on what went wrong; it's time to move past this.","plain":"We've spent enough time dwelling on what went wrong; it's time to overcome this.","note":"Encourages forward momentum and resolution.","audio":"audio/29d4c69a610ba0ef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-move-things-forward","text":"To move things forward","kind":"collocation","form":"to move things forward","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make progress or advance a discussion or project.","example":"What can we do to move things forward and get this contract signed by Friday?","plain":"What can we do to make progress and get this contract signed by Friday?","note":"Common in business to encourage progress and avoid stagnation.","audio":"audio/a200c27be669f4fa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-mull-it-over","text":"to mull it over","kind":"chunk","form":"mull it over","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To think about something carefully and at length, often before making a decision.","example":"I need a few days to mull it over before I can offer a comprehensive proposal.","plain":"I need a few days to think about it carefully before I can offer a comprehensive proposal.","note":"Suggests a process of deep, unhurried consideration, often for complex issues.","audio":"audio/15eb66a4bcf37253.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-my-understanding","text":"to my understanding","kind":"chunk","form":"To my understanding","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Based on what I believe I know or have been told.","example":"To my understanding, the train to Florence departs from platform 7, is that correct?","plain":"I believe the train to Florence departs from platform 7, is that correct?","note":"Used to confirm information politely, often when there's a slight uncertainty.","audio":"audio/c31226012c176d48.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-narrow-down-the-options","text":"to narrow down the options","kind":"collocation","form":"narrow down the options","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To reduce the number of available choices or possibilities.","example":"Let's narrow down the options to the top three most viable solutions for further analysis.","plain":"Let's reduce the number of options to the top three most viable solutions for further analysis.","note":"Describes the process of making a selection from a larger set of choices.","audio":"audio/ce2baa8c968a892c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-narrow-it-down","text":"To narrow it down","kind":"chunk","form":"To narrow it down","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To reduce the scope of a topic or choice to focus on specific aspects.","example":"Let's try to narrow it down to the top three priorities for next quarter.","plain":"Let's try to choose only the three most important things for next quarter.","note":"Practical and action-oriented, used to focus discussion.","audio":"audio/0b9c4c139f62eb5c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-notice-a-pattern-emerging","text":"to notice a pattern emerging","kind":"collocation","form":"noticed a pattern emerging","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To observe a recurring trend or sequence of events.","example":"I've noticed a pattern emerging in the customer feedback, suggesting a common usability issue.","plain":"I've observed a recurring trend in the customer feedback, suggesting a common usability issue.","note":"Used when identifying a trend that might indicate a systemic problem.","audio":"audio/03ba513c918bfae5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-offer-a-brief-summary","text":"To offer a brief summary","kind":"frame","form":"To offer a brief summary","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to introduce a short overview of the main points discussed.","example":"To offer a brief summary, our strategy focuses on innovation, efficiency, and customer satisfaction.","plain":"To give a short summary, our strategy focuses on innovation, efficiency, and customer satisfaction.","note":"A polite and concise way to recap.","audio":"audio/c8deadebdd43c335.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-offer-a-suggestion","text":"to offer a suggestion","kind":"collocation","form":"offer a suggestion","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To propose an idea or plan for someone to consider.","example":"May I offer a suggestion on how we might streamline this particular process?","plain":"May I propose an idea on how we might streamline this particular process?","note":"A polite and formal way to contribute an idea.","audio":"audio/ec9c9fa7195987cd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-offer-some-feedback","text":"to offer some feedback","kind":"chunk","form":"to offer some feedback","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To provide comments or suggestions for improvement.","example":"I wanted to offer some feedback on your presentation, if you have a moment.","plain":"I wanted to give you some comments on your presentation, if you have a moment.","note":"Standard, polite opening for giving feedback.","audio":"audio/c1a5cdc9f90935d5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-outline-the-next-steps","text":"to outline the next steps","kind":"collocation","form":"outline the next steps","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To describe the subsequent actions that need to be taken.","example":"Before we adjourn, let's quickly outline the next steps and assign responsibilities.","plain":"Before we adjourn, let's quickly describe the subsequent actions and assign responsibilities.","note":"Provides clarity and structure for moving forward after a discussion.","audio":"audio/7ba3149b78fbf2a9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-phrase-it-another-way","text":"To phrase it another way","kind":"chunk","form":"To phrase it another way","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to express the same idea using different words to improve clarity.","example":"To phrase it another way, the success of this project hinges on team collaboration.","plain":"The success of this project hinges on team collaboration.","note":"A slightly more formal alternative to \"to put it another way.\"","audio":"audio/ba32e9db555289e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-pinpoint-the-problem","text":"to pinpoint the problem","kind":"collocation","form":"pinpoint the problem","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To identify the exact nature or location of an issue with precision.","example":"We need to pinpoint the problem with the software before we can develop a fix.","plain":"We need to identify the exact problem with the software before we can develop a fix.","note":"Emphasizes the need for precise identification of the core issue.","audio":"audio/10f43dc758f06b9f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-place-this-in-context","text":"To place this in context","kind":"chunk","form":"To place this in context","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To provide the surrounding circumstances or background information for better understanding.","example":"To place this in context, our competitors launched a similar product last month.","plain":"To help you understand, our rivals released a similar product last month.","note":"Useful for providing perspective or background information.","audio":"audio/341936ffc1b83eaa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-play-devils-advocate","text":"To play devil's advocate","kind":"chunk","form":"To play devil's advocate","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To introduce a counter-argument or opposing viewpoint, often to stimulate discussion rather than express personal belief.","example":"To play devil's advocate, what if the market doesn't react as positively as we expect?","plain":"Let's consider the opposing view: what if the market doesn't react as positively as we expect?","note":"Used to introduce a challenging perspective in a non-confrontational way, often in a group discussion.","audio":"audio/b6b3c11ba34c9635.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-play-fair","text":"To play fair","kind":"chunk","form":"to play fair","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To negotiate honestly, reasonably, and without deception.","example":"We expect both parties to play fair and stick to the agreed-upon principles.","plain":"We expect both parties to negotiate honestly and adhere to the agreed principles.","note":"Emphasizes ethical conduct and mutual respect in negotiation.","audio":"audio/697c647f08bf01d1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-play-hardball","text":"To play hardball","kind":"chunk","form":"to play hardball","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To negotiate aggressively and uncompromisingly, often using tough tactics.","example":"They're clearly prepared to play hardball on the contract terms, so we need to be firm.","plain":"They are ready to negotiate aggressively on the contract terms, so we must be firm.","note":"Informal; describes a tough, unyielding negotiation style.","audio":"audio/e8c6306ba5a559a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-play-it-by-ear","text":"to play it by ear","kind":"chunk","form":"play it by ear","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To decide how to deal with a situation as it develops, rather than planning in advance.","example":"We don't have all the information yet, so let's play it by ear for the next few days.","plain":"We don't have all the information yet, so let's decide as the situation develops for the next few days.","note":"Suggests flexibility and adaptability, often when faced with unpredictable circumstances.","audio":"audio/e889103e581c6e20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-play-it-safe","text":"to play it safe","kind":"chunk","form":"play it safe","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To avoid risks and choose the safest or most cautious course of action.","example":"Given the uncertainties, I think we should play it safe and stick to the proven method.","plain":"Given the uncertainties, I think we should avoid risks and stick to the proven method.","note":"Implies a preference for caution and minimal risk, often in uncertain situations.","audio":"audio/fbf3faba0dee4598.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-prevent-misinterpretation","text":"To prevent misinterpretation","kind":"chunk","form":"To prevent misinterpretation","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something carefully to ensure it is understood exactly as intended.","example":"I'll send a follow-up email to prevent misinterpretation of our discussion points.","plain":"I'll send an email afterwards so no one misunderstands what we talked about.","note":"Professional and cautious, used to avoid errors or confusion.","audio":"audio/c136c660cda98069.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-prioritize","text":"to prioritize","kind":"chunk","form":"prioritize","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To determine the order of importance for tasks or issues.","example":"We have too many tasks; we need to prioritize what's most critical for this week.","plain":"We have too many tasks; we need to determine the order of importance for what's most critical for this week.","note":"A fundamental action in problem-solving and project management.","audio":"audio/a01ceedf0728dfef.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-provide-further-insight","text":"To provide further insight","kind":"collocation","form":"To provide further insight","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To offer a deeper or more detailed understanding of a topic.","example":"The report aims to provide further insight into consumer behaviour trends.","plain":"The report aims to offer a deeper understanding of consumer behaviour trends.","note":"Formal and academic, used when presenting analysis or research findings.","audio":"audio/c2315ddaea0271a1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-provide-the-necessary-context","text":"To provide the necessary context","kind":"collocation","form":"To provide the necessary context","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To give background information essential for understanding a situation or statement.","example":"First, I need to provide the necessary context for these financial projections.","plain":"First, I need to give you the background information for these money predictions.","note":"Formal and helpful, used to ensure a complete understanding.","audio":"audio/873cf052bc985ff2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-push-back-on","text":"To push back on","kind":"collocation","form":"to push back on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To resist or oppose a proposal, demand, or idea from the other party.","example":"We'll need to push back on their request for an exclusive license; it's too restrictive for us.","plain":"We'll need to resist their request for an exclusive license; it's too restrictive for us.","note":"Informal but widely used in professional settings. Implies active resistance.","audio":"audio/eea3c5352396ff98.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-push-for","text":"To push for","kind":"chunk","form":"to push for","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To strongly advocate for or demand something.","example":"We need to push for a quicker resolution to this dispute.","plain":"We need to strongly advocate for a quicker resolution to this dispute.","note":"Implies assertive action to achieve a desired outcome.","audio":"audio/c5e521cf2b14b4dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-figure-on","text":"To put a figure on","kind":"chunk","form":"to put a figure on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To state a specific amount of money or a number.","example":"It's difficult to put a figure on the exact cost until we finalize the specifications.","plain":"It's hard to state the exact cost until we finalize the specifications.","note":"Used when asked for a precise number, often implying uncertainty.","audio":"audio/f5daa12123c9d59e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-plan-in-place","text":"to put a plan in place","kind":"collocation","form":"put a plan in place","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To establish a detailed strategy or set of actions.","example":"Once we've identified the root cause, we can put a plan in place to prevent recurrence.","plain":"Once we've identified the root cause, we can establish a strategy to prevent recurrence.","note":"Emphasizes the formal creation and implementation of a strategy.","audio":"audio/9dc1524ca24fae94.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-proposal-on-the-table","text":"To put a proposal on the table","kind":"collocation","form":"to put a proposal on the table","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To formally present an idea or plan for consideration.","example":"We're ready to put a proposal on the table that addresses your concerns about the timeline.","plain":"We're ready to formally present an idea that addresses your concerns about the timeline.","note":"Similar to \"put something on the table,\" specifically for a proposal.","audio":"audio/538a662870ce60ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-a-stop-to","text":"to put a stop to","kind":"chunk","form":"put a stop to","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To cause something to cease or end.","example":"We need to put a stop to these recurring errors by implementing stricter quality checks.","plain":"We need to cause these recurring errors to end by implementing stricter quality checks.","note":"Implies decisive action to terminate an undesirable situation.","audio":"audio/399f864a6ed96f53.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-forward-a-proposal","text":"To put forward a proposal","kind":"collocation","form":"to put forward a proposal","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To formally present an idea or plan for consideration and discussion.","example":"We'd like to put forward a proposal that addresses both our concerns about the budget.","plain":"We'd like to formally present a plan that addresses both our concerns about the budget.","note":"Standard, professional language for initiating a discussion with a specific offer.","audio":"audio/429d690601091918.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-into-perspective","text":"To put it into perspective","kind":"collocation","form":"To put it into perspective","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain something by comparing it with other things, helping to judge its true importance or scale.","example":"To put it into perspective, our current sales figures are double what they were last year.","plain":"Our current sales figures are double what they were last year, which helps show their true importance.","note":"Provides context to help the listener understand the relative significance of information.","audio":"audio/a5b7aba931dcde46.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-more-precisely","text":"To put it more precisely","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it more precisely","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To rephrase a statement with greater accuracy or specificity.","example":"The model predicts a slight increase in volatility, or to put it more precisely, a wider confidence interval.","plain":"The model predicts a slight increase in volatility, or more precisely, a wider confidence interval.","note":"Used to refine a statement, making it more accurate or technical.","audio":"audio/8a2e4cc464fc0e10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-simply-then","text":"To put it simply, then","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it simply, then","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide a straightforward summary after a more complex explanation.","example":"To put it simply, then, our goal is to innovate faster than the competition.","plain":"In simple terms, our goal is to innovate faster than the competition.","note":"Used to simplify a complex idea, often as a concluding summary of a point.","audio":"audio/4ff68c4724abe33a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-it-visually","text":"To put it visually","kind":"chunk","form":"To put it visually","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to explain something using an image or metaphor to aid understanding.","example":"To put it visually, our current process is like trying to fit a square peg in a round hole.","plain":"Our current process is like trying to fit a square peg in a round hole.","note":"Introduces a metaphor or simile to create a clearer mental picture.","audio":"audio/10c78b6cd2e3324b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-our-heads-together","text":"to put our heads together","kind":"chunk","form":"put our heads together","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To collaborate and work together to solve a problem or generate ideas.","example":"Let's put our heads together after lunch and see if we can find a way forward.","plain":"Let's collaborate after lunch and see if we can find a way forward.","note":"An informal but common expression for teamwork and brainstorming.","audio":"audio/9fac2d26818d19cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-put-something-on-the-table","text":"To put something on the table","kind":"collocation","form":"to put our revised offer on the table","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To formally present an offer, proposal, or idea for consideration in a negotiation.","example":"We're ready to put our revised offer on the table, including an extended payment plan.","plain":"We're ready to present our revised offer, including an extended payment plan.","note":"Indicates a formal presentation of a proposal.","audio":"audio/e1aae2c4bca80659.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-raise-a-concern","text":"To raise a concern","kind":"collocation","form":"to raise a concern","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To bring up an issue or worry that needs to be addressed.","example":"I'd like to raise a concern about the proposed timeline; it seems overly ambitious.","plain":"I'd like to mention a worry about the proposed timeline; it seems overly ambitious.","note":"A polite and professional way to introduce a problem or objection.","audio":"audio/628204a7508a1421.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reach-a-consensus","text":"To reach a consensus","kind":"collocation","form":"to reach a consensus","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To arrive at a general agreement or shared opinion among a group of people.","example":"Our goal is to reach a consensus on the project scope before we allocate resources.","plain":"Our goal is to arrive at a general agreement on the project scope before we allocate resources.","note":"Implies a broader agreement than just \"reaching an agreement,\" often among multiple stakeholders.","audio":"audio/c69eb9714aa6cc18.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reach-an-agreement","text":"To reach an agreement","kind":"collocation","form":"to reach an agreement","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To come to a mutual understanding or settlement.","example":"Our goal is to reach an agreement that is fair and sustainable for everyone.","plain":"Our aim is to achieve a fair and sustainable mutual understanding for all.","note":"A standard, formal phrase for concluding a successful negotiation.","audio":"audio/cf08c16915b2eca2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-recap-our-discussion","text":"to recap our discussion","kind":"frame","form":"to recap our discussion","level":"C2","situations":["meetings","negotiation"],"meaning":"To briefly summarize the main points of the conversation.","example":"To recap our discussion, we've decided on three key areas for immediate action.","plain":"To summarize our discussion, we've decided on three key areas for immediate action.","note":"Professional way to provide a summary.","audio":"audio/335ae1a475aeadc3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reiterate-for-clarity","text":"To reiterate for clarity","kind":"frame","form":"To reiterate for clarity","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To repeat a point to ensure it is fully understood and unambiguous.","example":"To reiterate for clarity, the new policy takes effect from the first of next month.","plain":"To make it clear, the new policy takes effect from the first of next month.","note":"Used when a point is particularly important or might have been misunderstood.","audio":"audio/3e647a43c0c35330.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-reiterate-what-you-said","text":"To reiterate what you said","kind":"frame","form":"To reiterate what you said","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Used to repeat or summarize a previous statement for emphasis or clarity.","example":"To reiterate what you said, the main challenge is indeed resource allocation.","plain":"As you mentioned, the biggest problem is definitely how we distribute resources.","note":"A formal and clear way to confirm understanding or reinforce a point.","audio":"audio/e8e3d38b04096c3e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-resolve-any-ambiguity","text":"To resolve any ambiguity","kind":"chunk","form":"To resolve any ambiguity","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To clarify something that could be understood in more than one way.","example":"Let's review the contract terms again to resolve any ambiguity before signing.","plain":"Let's check the contract terms again to make sure there are no unclear parts before signing.","note":"Formal and precise, used when dealing with potentially unclear language.","audio":"audio/0e5429bb2f85dbc2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-review-the-situation","text":"to review the situation","kind":"collocation","form":"review the situation","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To formally examine and assess the current state of affairs.","example":"Let's schedule a meeting next week to review the situation after the initial implementation phase.","plain":"Let's schedule a meeting next week to formally examine the current state of affairs after the initial implementation phase.","note":"Implies a formal assessment or evaluation.","audio":"audio/c6b7c328c0d416bf.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-revisit-the-terms","text":"To revisit the terms","kind":"collocation","form":"to revisit the terms","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To review and potentially renegotiate the conditions or stipulations of an agreement.","example":"Given the new market data, we may need to revisit the terms of our original pricing agreement.","plain":"Given the new market data, we may need to review and potentially renegotiate the conditions of our original pricing agreement.","note":"Professional way to suggest reopening a discussion about established conditions.","audio":"audio/427c1932e95b33fc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-revisit-this","text":"to revisit this","kind":"chunk","form":"revisit this","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discuss or consider something again, often because new information has emerged.","example":"Given the new budget constraints, we might need to revisit this decision about the software upgrade.","plain":"Given the new budget constraints, we might need to discuss this decision about the software upgrade again.","note":"Suggests a need to re-evaluate a previous choice or plan.","audio":"audio/6cffbbf79e5ac3ec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-run-into-a-snag","text":"to run into a snag","kind":"collocation","form":"run into a snag","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To encounter an unexpected difficulty or obstacle.","example":"We've run into a snag with the data migration, which might delay the launch.","plain":"We've encountered an unexpected difficulty with the data migration, which might delay the launch.","note":"Informal but common, referring to a minor but disruptive problem.","audio":"audio/2ea003635fcc9fa4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-schedule-a-follow-up","text":"to schedule a follow-up","kind":"collocation","form":"schedule a follow-up","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To arrange a subsequent meeting or communication to check on progress or discuss further.","example":"Let's schedule a follow-up for next month to discuss the long-term impact of these changes.","plain":"Let's arrange a subsequent meeting for next month to discuss the long-term impact of these changes.","note":"A standard professional practice for ongoing management.","audio":"audio/62c370ad9b7474fb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-set-a-precedent","text":"To set a precedent","kind":"collocation","form":"to set a precedent","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To establish a rule or example that may be followed in similar future situations.","example":"We must be careful not to set a precedent by agreeing to such unusual payment terms.","plain":"We must be careful not to establish an example by agreeing to such unusual payment terms.","note":"Important consideration in negotiations, especially for long-term relationships.","audio":"audio/80ba4f066d24f024.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-set-the-record-straight","text":"To set the record straight","kind":"chunk","form":"To set the record straight","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To correct inaccurate information or a misunderstanding that has arisen.","example":"Just to set the record straight, I never said we would cut salaries, only bonuses.","plain":"I never said we would cut salaries, only bonuses, I want to correct that misunderstanding.","note":"Used when there's a need to correct a factual error or a misrepresentation of what was said.","audio":"audio/ddf8e3165c89f89d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-set-the-stage-for","text":"to set the stage for","kind":"chunk","form":"set the stage for","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To create the conditions or prepare for something to happen.","example":"My initial work on the project helped set the stage for its successful implementation later on.","plain":"My initial work on the project helped prepare for its successful implementation later on.","note":"Implies foundational work; often used when describing past contributions.","audio":"audio/6895d9287e8ccf36.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-shed-some-light-on","text":"to shed some light on","kind":"collocation","form":"shed some light on","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To clarify or explain something, making it easier to understand.","example":"Could you shed some light on why the project budget was suddenly increased?","plain":"Could you explain why the project budget was suddenly increased?","note":"Commonly used when seeking clarification or an explanation for a confusing situation.","audio":"audio/466d4611c2dd0452.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-simplify-for-clarity","text":"To simplify for clarity","kind":"chunk","form":"To simplify for clarity","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To make something less complicated to ensure it is easily understood.","example":"To simplify for clarity, we're talking about a phased rollout over six months.","plain":"To make it easier to understand, we're planning to launch it in stages over six months.","note":"Polite and professional, used when intentionally reducing complexity.","audio":"audio/849a3377c49d81cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-simplify-the-terminology","text":"To simplify the terminology","kind":"chunk","form":"To simplify the terminology","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain complex or technical words in simpler language.","example":"To simplify the terminology, \"synergy\" just means working well together.","plain":"To make the words easier to understand, \"synergy\" means working well together.","note":"Helpful and considerate, used when addressing a non-expert audience.","audio":"audio/2a9381c76ad18a12.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-simplify-things","text":"To simplify things","kind":"frame","form":"To simplify things","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Offers to explain something in an easier-to-understand way.","example":"To simplify things, we need to reduce overheads across the board.","plain":"To make it easier to understand, we need to reduce overheads across the board.","note":"Useful when the audience seems confused or the explanation was too complex.","audio":"audio/42e8f944c3db15d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-sketch-out-a-plan","text":"to sketch out a plan","kind":"collocation","form":"sketch out a plan","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To create a rough outline or preliminary version of a plan.","example":"Can you quickly sketch out a plan for the emergency response protocol?","plain":"Can you quickly create a rough outline of a plan for the emergency response protocol?","note":"Implies creating a basic, non-detailed plan, often as a starting point.","audio":"audio/339ed23a25c8be5e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-sleep-on-it","text":"to sleep on it","kind":"chunk","form":"sleep on it","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To delay making a decision until the next day, allowing time for reflection.","example":"This is a big decision, so I'd like to sleep on it before giving you an answer.","plain":"This is a big decision, so I'd like to wait until tomorrow before giving you an answer.","note":"An informal but widely accepted way to request time for careful consideration.","audio":"audio/0e3e11dc2d50d652.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-smooth-the-way","text":"To smooth the way","kind":"chunk","form":"to smooth the way","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To make progress or a process easier by removing obstacles.","example":"Your early approval would really help to smooth the way for the next phase of the project.","plain":"Your early approval would greatly help to make the next project phase easier.","note":"Suggests proactive action to facilitate future steps.","audio":"audio/afab0e736969f175.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-sound-out","text":"to sound out","kind":"chunk","form":"sound out","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To discreetly ask someone for their opinion or reaction before making a formal proposal.","example":"I'll sound out the team lead on the new project idea before presenting it to management.","plain":"I'll discreetly ask the team lead for their opinion on the new project idea before presenting it to management.","note":"Suggests a preliminary, informal inquiry to gauge reception or gather initial feedback.","audio":"audio/0aa0812ab8af4d86.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-speak-candidly","text":"To speak candidly","kind":"chunk","form":"To speak candidly","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To speak openly and honestly, sometimes about difficult or sensitive topics.","example":"To speak candidly, I don't think this project is viable with our current resources.","plain":"To be honest, I don't believe this project can work with the resources we have.","note":"Signals honesty and directness, often used before delivering difficult news.","audio":"audio/261b03e3013b17f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-specify-further","text":"To specify further","kind":"chunk","form":"To specify further","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To give more precise or detailed information about something.","example":"To specify further, the changes will affect only employees in the sales department.","plain":"To be more exact, the changes will only impact staff in the sales department.","note":"Formal and precise, used to add exact details.","audio":"audio/137e43636075f121.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-stand-firm-on","text":"To stand firm on","kind":"collocation","form":"to stand firm on","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To maintain one's position or demands without yielding.","example":"We need to stand firm on the quality standards for this product.","plain":"We must maintain our position regarding the quality standards for this product.","note":"Suggests resolve and determination; often used when a principle is at stake.","audio":"audio/3901fb09ec2497bc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-stand-ones-ground","text":"To stand one's ground","kind":"collocation","form":"to stand our ground","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To refuse to change one's position or yield to pressure during a negotiation.","example":"Despite their strong arguments, we decided to stand our ground on the intellectual property clause.","plain":"Despite their strong arguments, we decided to refuse to change our position on the intellectual property clause.","note":"Implies firmness and determination. Can be positive (principled) or negative (inflexible).","audio":"audio/3323f48955754e0b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-streamline-processes","text":"to streamline processes","kind":"collocation","form":"streamlined processes","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To make procedures or operations more efficient and simpler.","example":"I was responsible for identifying bottlenecks and streamlined processes to improve workflow.","plain":"I was responsible for identifying bottlenecks and made procedures more efficient to improve workflow.","note":"Common in operational or project management roles; shows efficiency focus.","audio":"audio/7f3929cdaf4bec33.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-suggest-a-compromise","text":"To suggest a compromise","kind":"collocation","form":"to suggest a compromise","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To propose a middle-ground solution where both parties make concessions.","example":"Given our differing views, I'd like to suggest a compromise on the delivery timeline.","plain":"Since we have different views, I propose a middle-ground solution for the delivery timeline.","note":"A direct and constructive way to move past a disagreement.","audio":"audio/7bf56ccb5ba4d2dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-sum-up-then","text":"To sum up, then","kind":"chunk","form":"To sum up, then","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce a brief summary of the main points discussed.","example":"To sum up, then, our findings suggest a complex interplay of genetic and environmental factors.","plain":"In summary, our findings suggest a complex interplay of genetic and environmental factors.","note":"A common, slightly informal but acceptable way to conclude a point or discussion.","audio":"audio/cf5521fce0590e8e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarise-my-main-points","text":"To summarise my main points","kind":"frame","form":"To summarise my main points","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To provide a concise overview of the most important ideas presented.","example":"To summarise my main points, collaboration and adaptability are key to our success.","plain":"In summary, collaboration and adaptability are key to our success.","note":"A clear signal that the speaker is moving towards a conclusion by restating core messages.","audio":"audio/e505288ac4600bec.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarize-briefly","text":"To summarize briefly","kind":"chunk","form":"To summarize briefly","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a concise overview of the main points discussed.","example":"To summarize briefly, our findings suggest a complex interplay of socio-economic factors.","plain":"In short, our findings suggest a complex interplay of socio-economic factors.","note":"A common phrase for introducing a concise summary, often used at the end of a section or presentation.","audio":"audio/771b7c4123d2b1e9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarize-my-qualifications","text":"to summarize my qualifications","kind":"frame","form":"To summarize my qualifications","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to briefly list one's relevant skills, experience, and education.","example":"To summarize my qualifications, I have 10 years of experience in marketing and a master's degree.","plain":"To briefly list my relevant skills and experience, I have 10 years in marketing and a master's degree.","note":"A formal way to provide a concise overview of one's professional background.","audio":"audio/9ea41058ceae89d2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-summarize-our-position","text":"To summarize our position","kind":"collocation","form":"to summarize our position","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To briefly state one's stance or demands in a concise manner.","example":"Just to summarize our position, we require a 30-day payment term.","plain":"To briefly state our stance, we need a 30-day payment term.","note":"Used to reiterate key points, often before making a final statement or moving on.","audio":"audio/3f7a9f3cbffd9800.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-table-a-proposal","text":"To table a proposal","kind":"collocation","form":"to table a proposal","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To formally present an idea or plan for discussion at a meeting.","example":"I'd like to table a proposal for a revised payment schedule that might work for both sides.","plain":"I want to present a revised payment schedule for discussion, which could suit us both.","note":"Formal, common in British English for 'present for discussion'; in American English, it can mean 'postpone'.","audio":"audio/261066b735d9d20c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-different-tack","text":"to take a different tack","kind":"chunk","form":"take a different tack","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To try a different method or approach to a problem.","example":"Our current strategy isn't working; we need to take a different tack to solve this.","plain":"Our current strategy isn't working; we need to try a different method to solve this.","note":"Suggests a change in strategy or direction.","audio":"audio/52225b87bdd7bb27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-hard-line","text":"To take a hard line","kind":"collocation","form":"to take a hard line","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To adopt a strict and uncompromising position.","example":"The board has decided to take a hard line on intellectual property protection.","plain":"The board has decided to adopt a strict position on intellectual property protection.","note":"Implies firmness and an unwillingness to make concessions.","audio":"audio/864600008988ba25.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-measured-approach","text":"to take a measured approach","kind":"collocation","form":"take a measured approach","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To deal with a situation carefully and thoughtfully, avoiding hasty actions.","example":"This is a sensitive issue, so we should take a measured approach to our response.","plain":"This is a sensitive issue, so we should deal with our response carefully and thoughtfully.","note":"Suggests prudence and a well-considered strategy.","audio":"audio/a43a31f662334239.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-pragmatic-view","text":"to take a pragmatic view","kind":"collocation","form":"take a pragmatic view","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To approach a situation in a practical and realistic way, focusing on what works.","example":"Given the limited resources, we need to take a pragmatic view of what's achievable.","plain":"Given the limited resources, we need to be practical and realistic about what's achievable.","note":"Emphasizes practicality over idealism, common in business discussions.","audio":"audio/8949bd5cdc4fbad7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-a-proactive-stance","text":"to take a proactive stance","kind":"collocation","form":"take a proactive stance","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To adopt an attitude or position of acting in anticipation of future problems or needs.","example":"Instead of waiting for problems, we should take a proactive stance on risk management.","plain":"Instead of waiting for problems, we should adopt an attitude of acting in anticipation of risk.","note":"Similar to \"to be proactive\" but emphasizes the deliberate adoption of this approach.","audio":"audio/b9e0285d42e5529e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-initiative","text":"to take initiative","kind":"collocation","form":"took initiative","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"To act independently and proactively, without needing to be prompted.","example":"I often took initiative to identify process improvements, even beyond my direct responsibilities.","plain":"I often acted proactively to identify process improvements, even beyond my direct responsibilities.","note":"A highly valued trait in employees; shows proactivity and leadership potential.","audio":"audio/fe33525c63a08c00.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-it-or-leave-it","text":"To take it or leave it","kind":"chunk","form":"take it or leave it","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To present an offer as final, with no room for further negotiation.","example":"This is our best and final offer; you can take it or leave it.","plain":"This is our best and final offer; you can accept it or reject it as it is.","note":"Very direct and firm, often used as an ultimatum. Can be aggressive.","audio":"audio/d0bf4247d1aef0a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-responsibility-for","text":"to take responsibility for","kind":"collocation","form":"take responsibility for","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To accept the duty of dealing with something or being accountable for it.","example":"I will take responsibility for coordinating the next phase of the project.","plain":"I will accept the duty of coordinating the next phase of the project.","note":"A formal way to state one's commitment to a task or outcome.","audio":"audio/c763e3dab117fb73.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-take-the-plunge","text":"to take the plunge","kind":"chunk","form":"take the plunge","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To commit to a course of action after hesitation, especially a significant one.","example":"After much deliberation, we decided to take the plunge and invest in the new technology.","plain":"After much deliberation, we decided to commit and invest in the new technology.","note":"Suggests a bold decision after a period of uncertainty or careful thought.","audio":"audio/d0c4e99c535ee2a4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-test-the-waters","text":"to test the waters","kind":"chunk","form":"test the waters","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To try something out cautiously before committing fully, to see how it goes.","example":"We should test the waters with a small pilot program before rolling out the new software company-wide.","plain":"We should try a small pilot program before rolling out the new software company-wide.","note":"Implies a cautious, experimental approach to gauge reaction or feasibility.","audio":"audio/b4e72417fdb1d5dd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-that-end","text":"to that end","kind":"chunk","form":"To that end","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"For that purpose; with that goal in mind.","example":"We need to improve our customer service. To that end, we're implementing new training programs.","plain":"We need to improve our customer service. For that purpose, we're implementing new training programs.","note":"Formal and concise, used to link an action to a previously stated goal.","audio":"audio/67dafabda4a20ad4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-think-outside-the-box","text":"to think outside the box","kind":"chunk","form":"think outside the box","level":"C2","situations":["interviews","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To think creatively and unconventionally, not limited by traditional ideas.","example":"We needed to think outside the box to solve that complex client problem effectively.","plain":"We needed to think creatively to solve that complex client problem effectively.","note":"A common idiom for innovation; can sound slightly cliché if overused.","audio":"audio/e9403e9226d56780.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-thrash-out-a-deal","text":"To thrash out a deal","kind":"collocation","form":"to thrash out a deal","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To discuss and resolve all the details of a deal, often vigorously.","example":"We spent all morning trying to thrash out a deal on the new distribution channels.","plain":"We spent the morning discussing and resolving all details for the new distribution channels.","note":"Informal and energetic; suggests an intense and thorough discussion.","audio":"audio/e06f8a4479cc16e4.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-touch-base","text":"to touch base","kind":"chunk","form":"touch base","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To briefly communicate with someone, often to check in or update.","example":"Let's touch base again on Friday to see how the new process is working out.","plain":"Let's communicate briefly again on Friday to see how the new process is working out.","note":"Informal yet professional, used for quick check-ins.","audio":"audio/7f633cc2d2f80379.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-try-a-phased-approach","text":"to try a phased approach","kind":"collocation","form":"try a phased approach","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To implement a plan in stages rather than all at once.","example":"Given the complexity, we should try a phased approach to the software rollout.","plain":"Given the complexity, we should implement the software rollout in stages.","note":"A common strategy for managing large or complex projects.","audio":"audio/f27a203413df2715.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-understand-your-rationale","text":"To understand your rationale","kind":"collocation","form":"to understand your rationale","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To grasp the underlying reasons or logic behind someone's decision or proposal.","example":"It would help us greatly to understand your rationale for these revised figures.","plain":"It would help us a lot to grasp the reasons behind these revised figures.","note":"A polite way to ask for justification, showing respect for their thought process.","audio":"audio/f14393954793bb47.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-unpack-this-a-bit","text":"to unpack this a bit","kind":"chunk","form":"unpack this a bit","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To analyze or discuss a complex issue in more detail.","example":"This feedback is quite broad; let's unpack this a bit to identify specific actionable points.","plain":"This feedback is quite broad; let's analyze this in more detail to identify specific actionable points.","note":"Suggests a collaborative and detailed examination of a topic.","audio":"audio/44eb63020803df57.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-use-an-analogy","text":"To use an analogy","kind":"collocation","form":"To use an analogy","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To explain a concept by comparing it to something else that is similar in some way.","example":"To use an analogy, our project management software is like a conductor leading an orchestra.","plain":"Our project management software is like a conductor leading an orchestra, to explain it with a comparison.","note":"Explicitly states that a comparison is being made to clarify a point.","audio":"audio/d42abc1f032514f9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-view-it-broadly","text":"To view it broadly","kind":"chunk","form":"To view it broadly","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"To consider a situation from a wider perspective, not focusing on small details.","example":"To view it broadly, our market share has actually increased over the last quarter.","plain":"If we look at the bigger picture, our market share has actually grown this quarter.","note":"Encourages a wider perspective, often used to reframe a discussion.","audio":"audio/0d646edea864f7d6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-walk-away-from","text":"To walk away from","kind":"collocation","form":"to walk away from","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To abandon a negotiation or deal because the terms are unacceptable.","example":"If they're unwilling to compromise on the core issues, we may have to walk away from the table.","plain":"If they're unwilling to compromise on the core issues, we may have to abandon the negotiation.","note":"Implies a willingness to end discussions if conditions aren't met. A strong negotiating tactic.","audio":"audio/3c25413021c0c8d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-weigh-the-options","text":"to weigh the options","kind":"chunk","form":"weigh the options","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To carefully consider all available choices before making a decision.","example":"Before we decide, let's take some time to weigh the options and their potential impact.","plain":"Before we decide, let's take some time to consider all the choices and their potential impact.","note":"Implies a thoughtful and analytical approach to decision-making.","audio":"audio/13a7a1390b9c32f2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-weigh-the-pros-and-cons","text":"To weigh the pros and cons","kind":"collocation","form":"to weigh the pros and cons","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation","problem-solving"],"meaning":"To carefully consider the advantages and disadvantages of a decision or proposal.","example":"Before we agree, let's take some time to weigh the pros and cons of this revised payment structure.","plain":"Before we agree, let's take some time to carefully consider the advantages and disadvantages of this revised payment structure.","note":"Implies a thorough and balanced evaluation.","audio":"audio/2d14d8de0d7ff00c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.to-weigh-up-the-options","text":"to weigh up the options","kind":"chunk","form":"weigh up the options","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"To carefully consider all available choices before making a decision.","example":"Let's take a moment to weigh up the options before we vote on the proposal.","plain":"Let's take a moment to carefully consider all available choices before we vote on the proposal.","note":"Similar to \"weigh the options,\" common in British English.","audio":"audio/bf707e763b358ecc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.tread-carefully","text":"tread carefully","kind":"collocation","form":"tread carefully","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To proceed with caution and sensitivity, especially in a difficult situation.","example":"We need to tread carefully when giving feedback to new team members.","plain":"We need to be very careful when giving feedback to new team members.","note":"Advises prudence and circumspection in actions or words.","audio":"audio/96c738aa1de863c2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.truly-exemplary","text":"truly exemplary","kind":"collocation","form":"truly exemplary","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Serving as an excellent model or example; outstanding.","example":"Your dedication to client satisfaction is truly exemplary.","plain":"Your dedication to client satisfaction is an excellent example for others.","note":"High praise, often for behavior or attitude.","audio":"audio/3ef6072e65ff25e7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.truly-impressed-by","text":"truly impressed by","kind":"chunk","form":"truly impressed by","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"to feel genuine admiration for someone's actions or qualities.","example":"I was truly impressed by how you handled that difficult client meeting yesterday.","plain":"I was very impressed by how you handled that difficult client meeting yesterday.","note":"Strong, sincere praise. Often used by a manager or senior colleague.","audio":"audio/ad7a8c450f66ef7c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.ultimately-the-question-remains","text":"Ultimately, the question remains","kind":"frame","form":"Ultimately, the question remains","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To introduce a fundamental, unresolved question at the end of a discussion.","example":"Ultimately, the question remains how these theoretical models translate into practical applications.","plain":"In the end, we still need to know how these theoretical models translate into practical applications.","note":"Used to highlight a persistent, overarching question, often at the conclusion.","audio":"audio/325ff248c9ce5f19.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.underscore-the-importance","text":"underscore the importance","kind":"collocation","form":"underscore the importance","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize how crucial or significant something is.","example":"I want to underscore the importance of timely communication for the success of this cross-functional team.","plain":"I want to emphasize that communicating on time is crucial for this team's success.","note":"A slightly formal but common way to highlight a critical aspect; often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/837b5ce10f385307.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.underscore-the-need","text":"underscore the need","kind":"collocation","form":"underscore the need","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity or requirement for something.","example":"The recent security breach serves to underscore the need for stronger data protection measures.","plain":"The recent security breach highlights that we really need better data protection.","note":"A formal way to stress the urgency or importance of a requirement.","audio":"audio/8aa2b1eb9c6f0827.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.unlike-x-our-study","text":"Unlike X, our study","kind":"frame","form":"Unlike previous studies, our study","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Highlights a distinction by stating how one's own work differs from others.","example":"Unlike previous studies, our study incorporates a longitudinal design, offering deeper insights.","plain":"Our study is different from previous ones because it uses a longitudinal design, offering deeper insights.","note":"A direct way to emphasize the novelty or unique aspect of one's work.","audio":"audio/ff99743d9f0358cb.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.until-next-time","text":"Until next time","kind":"chunk","form":"Until next time","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite and friendly way to say goodbye, anticipating a future meeting.","example":"Well, it's been great catching up. Until next time!","plain":"Well, it's been great catching up. Goodbye until we meet again!","note":"Versatile, can be used in both formal and informal contexts.","audio":"audio/6d33d33af98bd5d3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.up-to-a-point","text":"Up to a point","kind":"chunk","form":"up to a point","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To a certain degree, but with specific limitations.","example":"We can be flexible on the payment schedule up to a point, but not indefinitely.","plain":"We can be flexible on the payment schedule to a certain degree, but not forever.","note":"Similar to 'to some extent', but often implies a clearer boundary or limit.","audio":"audio/8e14b77628052800.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.wanted-to-commend-you-on","text":"wanted to commend you on","kind":"frame","form":"wanted to commend you on","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To express formal praise or approval for someone's actions or achievements.","example":"I wanted to commend you on how you handled that difficult client meeting.","plain":"I wanted to praise you for how you handled that difficult client meeting.","note":"A polite and slightly formal way to give specific praise.","audio":"audio/48b75e8e9a09b8ff.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-can-explore-that-further","text":"We can explore that further","kind":"chunk","form":"we can explore that further","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To suggest a deeper investigation into a topic at a later time.","example":"That's a complex issue, and we can explore that further in a dedicated workshop.","plain":"That's a complex issue, and we can investigate it more deeply in a dedicated workshop.","note":"A positive way to acknowledge a complex question without getting bogged down during the presentation.","audio":"audio/4dd8a8ea05e855f1.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-can-look-into-that-more","text":"We can look into that more","kind":"chunk","form":"We can look into that more","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates that a topic can be examined in more detail at a later point.","example":"We can look into that more in the Q&A session, if time permits.","plain":"We can examine that in more detail later, if time permits.","note":"A polite way to defer a detailed discussion without dismissing the topic.","audio":"audio/f358dd7c2785c745.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-cant-go-any-lower","text":"We can't go any lower","kind":"chunk","form":"We can't go any lower","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"We have reached our absolute minimum price or offer.","example":"I'm afraid we can't go any lower on the price without impacting our profit margins.","plain":"Unfortunately, we cannot reduce the price further without affecting our profits.","note":"A common phrase to signal the limit of a price concession.","audio":"audio/452c5f7ca06aab60.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-could-explore-the-possibility-of","text":"we could explore the possibility of","kind":"frame","form":"We could explore the possibility of","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to suggest investigating a potential course of action.","example":"We could explore the possibility of extending the deadline if the team needs more time.","plain":"We could investigate the potential course of action of extending the deadline if the team needs more time.","note":"A diplomatic and collaborative way to suggest a new avenue.","audio":"audio/94e29f65d677b264.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-could-potentially-offer","text":"We could potentially offer","kind":"frame","form":"We could potentially offer","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To suggest a possible concession or proposal, indicating flexibility.","example":"We could potentially offer a staggered payment plan to ease your initial cash flow.","plain":"We might be able to offer a staggered payment plan to ease your initial cash flow.","note":"A soft, tentative way to introduce a new offer, leaving room for negotiation.","audio":"audio/fd18ecf56dd65720.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-have-time-for-one-more","text":"We have time for one more","kind":"chunk","form":"We have time for one more","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Informs the audience that only one final question can be answered due to time constraints.","example":"We have time for one more question before we need to wrap up.","plain":"We can only answer one more question before we need to finish.","note":"Manages expectations about the Q&A session efficiently.","audio":"audio/c3e9ef5be165f3e8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-havent-met-have-we","text":"We haven't met, have we","kind":"chunk","form":"We haven't met, have we","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"A polite way to initiate an introduction when you suspect you haven't been introduced before.","example":"We haven't met, have we? I'm Sarah from the marketing team.","plain":"I don't think we've met before. I'm Sarah from the marketing team.","note":"Common in professional or semi-formal social settings. Invites an introduction.","audio":"audio/601f218e4520105e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-keep-losing-each-other","text":"we keep losing each other","kind":"chunk","form":"We keep losing each other","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Notes that the call repeatedly disconnects or experiences severe interruptions.","example":"This is frustrating; we keep losing each other, maybe we should try another platform.","plain":"Our call keeps disconnecting, which is making communication difficult.","note":"Expresses shared frustration over persistent connection problems. Informal.","audio":"audio/8d613de63fbc18a8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-might-want-to-reconsider","text":"We might want to reconsider","kind":"frame","form":"We might want to reconsider","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To politely suggest a change of mind or approach, implying the current idea isn't optimal.","example":"Given the new data, we might want to reconsider our initial strategy for market entry.","plain":"Given the new data, we should think again about our initial strategy for market entry.","note":"A collaborative and gentle way to express doubt about a decision or plan.","audio":"audio/03da519d6484535a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-move-forward","text":"we move forward","kind":"collocation","form":"we move forward","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"We progress or continue with a plan or action.","example":"I'd like to propose that we move forward with the pilot program next quarter.","plain":"I suggest we continue with the trial program in the next quarter.","note":"Used to suggest progression or taking the next step in a plan.","audio":"audio/4b05a1bdf777efde.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-also-consider","text":"We must also consider","kind":"frame","form":"We must also consider","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces an additional factor or perspective that needs to be taken into account.","example":"We must also consider the ethical implications of this technology before widespread implementation.","plain":"We should also think about the ethical implications of this technology before implementing it widely.","note":"Signals the introduction of a crucial, often overlooked, aspect.","audio":"audio/50ac7490fc3ab294.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-be-careful-not-to-conflate","text":"We must be careful not to conflate","kind":"frame","form":"We must be careful not to conflate","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To warn against mistakenly treating two distinct things as if they were the same.","example":"We must be careful not to conflate correlation with causation in our statistical analysis.","plain":"We must be careful not to confuse correlation with causation in our statistical analysis.","note":"A precise warning, often used in analytical or scientific contexts.","audio":"audio/b53b624b4ba88d9c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-not-lose-sight-of","text":"We must not lose sight of","kind":"frame","form":"We must not lose sight of","level":"C2","situations":["academic","persuading"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the importance of remembering or keeping a particular point in mind.","example":"We must not lose sight of the human element when discussing technological advancements.","plain":"We must remember the human element when discussing technological advancements.","note":"A strong reminder to keep a crucial aspect in focus.","audio":"audio/8763f3fbd9fc579f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-must-remain-open-to","text":"We must remain open to","kind":"frame","form":"We must remain open to","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the necessity of being receptive to new ideas, evidence, or possibilities.","example":"We must remain open to alternative interpretations of the data as new research emerges.","plain":"We need to be receptive to alternative interpretations of the data as new research emerges.","note":"Encourages intellectual flexibility and a non-dogmatic approach.","audio":"audio/cff3a8a35c5cb1fa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-address-the-question-of","text":"We need to address the question of","kind":"frame","form":"We need to address the question of","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"States the necessity of discussing or resolving a specific query or issue.","example":"We need to address the question of how these findings translate into practical applications.","plain":"We must discuss how these findings translate into practical applications.","note":"A formal way to introduce a key question that requires attention.","audio":"audio/ecb3a8508c30006e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-clear","text":"We need to be clear","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be clear","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of clear communication or understanding.","example":"We need to be clear about the project's scope from the outset to avoid misunderstandings.","plain":"It's necessary to be clear about the project's scope from the outset to avoid misunderstandings.","note":"Stresses the importance of precision and transparency.","audio":"audio/7ece343ff300fc08.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-clear-that","text":"We need to be clear that","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be clear that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Used to state something unequivocally, ensuring no misunderstanding.","example":"We need to be clear that this decision will have long-term financial implications.","plain":"We must state clearly that this decision will have long-term financial implications.","note":"Emphasizes the need for clarity and directness on a particular point.","audio":"audio/9fb4563460cb4ef5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-flexible","text":"We need to be flexible","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be flexible","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of adaptability.","example":"We need to be flexible in our approach to accommodate unforeseen market changes.","plain":"We must be adaptable in our approach to accommodate unforeseen market changes.","note":"Encourages openness to change and new ideas.","audio":"audio/ed70ced290d3d436.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-mindful","text":"We need to be mindful","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be mindful","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of being aware of something.","example":"We need to be mindful of the cultural sensitivities when launching this product globally.","plain":"We must be aware of the cultural sensitivities when launching this product globally.","note":"Highlights the need for careful consideration and awareness.","audio":"audio/308046113e365702.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-prepared-for","text":"We need to be prepared for","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be prepared for","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of anticipating and planning for future events.","example":"We need to be prepared for increased demand during the holiday season.","plain":"We must get ready for increased demand during the holiday season.","note":"Encourages foresight and readiness for future scenarios.","audio":"audio/c73d46cc82eecbaa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-proactive","text":"We need to be proactive","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be proactive","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of taking initiative rather than reacting.","example":"We need to be proactive in addressing potential security vulnerabilities.","plain":"We must take action early to address potential security vulnerabilities.","note":"Encourages foresight and preventative measures.","audio":"audio/c63ed036ef051e82.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-be-strategic","text":"We need to be strategic","kind":"frame","form":"We need to be strategic","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of careful planning and foresight.","example":"We need to be strategic in how we allocate our marketing budget for maximum impact.","plain":"We must plan carefully how we allocate our marketing budget for maximum impact.","note":"Encourages thoughtful, long-term planning.","audio":"audio/04644506954d7eb8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-need-to-take-into-account","text":"We need to take into account","kind":"frame","form":"We need to take into account","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To emphasize the necessity of considering specific factors.","example":"We need to take into account the feedback from our early adopters.","plain":"We must consider the feedback from our early adopters.","note":"Highlights factors that must be included in decision-making.","audio":"audio/0aaba71b5349ca38.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-seem-to-be-diverging","text":"We seem to be diverging","kind":"chunk","form":"We seem to be diverging","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to point out that the discussion is straying from the main topic.","example":"I feel we seem to be diverging from the main objective of this meeting.","plain":"I feel we are moving away from the main objective of this meeting.","note":"Polite and diplomatic, used to gently guide the conversation back on track.","audio":"audio/a39e1abb84562373.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-seem-to-be-experiencing","text":"We seem to be experiencing","kind":"frame","form":"We seem to be experiencing","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates that there are technical issues affecting the call or video conference.","example":"We seem to be experiencing some technical difficulties with the platform today.","plain":"It looks like we are having some technical problems with the platform today.","note":"A polite, slightly formal way to acknowledge a problem, often used in professional settings.","audio":"audio/34f4e5f5e486d51f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-be-aware-of","text":"We should also be aware of","kind":"frame","form":"We should also be aware of","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional factor that requires vigilance.","example":"We should also be aware of the regulatory changes coming into effect next year.","plain":"We also need to know about the regulatory changes coming into effect next year.","note":"Highlights the need for awareness of external factors.","audio":"audio/f6ef61f85bc198cc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-be-mindful-of","text":"We should also be mindful of","kind":"frame","form":"We should also be mindful of","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional factor that requires careful consideration.","example":"We should also be mindful of the potential for negative public perception.","plain":"We also need to be aware of the potential for negative public perception.","note":"Highlights the need for careful consideration and awareness.","audio":"audio/e7e8f66c855f8c9f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-bear-in-mind","text":"We should also bear in mind","kind":"frame","form":"We should also bear in mind","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional important consideration.","example":"We should also bear in mind the feedback from our recent customer survey.","plain":"We should also remember the feedback from our recent customer survey.","note":"Adds another relevant point to the discussion.","audio":"audio/529979ebe3729d65.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-consider-the","text":"We should also consider the","kind":"frame","form":"We should also consider the","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional factor or perspective for discussion.","example":"We should also consider the ethical implications of using this new technology.","plain":"We also need to think about the ethical implications of using this new technology.","note":"Broadens the scope of the discussion, ensuring all angles are covered.","audio":"audio/358345069ea7652a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-factor-in","text":"We should also factor in","kind":"frame","form":"We should also factor in","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional element that needs to be considered.","example":"We should also factor in the potential for supply chain disruptions.","plain":"We also need to consider the potential for supply chain disruptions.","note":"Ensures all relevant variables are included in planning.","audio":"audio/8cc6edca1533bf20.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-also-take-into-account","text":"We should also take into account","kind":"frame","form":"We should also take into account","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To introduce an additional factor that needs to be considered.","example":"We should also take into account the feedback from our recent customer survey.","plain":"We also need to consider the feedback from our recent customer survey.","note":"Ensures all relevant variables are included in planning.","audio":"audio/d955a0329f7ee36f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-be-cautious-about","text":"We should be cautious about","kind":"frame","form":"We should be cautious about","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Advises carefulness or restraint regarding a particular action or conclusion.","example":"We should be cautious about extrapolating these results to different demographic groups.","plain":"We need to be careful about applying these results to different demographic groups.","note":"A common academic warning against overgeneralization or premature conclusions.","audio":"audio/98d3a3773cb66892.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-bear-in-mind","text":"We should bear in mind","kind":"frame","form":"We should bear in mind","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To remind others to remember an important fact or consideration.","example":"We should bear in mind the potential for unforeseen technical challenges.","plain":"We should remember the potential for unforeseen technical challenges.","note":"A slightly formal way to introduce a cautionary point.","audio":"audio/579ed63917a4e01c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-catch-up","text":"we should catch up","kind":"chunk","form":"We should catch up","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A suggestion to meet and talk about recent events in each other's lives.","example":"It's been ages, we should catch up over coffee soon.","plain":"It's been ages, we should meet and talk over coffee soon.","note":"A very common, friendly suggestion to reconnect with someone you haven't seen in a while.","audio":"audio/8f51dbcfbcd22b69.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-perhaps-acknowledge","text":"We should perhaps acknowledge","kind":"frame","form":"We should perhaps acknowledge","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Suggests that a particular point or fact ought to be recognized, often to add nuance.","example":"We should perhaps acknowledge the limitations of our sample size when interpreting these results.","plain":"We should probably recognize the limitations of our sample size when interpreting these results.","note":"A polite and diplomatic way to introduce a qualifying point.","audio":"audio/ae6505372dedc04a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-perhaps-consider","text":"We should perhaps consider","kind":"frame","form":"We should perhaps consider","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a suggestion for further thought or investigation in a polite and tentative manner.","example":"We should perhaps consider the ethical implications of applying this technology more broadly.","plain":"We should think about the ethical implications of applying this technology more broadly.","note":"A polite and collaborative way to suggest an idea or area for discussion, common in group settings.","audio":"audio/6bc67ca607df2e40.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-perhaps-qualify","text":"We should perhaps qualify","kind":"collocation","form":"We should perhaps qualify","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"To add a condition or limitation to a statement, making it less absolute.","example":"We should perhaps qualify our assertion by acknowledging the specific cultural context.","plain":"We should limit our assertion by acknowledging the specific cultural context.","note":"A polite and academic way to refine a previous statement or claim.","audio":"audio/d94b307ab6a54c39.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-prioritize","text":"We should prioritize","kind":"frame","form":"We should prioritize","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest focusing on certain tasks or goals over others.","example":"We should prioritize developing a robust cybersecurity framework for our new system.","plain":"We need to focus on developing a robust cybersecurity framework for our new system.","note":"Directs attention to the most important tasks.","audio":"audio/76ab0bdeca657af0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-probably-call-it-a-day","text":"we should probably call it a day","kind":"chunk","form":"we should probably call it a day","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Suggests ending the work or discussion for the day.","example":"It's getting late, so I think we should probably call it a day.","plain":"It is time for us to stop working or discussing for today.","note":"Informal and friendly, often used to end a long or tiring session. Common.","audio":"audio/51ec14f0f0c8409a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-strive-for","text":"We should strive for","kind":"frame","form":"We should strive for","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"Suggests an ideal or ambitious goal that a group should work hard to achieve.","example":"We should strive for continuous improvement in our service delivery and customer support.","plain":"We should work hard to constantly make our service and customer support better.","note":"Encourages ambition and a commitment to excellence.","audio":"audio/1d331875d7d101df.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.we-should-strive-to","text":"We should strive to","kind":"frame","form":"We should strive to","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To suggest aiming for a particular goal or standard.","example":"We should strive to create a more inclusive and diverse workplace environment.","plain":"We should try hard to create a more inclusive and diverse workplace environment.","note":"Encourages effort towards an ideal outcome.","audio":"audio/8ae8c187a77a4b02.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.wed-love-to-have-you","text":"we'd love to have you","kind":"chunk","form":"We'd love to have you","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A warm and enthusiastic invitation for someone to join or visit.","example":"We're having a small gathering next Saturday, we'd love to have you!","plain":"We're having a small gathering next Saturday, we really want you to come!","note":"A very common, friendly way to extend an invitation, showing genuine desire for their presence.","audio":"audio/720af9c0f4cd0558.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-circulate-the-minutes","text":"We'll circulate the minutes","kind":"chunk","form":"We'll circulate the minutes","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A statement indicating that the official record of the meeting will be distributed.","example":"We'll circulate the minutes of this meeting, including all action points, by tomorrow morning.","plain":"We will send out the official notes of this meeting, including all action points, by tomorrow morning.","note":"Standard professional practice, ensures everyone has a record of decisions and tasks.","audio":"audio/e4f3baeac810b440.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-delve-into-that-later","text":"We'll delve into that later","kind":"chunk","form":"we'll delve into that later","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To indicate that a topic will be discussed in more detail at a subsequent time.","example":"That's an excellent question, and we'll delve into that later in the Q&A session.","plain":"That's an excellent question, and we will discuss it in more detail during the Q&A session.","note":"Useful for deferring questions or complex topics to maintain presentation flow.","audio":"audio/8789a655ae8bc40f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-get-to-that-shortly","text":"We'll get to that shortly","kind":"chunk","form":"We'll get to that shortly","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Informs the questioner that their point will be covered very soon in the presentation.","example":"We'll get to that shortly when we discuss the implementation phase of the project.","plain":"We will discuss that very soon when we discuss the implementation phase of the project.","note":"A polite way to defer a question that is already part of the planned content.","audio":"audio/11ae009924a89370.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-need-to-revisit","text":"We'll need to revisit","kind":"frame","form":"We'll need to revisit","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To indicate that a topic or issue will require further discussion or attention later.","example":"This issue is more complex than anticipated, so we'll need to revisit it next week.","plain":"This issue is more complex, so we will need to discuss it again next week.","note":"Professional and practical, used to defer a topic for future consideration.","audio":"audio/bec603a6c422e3b7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-revisit-that-later","text":"We'll revisit that later","kind":"chunk","form":"We'll revisit that later","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Indicates that a topic will be discussed again in more detail at a subsequent time.","example":"We'll revisit that later when we discuss the long-term financial projections.","plain":"We will discuss that again later when we discuss the long-term financial projections.","note":"Useful for deferring questions or complex points without dismissing them.","audio":"audio/4281c82ba2053eb8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-at-an-impasse","text":"We're at an impasse","kind":"chunk","form":"We're at an impasse","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"To state that the negotiation has reached a point where no further progress can be made.","example":"It seems we're at an impasse regarding the project scope; neither side is willing to budge.","plain":"It seems we've reached a point where no further progress can be made regarding the project scope.","note":"Professional and direct, indicating a deadlock. Often precedes a call for a break or mediation.","audio":"audio/f72f77b844fa47e2.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-getting-off-track","text":"We're getting off track","kind":"chunk","form":"We're getting off track","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A gentle way to point out that the discussion is straying from the main topic.","example":"I think we're getting off track from our main objective for this meeting.","plain":"I think we are deviating from our main objective for this meeting.","note":"Polite and common, used to redirect the conversation back to the agenda.","audio":"audio/a427f9dce3d3d2dc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-having-some-connectivity-issues","text":"We're having some connectivity issues","kind":"chunk","form":"We're having some connectivity issues","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States that there are problems with the internet or network connection.","example":"We're having some connectivity issues, so I might drop off the call unexpectedly.","plain":"Our internet connection is bad, so I might get disconnected from the call.","note":"A professional and common way to describe general network problems.","audio":"audio/39aea61946d57acd.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-making-good-progress","text":"We're making good progress","kind":"chunk","form":"We're making good progress","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A positive statement indicating that work is advancing effectively.","example":"Despite the initial challenges, I feel we're making good progress on the project.","plain":"Despite the initial challenges, I feel we are progressing well on the project.","note":"Encouraging and positive, used to acknowledge achievements and maintain morale.","audio":"audio/0b3d4c4904356ddc.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-on-the-same-page","text":"We're on the same page","kind":"chunk","form":"We're on the same page","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To confirm that everyone shares the same understanding or agreement.","example":"So, just to confirm, we're on the same page regarding the project's priorities?","plain":"So, just to confirm, we all agree on the project's priorities?","note":"Informal but common, used to ensure consensus and avoid misunderstandings.","audio":"audio/73d3cd735fc98897.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.were-running-a-bit-short-on-time","text":"We're running a bit short on time","kind":"chunk","form":"We're running a bit short on time","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To politely indicate that there is not much time left for the presentation or Q&A.","example":"We're running a bit short on time, so I'll take just one more question.","plain":"We don't have much time left, so I'll take just one more question.","note":"A polite and common way to manage time and signal the nearing end of a session.","audio":"audio/a95b95368a54c7e0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.weve-reached-the-end-of-our-allotted-time","text":"We've reached the end of our allotted time","kind":"chunk","form":"We've reached the end of our allotted time","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"States that the scheduled duration for the call or meeting has finished.","example":"We've reached the end of our allotted time, so I'll have to let you go now.","plain":"Our scheduled time is up, so I need to end the call.","note":"A polite and professional way to end a call when time is limited.","audio":"audio/52b3e251ccf93b27.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.weve-run-out-of-time","text":"we've run out of time","kind":"collocation","form":"we've run out of time","level":"C2","situations":["meetings","calls"],"meaning":"States that the allotted time for the call or meeting has expired.","example":"Unfortunately, we've run out of time, so we'll have to continue this next week.","plain":"Our scheduled time for this discussion has finished.","note":"A direct but neutral way to end a meeting due to time constraints. Professional.","audio":"audio/30d813cdf6905721.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.weigh-in-on","text":"weigh in on","kind":"collocation","form":"weigh in on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"To contribute to a discussion or give an opinion.","example":"I'd like to invite Sarah to weigh in on the technical feasibility of this approach.","plain":"I'd like to invite Sarah to give her opinion on the technical feasibility of this approach.","note":"Implies adding a considered opinion, often an expert one.","audio":"audio/54d109e9f1ae6795.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-thats-certainly-a-development","text":"Well, that's certainly a development","kind":"chunk","form":"Well, that's certainly a development","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Acknowledges a new piece of information or event, often implying it's significant or unexpected.","example":"The CEO resigning without warning, well, that's certainly a development.","plain":"The CEO resigning without warning is definitely a significant new event.","note":"A neutral, often understated way to react to significant or surprising news.","audio":"audio/0d23a997175fb2f5.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-thats-certainly-something","text":"Well, that's certainly something","kind":"chunk","form":"Well, that's certainly something","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"An understated, often ironic, reaction to something unusual or significant.","example":"He just announced he's moving to Antarctica? Well, that's certainly something.","plain":"He just announced he's moving to Antarctica? That is certainly unusual.","note":"A common, understated, and often ironic reaction to surprising or odd news.","audio":"audio/075b892c556d71a3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.well-thats-certainly-unexpected","text":"Well, that's certainly unexpected","kind":"chunk","form":"Well, that's certainly unexpected","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the unexpected nature of news, often with a neutral or slightly negative tone.","example":"They decided to merge with our competitor? Well, that's certainly unexpected.","plain":"They decided to merge with our competitor? That is definitely not what we expected.","note":"A common, slightly understated reaction to surprising news.","audio":"audio/3bfce505e6ede4aa.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.went-above-and-beyond","text":"went above and beyond","kind":"chunk","form":"went above and beyond","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"to do more than what is required or expected.","example":"The way you supported the new hires really went above and beyond, thank you.","plain":"You supported the new hires more than was required, thank you.","note":"Expresses appreciation for extra effort. Common in professional settings.","audio":"audio/04613e40e3de58a0.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-a-pity","text":"What a pity","kind":"chunk","form":"What a pity","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses regret or sadness about something unfortunate.","example":"What a pity that your flight was delayed; I hope you still make it on time.","plain":"It is regrettable that your flight was delayed; I hope you still make it on time.","note":"A slightly more formal or classic way to express regret than \"that's a shame.\"","audio":"audio/0d73ad8b5ebd5891.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-a-relief-to-know","text":"What a relief to know","kind":"frame","form":"What a relief to know","level":"C2","situations":["reacting"],"meaning":"Expresses comfort or reassurance upon gaining specific knowledge.","example":"What a relief to know that the missing documents have been found.","plain":"It is very comforting to know that the missing documents have been found.","note":"A common expression of relief, emphasizing the knowledge gained.","audio":"audio/d15cf4eceb1b863b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-are-our-options-here","text":"what are our options here","kind":"chunk","form":"What are our options here","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A question asking for the available choices or courses of action.","example":"The client rejected our proposal, so what are our options here?","plain":"The client rejected our proposal, so what are our available choices?","note":"A direct question to initiate discussion about possible solutions.","audio":"audio/077594d40665c36c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-are-the-key-priorities","text":"what are the key priorities","kind":"frame","form":"What are the key priorities","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Asks about the most important tasks or goals that need to be addressed.","example":"What are the key priorities for this department in the upcoming quarter?","plain":"What are the most important tasks for this department in the upcoming quarter?","note":"A practical question for candidates to understand immediate challenges and focus areas.","audio":"audio/e199245f189cee94.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-does-success-look-like","text":"what does success look like","kind":"frame","form":"What does success look like","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Asks for a clear definition of what achieving goals or performing well in a role entails.","example":"What does success look like for someone in this position within the first six months?","plain":"How do you define achieving goals for someone in this position within the first six months?","note":"A strategic question for candidates to understand expectations and performance metrics.","audio":"audio/05936a673dc07a55.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-exactly-do-you-mean-by","text":"What exactly do you mean by","kind":"frame","form":"What exactly do you mean by","level":"C2","situations":["negotiation"],"meaning":"A direct request for precise clarification of a statement or term.","example":"What exactly do you mean by 'expedited delivery' in this context?","plain":"Please tell me precisely what you mean by 'expedited delivery' here.","note":"Used when a term or phrase is ambiguous and needs specific definition.","audio":"audio/f34f65f65956605d.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-i-meant-to-imply-was","text":"What I meant to imply was","kind":"frame","form":"What I meant to imply was","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Clarifies an earlier statement, explaining the intended underlying message.","example":"What I meant to imply was that the current system has inherent limitations.","plain":"My intention was to suggest that the current system has inherent limitations.","note":"Useful for clarifying a subtle or indirect message that might have been missed.","audio":"audio/c0d1d50e4a84a721.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-id-like-to-do-is","text":"What I'd like to do is","kind":"frame","form":"What I'd like to do is","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"To state your intention or preference.","example":"What I'd like to do is reschedule my train ticket for later today.","plain":"I want to reschedule my train ticket for later today.","note":"A polite and slightly indirect way to express a desire.","audio":"audio/e54af2e692578c6e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-id-like-to-do-now-is","text":"What I'd like to do now is","kind":"frame","form":"What I'd like to do now is","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"To introduce the speaker's next intended action or topic.","example":"What I'd like to do now is open the floor for any questions you might have.","plain":"I want to open the floor for any questions you might have.","note":"A polite and clear way to signal a transition, often to a different activity like Q&A.","audio":"audio/fa2a16598c48812a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-im-driving-at","text":"What I'm driving at","kind":"frame","form":"What I'm driving at","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to explain the underlying point or ultimate intention of one's statement.","example":"What I'm driving at is that we need to consider the long-term implications of this decision.","plain":"We need to consider the long-term implications of this decision.","note":"Informal. Used to clarify the main message or purpose.","audio":"audio/5eba0e657ae25f10.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-im-trying-to-convey","text":"What I'm trying to convey","kind":"frame","form":"What I'm trying to convey","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to express the main message or idea one is attempting to communicate.","example":"What I'm trying to convey is that flexibility is paramount in this evolving market.","plain":"Flexibility is paramount in this evolving market.","note":"Similar to \"what I'm trying to get across,\" but slightly more formal.","audio":"audio/9c43e94e37ea448b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-im-trying-to-get-across","text":"What I'm trying to get across","kind":"frame","form":"What I'm trying to get across","level":"C2","situations":["explaining"],"meaning":"to explain the main message or idea one is attempting to communicate.","example":"What I'm trying to get across is the importance of cross-departmental collaboration.","plain":"The importance of cross-departmental collaboration is what I'm trying to communicate.","note":"Used when you want to ensure your main message is understood.","audio":"audio/b08e8ce5f9346368.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-if-we-tried","text":"what if we tried","kind":"frame","form":"What if we tried","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to propose an idea or approach for consideration.","example":"What if we tried a different vendor for the raw materials to cut costs?","plain":"How about we try a different vendor for the raw materials to cut costs?","note":"An open-ended question inviting collaborative brainstorming.","audio":"audio/a6ff19f1360a5c2c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-time-suits-you-best","text":"What time suits you best","kind":"collocation","form":"What time suits you best","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"Asks for someone's preferred time for a meeting or activity.","example":"What time suits you best for our meeting on Tuesday?","plain":"What time is most convenient for you for our meeting on Tuesday?","note":"A standard, polite way to inquire about preferred timing.","audio":"audio/56dd182177e6a1b8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-weve-agreed-is","text":"What we've agreed is","kind":"frame","form":"What we've agreed is","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"Used to state the decisions or consensus reached during the meeting.","example":"What we've agreed is that Sarah will lead the new task force, starting next Monday.","plain":"We have decided that Sarah will lead the new task force, starting next Monday.","note":"Clear and definitive, used to confirm decisions and avoid ambiguity.","audio":"audio/e843aeb39b90a479.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.what-worries-me-is","text":"What worries me is","kind":"frame","form":"What worries me is","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"Used to highlight a specific anxiety or aspect of a situation that causes apprehension.","example":"What worries me is the potential for this issue to escalate if we don't act quickly.","plain":"I'm anxious about the potential for this issue to escalate if we don't act quickly.","note":"Emphasizes the specific element causing concern, often used when a problem is developing.","audio":"audio/9682d3ff7c2399ac.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-important-to-grasp-is","text":"What's important to grasp is","kind":"frame","form":"What's important to grasp is","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Emphasizes the key understanding or concept that the audience should take away.","example":"What's important to grasp is that this isn't a quick fix, but a long-term strategy.","plain":"The key thing to understand is that this isn't a quick fix, but a long-term strategy.","note":"Focuses the audience on the core message or insight.","audio":"audio/579949785a9e680a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-the-culture-like","text":"what's the culture like","kind":"frame","form":"What's the culture like","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Asks about the typical atmosphere, values, and behaviors within a workplace.","example":"What's the culture like here, and how would you describe the team dynamics?","plain":"How would you describe the typical atmosphere and team dynamics here?","note":"A common and important question for candidates to assess fit beyond job responsibilities.","audio":"audio/4cae7730ae87289c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-the-root-cause","text":"what's the root cause","kind":"collocation","form":"what's the root cause","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A question asking for the fundamental underlying reason for a problem.","example":"Before we implement any fixes, we need to understand what's the root cause of these system failures.","plain":"Before we implement any fixes, we need to understand the fundamental reason for these system failures.","note":"Focuses on deep analysis rather than superficial solutions.","audio":"audio/057147a4a787850c.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.whats-the-status-update","text":"What's the status update","kind":"collocation","form":"What's the status update","level":"C2","situations":["problem-solving"],"meaning":"A request for current information on the progress of a task or project.","example":"Before the meeting, could you send out a quick email with what's the status update on your tasks?","plain":"Before the meeting, could you send out a quick email with current information on the progress of your tasks?","note":"A common and direct way to ask for progress reports.","audio":"audio/3e771f583397b1e3.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.when-you-have-a-moment","text":"when you have a moment","kind":"frame","form":"When you have a moment","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask for someone's attention or to speak when they are free.","example":"When you have a moment, I'd like to discuss the upcoming presentation.","plain":"When you are free, I want to discuss the presentation.","note":"A gentle way to request attention without interrupting immediately.","audio":"audio/e3d3f132c74ea212.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-do-we-stand-on","text":"Where do we stand on","kind":"frame","form":"Where do we stand on","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A question asking for an update on the current status or progress of a particular issue.","example":"Before we conclude, where do we stand on the outstanding action items from last week?","plain":"Before we finish, what is the current status of the action items from last week?","note":"Direct and practical, used to check progress or get an update. Professional.","audio":"audio/417209d58f8fa41e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-i-excel-is","text":"where I excel is","kind":"frame","form":"Where I excel is","level":"C2","situations":["interviews"],"meaning":"Used to specify a particular area or skill in which one performs exceptionally well.","example":"Where I excel is in building strong client relationships and understanding their needs.","plain":"I perform exceptionally well in building strong client relationships and understanding their needs.","note":"A confident way to highlight a specific area of expertise or talent.","audio":"audio/83680adf80cd592b.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.where-was-i-going-with-that","text":"Where was I going with that","kind":"chunk","form":"Where was I going with that","level":"C2","situations":["presenting"],"meaning":"Asks rhetorically or to the audience for a reminder of the point being made.","example":"Where was I going with that? Oh yes, the importance of stakeholder engagement.","plain":"What was my point? Oh yes, the importance of stakeholder engagement.","note":"Informal and conversational, used to get back on track.","audio":"audio/c0465271e32d743a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.while-i-agree-up-to-a-point","text":"While I agree up to a point","kind":"frame","form":"While I agree up to a point","level":"C2","situations":["disagreeing"],"meaning":"To acknowledge partial agreement before introducing a point of disagreement or qualification.","example":"While I agree up to a point, I think the cost estimates are overly optimistic.","plain":"I agree with some of what you said, but I think the cost estimates are overly optimistic.","note":"A diplomatic way to show you've listened and found common ground before expressing a difference.","audio":"audio/fb605f3fc6de7350.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.while-x-focuses-on","text":"While X focuses on","kind":"frame","form":"While X focuses on","level":"C2","situations":["academic"],"meaning":"Introduces a comparison by stating what one entity emphasizes before presenting a different focus.","example":"While X focuses on individual agency, our research highlights systemic influences.","plain":"X emphasizes individual agency, but our research highlights systemic influences.","note":"Useful for setting up a direct comparison of different approaches.","audio":"audio/a7bf84b4c7445594.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.who-will-take-responsibility-for","text":"Who will take responsibility for","kind":"frame","form":"Who will take responsibility for","level":"C2","situations":["meetings"],"meaning":"A question to assign ownership of a specific task or outcome.","example":"Who will take responsibility for drafting the project proposal by Friday?","plain":"Who will be in charge of drafting the project proposal by Friday?","note":"Direct and crucial for assigning tasks and ensuring accountability.","audio":"audio/3692b99deab657db.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.with-the-best-intentions","text":"with the best intentions","kind":"chunk","form":"with the best intentions","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Done with good motives, even if the outcome isn't perfect.","example":"I know the decision was made with the best intentions, but it caused some issues.","plain":"I know the decision was made with good motives, but it caused some issues.","note":"Acknowledges good motives before delivering criticism.","audio":"audio/439b74c5c493dae6.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.without-further-ado","text":"without further ado","kind":"chunk","form":"without further ado","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Without any more delay or fuss.","example":"Without further ado, let's head to the airport to catch our flight.","plain":"Let's go to the airport now to catch our flight.","note":"Slightly formal, often used to transition to an action.","audio":"audio/8ffcb77040031e5f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.worth-bearing-in-mind","text":"worth bearing in mind","kind":"chunk","form":"worth bearing in mind","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"Something important to remember or consider.","example":"It's worth bearing in mind that our budget for this quarter is quite limited.","plain":"Remember that our budget for this quarter is quite limited.","note":"A gentle reminder or a subtle warning about a factor to consider.","audio":"audio/4c76bd7cbd436d3a.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.worth-revisiting","text":"worth revisiting","kind":"chunk","form":"worth revisiting","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"something that should be looked at or discussed again.","example":"I think the timeline for this project might be worth revisiting.","plain":"I think we should look at the timeline for this project again.","note":"Polite suggestion to reconsider something.","audio":"audio/ccefc0363796e4ce.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.worth-the-effort","text":"worth the effort","kind":"chunk","form":"worth the effort","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"Something that justifies the time or energy spent on it.","example":"The hike to the viewpoint was challenging, but definitely worth the effort.","plain":"The hike was hard, but the view made it worthwhile.","note":"Used to describe something rewarding despite difficulty.","audio":"audio/dafb5fa5d0eaf201.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.would-you-be-free","text":"Would you be free","kind":"frame","form":"Would you be free","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite way to ask about someone's availability for a meeting or activity.","example":"Would you be free for a quick coffee sometime next week?","plain":"Are you available for a quick coffee next week?","note":"A common, polite way to initiate plans. More open-ended than asking about a specific day.","audio":"audio/3fe258dc0ffca980.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.would-you-care-to-join","text":"Would you care to join","kind":"chunk","form":"Would you care to join","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A very polite and somewhat formal way to invite someone to an activity.","example":"Would you care to join us for a concert next month?","plain":"Would you like to join us for a concert next month?","note":"More formal than 'Do you fancy' or 'Would you like to'. Elegant and refined.","audio":"audio/8ba662329c4c99a7.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.would-you-mind","text":"Would you mind","kind":"frame","form":"Would you mind","level":"C2","situations":["travel"],"meaning":"A very polite way to ask someone to do something.","example":"Would you mind telling me where the nearest ATM is?","plain":"Could you tell me where the nearest ATM is?","note":"Extremely polite, often followed by a gerund.","audio":"audio/60cbdb77c25a9988.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.yield-positive-results","text":"yield positive results","kind":"collocation","form":"yield positive results","level":"C2","situations":["persuading"],"meaning":"To produce favorable or successful outcomes.","example":"I'm confident that this new marketing campaign will yield positive results for our brand awareness.","plain":"I believe this new marketing campaign will bring good outcomes for how well people know our brand.","note":"Used to express optimism about the success of an action or plan.","audio":"audio/7e646540bcc9754e.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-can-imagine-my-surprise","text":"You can imagine my surprise","kind":"chunk","form":"You can imagine my surprise","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"Invites the listener to empathize with the speaker's unexpected reaction.","example":"You can imagine my surprise when I saw him working at the coffee shop after all these years.","plain":"You can understand how surprised I was when I saw him working at the coffee shop after all these years.","note":"A common way to build anticipation for a surprising revelation.","audio":"audio/a5a6d37072642fab.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-could-say-that","text":"You could say that","kind":"chunk","form":"You could say that","level":"C2","situations":["small-talk"],"meaning":"Acknowledging that someone's statement is true, often with a hint of understatement or irony.","example":"\"It's been a challenging week.\" \"You could say that, I've barely slept.\"","plain":"\"It's been a challenging week.\" \"Yes, it has been very challenging, I've barely slept.\"","note":"Often used to agree with an understatement, implying the situation is worse.","audio":"audio/84077bd448f33746.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.you-should-have-seen","text":"you should have seen","kind":"frame","form":"you should have seen","level":"C2","situations":["storytelling"],"meaning":"expresses that something was so remarkable it needed to be witnessed","example":"You should have seen the queue for tickets; it stretched around the block!","plain":"The queue for tickets was incredible; it stretched around the block!","note":"Invites the listener to imagine the scene, adding vividness to the story.","audio":"audio/bb8217c8ff94dfd9.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youre-freezing-up","text":"you're freezing up","kind":"chunk","form":"You're freezing up","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Indicates that the other person's video or entire connection is temporarily stopping.","example":"I think you're freezing up again; your picture isn't moving at all.","plain":"Your video is stopping and starting, or your connection is pausing.","note":"Common for video calls. Similar to 'your video is frozen' but implies a temporary state.","audio":"audio/528413289b64f693.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youre-most-welcome","text":"you're most welcome","kind":"chunk","form":"You're most welcome","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A very polite and warm way to say 'you are welcome' or to invite someone.","example":"Thank you for the invitation! You're most welcome to stay at our place.","plain":"Thank you for the invitation! You are very welcome to stay at our place.","note":"A formal and gracious way to extend an invitation or respond to thanks. More formal than 'you're welcome'.","audio":"audio/047b2f8a93702cba.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youre-too-kind","text":"You're too kind","kind":"chunk","form":"You're too kind","level":"C2","situations":["socialising"],"meaning":"A polite and appreciative response to a compliment, gift, or act of generosity.","example":"Oh, you're too kind for bringing such a thoughtful gift!","plain":"Oh, that's very generous of you for bringing such a thoughtful gift!","note":"A common, slightly self-deprecating way to accept a compliment or gift graciously.","audio":"audio/b03427a26b393ce8.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.youve-raised-a-valid-point","text":"you've raised a valid point","kind":"collocation","form":"You've raised a valid point","level":"C2","situations":["feedback"],"meaning":"To acknowledge that someone has brought up a reasonable and important issue.","example":"You've raised a valid point about the potential impact on our budget.","plain":"You've brought up a reasonable and important issue about the potential impact on our budget.","note":"Acknowledges the merit of someone else's argument, even if you don't fully agree.","audio":"audio/93fad6216a348c7f.m4a"},{"id":"phrase.your-video-is-frozen","text":"your video is frozen","kind":"collocation","form":"your video is frozen","level":"C2","situations":["calls"],"meaning":"Informs someone that their video feed has stopped moving on a video call.","example":"Just to let you know, your video is frozen, so we can't see you moving.","plain":"Your picture on the video call has stopped moving.","note":"Direct and practical for video calls. Neutral tone.","audio":"audio/6f8609ccaa6cc751.m4a"}]}}